      "transcript_text": "Ok, let us talk about principle 2. Again, I will not reveal what this principle is all about. I will pause two choices before you and your job is to pick up one of the two choices depending on your preference. Here are the two choices. choices. Choice 1 entails receiving 1 lakh rupees exactly with 100% probability which means that there is no risk. Choice 2 entails receiving 2 lakh rupees with 50% probability and another nothing with 50% probability. Ok, if I were to ask you as to what is the same thing? with the expected value of the two choices, what would your answer be? As you can figure out, your expected value is essentially a probability weighted outcome. And here what is your expected value in the case of choice 1? It is simply going to be 1 lakh rupees multiplied by 100% or 1 which will come out as simply 1 lakh rupees. In case of choice 2, let us try to find out the expected value. Here the expected value is going to be 2 lakh rupees into 0.5 plus 0 into 0.5. Again, the expected value here also comes out to be 1 lakh rupees. So, your expected value is essentially the same in the case of choice 1 and choice 2. But what actually differs is the risk. Choice 1 does not entail any risk while choice 2 is risky. Let me put this question to you in an alternative way. Suppose you were employed and suppose your employer gives you two options to draw your salary. And suppose your employer says under choice 1, it is going to give you 1 lakh rupees month after month for sure. And under choice 2, it is going to give you 2 lakh rupees if the company does well and nothing if the company does not do well. Let us assume that there is exactly 50% chance of company doing well and another 50% chance of company not doing well. Put differently. In the case of choice 1, you are going to draw fixed salary of 1 lakh rupees month after month. In the case of choice 2, on the other hand you will receive all your salary as variable salary. Which of the 2 choices therefore would you prefer? Choice 1 or choice 2. Just think about it. So my guess is that a vast majority of you if not all are going to prefer choice 1 over choice 2. Can you think of a reason as to why you chose choice 1 over choice 2? You chose choice 1 over choice 2 because human beings in general are risk-a-verse because you are risk-a-verse and therefore you would prefer a safe rupee over a risky rupee at any given point in time. In other words we can say that a safe rupee is worth more than a risky rupee. Okay. Now let me ask you another alternative question. Given that a vast majority of you have preferred choice 1 over choice 2, my question to you is if I were to make you indifferent between choice 1 and choice 2, how will I have to model you? If I choose choice 2, what will I have to do to make you indifferent between choice 1 and choice 2? Maybe you might say that for you to be indifferent between choice 1 and choice 2, you will want a higher probability of receiving 2 lakh rupees. So let us say that your probability of receiving 2 lakh rupees goes up from 50% to 60%. And probability of receiving nothing goes down to 40%. Maybe in that case you will be indifferent between choice 1 and choice 2. But in this case what happened? Your expected value from choice 2 went up. What was your expected value from choice 2 now? Your expected value from choice 2 now is going to be 1 lakh 20,000 rupees. What does that tell you? And now you are indifferent between choice 1 and choice 2. What does that tell you? It tells you that in order to compensate for higher risk in choice 2 over choice 1, you required additional return to compensate for the extra risk. In other words, I can say that risk and return go hand in hand. If you as an investor were to bear extra risk, you are going to demand additional return or premium for taking over extra risk. In other words, this is what our principle 2 is all about. So this principle is about risk and return going hand in hand. So these were the two principles that I had in my mind and these two principles as I told you previously are already there at the back of your minds. And now we are going to make use of these two principles throughout our course.",
