      "transcript_text": "[music] Let me ask you one question. My question to you is suppose price of the bond was not given to you. Suppose yield to maturity on this bond was 4.25%. In that case what would have been the price of the bond? So essentially this price is now not given to you. So we are saying if yield to maturity r equals 4.25% what will your price be? Can you try to solve this using your calculators? If you solve this, you will find that your price comes out as €100. What did you notice? What is this 4.25%. This 4.25% 25% is nothing but it is your coupon rate. What is this 100? This 100 is nothing but it is your face value. And therefore what do you notice? You notice that essentially if your R is equal to your coupon rate in that case your price equals your face value. You must be wondering that it is coincidentally happening that when R is equal to 4.25% 25% our price is equal to the face value. You might not be able to believe me at this stage. And therefore let us try to find out as to what happens to the bond price in a very general case when the coupon rate equals yield to maturity R. So here what happens to the price of a bond when coupon rate is equal to yield to maturity R. Okay. Now we understand that how do we compute our coupon? Our coupon is equal to our coupon per period is equal to coupon rate per period multiplied by your face value. Isn't this how we arrive at our coupon every period? Yes. So let us say that we denote our coupon by C and our face value by F. It is given to us that our coupon rate is equal to R. So therefore I am substituting coupon rate with R. So what does this imply? So this implies that my C by R is equal to F. Okay. So let me call this as equation 1. Now you understand as to how do we find out our bond price? Our bond price P equ= C by R into 1 - 1 / 1 + RA to ^ T plus F / 1 + RA to ^ T. Okay. Now from equation 1, can I not substitute the value of C by R which is F? I can. So therefore what do I get? So my price equals f into 1 - 1 / 1 + r whole raised ^ t + f / 1 + ris power t. Let us expand this. What do we get? F - F / 1 + RA ^ T plus F divided by 1 + R whole raised to ^ T. So the second term and third term they get cancelled out. And what does that imply? That implies that your price is equal to F. Therefore, what happens when your coupon rate equals R? When your coupon rate equals R, your price is equal to face value. In a similar way, in a similar way, what happens when when your coupon rate when coupon rate is greater than r, in that case it will imply that your price is greater than face value. And similarly when your coupon rate is less than your yield to maturity R. This will imply that your price is less than F. So this brings us to classification of bonds depending on the price of the bond or depending on the yield to maturity of the bond. Okay. Now we have three kinds of bonds. So therefore let us talk about classification of bonds. depending on their price or yield to maturity R. So first of all as you understand it is very much possible that the price of a bond can equal to face value of the bot. In other words, the bond is trading at a price equal to its par value or face value. And we call such a bond as a par bond. So therefore, in the case of a par bond, price will be equal to face value. And we understand that when price is equal to face value, this also implies that your coupon rate, your coupon rate is equal to yield to maturity R. However, price of a bond need not equal to its face value. it is possible for price of the bond to be greater than its face value. So when the price of a bond is greater than its face value or in other words when the bond is trading at a price which is at a premium to its face value we call such a bond as a premium bond. we call such a bond as as a premium bond. So therefore in the case of a premium bond price will be greater than face value and when price is greater than face value it will imply that our coupon rate is going to be greater than yield to maturity in that case. And finally we have another case in which price of the bond is less than its face value which means that the bond is currently trading at a price which is at a discount to its face value and therefore we call such a bond as a discount bond. So therefore in the case of a discount bond price will be less than face value. And we know that when price is less than face value this will imply that our coupon rate is going to be less than yield to maturity. By the way, these three classifications that we have talked about, they also hold in the converse way. Which means that when coupon rate is equal to yield to maturity, in that case price will be equal to face value. Similarly, when coupon rate is greater than yield to maturity, then your price will be greater than face value. And finally when your coupon rate is less than r your price will also be less than face value. So here we have either side implies sign. I have one question for you. Now my question is is it possible for a premium bond to be a discount bond at some point during the life of the bond? Suppose the bond is premium bond today. Can this bond be a discount bond in the future is what my question to you is. The answer is yes. Why? Because price of a bond keeps fluctuating. Price of a bond keeps changing with changes in market conditions. Yield to maturity yield to maturity on a bond keeps changing with changes in market conditions. So therefore it is possible that today your coupon rate is higher than your yield to maturity and therefore the bond is a premium bond today. However, in the future it is possible that your yield to maturity might rise and therefore it is possible that your coupon rate will be less than your yield to maturity in the future. When your yield to maturity is greater than your coupon rate, what happens? Your price becomes lesser than your face value. In other words, your bond becomes a discount bond. So therefore, it is certainly possible for a premium bond today to be a discount bond to be a discount bond in the future and vice versa. >> [music]",
