      "transcript_text": "[music] >> Now let us talk about interest rate risk. Let us first of all look at yields of 10-year US Treasury bonds starting from 1900 until 2026. Here this plot shows you how your yields on 10-year US Treasury bonds have varied. As you can figure out there have been years when your yield was as low as 2%. On the other hand there have also been years when your yield has been as high as 14%. In other words your yield to maturity varies. And if your yield to maturity varies the price of the bond will also vary. Let us look at our bond pricing equation. We know that the price of a bond equals C by R into 1 minus 1 divided by 1 plus R whole raised to the power T. Plus F divided by 1 plus R whole raised to the power T. Here let us talk about this C. Is this C a constant or is this C a variable? This C is determined using coupon rate and face value and since none of them change, therefore the C is a constant. Similarly, if we talk about face value F, this face value F also does not change and therefore it is also a constant. Now, let us talk about T. With every passing period, your T is going to decrease. However, this decrease in T is going to be a predictable decrease. You understand that with every passing period, your T will come down by one. Yes? So, therefore, the only variable which is actually a variable which varies in an unpredictable manner in this particular equation is your R. So, this is the primary variable which actually varies in an unpredictable manner. And we understand that price of a bond is inversely proportional to yield to maturity. So, if the yield to maturity on a bond rises, the price will fall. And similarly, when yield to maturity on a bond falls, the price of a bond will go up. In other words, yields and prices move in opposite directions and we have seen in this slide that our yield to maturity varies with changes in market conditions. Now, you must be wondering, for example, how does yield vary with changes in market conditions? Let us take one example. Suppose there is a financial crisis or any other crisis in the economy. Whenever there is a crisis, typically, then there is flight to safety, which means that investors rush to buy safe assets. Can you tell me which is the safest asset in an economy? That asset which does not carry any risk. That asset is government bonds. Because government is the last entity which is expected to default on its obligations. And therefore, government bonds are the safest assets in an economy. So, when there is a flight to safety, when investors rush to buy safe assets, they will be buying government bonds during such a crisis. And therefore, what happens to the demand for government bonds? The demand for government bonds, as you can make out, it goes up. And therefore, now investors are going to be content with lesser expected return on government bonds. Or in other words, your price of the bonds, government bonds, is going to go up. So, therefore, whenever there is a crisis, typically, what happens is that your yield to maturity on government securities will come down and therefore their prices will shoot up. So, if you can predict a crisis, essentially you can make huge money. However, since you cannot predict crisis, therefore you cannot make money easily. Now, let us talk about interest rate risk. You understand that the price of a bond changes with changes in yield to maturity or interest rates. So, as interest rates in the economy fluctuate, the yields that investors demand to invest in bonds will also change. So, if the interest rates rise, the yield to maturity on the bonds will also rise and vice versa. However, you understand that not all bonds carry the same amount of interest rate risk. First of all, what is interest rate risk? Interest rate risk tells us how sensitive is the price of a bond to changes in interest rate or yield to maturity. The more sensitive the price of a bond to changes in interest rates, the greater the interest rate risk that the bond is carrying. Now, different bonds will carry different interest rate risk. Let me illustrate this by asking you a question. My question is, which of the following two bonds do you think carries greater interest rate risk? Is it 3-year zero coupon bond or is it 30-year zero coupon bond? Assume that these two bonds have been issued by the same issuer and these two bonds have the same face value. So, one is a 3-year zero coupon bond and the other one is a 30-year zero coupon bond. You have to tell for a given change in interest rates or yields the price of which of these two bonds fluctuates more. What do you think? The answer is 30-year zero coupon bond carries greater interest rate risk. Why? Let me illustrate that to you. You understand that the price of a zero a price of a zero coupon bond is simply going to be F divided by 1 + R whole raised to the power T because you do not get any coupons from a zero coupon bond. Now, you have two kinds of bonds. You have 3-year zero coupon bond and you have a 30-year zero coupon bond. So, what is going to be the price of three year zero coupon bond when your R is 0%? Let us say that the face value of each of the bonds is $1,000. So, in this case, your price is going to be $1,000 because your R is 0% even in the case of a 30 year zero coupon bond your price is going to be again $1,000. Now, let us see what happens when your R changes to 10% let us say. What will be the price of three year zero coupon bond? It will be $1,000 divided by 1.1 cubed. On the other hand, the price of a 30 year zero coupon bond will be 1.1 raised to the power 30. So, therefore, what do you observe? You clearly observe that $1,000 divided by 1.10 cubed is far far greater than $1,000 divided by 1.1 raised to the power 30. In other words, when our yield changes from 0 to 10% the price of which of the two bonds has changed more? Is it the three-year zero coupon bond or is it the 30-year zero coupon bond? Essentially, the price of a 30-year zero coupon bond has changed much more for the same change in interest rate relative to the changes in price of a three-year zero coupon bond. So, therefore, we can conclude that a 30-year zero coupon bond carries greater interest rate risk relative to an otherwise identical three-year zero coupon bond. And why is this happening? This is happening because the present value of a near-term cash flow is affected lesser by changes in discount rates than the present value of a distant cash flow. And therefore, what is our conclusion? Our conclusion is the price of a long maturity bond is more sensitive to changes in interest rates than the price of an otherwise identical short maturity bonds. >> [music]",
