      "transcript_text": "[music] So far we have talked about yield to maturity which represents your expected return from a bond as a bond holder. Now we will talk about actual return that you earn if you hold a bond for some period and this return we will call as rate of return or simply holding period return. How do we compute your rate of return? Your actual rate of return over a period you hold a bond is equal to your total income per period per rupee invested. In other words, your rate of return from a bond is total income divided by your investment. Now if you hold a bond for a period, what is your income going to be? Your income is going to come in the form of coupons and change in price. So you have bought the bond today. You are holding this bond for some time. At the end of your holding period, you will be selling off the bond. And before you sell it off, you might receive a coupon as well. So therefore, your total income from a bond is coupon income plus change in price. And if you divide this by your initial price, which represents your investment, this will give you the rate of return on your bond for this period. Now the question is how is this rate of return different from your yield to maturity. Now this rate of return which we are computing this is your realized or actual return over the holding period. On the other hand, if we talk about yield to maturity, that is your expected return. That is your return which you expect from a bond based on its riskiness. And clearly your expected return may or may not equal your realized return. And therefore your realized return is essentially backward-looking and your yield to maturity is forwardlooking. Let us look at an example. On May 15, 2008, the US government sold dollar 9 billion of 4.375% of bonds maturing in February 2038. 4.375% bonds maturing in Feb 2038. The bonds were issued at a price of 96.38% and offered a yield to maturity of 4.6%. On August 15, 2008, the bond made its first semianual coupon payment. By mid December of the same year, the price of the 4.375s of 2038 had reached 138.05% 05% of face value and the yield had fallen to 2.5%. So the question before you is very very simple. You have to compute rate of return on this bond for the 7 month period. We understand that our rate of return on a bond, it is equal to your coupon payments plus your change in price over your holding period. and this divided by your initial price. Okay. So let me denote our coupon by C. The total coupon that you are going to receive and let us say that you had purchased this bond for a price of P1 and the price at the end of your holding period let us say it is P2. So therefore your change in price is P2 minus P1 and this will be divided by your initial price which is nothing but P1. Now what is our P1 here? P1 is the price of your initial purchase of this bond. What is this price? So if you look at price in mid of May, price in mid of May 2008, how much was this price? This was 96.38%. And this is as a percentage of face value. And since we are talking about US government bonds, the face value is $1,000. So therefore, price at which you purchase this bond in miday 2008 is $963.8. Okay. What is your price in mid of December? What is your price in mid of December 2008? How much was that? It was $138.05% again of what? Of $1,000 of face value, which gives you dollar 1380.5. And during this period you have received some coupon payment. How much coupon payment did you receive? Your coupon payment depends on your coupon rate. What is your coupon rate? The coupon rate is given to you as 4.375%. Again since this is US government bond this pays you coupons semiannually. So therefore coupon rate every half year is 4.375% divided by two and this percentage is of face value which is dollar,000. Yes. So how much are you going to get as your coupon? So you received a coupon of 21.875 in mid of August and now you have to find your rate of return. So therefore your rate of return will be your coupon which is 21.875 875 plus change in price which is P2 minus P1 which is 1380.5 minus 963.8 this whole divided by 963.8 how much do you get as your rate of return over this 7-month period? It comes out as 0.4 4 55. Essentially, this is 45.5%. So, you got a return of 45.5% over this 7 month period. So, this represents your holding period return or actual rate of return over this 7-month period. Now I have one question for you. My question is can you look at this period we are talking about 2008 year. What happened in this year? So in this year global financial crisis set in and therefore as we discussed previously whenever there is a crisis how do investors behave? they rush to buy safe assets and US government bonds are perhaps the safest assets and therefore the demand for US government bonds went up and therefore when demand goes up what happens to your yield to maturity the return that investors require from these bonds now investors will be content with lower expected return on these bonds. In other words, your yield falls and your prices therefore shoot up. So that was the reason as to why the prices in mid of December 2008 became 138.05% of face value. So if you notice this bond was essentially issued at a discount at a discount to its face value but by mid of December this bond became a premium bond and if you knew to predict the crisis you could have made a huge money. Of course, having said that, we do not know how to predict a crisis.",
