      "transcript_text": "Now let us talk about how changes in yields affect your bond returns. So let us look at one example. A six-year government bond makes annual coupon payments of 5% and all the first a yield of 3% per year annually compounded at the time of issuance. Suppose one year later the bond yields 2%. The question before you is what return did the bond holder earn during the year? Now before we try to find rate of return we should try to put our cash flows on a timeline. So let us do that. Let us put our cash flows on a timeline. So this is a six-year government bond. So therefore at the end of every year you will be receiving your coupon payment. So this is a six-year government bond. So therefore at the end of every year you will be receiving your coupon payment. And let us say that for the sake of simplicity let us assume that our face value F is dollar hundred. You can assume any face value and it is not going to affect your answer. So therefore what is going to be your coupon every year? Therefore your coupon every year will be dollar five. Your coupon every year is dollar five. At the end of the sixth year which is your year of maturity you will also get your face value. along with your last coupon. So therefore you will get a total amount of hundred and five dollars. Now can we find out the price of this bond given that our R at this time was 3%. Yes can we find out our price at time t is equal to zero. when our R is 3%. What will that be equal to? That will be equal to C by R. C here is five. C here is five divided by 0.03 into 1 minus 1 divided by 1.03 raised to the power 6 plus 1000 divided by 1.03 raised to the power 6. So what do you get as your P0? You will get your P0 as 110.834 dollars. Now if you look at the question it says that the yield one year later has changed to 2%. So which means that so your yield one year later has become 2%. So can we not find out our price of the bond at the end of one year when your yield has essentially decreased to 2%. Now remember that at the end of one year from today this bond will mature in another five years. So therefore our time will reduce to five years. So therefore let us try to find out our price at the end of one year. The price at the end of one year therefore it will be equal to C by R which is 0.02 now 1 to 1 minus 1 divided by 1.02 raised to the power 5 now. It will not be raised to the power 6 because at the end of one year from today the bond will mature in another five years. Plus 1000 divided by 1.02 raised to the power 5. So how much do you get as your price at the end of one year from today when your R becomes 2% you will get 114.1400 dollars as your price. So now can we not compute our rate of return? What will be our rate of return on this bond? Our rate of return will be coupon that you are receiving at the end of one year plus change in price which is p1 minus p0 divided by your initial price of p0. So you are receiving a coupon of $5 plus change in price which is 114.1400 minus 110.834 divided by your initial price of 110.834. How much rate of return do you get? You will get 7.49% yes. So as you can notice this 7.49% this essentially is greater than your initial YTM of 3%. Therefore what conclusion do you draw from this particular example? The conclusion that you draw from this particular example is that if your yield to maturity decreases over a period your rate of return over the period will be higher than your initial YTM. Yes.",
