      "transcript_text": "So far, we have focused our attention largely on government bonds. Now we are going to talk about corporate bonds. Corporate bonds means bonds which are issued by corporations or companies. Let me ask you a question. My question is, can corporate bonds default on their payments to investors? Whatever payments, corporate bonds have promised to investors, can they default on those payments? The answer is yes. Corporate bonds can indeed default on their promise payments to investors. These payments that corporations have promised to bondholders, they represent the best case scenario. The corporation is never going to pay investors more than it has promised. However, when the times are not good, when the corporation is having a lot of difficulty, in that case a corporation can well default on its obligations to investors. So in other words, corporate bonds carry a risk of default or default risk as we simply call it as. You might have another question. If corporate bonds have a default risk, how do we judge the safety of corporate bonds? The safety of corporate bonds can be judged by credit ratings provided by credit rating agencies. For example, if we look at the US setting, so there we have credit rating agencies such as Moody's, Standard & Poor's and which. In the Indian setting, we have credit rating agencies such as Grissel, Care, Iqra, India ratings and Brickwork. Now based on these credit ratings which are given by credit rating agencies, we can broadly classify corporate bonds into two broad one is investment grade bonds and the second one is speculative grade or junk grade bonds. So what are investment grade bonds? Investment grade bonds are those bonds which are rated highly, typically triple B and above if we look at S&P and Fitch rating. On the other hand, speculative or junk bonds are those bonds which are rated double B and below. So obviously, speculative grade bonds are risky. In other words, they carry a greater risk of default. Now let me ask you another question. What kind of bonds do you think have higher yields? As you can make out, lower rated bonds have higher yields. Why? Because lower rated bonds carry greater default risk. As you understand, greater the riskiness of a bond, higher is going to be the return that investors expect from these bonds because risk and return go hand in hand. So if you look at the table here which shows you prices and yields of selected bonds in August 2023, what do you notice? You notice that as we go down, the S&P rating comes down from triple A to triple C. In other words, the risk of default goes up and therefore what happens to the yield as a consequence. So yield as you can observe, it increases which is not a surprise. Now let me ask you one another question. In addition to default risk, what other risks do you think affect yields on bonds? We have talked about one such risk before. That risk is interest rate risk. So as you understand, long term bonds whose prices are more sensitive to changes in interest rates carry greater interest rate risk. Other than interest rate risk, can you think of other kinds of risks? For example, we could have something like liquidity risk. What is liquidity risk? So essentially we understand that some bonds are less liquid relative to other bonds. What does that imply? That implies that less liquid bonds are more difficult and more expensive to trade in large quantities and at short notices. In other words, less liquid bonds carry greater liquidity risk. Why do you think yields on corporate bonds are higher than those of government bonds? yields on corporate bonds are higher than those of government bonds because corporate bonds carry greater risk relative to government bonds. But the question is it is which risk which is greater in corporate bonds than it is higher in government bonds. It is first of all default risk and then it is liquidity risk. Why? Because corporate bonds, as you understand, they carry a greater risk of default while government bonds do not carry any default risk. Because a government, even if it does not have enough money at hand, it can always print currency. And therefore, you expect government to be the last entity to be fault on its obligations. In other words, we can say that government bonds carry virtually no default risk. Also, if you compare corporate bonds with government bonds on liquidity risk, you will realize that corporate bonds carry greater liquidity risk. Corporate bonds are not as liquid as government bonds. And therefore, the yields on corporate bonds are higher than those of government bonds.",
