      "transcript_text": "Now we will talk about nominal and real cash flows and nominal and real interest rates. First of all, let us talk about nominal versus real cash flows. Suppose you invest rupees 100 in a one-year bond, that makes a single payment of rupees 110 at the end of one-year from today. Then, what is your nominal cash flow? Your nominal cash flow from this one-year bond is 110 rupees. Now, we understand that prices of goods and services in general can change over time. What do we call a rising prices as inflation? Similarly, what do we call a fall in prices as deflation? Okay, how do we track the general level of prices in an economy? We track the general level of prices in an economy through consumer price index, which is also referred to as CPI in short. But you must be wondering what is CPI? CPI is an index that measures retail inflation in an economy by tracking the changes in the prices of a basket of consumer goods and services. So, changing CPI from one year to the next year, measures the rate of inflation over the year. Suppose, prices rise over by the year by 7%. What does that imply? It implies that 1.07 rupees after a year will have the same purchasing power as one rupee today. In other words, 1.07 rupees that you are going to receive after a year can buy you the same amount of goods and services. as 1 rupee today. So, therefore, if you receive 110 rupees after a year, how much purchasing power will those 110 rupees have in terms of 2 days purchasing power? That will simply be 110 divided by 1.07 and what does that come out as? That comes out as 110 rupees per year. 102.80 rupees today. In other words, we can say that our real cash flow, which means cash flow in terms of 2 days purchasing power is 102.80 rupees. So, therefore, can we mathematically express the relationship between real cash flow and nominal cash flow? Yes. So, let us say that we denote our real cash flow at the end of your tea by CF real comedy, we define or denote nominal cash flow as CF nominal at the end of your tea by CF norm comedy and let us say I denotes our inflation rate. Our inflation rate. So, in that case, can we not express real cash flow at the end of your tea in terms of nominal cash flow and inflation? We can. What will that be? So, real cash flow at the end of tea years from today, that is going to equal your nominal cash flow at the end of tea years divided by 1 plus i whole raise to the power T. So, where i represents your rate of inflation and T represents the time when you are going to receive your nominal cash flow. For example, in this case, you understand that your nominal cash flow was given to you at the end of year 1 as rupees 110. You also understand that the rate of inflation in the example that we just discussed was given to you as 7%. So, from here could we have not found out our real cash flow at the end of 1 year from today? Yes. That would have been 110 divided by 1 plus 0.07 raised to the power 1. And if you solve it, it will give you rupees 102.80. Now, I must tell you that in real life, more often than not cash flows are given to you in nominal terms. In financial markets, we often express cash flows in nominal terms. And therefore, using this formula, we can always find out our real cash flow.",
