      "transcript_text": "Now let us talk about nominal and real interest rates. First of all, let us talk about nominal rate of return. You invested 100 rupees today and you are going to get 110 rupees after a year in nominal terms. Therefore, what is your nominal rate of return? Your nominal rate of return as you can figure out it is 10%. Now you understand that you invested 100 rupees and we computed our cash flow in terms of real terms at the end of year 1. What did it come out as? It came out as 102.80 in real terms and therefore since you invested 100 rupees today and you are going to get 102.80 rupees in real terms after a year. So therefore, what is your real rate of return? Your real rate of return therefore is 2.8%. Okay. What does it mean? What does that tell you? It tells you that a nominal rate of return of 10% is equivalent to a real rate of return of 2.8% when the inflation is 7%. Now can we express relationship between real interest rates, nominal interest rates and inflation rate? Yes, let us see what that relationship is. R real equals 1 plus R nominal divided by 1 plus i. So here R real represents our real interest rate. R norm represents our nominal interest rate and i represents our inflation rate. Now let us talk about the example. So in the example, we were given with our R nominal as 10 percent if you recall and our rate of inflation was given to us as 7 percent. So if these two are given to us, can we not find R real using this formula? Yes, we can. So therefore it implies that 1 plus R real equals 1.1 divided by 1.07. So from here this implies that our R real is going to be 1.10 divided by 1.07 minus 1. And if you solve this, you will get 0.0208. You will get 0.028 which is equivalent to 2.8 percent. So therefore it tells us that when our nominal interest rate is 10 percent and expected inflation over the year is 7 percent, then it translates to a real interest rate of 2.8 percent. Now let us try to approximate the relationship between real interest rate and nominal interest rate when your inflation is expected to be low. We understand that 1 plus real rate of interest equals 1 plus nominal rate of interest divided by 1 plus i where i is the inflation rate. So let us try to simplify this. So this implies 1 plus R real into 1 plus i equals 1 plus R nominal. Let us further simplify this. So this implies 1 plus i plus R real plus R real into i equals 1 plus R nominal. Now if you see here 1 gets cancelled out from both the sides and also this term is going to be very small when your inflation is low. And therefore as an approximation we can assume this rate to be 0, this product to be 0 when inflation is very low. So this implies that our real rate of interest i plus R real is approximately equal to R nominal. This further implies that our real interest rate is approximately equal to our nominal interest rate minus i. So this is an approximate relationship between real interest rate nominal interest rates and inflation when our inflation is low. In the previous case which we discussed just a while ago we understand that our nominal interest rate was 10 percent, our inflation was 7 percent and therefore our R real it would be close to 3 percent. Now when you want to arrive at either present value or net present value you can do either of the following. You can either discount your nominal cash flows using nominal discount rate or you can discount your real cash flows using real discount rate. However you should not discount your nominal cash flows using real discount rate or real cash flows using nominal discount rate. So as long as you are going to treat inflation consistently you are going to get the same PV or NPV. It does not matter whether you discount nominal cash flows using nominal discount rate or real cash flows using real discount rate. Our PV or NPV is going to be the same.",
