      "created_at": "2026-11-04 08:38:00",
      "transcript_text": "Now let us talk about inflation index bonds. You understand that most bonds promise you a fixed nominal rate of interest. That is coupon payments and payment of face value at maturity are fixed in nominal terms. However, we do have something called as inflation index bonds. Inflation index bonds make it possible to earn a fixed real rate of interest. Which means that coupon payment that you are going to get period after period will remain constant in real terms. And similarly, payment of face value which you are going to get at the time of maturity that will remain fixed in terms of real terms. However, in terms of nominal value it will be different. Now what are the examples of inflation index bonds in India and in other countries? In the US, the US we have something called as Treasury inflation protected securities or tips which are essentially your inflation index bonds. Similarly, in the Indian setting we have something called as inflation index bonds or IIBs. Now as we discussed previously, real cash flows on such bonds are fixed. But nominal cash flow is not the same. Those will increase as the CPI increases. Let us take one example. Suppose that the US Treasury issues 3 percent 20 year tips at a price equal to its face value of dollar thousand. If during the first year the CPI rises by 10 percent then what would happen to your coupon payment? Your coupon payment on the bond will increase by 10 percent and it will be 30 into 1.1 which is going to be 33 dollars. Why did we have a coupon of 30 dollars? Because this is a Treasury bond which is carrying a coupon rate of 3 percent. Now how much amount would you receive at the time of maturity? In addition to your coupon you will receive face value of dollar thousand in real terms which means that you are going to receive 1000 into 1.1 which is going to be 1100 dollars at the time of maturity. The purchasing power of the coupon and face value remains constant. And what is that? It is 33 dollars divided by 1.10 which comes out as dollar 30. And also if you look at your face value what does that come out as? Since you are going to receive 1100 if you divide it by 1.1 that gives you 1000 dollars. So therefore it tells you that your cash flows in real terms remain constant in an inflation index bond. However your nominal cash flow will change with changes in CPI or inflation.",
