      "transcript_text": "Now we are going to talk about another financial asset which is a stock or a share. We will talk about trading in shares and valuation of shares. First of all, let us delve ourselves into trading in shares. Even before we move into trading in shares, let me tell you, whenever you want to trade any security in the financial market, you often have to deal with intermediaries. For example, when you want to buy or sell a bond, more often than not, you have to deal with a bond dealer. Similarly, when you want to buy or sell a share, you have to deal with a stock broker. And therefore, it is important for us to understand as to what is the difference between a bond dealer and a stock broker. Before, we try to discuss these differences between a bond dealer and a stock broker. Let me ask you, one simple question. question. All of you would have come across dealers and brokers in your day to day lives, isn't it? So, first of all, let us try to see as to how a dealer is different from a broker. And then we will talk our, the differences between a bond dealer and a stock broker. So, let us say that we are talking about a car dealer and a real estate broker. Can you think of as to how a car dealer is different from a real estate broker? Let me give you a hint. Who holds an inventory? As you can figure out, car dealer holds an inventory of cars. While a real estate broker does not hold an inventory of houses, a real estate broker simply brings buyers and sellers together. So, therefore, our first difference is in terms of inventory. A car dealer or any dealer for that matter holds an inventory, while the broker does not hold an inventory. Now, can you tell me, how do car dealers make money? Car dealers, as you can make out, they buy cars at a lower price from automobile companies and then they sell them at higher prices to consumers. In other words, car dealers or any dealer for that matter buys low and sells high. And the difference between the selling price and the buying price is the profit a dealer makes. The source of income in the case of a car dealer, it is your selling price minus buying price. In financial market, the price at which a dealer is willing to sell is what we call as the ask price. As we discussed previously, and the price at which a dealer is willing to buy a given asset is called as the bid price. So, essentially, the source of income for a dealer is the difference between the ask price and the bid price. And this difference is also called as something called as bid ask spread. This is called as bid ask spread. On the other hand, how does a real estate broker make money? A real estate broker, as you can figure out, makes money through brokerage commission. Brokerage commission is essentially a source of income for a real estate broker or for that matter any kind of broker. I hope this makes the difference between a dealer and a broker, amply clear. And these are precisely the differences between a bond dealer and a stock broker. A bond dealer maintains an inventory of bonds, while a stock broker does not maintain an inventory of stocks. Similarly, if we look at the source of income for a bond dealer that source of income is bid ask spread, while the major source of income for a stock broker is brokerage commission. Also, as we discussed previously, bonds are usually traded in the dealer markets. On the other hand, if we talk about shares or stocks, they are traded in the broker markets. Now, our stock market consists of a primary market and a secondary market. What is a primary market? A primary market is that market where new shares are brought and sold to investors. So, essentially, in the primary market, companies sell shares to raise money. What is a secondary market? In the secondary market, existing shares are traded among investors. Investors do not trade directly among themselves. Usually, the orders go through a broker. So, if we talk about our secondary markets, in the US, we have New York Stock Exchange and Nasdaq as principal stock exchanges or principal secondary markets. On the other hand, in the Indian setting, we have NSC and BSC as our principal stock exchanges. And these stock exchanges, as you can figure out, they are our secondary markets because on these markets, existing shares are traded among investors.",
