      "notes_markdown": "# CF M03 C24 Applying Market Orders to a Limit Order Book\n\n## 1. Executive Mental Model & Core Intuition\nThis lecture explains how market orders execute against a limit order book. A limit order book displays bid prices (buyers) and ask prices (sellers). Bid prices are arranged in descending order so the highest bid appears at the top. Ask prices are arranged in ascending order so the lowest ask appears at the top. Market orders execute immediately at the best available price: a market sell order executes at the highest bid price, while a market buy order executes at the lowest ask price. The core intuition is that market orders prioritize speed and certainty of execution over price improvement.\n\n## 2. Concise Point-Wise Conceptual Mechanics (Cause & Effect)\n* **Limit Order Book Structure:** The bid side lists buyers willing to purchase shares at various prices, ordered from highest to lowest. The ask side lists sellers willing to sell shares at various prices, ordered from lowest to highest.\n* **Parameter Shift: Market Sell Order → Execution Price:** When an investor places a market sell order, the execution price is determined by the highest bid price in the book. This is because the seller seeks the highest possible price.\n* **Parameter Shift: Market Buy Order → Execution Price:** When an investor places a market buy order, the execution price is determined by the lowest ask price in the book. This is because the buyer seeks the lowest possible price.\n* **Real-World Example from Lecture:** Using Boeing's limit order book from November 20, 2017. Bid prices were $263.76 (1100 shares) and $263.73 (100 shares). Ask prices were $264.07 (200 shares) and $264.12 (1 share).\n\n## 3. Conceptual Distinctions & Versus Matrix\n| Dimension | Market Order | Limit Order | MCQ Diagnostic |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Execution Guarantee | Executes immediately at best available price | Executes only if price condition met | Examiner tests whether student understands execution certainty vs. price control. |\n| Price Certainty | No price guarantee; executes at prevailing market price | Price guaranteed or better if limit reached | Examiner tests recognition of trade-offs between speed and price. |\n| Order Book Interaction | Hits the best bid or ask immediately | Provides liquidity to the order book | Examiner tests understanding of order flow dynamics. |\n| Investor Intent | Prioritize speed of execution | Prioritize price target | Examiner tests behavioral motive identification. |\n\n## 4. Key Formulas & Numerical Application\n*Conceptual Session: No mathematical formulas introduced in this lecture.*\n\nThe lecture focuses on the mechanics of order execution using real-time price data from a limit order book. The primary learning objective is to understand the directional relationship between order type and execution price.\n\n## 5. MCQ Trap Radar & Examiner Gotchas\n* **Trap 1: Confusing Bid and Ask Sides.** Students often reverse which side a market order looks at. A market sell order looks at the bid side; a market buy order looks at the ask side.\n* **Trap 2: Assuming the First Listed Price is Always the Execution Price.** The table arranges bids in descending order and asks in ascending order. The highest bid is at the top of the bid column; the lowest ask is at the top of the ask column. Examiners set distractors using prices from deeper in the order book.\n* **Trap 3: Ignoring Order Book Depth.** Execution occurs at the best available price, but the order size must be accommodated. If a market order exceeds the size at the best price, it will \"walk down\" the order book, executing at progressively worse prices.",
