      "transcript_text": "Now, let us try to look at one example based on this. So, let us look at this example. XYZ company will pay dividends of dollar 3.24 and dollar 3.5 at the end of 1, 2 and 3 years from today respectively. The stock is expected to sell for $94.48 at the end of 3 years from today. Given the cost of equity as 12% per year, you have to find out the current price of the stock. So, as a first step, let us try to put our cash flows on a timeline. So, here, as you can figure out, your holding period is 3 years. At the end of 1 year, from today, you will receive Dye 1, which is dividend at the end of first year. At the end of year 2, you will receive a dividend of Dye 2. At the end of year 3, you will receive a dividend of Dye 3 and after which the stock is expected to sell at a price of P3. Therefore, in this case, our current stock price will simply be Dye 1 divided by 1 plus R plus Dye 2 divided by 1 plus R whole square plus Dye 3 divided by 1 plus R whole cube. So, what is our R here? If you look at the problem, our R is given to us as 12%. What is our Dye 1 here? Our Dye 1 is 3. 3 dollars. So, 3 divided by 1.12 plus what is our Dye 2? It is 3.24 divided by 1.12 square. What is our Dye 3? It is 3.5. So, therefore, this particular ratio will be 3.5 divided by 1.12 cube. And finally, what is our P3? Our P3 is 94.48 dollars and we will discount this to find out its present value, which means that we will divide it by 1.12 cube. What does that give us? That gives us 75. So, it tells us that our current stock price should be 75 dollars. Hope this example makes the point clear that the current stock price is equal to present value of dividends expected until H years and then you will add to it the present value of your terminal value at the end of H years.",
