      "notes_markdown": "# CF M03 C35 Challenges of Stock Valuation by Fundamentals\n\n## 1. Executive Mental Model & Core Intuition\nStock valuation by fundamentals relies on estimating future cash flows to shareholders. A primary challenge arises when companies do not pay cash dividends. For example, growing companies like Amazon have never paid a cash dividend, making it very difficult to estimate cash dividends far into the future. This creates a fundamental problem for valuation models that depend on dividend streams. Furthermore, even when dividends are expected, the valuation estimate is highly sensitive to the assumed dividend growth rate. A small error in this growth rate assumption leads to large changes in the estimated stock price, meaning the valuation is only as correct as the underlying growth assumptions. Finally, companies may return cash to shareholders through share repurchases rather than cash dividends. Repurchases are far more irregular than dividends, making it difficult to forecast cash flows from repurchases, which complicates the application of discounted cash flow valuation methods when payouts take this form.\n\n## 2. Concise Point-Wise Conceptual Mechanics (Cause & Effect)\n* **Challenge 1: Long Delay in Dividends.** When cash dividends are far into the future, this method of stock valuation becomes difficult to use. Companies like Amazon, which have never paid a cash dividend, illustrate this problem clearly.\n* **Challenge 2: Sensitivity to Growth Rate Assumptions.** There is significant uncertainty in forecasting firm dividend growth rates and future dividends. Even small changes in the assumed dividend growth rate G can lead to large changes in the estimated stock price. The estimated stock price is very much sensitive to the dividend growth rate assumption; a small error in this growth rate will lead to large changes in the estimated stock price.\n* **Challenge 3: Irregularity of Share Repurchases.** A company may not always pay out cash in the amount of cash that the firm of dividends. Companies can also pay out cash through repurchase of shares. Repurchases are far more irregular than dividends. Therefore, it is very difficult to forecast cash flows from repurchases than cash flows from dividends. Consequently, it will be very difficult to apply the discounted cash flow valuation method when cash is paid out in the form of repurchases rather than cash dividends.\n\n## 3. Conceptual Distinctions & Versus Matrix\n| Dimension | Concept A | Concept B | MCQ Diagnostic |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Payout Form | Cash Dividends | Share Repurchases | Examiner tests ability to distinguish regular dividend streams from irregular repurchase events in valuation sensitivity. |\n| Forecast Certainty | Higher certainty for established dividends | Lower certainty due to irregular timing | Examiner tests recognition of forecast difficulty. |\n| Price Sensitivity | Less sensitive to single-period growth changes | Highly sensitive to growth rate G assumptions | Examiner tests understanding of parameter sensitivity in the constant growth formula. |\n\n## 4. Key Formulas & Numerical Application\n*Conceptual Session: No mathematical formulas introduced in this lecture.*\n\nThe lecture focuses on the practical challenges and limitations of applying fundamental valuation methods. It does not introduce new mathematical formulas but rather discusses the qualitative difficulties in estimation and forecasting.\n\n## 5. MCQ Trap Radar & Examiner Gotchas\n* **Trap 1: The \"No Dividend\" Fallacy.** Assuming a company that never pays dividends cannot be valued. The examiner tests whether students recognize that valuation methods must adapt when dividends are absent, turning to other cash flow signals.\n* **Trap 2: The Small Growth Error Trap.** Believing that a 1% error in the dividend growth rate G has a negligible impact on stock price. The examiner tests awareness that the valuation is highly sensitive to G; a small error leads to large price changes.\n* **Trap 3: The Dividend vs. Repurchase Substitution Trap.** Assuming repurchases and dividends are interchangeable in valuation models. The examiner tests the understanding that repurchases are irregular and far more difficult to forecast than dividends, complicating DCF application.",
