      "transcript_text": "Now, what do you think are challenges to the use of stock valuation by fundamentals? There are couple of challenges which are obvious. The first one is, this method of stock valuation is difficult to use when cash dividends are far into the future. For example, there are certain companies which have never ever paid a cash dividend. There are growing companies like Amazon who have never paid a cash dividend and therefore it is very difficult to estimate cash dividends from such companies far into the future. Now, another challenge with this approach is that there is a lot of uncertainty associated with forecasting of firms dividend growth rate and future dividends. For example, you understand that if we have a constant growth stock in such a case, your current stock price equals dividend received at the end of the first year which is expected dividend of course divided by R minus G. Now, the problem here is that even small changes, even small changes in the assumed dividend growth rate G can lead to large changes in the estimated stock price. In other words, your estimate of stock price is as correct as your stock price or assumptions and your estimate of stock price is very much sensitive to your dividend growth rate assumption. Even a small error in this growth rate will lead to large changes in the estimated stock price. And finally, one last challenge which we encounter using this approach is that a company may not always pay out cash in the amount of cash that the firm of dividends. A company can also pay out cash through repurchase of shares. Now, if the company is paying out cash through repurchase is they are not going to invalidate the discounted cash flow valuation model which we have talked about. However, you must remember that repurchase are far more irregular than dividends. And therefore, the amount of cash that is very difficult to forecast cash flows from repurchases than cash flows from dividends. In other words, it will be very difficult for us to apply discounted cash flow valuation method when our cash is going to be paid out to us in the form of repurchases rather than cash dividends.",
