      "transcript_text": "Now let us talk about another technique. Let us talk about Payback Period. Payback Period as the name implies, it represents the number of fears it takes you to recover your initial investment from the project. What is your acceptance criterion? You will accept those projects that will pay you back within a certain period. In other words, your payback period from the project should be less than or equal to some specified cut of period. Now the question is, who decides this specific cut of period? This specific cut of period is decided by the management. In other words, you do not have a very clear benchmark for project acceptance. This benchmark depends on the taste and preferences of managers. Okay now let us talk about some of the advantages of the Payback Period method. The first is payback is easy to understand and easy to compute. This particular method is biased towards liquidity. In other words, this will favor those projects which are going to pay us back quickly. However, payback has a number of disadvantages. You understand that we do not have a clear benchmark for project acceptance. That is one of the shortcomings of the payback period technique. Another shortcoming is that it is biased against long term projects which essentially implies that it ignores all cash flows received after the cut of period. Another thing which we are already familiar is that it ignores the time value of money. Which means what? Which means that it gives the money. That is equal weightage to all cash flows before the cut of period. Let us look at one example. So, as you can see in this table, we have three projects. Projects A, B and C. Each of these projects involve an initial investment of $2000 today. Projects A is going to give you $500 at the end of year one, another 500 at the end of year two and $5000 at the end of year three. Projects B is going to give you $500 at the end of year one. And 1800 at the end of year two. And finally, Projects C is going to give you $1800 at the end of year one and $500 at the end of year two. Now, can you compute the payback period and NPV for each of the projects? You can assume the opportunity cost of capital for each project as 10%. What is your payback period from project A? How many years does it take you to recover your initial investment of $2000 from project A? So, as you can see, you will be able to recover your initial investment of $2000 from project A by the end of year three. So, your payback period for project A is three years. For project B, it is two years and for project C, it is again two years. Now, what is your NPV for project A? Your NPV for project A is $2624 for project B, it is negative $58 and for project C, it is plus $50. Now, can you tell me what mistake you think we would make if we insist on only taking projects with a payback period of two years or less? Which projects would be accept using payback period technique in this case? So, we will clearly accept projects B and C and we will reject project A. And therefore, what is the mistake we are making here? Remember that NPV is the most superior of all the capital budgeting techniques. If you compare our decision of project acceptance or rejection based on NPV, what would that be? You would clearly accept projects A and C using NPV and reject project B. So, therefore, when you use payback period as your capital budgeting technique, what is the mistake that you are making? In this case, as you can figure out, you are rejecting project A which you should have accepted and you are accepting project B which you should have rejected. Now, therefore, based on this example, what disadvantage is of the payback period? Do you notice? So, as you can see, this particular method is ignoring all cash flows from project A after two years. You understand that you are getting $5,000 from project A at the end of year three which has been ignored by the payback period technique. In other words, payback period technique ignores all cash flows after the cut of date. What is the other disadvantage? You notice about the payback period technique from this example. You notice that payback period gives equal weightage to cash flows before the cut of period. For example, it does not matter whether you receive $500 in year one and $1800 in year two or you receive $1800 in year one and $500 in year two. In other words, payback period method ignores the time value of money.",
