      "transcript_text": "Let us now move on to Internal Rate of Return or IRR. What is IRR? IRR is that discount rate which makes NPV equal to 0. Okay. Let me ask you a simple question. Suppose you invest 100 rupees today and suppose you are going to get 150 rupees after a year. How much is your rate of return in this case? As you can make out mentally, if you invest 100 rupees today and if you are going to get 150 rupees after a year, you are making a profit of 50 rupees over this one year period and your rate of return is 50%. How did you arrive at 50%? It is your profit as a proportion of your initial investment. Your profit is 50, your initial investment is 100 and 50 divided by 100 which is nothing but 0.5 or 50%. Now, let us think of it as a project. Let us try to compute IRR on this project. You are investing 100 rupees today and you are going to get 150 rupees after a year. In this case, how would we compute our NPV? Our NPV from this project will be minus 100 plus 1.5 rupees per year. 150 divided by 1 plus R. Now, my question to you is what is this R? This R is nothing but it is discount rate or you can also call it as opportunity cost of capital. Now, what is my IRR by definition? By definition, IRR is that discount rate which makes my NPV equal to 0. In other words, if I put my NPV equal to 0, my R will become my IRR. So, let us try to solve for IRR in this equation. So, this implies 100 equals 150 divided by 1 plus IRR. This implies 1 plus IRR equals 150 divided by 100. This implies your IRR equals 100 divided by 100. This implies your IRR equals 100 divided by 100. equals 0.5 or this is 50 percent. Isn't this the same return we obtained using our mental calculation? Yes, it is the same 50 percent which we obtained while calculating mentally. So, essentially we can easily compute rate of return for a single year period. However, when we have to compute rate of return for a multi-year project, we cannot compute it that easily. So, therefore, when you have a multi-year project or a multi-year investment to compute rate of return on that investment, you have to use an IRR. Now, what is your acceptance criterion? Under what condition will you accept a project using IRR method? Recall rate of return under what condition do you accept a project using rate of return? On the project is greater than the opportunity cost of capital for the project. In a similar way, you accept a project based on the IRR method if the IRR of the project is greater than its opportunity cost of capital. Now, what are the advantages of using IRR? It has several advantages. First is it takes into account time value of money. The second is we have a very clear benchmark for project acceptance. We accept only those projects whose IRR is greater than opportunity cost of capital. In other words, our benchmark is opportunity cost of capital which does not depend on the taste and preferences of managers. Also, IRR is easy to understand and communicate. If you want to tell a layperson that you would accept only those projects which are going to give you at least let us say 20% return, even the layperson will understand. The layperson will understand that you are going to accept a project as long as it gives at least some minimum return. So, therefore, this method is easier to communicate to a layperson than your NPV. However, similar to other techniques, this technique also has its own set of limitations or disadvantages. For example, it is possible that your project may not have any IRR. It is possible that your project has multiple IRRs. Also, as you will see going forward, IRR as a method is unreliable in ranking mutually exclusive projects which differ in their scale of investment or which differ in their economic lives. Also, IRR as a method does not distinguish between investment and borrowing decisions. So, we will be talking about each of these disadvantages in some detail as we go along.",
