      "transcript_text": "Now let us look at an example. You can purchase a turbo powered machine tool gadget for $4000. The investment will generate $2000 and $4000 in cash flows at the end of one and two years from today respectively. The question before you is, what is the IRR on this investment? As a first step to solve this kind of a problem, you should try to put your cash flows on a timeline. So let us put our cash flows on a timeline. So we have a two year project. The project involves an initial investment of $4000 today. It is going to give you $2000 at the end of year one and another $4000 at the end of year two. Now what will be the NPV of this project? The NPV of this project will essentially be minus $4000 plus $2000 divided by one plus R plus $4000 divided by one plus R whole square. Now let me again ask you this question. What is this R here? This R is nothing but it is your discount rate or you can also call it as opportunity cost of capital. Okay now how do we define our IRR? Our IRR is that discount rate which makes our NPV equal to 0. So which means that if we put NPV equal to 0 in this equation, then our R will essentially become IRR. Okay now as you can notice this is going to be an equation of degree 2 or a quadratic equation. Obviously it is not going to be very easy for us to solve this equation. Now in practice we are interested in only those values of IRR which are real and positive. If you solve this equation, you will get only one real and positive number which will come out as 28.08% You can also solve this equation using Microsoft Excel in which case you will be making use of the formula or function called as IRR and this function requires only one argument and that argument is your array of cash flows. This is your Excel function. Okay now my question to you is why is IRR called as internal rate of return? Why is IRR not called as external rate of return? Why internal? Why not external? The answer is simple. Computation of IRR requires only magnitude and timing of cash flows which are internal to the project. IRR calculation does not require anything which is external to the project and therefore IRR is called as internal rate of return. Remember that IRR calculation does not require opportunity cost of capital. However, if you were to decide whether to accept or reject the project only in that case you would require IRR to be compared with opportunity cost of capital. Now under what condition will you accept this project? You will accept this project as long as your opportunity cost of capital is less than 28.08%. Okay now let us try to construct a graph plotting NPV as a function of the discount rate. So let us say that we have discount rate or opportunity cost of capital on the x axis. And let us say that we have NPV on the y axis. Now if you plot NPV as a function of the discount rate how will your graph look like? This is how your graph looks like. What is this point where your NPV is 0? So this discount rate which makes your NPV equal to 0 this is clearly your IRR. Now as you can observe in this range, in this range what is happening? In this range as you can figure out your IRR is greater than your opportunity cost of capital. Okay now what is happening to NPV in this range? As you can see in this range your NPV is positive. In other words we notice that in this particular function when IRR is greater than opportunity cost of capital. In that case it is implying that our NPV is a positive number. Similarly what is happening when IRR is equal to opportunity cost? When IRR is equal to opportunity cost we notice that our NPV is 0. Now let us also see as to what happens in this range? In this range if you figure out what is happening is that your IRR is less than your opportunity cost. And in this range what is happening to your NPV? As you can see your NPV is negative in this particular range. Okay so this implies that when the our IRR is less than opportunity cost in that case our NPV is less than 0. Okay now under what condition do you accept a project based on NPV? You accept a project based on NPV if NPV is greater than 0. Similarly under what condition do you accept a project using IRR method? You accept a project using IRR method when your IRR is greater than opportunity cost. Similarly when do you reject a project using NPV method you reject a project using NPV when NPV is less than 0. When do you reject a project using IRR method? You reject a project using IRR method when IRR is less than opportunity cost. What do you notice from this particular example? You notice that both your NPV and IRR are leading to the same decision in this particular case. So whenever IRR is greater than opportunity cost of capital we are also finding NPV to be a positive number. Similarly whenever IRR is less than opportunity cost of capital we are also finding our NPV to be negative. In other words in this particular example both IRR and NPV are giving us the same decision. However this is happening under one particular assumption. You know what is that assumption? That assumption is as long as our NPV is a decreasing function of the discount rate in that case both NPV and IRR rules are going to give us the same answer about accepting or rejecting a project. In this particular example as you can see our NPV, our NPV is a decreasing function of the discount rate. And therefore both NPV and IRR are giving us the same decision. But for example, for example if we had a plot of this kind and in this case let us say our NPV function was of this kind. Here as you can make out NPV is an increasing function of the discount rate. And in this case NPV and IRR will not give us the same decision. So therefore for NPV and IRR to give us the same decision NPV has to be decreasing NPV has to be decreasing. Increasing function of the discount rate. It is worth mentioning that you should not get confused between the IRR and opportunity cost of capital. Often it is not unusual for people to get confused between IRR and opportunity cost of capital. Remember that your IRR is a profitability measure that depends solely on the amount and timing of project cash flows. On the other hand your opportunity cost of capital. It depends on the risk of a project. And how do we define opportunity cost? Opportunity cost is the expected return in the financial market at the same level of risk as that of your project. Higher the risk of your project greater will be the opportunity cost.",
