      "transcript_text": "Now, let us talk about, again, mutually exclusive projects which differ in their economic lives. Let us consider two projects, J and K and these projects are mutually exclusive projects. While project J has an economic life of three years, project K is a perpetual project. If you look at the cash flows from the project, project J involves an initial investment of $9,000 today. It will give you $6,000 at the end of year one, $5,000 at the end of year two, and another $4,000 at the end of year three. On the other hand, if you look at project K, it also involves an initial investment of $9,000 today, and it will give you $1,800 at the end of every year forever. In other words, this is a perpetual project. In this case, note that both the projects have similar scale of investment, which is $9,000. However, the two projects are differing in terms of their economic lives. While project J has an economic life of three years and project K has a perpetual economic life. and opportunity cost of capital of 10%. Based on that, if you look at NPV, NPV of project J comes out to be $3,592. While the NPV of project K comes out to be $9,000. On the other hand, if you look at the IRR, IRR for project J, comes out to be 33%, and that for K comes out as 20%. Based on the NPV, which decision would you take? Obviously, project K is superior to project J when we look at NPV. However, IRR is giving us a different story. IRR is telling us that project J should be accepted over project K because IRR of project J is higher than that of project K. Again, we are getting a different answer using IRR because IRR is unreliable in ranking mutually exclusive projects, which differ in their economic lives. Now, the question is, why is it happening? The answer is simple. If you look at the figure, you notice that when your opportunity cost of capital is 15.6%, both the projects have the same NPV. When your opportunity cost of capital is more than 15.6%, project J is superior to project K. Further, when your opportunity cost of capital is lower than 15.6%, in that case, project K has higher NPV relative to project J. Therefore, since our opportunity cost of capital is 10%, which is less than 15.6%. Therefore, at this opportunity cost of capital, project K has a higher NPV relative to project J and therefore, we should choose project K over project J. Now, the question before us is, can we still somehow use IRR technique to compare two mutually exclusive projects that differ in their economic lives? The answer is, again, yes, we can. And for that, we have to compute again incremental cash flows and then we have to compute IRR on those incremental cash flows. Now, here, the question before us is, we have projects J and K and therefore, we have to compute incremental cash flows. So, shall we do J minus K or will we do K minus J? What do you think? Remember, we want our first cash flow to be a negative cash flow. Because we want our incremental project to resemble a traditional investment project which has a cash outflow followed by a series of cash inflows. So, therefore, in this case, you will be doing K minus J. Because K minus J has a cash outflow of 4200 dollars at the end of one year from today. Now, if you compute your IRR on this incremental project K minus J, it will come out as 15.6 percent. Obviously, it is not easy to compute IRR of this incremental project using pen and paper. And therefore, we have to use Excel. In this case, however, let us assume that IRR of this incremental project is 15.6 percent. Now, based on this, what decision will you take? So, you will compare IRR of this incremental project with opportunity cost of capital. Since our opportunity cost of capital is 10 percent and since our IRR is higher than our opportunity cost of capital, this tells us that project K is incrementally superior to project J when the opportunity cost of capital. So, the opportunity cost of capital is 10 percent. So, therefore, at this opportunity cost of capital of 10 percent, we should accept project K over project J. Now, let me pose a question to you. My question is, can you use the incremental cash flow trick under all circumstances? The answer is no. It can only be used if projects have the same risk and hence the same opportunity cost of capital. If the two projects have different opportunity cost of capital, then you cannot use incremental cash flow trick. Now, another question you might have is, are there any other problems with finding out IRR on incremental cash flows? The answer is yes. You could have multiple IRRs or you could also have no IRR for your incremental cash flows. So, therefore, this technique of computing IRR on incremental cash flows is not a full proof technique. And therefore, if given a choice, we should always prefer NPV over any other capital budgeting technique. However, as we saw earlier, managers often use IRR as often as NPV. Why? Why do you think IRR is as popular as NPV? Despite having so many limitations, one answer is that IRR for its computation does not require opportunity cost of capital. If you have to compute IRR, you only need magnitude and timing of cash flows from your project. But if you want to compute NPV, you require not only magnitude and timing of cash flows from the project, but you also require opportunity cost of capital. Sometimes, managers do not know the exact opportunity cost of capital for a project. So, in that case, managers will still be able to compute the IRR, but they will not be able to compute precise value of NPV. Because NPV depends on not just your cash flows from the project, but also the opportunity cost of capital. So, therefore, IRR is used as often as NPV, because it allows us to compute the number even if we do not have opportunity cost of capital.",
