      "transcript_text": "Now let us talk about Rule 2 of estimating project cash flows. Rule 2 says that you have to estimate cash flows on an incremental basis. Remember that the value of a project depends on all the additional capital or incremental cash flows that follow from project acceptance. So you have to look at all those additional or incremental cash flows which result from project acceptance. So the first rule here is that you have to take into account all incidental effects. What is an incidental effect? An incidental effect means the effect that the acceptance of a project is going to have on the existing business of the firm. Sometimes it is possible that your project might have a negative incidental effect and sometimes your project might have a positive incidental effect. What is negative incidental effect? For example, if a new project can you be able to analyze the sales of existing business of the firm, then we call this effect as a negative incidental effect. And clearly what is negative incidental effect? Is it a cash inflow or is it a cash outflow? It is clearly a cash outflow. For example, let us say that there is an automobile company and this company makes internal combustion. So the combustion engine based two-wheelers. Now suppose this company takes up a project two manufacturer electric vehicles. Now as a consequence of the new project, two manufacturer electric vehicles. What may happen to the sales of internal combustion engine based two-wheelers? It is possible that the sales of the existing two-wheelers might come up. and therefore in this case it is a negative incidental effect. Similarly, your project can have a positive incidental effect as well. So if your project helps the firm's existing business grow, then it is clearly a positive incidental effect. And what is this positive incidental effect? Is it a cash inflow or is it a cash outflow? This is clearly a cash inflow. Now the next important point to remember is that when you forecast cash flows, you should not just look at product sales, but you should also recognize after sales cash flows to come later from the project. For example, if your company is setting up a new plant to manufacture electric vehicles, then it should not only take into account the sales of electric vehicles, but it should also take into account. The revenue that the company is going to earn from services and spear parts. The next important point to remember is that you should think about or take into account salvage value. So what is salvage value? So you understand that after the project completes its useful life, your equipment may have some residual value, which you can get after disposing of the equipment. And the salvage value clearly is a cash inflow for you. However, when you look at this cash inflow, this should take into account taxes as well. So salvage value, net of any taxes is what you should take into account. Now the next point pertains to the opportunity cost. You should include the opportunity cost of using an existing resource in the project. The cost of a resource, as you know, may be relevant to the decision even when no cash changes hands. For example, suppose a new investment project, which is a perpetual project, requires an existing piece of land. Now this piece of land could otherwise be sold for rupees 50 lakhs today. In other words, this resource is not free. This resource has an opportunity cost. And what is that opportunity cost? This is the cash flow that this land could generate for the company if the project were rejected and the resource for sold or put to some other productive use. For a perpetual project, what is going to be our opportunity cost? For a perpetual project, the opportunity cost of using an existing resource will be equal to its market price. For example, in the example we were talking about just a while ago, you know that the project required land, which could have otherwise been sold for 50 lakh rupees. And therefore, what is the opportunity cost here? The opportunity cost of using land in this perpetual project is 50 lakh rupees, which is the market price or market value of the land. Now if your project has a finite life, what is going to be opportunity cost of using an existing resource for a project which has a finite life? In this case, opportunity cost will be equal to the market rent of the resource over the life of the project. Now what is opportunity cost? Is it a cash inflow or is it a cash outflow? This is clearly an incremental cash outflow. Now the next point is you should be aware of allocated overhead costs. What are overhead costs? Overhead costs are also called as overheads. And they refer to expenses which are incurred to support the company's business. Now overheads may not be tied to any particular project. However, they must be paid for somehow. Therefore, when the accountant assigns existing overhead costs to the firm's projects, usually what happens is that the accountant makes a charge for it. For example, if we think about CEO salary, CEO salary as you understand, it may not be attributable to a project. However, this salary needs to be paid. And this salary therefore, an accountant allocates to different projects. And therefore, this allocation of CEO salary is allocation of existing overhead costs. And therefore, allocation of existing overhead costs. Is it an incremental cash flow? Clearly, if you are merely allocating existing overhead costs, it is not an incremental cash flow. We should include only the extra expenses that would result from the project as our incremental cash outflow. If a project is not going to result in extra expenses, the allocation of existing overhead costs should not be included in your project cash flows. Now another point you should remember is that you should forget some costs. What are some costs? Some costs are essentially costs which have been incurred in the past. They are irreversible cash flows. You cannot recover irrespective of what you do. And therefore, your decision to accept or reject the project will not affect your sunk costs. And therefore, how should you treat your sunk cost? Sun cost therefore should be ignored. And therefore, there will be no cash flow on account of sunk costs.",
