      "transcript_text": "Now let us talk about Rule 3 to estimate project cash flows. So, Rule 3 says that you should treat inflation consistently. What does that mean? It means that when you have nominal cash flows, you should discount those non-monials. nominal cash flows using nominal interest rates. Similarly, when you deal with real cash flows, you should discount these real cash flows using real discount rate. In each of the two cases, you will get the same and PV. However, having said that, you cannot discount nominal cash flows using real discount rate and real cash flows using nominal discount rate. Also, interest rates are usually coated in nominal terms rather than real terms in financial markets. And therefore, when you have to discount these nominal cash flows, more often than not, you will use nominal discount rate to discount these nominal cash flows. Now let us come to Rule 4. This rule says that you should separate investment and financing decisions. It is a standard practice to forecast cash flows, assuming the project is all equity financed, which means that you are ignoring the financing side effects. Any additional value that results from financing decisions is considered separately. But why? This is because you would want to know whether the project in itself is a value-adding project or not. And therefore, this particular rule allows you to separate the analysis of investment decision from that of the financing decision. For example, suppose a firm is going to finance a new project by issuing debt. While estimating cash flows, how should you treat the proceeds from the debt issue? And the interest and principal repayments on the debt used to finance the project. What would you do? You would simply ignore the cash flows on account of your financing decision. Why? Because as Rule 4 says, we should separate investment and financing decisions. Now let us come to the last rule, which is Rule 5. This rule says that you should forecast your cash flows after taxes. Why? Because taxes are also cash expenses just like your wages and raw material expenses. And therefore, cash flows should be estimated on an after tax basis.",
