          "model_answer": "Rule 3: Inflation Consistency. When estimating project cash flows, you must match the type of cash flow with the appropriate discount rate. If you have nominal cash flows (not adjusted for inflation), you must discount them using a nominal interest rate. If you have real cash flows (adjusted to remove inflation), you must discount them using a real discount rate. Mixing nominal cash flows with a real discount rate, or real cash flows with a nominal discount rate, will produce an incorrect present value. For example, if a project's revenue projections are in today's dollars (real), but you use the company's quoted bond yield (nominal) to discount them, your valuation will be wrong.\n\nRule 4: Separation of Investment and Financing Decisions. When forecasting project cash flows, it is standard practice to assume the project is all-equity financed. This means you should ignore the cash flows associated with financing, such as the proceeds from issuing debt or the interest and principal repayments on that debt. The goal is to determine whether the project itself creates value through its operations. For example, if a firm is building a new factory and plans to finance it with a bank loan, you would ignore the loan proceeds and the interest payments when calculating the project's operating cash flows. You would only focus on the cash generated by the factory's operations.\n\nRule 5: After-Tax Cash Flows. Cash flows must be estimated on an after-tax basis because taxes are a real cash expense, similar to wages or raw material costs. When evaluating a project, you should forecast the cash flows available after paying taxes. For example, if a project generates $5 million in pre-tax operating cash flows and the corporate tax rate is 30%, the after-tax cash flow for valuation purposes would be $3.5 million per year. Using pre-tax cash flows would overstate the project's value because it ignores this mandatory cash outflow.",
