      "transcript_text": "Now let us consider the benefit of diversification. So let us first of all consider a portfolio which comprises of two stocks. Now before we can consider the benefit of diversification, getting to studying the portfolio risk it is important for us to know two important terms which you might have come across already. The first term is covariance and the second term is correlation. If I may ask you what is covariance? What does covariance tell you? If you may have recall covariance tells you about relationship between two securities. If the covariance is positive it tells you that the two variables or two securities they are positively related. On the other hand if the covariance is negative it tells you that the two variables are negatively related. Now the question is what is the lower bound and upper bound on covariance as a number? Is there a lower and an upper bound? The answer is no. covariance can take values starting from minus infinity to plus infinity theoretically speaking. Okay. Now the question is how do we define covariance? So covariance first of all covariance between two variables x and y we will denote it by sigma xy and what is it equal to? It is equal to summation xi minus xy. x bar into yi minus y bar this whole divided by n minus 1 and as we just discussed covariance can take values starting from minus infinity to plus infinity. Now if covariance between x and y is positive it tells us that x and y are positively related. Which means that if x increases y is also going to increase. Similarly if sigma xy is negative it tells us that x and y are negatively related. Now my question to you is what is covariance of a variable with itself? Let us say covariance of x with x we will denote it by sigma xx and what is it going to be equal to? If you substitute or replace y with x. What will you get? You will get summation xi minus x bar into xi minus x bar divided by n minus 1. This will give you summation xi minus x bar whole square divided by n minus 1. What is this term? If you recall this is nothing but variance of x. In other words covariance of a variable with itself is nothing but variance of that variable. Now let us look at another concept which is that of correlation. First of all correlation between two variables x and y is denoted by rho xy and what is it equal to? It is simply equal to sigma xy which is covariance of x and y. This whole divided by sigma x into sigma y. Now this correlation coefficient or correlation is there any upper and lower bound on the values of this coefficient? The answer is yes. correlation between two variables cannot be more than plus 1 and it cannot be less than minus 1. In other words correlation takes values between minus 1 to plus 1. So if two variables have a correlation of plus 1 between them it tells us that the two variables are perfectly and positively correlated. If the two variables on the other hand have a correlation coefficient of negative 1 it tells us that the two variables are perfectly and negatively correlated. Now what is the difference between covariance and correlation? After all both of them are telling us how the two variables are related. But what is the difference between the two? The difference between the two is that while covariance gives us the direction of the relationship between two variables correlation on the other hand not only tells us about the direction between the two variables it also tells us about the strength of the relationship. So covariance it gives us direction of the relationship between two variables. x and y. Now having said that you must remember that whether it is covariance or it is correlation both of them capture only the linear relationship between the two variables. They do not measure a non-linear relationship between two variables.",
