      "transcript_text": "Now let us look at an example to compute portfolio variance for a two-stock portfolio. Suppose you invest $55 in stock 1 and $45 in stock 2. The expected return on on stock 1 is 10% and that on stock 2 is 20%. Assume a correlation coefficient of 0.65 between the two stocks and that the standard deviation of returns on stock 1 is 17.1% and that on stock 2 is 20.8%. The question before you is what is the risk which means portfolio variance or portfolio standard deviation and the expected return on your portfolio. Now let us first of all compute the portfolio variance. We already understand that in order to find out portfolio variance, for a two-stock portfolio, what will that be? It will be equal to sigma p square, it is equal to x1 square sigma 1 square plus x2 square sigma 2 square plus 2 x1 x2 rho 1 2 into sigma 1 sigma 2. What is your x1 here? You have invested $55 in stock 1 out of how much out of your total funds of $100. You have invested $55 in stock 1. So which means that your x1 is 0.55 and your x2 similarly is 0.45. So here our x1 is 55 divided by 55 plus 45 which is 0.55. Our x2 is 45 divided by 55 plus 45. Which is 0.45? What is our sigma 1? Our sigma 1 is given to us as 17.1%. What is our sigma 2? Our sigma 2 is given to us as 20.8%. Do we have information on the correlation coefficient as well? Yes, it is given to us as 0.65. So let us substitute these values in our portfolio variance equation. So it is going to be 0.55 square into sigma 1 square which is 17.1 square plus x2 which is 0.45 square of that into sigma 2 square which is 20.8 square plus 2 into x1 which is 0.55 into x2 which is 0.45 into rho 1 2 which is 0.65 into sigma 1 which is 17.1 into sigma 2 which is 20.8. If you solve this you will get your sigma p square as 290.50 or you can say that or your sigma p which will be square root of 290.50 it will come out as 17.04% okay. Also let us compute expected return on the portfolio as we had seen earlier expected return on the portfolio is equal to weighted average of the returns on individual stocks that form the portfolio. So in this case it will be simply x1 into r1 plus x2 into r2. What is our expected return on stock 1? Our expected return on stock 1 is 10% and expected return on stock 2 is 20%. So therefore what will be our expected return on the portfolio rp it is going to be 0.55 into x1 which is 10% plus 0.45 into x2 plus 0.45 into r2 which is 20%. So how much will you get as your portfolio expected return you will get it as 14.5%. So your portfolio expected return is 14.5% and your portfolio standard deviation is 17.04% so what did you notice as far as the portfolio risk is concerned? So your individual stocks if you notice has standard deviations of 17.1% and 20.8% respectively but what is your portfolio standard deviation? It is 17.04% which is less than the standard deviation of returns on stock 1 as well as standard deviation of returns on stock 2. Why is it happening? It is happening because of diversification. Earlier you had these stocks individually now you have formed a portfolio of these two stocks and therefore now because of the benefit of diversification your portfolio standard deviation has come down. It is less than the standard deviation of each of the stocks in the portfolio. Now my question to you is how will the risk of your portfolio change if the correlation coefficient between the two stocks in your portfolio becomes 1.8, 0.50 and minus 1? If you compute your portfolio standard deviation under each of these correlation values what will you realize? You will realize that your portfolio standard deviation is 18.77% when your correlation is plus 1 and it is 0.04% when your correlation coefficient is minus 1. What is your weighted average of standard deviation of individual securities forming the portfolio? If you compute that weighted average let us do that. So your or your weighted average of standard deviations of stocks what will that come out as? That will be x1 sigma 1 plus x2 sigma 2. x1 is 0.55 sigma 1 is 17.1% x2 is 0.45 and sigma 2 is 20.8% and if you solve this what will you get? You will get 18.77% okay what do you notice? You notice that your portfolio standard deviation is equal to the weighted average of the standard deviations of individual stocks when your correlation coefficient is plus 1. You also notice that your portfolio standard deviation is minimum when the correlation coefficient between the two stocks is negative 1. Also you notice that your portfolio standard deviation declines as the correlation coefficient between the two securities comes down from plus 1 to minus 1.",
