      "transcript_text": "Now let us talk about asset and project cost of capital. Let us begin our discussion with asset cost of capital. You understand that a firm is nothing but a collection of assets. So, you know that a firm is nothing but a collection of assets. Let me represent those assets by letter A. So, A represents our assets. Also, these assets as we discussed previously, they need to be funded. These assets are not going to drop off from the sky and therefore, a company can fund them using debt. A company can also fund them using equity. So, my DE denotes debt and my E DE notes equity. Okay, now what is asset cost? So, let us first of all look at the asset side of the company. So, if we look at the asset side of the company, we can say that our asset cost of capital is the opportunity cost of capital for investment in all of the firms assets. Now, let us look at the debt and equity side of the company. Here, we can say that the asset cost of capital is nothing but expected return on a portfolio of all of the company's debt and equity. And if the company is entirely equity financed, for example, a company like Infosys. So, for such a company, the asset cost of capital will simply be equal to expected return on the firms stock. Now, this asset cost of capital, it is the appropriate discount rate for firms average risk projects. It is applicable for expansion of companies existing business. However, if a company is undertaking new projects which are either more risky or less risky compared to firms existing business, then our asset cost of capital is not the appropriate discount rate for these kind of projects. Now, let us talk about project cost of capital. We measure the risk of each project by its beta. If a project has high beta, it means that the project is highly risky. Similarly, if a project has low beta, it means that the project is less risky. If we look at the security market line or SML, which means that if we plot expected return or required return on beta. So, let me call this expected return as the required return. So, if we draw this plot between required return and beta, we will have this SML. What does this SML tell us? This SML tells us that as the beta of a project goes up, the return that investors require from this project also goes up. Or in other words, as the beta of a project goes up, its opportunity cost also goes up and therefore, investors will require higher return as their opportunity cost of capital. Now, the question is which projects should have firm accept or reject those lying above the SML or those lying below the SML? Suppose we have a project which lies here. Now, so let us say that this project has a beta of beta 1 and let us say that this project is going to give you a return of R1 dash. Now, if we look at the SML, so corresponding to a beta of beta 1, you would have required a return of R1. Yes, however, in this case, how much return are you actually getting? Now, for such a project, because R1 dash is greater than R1, so what we will do? We will accept this project. This project is essentially a positive and PV project. Why is this project a positive and PV project? The reason is simple because this project is giving us more return than we require from this project based on its riskiness. So, here, here what is R1? R1 is our required return or we can also call it as opportunity cost of capital. What is R1 dash? R1 dash is the return that the project is going to give us. In a similar way, if you have a project which lies below the SML, now suppose you have a project which lies below the SML, say here. Let us say the beta of the project is beta 2 and let us say that the project is going to give you a return of R2 dash. However, based on the SML, how much return do you expect from the project? So, based on the SML, you expect a return of R2. But how much return are you getting? You are only getting a return of R2 dash. So, here in a similar way because R2 dash is less than R2. R2 is our required rate of return or opportunity cost of capital. So, in this case we will simply reject the project. Why have we rejected this project? We have rejected this project because this project is giving us lower return than we require from the project. In other words, we can say that this is a negative NPV project. So, based on the discussion, what do we conclude? We conclude that we should accept those projects which lie above the SML and we should reject those projects which lie below the SML. Now, let us talk about asset cost of capital rule. Now, some companies accept any project regardless of its risk as long as the project offers a higher return than the companies as it cost of capital. So, this rule we call as asset cost of capital rule. You accept the project if the return is higher than the asset cost of capital and you reject the project if the return is lower than asset cost of capital. Now, the question before us is what will happen if a company uses the asset cost of capital rule to accept or reject projects? Let us again draw the SML. So, this is our SML security market line and let us say that our asset cost of capital is RA. Now, what do we have on the x axis? On the x axis we have beta and what do we have on the y axis? On the y axis we have expected return. Now, this is our RA. Now, suppose there is a project which lies below the SML but above the asset cost of capital. Suppose we have a project which lies below the SML and this project is going to give us return which is higher than the asset cost of capital which is RA. What will you do? Since this project is below the SML this is a negative NPV project and therefore we should reject this project. However, what will asset cost of capital rule do? Asset cost of capital rule will say that you accept all those projects which are going to give you return in excess of the asset cost of capital. So, therefore what will happen is we will end up accepting some negative NPV projects. Also, let us say that if we have a project here clearly this project is above the SML. This is a positive NPV project. However, return on this project is less than the asset cost of capital and therefore what will the asset cost of capital rule do? It will say that you reject these projects and therefore what will happen is we will end up rejecting some positive NPV projects. So, therefore if a company uses the asset cost of capital rule to accept or reject projects what will happen is that many negative NPV projects will get accepted and many positive NPV projects will get rejected. Now, what do we conclude from this? We conclude that the appropriate discount rate or opportunity cost of capital for a project depends on the risk of the project and it does not depend on the company undertaking the project. If it is so, our obvious question will be why is then so much time spent estimating the asset cost of capital? If we cannot make use of asset cost of capital to accept or reject projects why are we spending so much time estimating the asset cost of capital? By the way, asset cost of capital is also called as the company cost of capital or simply the cost of capital. So, back to our question why do we spend so much of time estimating asset cost of capital? The reason is that many projects in general are expansions of a company's existing business and for these average risk projects the asset cost of capital is the right discount rate. There is another reason as to why a lot of time spent estimating asset cost of capital and that reason is that managers in general have good intuition about relative risk but not about absolute risk. And in these situations asset cost of capital acts as a useful starting point for setting this count rates because it is easier to add to or subtract from the asset cost of capital than to estimate a project's cost of capital from scratch. So, therefore, once a manager knows that this project is more risky than companies existing business the manager will add some markup on the cost of capital of the company to arrive at projects cost of capital.",
