      "transcript_text": "Let us again look at Asset Cost of Capital. So, what is asset cost of capital? Asset cost of capital as we know it is the opportunity cost of investment in all of the firms assets. Also, it is the expected return on the portfolio of all of the companies outstanding debt and equity. If we draw the market value balance sheet of a company, so we have assets on the one hand and we have debt and equity on the other hand. Now, how can we define our asset cost of capital? So, let us denote our asset cost of capital by R A, our cost of equity on expected return on equity by R E and cost of capital. Cost of debt or expected return on debt by ID. Now, you can view a company as a portfolio. It is a portfolio of assets on the one hand and a portfolio of debt and equity on the other hand. And we know that what is the expected return on a portfolio? expected return on a portfolio is weighted average of returns on securities that form this portfolio, which means that our R A will be weighted average of our cost of debt and cost of equity. However, what are these weights going to be? So, when we look at weights, we have to look at weights in terms of market values and not in terms of book values. So, therefore, here what will our asset cost of capital be? Here, our asset cost of capital will be equal to weighted average of the cost of debt and here the weight of debt is d divided by d plus e into cost of debt plus weight of equity. Which is e divided by d plus e into cost of equity, which is R E. Now, here what is your e? Your e is market value of equity. How will you compute your market value of equity? It will simply be equal to number of shares. Outstanding number of shares outstanding multiplied by price per share. And by the way market value of equity is also called as market capitalization. Now, what is d here? d here is again market value of debt. However, in practice market value of debt is not very different from the book value of debt and therefore, we can assume book value of debt as its market value. But in general, we cannot take book value of equity as its market value. Now, let me ask you one question. My question is what do you think should be the relationship between R D, R E and R A intuitively? Which of the three do you think should be the least and which of the three do you think should be the highest? Think about in terms of riskiness. Which of the three do you think is least risky? Is it the debt? Is it the equity or is it the assets? It is the debt which is least risky out of the three. Why is that least risky? To know that let us construct very quickly a multi step income statement. In a multi step income statement, you start with sales. You subtract from the sales cost of goods sold to arrive at your gross profit. From this gross profit, you subtract operating expenses and you will arrive at then something called as e bit. Which is nothing but earnings before interest and taxes. Now, if the company has issued debt or taken loans, the company needs to service those loans or debt, which means that the company will be required to pay interest on the outstanding debt. So, therefore, a part of this e bit will go to service debt in terms of paying interest. And you will have something called as e bit. earnings before taxes and once you subtract taxes, what will you arrive at? If you subtract taxes, you will arrive at something called as profit after tax. Now, a part of this profit can be paid out as dividend and another part can be retained and reinvested. Okay, here what is the income to bond holders? If you see here, this is your income to bond holders. So, your interest is the income to bond holders. What is your income to shareholders? Dividend is your income to share holders. What do you notice here? You notice here that first of all, a company pays interest on the outstanding debt. And then if there is anything left, then the company can decide to pay out a part of that as dividend. In other words, as you can see here, there is much higher probability of a company paying interest period after period than the company is able to pay dividends period after period. In other words, we can say that our equity is a residual claim on the cash flows which are generated by assets of the company. And this particular claim stands behind debt. In other words, we can say that our debt is much more safer than our equity. And therefore, since that is less risky relative to equity and therefore, our cost of debt is also lower than the cost of equity. Our ID will be lower than our RE. Why is it so? Because that is less risky relative to equity. In other words, we can say that beta of debt is lower than beta of equity. And RE, which is weighted average of the cost of debt and cost of equity, it lies between RD and RE. In other words, we can say that our RE has to be greater than or equal to RE. And RE in turn has to be greater than or equal to RD.",
