      "subjective_questions": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "question": "Based on the lecture transcript, explain the hierarchy of risk and cost of capital among equity, debt, and assets using the Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM). In your answer, explain how the beta of assets is derived and how the Security Market Line (SML) illustrates this relationship. Provide a real-world example from the lecture to support your explanation.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The cost of capital hierarchy, as explained in the lecture, follows the ranking: Cost of Equity (RE) > Cost of Assets (RA) > Cost of Debt (RD). This ordering is driven by the beta coefficients in the CAPM formula. Equity represents a residual claim on the firm's assets and cash flows, making it the riskiest investment; therefore, it has the highest beta ($\beta_E$). Debt holders have a prior claim and face lower risk than equity holders, resulting in a lower beta for debt ($\beta_D$), which the lecturer notes is greater than zero but less than the beta of assets. The beta of assets ($\beta_A$) is not directly observed if debt is not traded; rather, it is estimated as a weighted average of the beta of debt and the beta of equity, reflecting the overall risk of the firm's operations. On the Security Market Line (SML), which plots expected return against beta, the risk-free asset sits at beta = 0. Since $\beta_D < \beta_A < \beta_E$, the expected return (cost of capital) for debt (RD) lies lowest on the SML, followed by assets (RA), and finally equity (RE) at the highest point. A real-world example from the lecture illustrates this: Infosys, with over 2 million shareholders holding over 4 billion shares, demonstrates why owners cannot run daily operations and must delegate authority to professional managers. This delegation necessitates the separation between ownership and management, influencing the risk profile and cost of capital structure. Examiners may test this concept by asking students to rank the costs of capital for different firm types or explain why a firm's cost of equity is typically its highest expense.",
          "rubric": [
            {
              "criterion": "Core conceptual understanding of risk hierarchy (RE > RA > RD)",
              "marks": 4
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
