          "model_answer": "The long-term perspective of a family business acts as a strategic buffer during crises because the firm is viewed through a multi-generational lens, rather than a short-term quarterly lens. As discussed in the lecture, family firms have the patience to \"play the long game\" and wait for results to manifest, unlike professionally managed firms driven by share price movements every quarter. \n\nDuring a crisis such as a recession, this perspective translates into specific survival behaviors. The lecture notes that stakeholders, suppliers, customers, and even employees, are perceived not just as business counterparts but as an extended \"family.\" This relational capital enables the firm to negotiate delayed payments with suppliers and extend credit to customers during tough times, as \"everyone knows that we have to be the entire family has to be in it together to help tide over this face.\" Furthermore, the family's history of surviving wars and recessions means they are \"used to these sacrifices,\" allowing them to cut expenses and close ranks temporarily, knowing that \"a two-year recession hardly makes sense\" in the context of a founder's vision spanning three generations. This contrasts with private firms that may \"cut and run\" at the slightest opportunity; family firms are \"in it for the long term.\"\n\nKey examples from the lecture include the Marvari community's \"thousand day rule,\" which exemplifies patient capital, and the Titan watch series, which illustrates the difficulty of making long-term decisions in the absence of family involvement. The lecture also highlights the family firm's culture of viewing employees as an extended family, which fosters reciprocal loyalty during crises.\n\nMarking Rubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding of long-term perspective vs. short-termism (3 marks).\n- Identification of relational capital/stakeholder as family as a crisis mechanism (3 marks).\n- Application of the \"no exit\" philosophy and sacrifice tolerance (2 marks).\n- Integration of specific lecture examples (Titan, Marvari, stakeholder as family) (2 marks).",
