      "transcript_text": "Let's have a look at what is a stakeholder theory of firms. We have seen that uh in non-family firms, they form they often talk about uh shareholder primacy. Everything should be done to maximize the value of the shareholder. The shareholder is also the owner of the firm. He has some shares in the business and therefore is considered a owner shareholder. The stakeholder theory is slightly different from the shareholder primacy theory in the sense that it says that we must give primacy to all stakeholders. How do they differ? Shareholder is only the owner, one who owns shares. The stakeholder is everyone else involved in the system. It includes the supplier, it includes their customer, includes their employees, and so on. So, all these people put together, we call them as stakeholders. The stakeholder theory says we should keep the interests of all these stakeholders together for the long-term survival of the firm and increase in value of the firm. This is much broader. Family firms often give a lot more importance uh to all these stakeholders as compared to a uh non-family firm where shareholder is still given primacy. So, family firm will look at uh all these suppliers also as family. All these employees also as family. They might have started with one customer with whom they have a very deep relationship. Similar relationship will exist with all their suppliers also. In many cases, you will also find some of them are part of family also. Some customers may be those who are, you know, running some other business in the family. Some of the employees may be family members themselves. Some suppliers from from whom they buy some equipment or some consumable anything for their business will also be a a family member. Maybe a distant family member. So, they are far more closer. Secondly, even if they are not family members, the relationship they have with all these stakeholders is fairly deep in a family business. These people have been working together for many years, decades sometimes. And while the other family, if it's that's also a family business, would have passed on the same relationship down the generations. This family business will also pass on relations down the down the generations. These relations last for a long long time. It also explains in what happens in terms of we have discussed in terms of crisis, these stakeholders don't let go of each other either. They know that they have worked together for a long time. All of them have benefited by being in this close relationship with everyone. And they know that these tough times do not last. These kind of relationship last longer. So, that's really the value chain that a stakeholder theory recommends that we should build and we see a family firms building that. It goes just beyond the stakeholders. For example, the community while is also benefited a lot by these kind of family businesses. They together invest in the community. They together celebrate functions, all the stakeholders I mean, suppliers, all of them. A marriage in the family of the supplier is almost considered like a marriage in in this family businesses marriage. So, they they attend in full force. They contribute. That way, the stakeholder bonds that exist in a family business are much much stronger. They endure tough times and that to some extent explains the success and survivability of the family firms. >> [music]",
