      "transcript_text": "Next is the socio-emotional wealth theory. The socio-emotional wealth theory highlights that family wealth is not necessarily only financial. Issues and concerns like legacy, identity, emotional connections, all of these are also important wealth. Right? And these are socio-emotional wealth. Right? They capture the social dimensions and the emotional aspects of uh the wealth, which is not measured in pure currency terms. And therefore, it is a very important theory for us to understand. Baron and colleagues have given a framework called as the FIBER framework to understand and capture the socio-emotional wealth of a family business. The FIBER framework has five elements in it. First is the F, the family control and influence. Family control and influence refers to the extent to which family members exert their authority over strategic decisions either through board compositions, management, etc. Right? The second part of the FIBER framework is the identification that the family members have with the family business. This is the psychological overlap between the family's identity and the company's brand image or reputation. The third element in the FIBER framework is the binding social ties. The binding social ties refer to the reciprocal long-term social capital and relationships which are built within the firm as well as with external com- communities and complementers including the suppliers, the workers, the local community, and the local government. The fourth element is the emotional attachment. This refers to the affective charge, shared history, and emotional bonds, both positive and negative, which are embedded within the organization. And the last is the R, which is the renewal of the family bonds or the dynastic element within the FIBER framework. This refers to the desire to pass on the business as a going concern to future generations. In other words, the intent to transfer wealth across generations in order to keep the family legacy alive across time. Together, these five elements comprise the FIBER framework, which we can use to study and understand the of the twin objectives of a family business, the financial as well as the socioemotional wealth. The socioemotional wealth theory is especially important because it can be used to explain why sometimes seemingly not so logical decisions along the financial dimension of a family business can make perfect sense when you understand the socioemotional wealth dimension of the family business.",
