          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The FIBER framework, developed by Baron and colleagues, consists of five elements that capture socio-emotional wealth (SEW): (1) Family control and influence (F), referring to authority over strategic decisions through board composition and management; (2) Identification (I), the psychological overlap between family identity and company brand; (3) Binding social ties (B), reciprocal long-term social capital with employees, suppliers, the local community, and government; (4) Emotional attachment (E), affective bonds and shared history embedded in the organization; and (5) Renewal of family bonds (R), the intent to pass the business as a going concern to future generations to keep the legacy alive. \n\nSocio-emotional wealth theory is important because it explains why family businesses may make decisions that appear financially suboptimal. For example, a family might retain an underperforming relative in management (protecting emotional attachment) or resist going public (protecting family control and influence), even if these actions reduce short-term financial returns. The theory posits that family businesses have twin objectives: financial wealth maximization and socio-emotional wealth preservation. \n\nA real-world example from the lecture involves Infosys, which has over 2 million shareholders holding over 4 billion shares. This structure illustrates why owners cannot run daily operations and must delegate authority to professional managers, highlighting the tension between family control (a SEW element) and professional management necessary for financial growth. Another example is Gautam Adani and Mukesh Ambani, where net worth is a stock variable measured at a point in time, whereas profit is a flow variable measured over a time period, illustrating the different dimensions of wealth.",
