      "transcript_text": "There is a resource based view of every firm. Generally referred to it as RBEs and Times. In RBE, an organization is considered a bundle of resource and capabilities. These resources and capabilities can be both tangible and as well as intangible. What is tangible in this case would be finance, can be people, manpower, human capital, the technology being used. All these are clearly tangible resources. What can be intangible resources, let's say a company is heavily into patents. So, patents are intangible things in times. Brand reputation, culture, these are clearly intangible. You cannot clearly measure culture as much as you can measure the amount of cash available in tangible. So, RBE theory is a combination of both these tangibles and intangibles. In a family business, RBE is somewhat slightly different because what happens is in a family business, when business and family mix. They create some resources which go beyond the standard RBE theory. They can create some unique mix of capabilities and resources that are available to families alone. And that can really create some very strong, competitive advantages. Compreter to advantages for the firm which can help the firm grow faster as a result have an impact on both the family and the business. This concept where family and business mixing and creating a special advantage under this theory is referred to as familyness. Familyness refers to a unique bundle of resources which are created as a combination and interaction between business level resources, family level resources and individuals within the family at that level, the individual family member level resources. These dimensions could include structural, cognitive, relational as well as the family involvement. Structural dimensions would refer to the social interactions and networks that emerge within the family as well as external to the family at the business level. The cognitive dimension of these of familyness refers to the shared vision and purpose, unique language, stories and culture both within the family as well as at the business level. The relational dimension of familyness refers to the crust, norms, obligations and identity. And the family involvement, essence and organizational identity is the fourth dimension of familyness. Familyness is unique to every different family and a family firm. If you refer back to our discussion on the entrepreneurial family galaxy, you will appreciate that one entrepreneurial family can have different family firms that they operate as well as family trust and other family vehicles. Each one of them can have a unique familyness that is applicable to that particular vehicle because the members who are involved with that particular firm could be very different from another firm owned by the same family.",
