      "transcript_text": "The transaction cost economics is a different way to look at the firm and explain the scope of the firm. The transaction cost economics looks at the firm as an excess of contracts. These contracts can either be engaged in the external market or can be internalized within the firm. When it is cheaper to perform a transaction within the firm and therefore the transaction is internalized versus if it is as much or maybe even cheaper to engage in an external market transaction, that difference is what explains the boundary of the firm. The firm will only engage in those transactions that it can do at a cost lower than engaging in the same transaction in the external market. For example, should the firm outsource manufacturing or not? If it is cheaper for the firm to do manufacturing internally, it will engage in manufacturing by itself, otherwise it will simply outsource an engage in an external market transaction with an outside supplier. There are a few important differences for a family firm when it comes to the application of transaction cost economics. Because the family firm and the firm family is looking not only at maximizing the financial wealth but also the social emotional wealth. And these two twin goals or twin objectives of the family business are what drive the transaction cost economics perspective of explaining the scope of a family business. Theoretically there are also two very important things that we need to consider. The first is asset specificity. The firm is the firm. Asset specificity to explain in very simple terms, think about a firm buying a plant and machinery or a piece of equipment. If that equipment needs to be modified in order to do that particular firm's job and cannot easily be applied in any other firm's context, that kind of an asset is highly specific to that particular firm. However, if the same piece of equipment without any modification, equipment can easily be transferred to and applied in other contexts or other firms has no specificity. So, asset specificity is a very important thing. Now, within the family business context, you remember the concept of familyness that we discussed. This is very unique, the relationships of every family, the social capital of every family, the reputation that every family has. These are very specific to that particular family. The family alone. These are what we call as specific assets. On the other side are generic assets. Think about 100 rupees note. Whether you carry 100 rupees note or I carry 100 rupees note, it is fungible. They are the same value. This is a generic asset. There are what we call as generic non-tradable assets. These generic non-tradable assets cannot be easily moved across firm. And for family firms, there are four very important generic non-tradable assets. The first to refer to social capitals, bonding social capital and bridging social capital. Bonding social capital refers to the social capital that works within the family form and within that particular family. On the other side, you have bridging social capital, which is talking to or referring to how this this particular family and its individuals or the business individuals transact with members outside their particular network. These are bridges that are formed between different networks and that refers to the bridging social capital of the family business. The third is a reputational asset. Over a period of time, every family and therefore every family business develops and maintains its own reputation. And many times families and family businesses go to a deep extent and engagement with their local community with the government, with their employees in how the shape and maintain and build this reputation. And this reputation cannot be transferred across family businesses. It is very unique to that family and that family business and therefore it is a generic non-tradable asset. The fourth type is the tacit knowledge. Tastet knowledge basically refers to knowledge that cannot be easily codified. If knowledge can be codified, it becomes explicit knowledge. An explicit knowledge can easily be moved across contexts or individuals. But tacit knowledge, since it is not codified, is very unique to that particular individual or system within which it operates. And every family and every family business has their own way and their own tacit knowledge about how their business needs to be run. What rules and norms that are not codified but understood by every family member and every employee of that particular family business. This is again a generic non-tradable asset that every family business has which is unique to themselves. A transaction cost economics approach to understanding a family business takes into consideration all these assets which are very unique to a family business as well as those that are easily tradable across businesses or across family businesses in considering and defining the scope of the family business.",
