          "model_answer": "The firm will internalize a transaction when the cost of performing it internally is lower than the cost of engaging in the same transaction in the external market. This boundary decision is driven by two key factors discussed in the lecture.\n\nFirst, asset specificity plays a central role. If the transaction involves assets that are highly specific, such as a firm buying a plant and machinery that needs modification for the firm's particular job and cannot easily be applied in any other firm's context, the cost of external market engagement increases. The asset cannot be redeployed, making outsourcing inefficient. Therefore, the firm internalizes such transactions to avoid these high external transaction costs.\n\nSecond, the twin objectives of the family firm, maximizing financial wealth and maximizing social emotional wealth, shape this decision. Unlike a non-family firm focused solely on profit maximization, the family firm may internalize transactions not only for financial efficiency but also to preserve social emotional wealth. For example, internalizing certain operations may help protect the family's reputation or maintain bonding social capital, which are generic non-tradable assets unique to the family. By keeping transactions internal, the family can safeguard these intangible assets that contribute to their social emotional wealth, even if external markets might sometimes be financially cheaper.\n\nIn summary, the firm internalizes transactions when internal costs are lower, particularly when asset specificity makes external engagement costly, and when internalization supports the dual goals of financial and social emotional wealth.",
