      "transcript_text": "The third important framework is the ownership competence framework. Ownership competence looks at ownership as an active strategic asset and a skill set that contributes unique value to the business. It can be defined as the ability of family shareholders to act for the business. As informed decision makers and responsible stewards creating value for the enterprise while maintaining family unity and harmony. There are three important dimensions to ownership competence. The first is the matching competence. Matching competence refers to what to own. What businesses should I operate in? What assets should I own? The second is the governance competence. How should I own it? The third dimension is the timing competence. This refers to when to own. These three are very important dimensions that capture how the owner has a competent individual or a set of individuals contribute to creating unique value for the family firm. There are three other aspects that are of important for us to note. The first is the ownership approach. Should I look at this as a custodian or should I look at this as an entrepreneur? custodian would mean that I am only owning it at this phase and I need to transit and pass on to the next generations whereas entrepreneurial approach to this could mean that I could take a lot more risk. The second is the ownership rights. Should I have an involved people with non-woting rights or with voting rights? And the third is the ownership activity. How active or passive role should I play as an owner across these three dimensions? These together comprise the ownership competence framework in trying to understand how an owner adds value to the firm.",
