          "model_answer": "The three dimensions of ownership competence are matching competence, governance competence, and timing competence. Matching competence refers to what businesses or assets the owner should operate in. Governance competence refers to how the ownership structure is set up, including the type of ownership rights (voting vs. non-voting) and the governance mechanisms in place. Timing competence refers to when the owner should acquire, hold, or exit assets.\n\nThe ownership approach, whether custodian or entrepreneurial, significantly influences these decisions. A custodian approach focuses on holding the business for the next generation and passing it on, prioritizing family harmony and wealth preservation. This approach would lead to more conservative decisions regarding matching (selecting stable, established businesses), governance (setting up structures for succession), and timing (holding assets for the long term). In contrast, an entrepreneurial approach allows for taking on more risk, which could lead to more aggressive decisions in matching (diversifying into high-growth sectors), governance (taking more active control or restructuring), and timing (entering or exiting positions based on market opportunities).\n\nA real-world example from the lecture is Infosys, which has over 2 million shareholders holding over 4 billion shares. This structure illustrates why owners cannot run daily operations and must delegate authority to professional managers. The sheer number of shareholders makes it impractical for them to be involved in day-to-day decision-making, highlighting the importance of governance competence in setting up appropriate structures for delegation and control.",
