      "transcript_text": "In a family form, each of these resources can be grouped according to the types of capital they bring to the family business. Each of them have a very distinctive characteristic when it comes to family terms. Each of them contribute to the success of the family form. We shall look at these types of capital. The first would be human capital. What it means is that we have a very stable and extremely committed workforce who have the experiences, who have the knowledge, who help the business grow and ensure success for the family business. The second type of capital is a social capital. What do we mean here? We mean relationships, connections that the family has built across these timelines. And what do we mean? What do we with whom? The relationships are with their own employees, their multiple stakeholders will talk of. All of them suppliers, customers, community. These are relationships that have been built and are extremely strong. When you combine that with our influence, it can really mean a lot of political capital for the family. What are the characteristics for this in the family form? They have access to people who can make things happen. When you have social capital, it means really long-term relationships that help them when really they are going through a tough phase. These stakeholders with those deep relationships don't back off and they are tough times for the family business. The third kind of capital is the financial capital. What are we talking of here? We are talking of internal accruals, retained capital, investments that the family itself is able to make. All these contribute to the financial funding. Typically in family firms, what is retained is plowed back into the business. We spoke about how they are frugal, they don't spend unnecessarily. They put more and more money back into the business and help the succession of the business, help them grow, survive. What are we talking of really? In a family firm, we are talking of very, very patient financial capital that is able to last much, much longer. Lastly, the kind of capital we are talking of is really intangible one, which is heritage. What is heritage here? When we mean we mean the shared values and affection for the past. Family businesses sometimes very, very nostalgic. People have brought up on stories on how the family has grown from what way, how it was at that point of time and right from a young age, they start getting very, very involved, emotionally involved with the family. And that contributes a lot to the success of the firm at a later date. So, there is a lot of history, there is a lot of traditions that the family follows and this is something that is inculcated in every family member and multiple stakeholders as well. Every stakeholder knows what this family's values are. The employees know what the values are, what culture, what is the history, how do they interact with the community? All this holds them in very good state. There are also very, very specific cultural and historical elements that are passed on from one generation to another. And every generation is brought up on that, adds to that sense of identity, their pride of being involved in this family and business. And that when you have that kind of an identity and connect with both the family and the business, it helps the survival, the success of both the family and the business.",
