      "transcript_text": "Family Business is not a modern deviation from real corporate capitalism. Instead, it is an original and still dominant form of enterprise. What we now call the modern corporation, which is proficientally managed, widely held and ran by hired executives who are answerable to dispersed shareholders is historically speaking the exception and a relatively recent and geographically narrow phenomena at that. Let us take a walk down history and see how poor several years and decades in centuries, family business and family enterprise has survived and thrived. Why family firms exist everywhere? Why India in particular became one of the most family dominated large economies in the world and how family enterprise has actively shaped and not just survived every major shift in its operating environment. Family rent, trade and production predates the limited liability publicly traded joint stock corporations, which many of us have very familiar with. Even before the Dutch East India Company, which are set up in 1602 and the English East India Company in the 1600, pioneered permanent, redable share capital, virtually all organized commerce, long-distance trade caravans, craft gills, banking houses, shipping ventures were financed and run by families, kinship networks or partnerships bound by personal trust rather than formal contract or commercial law. Let us look at two illustrative early modern dynasties, which are worth naming in our discussion, because they show how family capital operates at the scale of sovereign finance, the Medici family and the F F family. The Medici family is native to the Florence area and thrived in the 15th to 18th centuries. The Medici family built one of your ops first through international banking networks. They finance the papacy and multiple European crowns. The Medici family used commercial wealth to acquire explicit political power. In fact, you will be surprised that three Medici family members became popes and the family ruled Florence for generations. This is an early illustration of how family economic power and political power reinforce each other. Another example is a Fugger family from Oxford in the 16th and 17th centuries. The Fugger family became bankers to the Holy Roman emperors, most famously financing Charles V's 1519 Imperial election. The Fugger family built a trading and mining empire spanning silver, copper and textiles across Europe. The Fugger's pioneer early double entry bookkeeping and a rudimentary international news and intelligence service to manage a business empire that stretched from Spain to Hungary and illustrates how even 500 years ago, a family firm's competitive edge often came from information networks and not just financial capital. In 2008, the Bank of Korea published a study. This study identified close to 5,000 and odd companies worldwide that were more than 200 years old and spanning 41 countries. In this study, and this list, Japan alone accounted for 56% that is roughly 3,000 and odd firms across the global total. In the second place, where German family firms close to about 15% about 800 and odd firms followed by the Netherlands about 4% 200 odd firms and then France. Africa and the Americas together represented under 5% of this list. A gap possibly best explained by colonial disruption, war and the comparatively late start of formal western style enterprise in those regions rather than by any absence of family commerce. Let's start with Japan, a global epicenter of business longevity. Look at Congo Gumi, founded in 578 CE in Osaka. It is a temple carpentry firm founded by artisans, invited from the Korean Kingdom of Begg J to build a temple in Japan, one of the first Buddhist temples, and for roughly 14 hundred years, and across more than 40 generations, the Congo family passed leadership down. If there wasn't a direct mail descendant, often to an adopted son-in-law, a practice called as Mukoyoshi rather than a biological son, when there was no capable natural air. This deliberate adopt for competence and not just for bloodline, succession-non is arguably the single most important reason for the firm's extraordinary run. The company survived feudal wars, the major restoration, both world wars, and even pivoting into coffin making after a post-World War II construction stimulus ended. And after a period of time, unfortunately, over expansion ended, forced the company to become a subsidiary of the Takamatsu group in 2006. It continues to build and restore temples today as part of that larger firm. Another example from Japan is the Nishiyama on St. Kirinkan, founded roughly in 705 CE, and Oshiriokan, founded roughly in 718 CE. Both of these are hot spring ends, and both are recognized by Guinness among the world's oldest continuously operating hospitality businesses. Most of them are run by the same family lineage for over 1300 years, 40 to 50 generations. Oshiriokan's longevity has been credited partly to intergenerational pride, which extends beyond the owning family itself. Certain staff positions, not just ownership, have themselves been passed down within employee families for generations. This embeds institutional memory deep into day-to-day operations, not just at the ownership level. Notably, Nishiyama saw its first non-family outsider manager take charge only in 2017. This illustrates that even the most bloodline committed multi-century firms may eventually face a professionalization inflection point. Japan's overall stock of long-lived enterprise is not just a handful of famous outliers. Independent estimates put the number of Japanese firms over 100 years old at roughly 20 to 30,000, and Japan is home to 24 of the world's 50 oldest companies founded centuries earlier. In India, considered the example of Gemseji Tata and India's first steel plant on the strategic logic behind it. Gemseji Nasservanji Tata began as a textile trader, rather than continuing to expand within textiles where the Parsi and the Marwadi competition was already intense. He identified steel and industry that India had no domestic capability at all as a nation-building opportunity. He personally scouted for iron ore and coal deposits in Central India years before Tata Steel's 1907 founding, and the project was completed only after his death by son Dura Bjita. This is an early example of a family firm's founder making an investment decisions on a time horizon decades that no purely professionally managed quarterly earnings. The quarters of quarter-tech philosophy driven company of that era would likely have undertaken. And such examples directly illustrate the importance of the long horizon patient capital mechanism, the modern performance literature associates with family ownership. Other example from India is the Gautrej family group, founded roughly in the year 1897, and the Tata group and the Wadi group are among India's best known long-lived family enterprises. Both, all the three of them, the Gautrej group, the Tata group and the Wadi group will pass their 125th anniversary. Two additional less commonly cited examples were naming here are Forbes and Company founded in Bombay in the year 1767, which is one of the oldest companies still operating in India, and tracing its roots to colonial area trading before being acquired by the Shapuji Polynji group and continued today in engineering logistics and IT. The Muruga Pargrou is a Chetier origin firm that began as a money lending business in colonial Burma or Myanmar before returning to India post independence and diversifying into agriculture, financial services and engineering, employing more than 70,000 people. A quick reference, look at this table, which consolidates many of these named examples alongside a few additional countries spanning a broad world tour of very, very old family businesses that continue to thrive.",
