      "transcript_text": "Let's look at a few industrial era family dynasties. Starting in Europe, let's look at the Rothschild family. It is a European banking family which thrived in the 18th century and onward. The built a pan-European banking network famously using a private courier and information system that rival many governments in the speed of information transfer. The financed wars, government debt and infrastructure across Britain, France, Austria and beyond. Across the Atlantic, in the USA, John D. Rockefeller founded Standard Oil in 1870. The Rockefeller family built the first great American industrial trust. and later broken up by the 1911 Standard Oil Antitrust case. This is an example of an early American family parallel to the concentration of economic power anxieties that later drew India's MRTP Act. Again in the US, we see the Ford family. The Ford Motor Company was founded in 1903 and the Crip family, the German steel and armaments which was founded in the United States. In 1811, both of these illustrate the industrial scale of family capitalism of the late 19th and early 20th centuries. And both families fortunes were deeply entangled with the political and military history of their countries through both world wars. A reminder that family firm history is inseparable from national political history and not just a pure economic story.",
