      "transcript_text": "Next, is the Indian Family Business Landscape during the colonial era, during which the managing agency system thrived. As the East India Company and later British trading firms established foot holes in India, Indigenous financhers did not remain passive lenders. They actively moved from pure money lending into becoming brokers and agents for British trading houses. For example, the Andrew Yulebird and Company. They facilitated the export of raw materials such as cotton, opium, jute, indigo and the import of finished manufactured goods. This was the first major strategic pivot in Indian Family Business History. From financing, financing to trade intermediation to eventually direct industrial ownership. How did the managing agency system actually work? The managing agency system which formalised from around the 1820s onward was the dominant form of Indian business organisation for well over a century. Technically it worked as follows. A managing agent, typically an individual, partner membership or firm would be appointed by the founding promoters who are often the agency itself to manage one or more separate joint stock companies on a long term contractual basis. The managing agent handled procurement sales financing and day-to-day operations for the companies under its umbrella. And in return earned a commission based fee. Commonly about 5% on sales or consignment value or up to 10% of net profits plus additional fees for underwriting, interest on fund small billised and office or administrative charges. Critically the managing agent typically contributed only a small fraction of the actual risk capital of the companies it controlled. Most of the capital came from a wide base of relatively passive outside shareholders who are attracted by the agency's reputation and track record and not by any deep due diligence capacity of their own. There was no developed securities analysis industry, credit rating system or strong minority shareholder protection laws at that time. This structure led a single managing agency house pyramid a comparatively small amount of family capital into controlling over a large number of separate public companies. A structural ancestor of the pyramid building company structure of business groups that we see today. Managing agency controlled firms were disproportionately the largest most capital intensive companies in the economy. Precisely the firms were the gap between small agency ownership stakes and large agency operational control was most consequential for corporate governance. Initially, British run managing agencies held a near monopoly over capital intensive sectors like jute. But by the early 20th century even before the second world war, Marwadi entrepreneurs led by the Bill of Family had actively built up their own managing agencies and progressively gained control of the jute industry away from established British agency houses. Using the various same structural tool, managing agency controlled with limited own capital that the British had pioneered against them. James G. Tata founded the Tata group in 1868 in the textile space and later founded Tata steel in 1907. One of the first Indian owned steel plants in Asia deliberately building direct industrial ownership and not merely as an agency fee business. At a time, when most Indian commercial capital was still organized around trade and finance rather than manufacturing. The same feature that made the managing agency system attractive to entrepreneurs small own capital and large control was also its central governance weakness. Agents were paid first out of companies profits via the commission regardless of how the companies might be. minority shareholders failed. This created a well documented pattern of profit-sifining and weak accountability to outside investors. This is a direct historical ancestor of today's regulatory concern with related party transactions and annelling in promoter controlled listed companies. By Indian independence in 1947, the system was already straining under its own concentration of the United States. Nations. The narrow government progressively restricted managing agencies through the companies act of 1956 and the system was formally abolished by the Indra Gandhi government in April of 1970.",
