      "transcript_text": "The Post-Independence or the Licensing Raj era of 1951 to 1991 is another very interesting chapter of Family Businesses in India. Industrial Licensing, Import Substitution and Capital Controls constrained new entry during this period. But existing Family Business houses did not simply benefit passively from reduced competition. They actively lobbied for and navigated the licensing bureaucracy, cultivated political relationships to secure scarce industrial licenses and import quotas and diversified opportunistically into whatever sectors the government made accessible to them. This produced the sprawling and unrelated diversification conglomerate structure that still characterizes many large Indian business houses today. This is an example of a strategic response to licensed driven opportunity and not simply an accident of history. The Monopoly's and restrictive trade practices act of 1969 was enacted specifically to curb the economic concentration of large mostly family control business houses. Tatters and Billas in particular were explicit targets of that era's political debate over industrial concentration. This is a useful reminder that the tension between family business scale and public concern about concentrated economic power is not a new 21st century phenomena in India. Family houses actively restructured around the MRTP era constraints. Rather than growing a single large licensed entity which would attract regulatory scrutiny once it crossed asset thresholds, many groups fragmented ownership across many smaller family branch controlled entities. A structural response to regulation that itself shaped the ownership patterns which are still visible in some business families today.",
