      "transcript_text": "Let us have a look at the financial and economic performance of family firms in this topic now. Does family ownership actually help or hurt the financial performance is a question that we are trying to address? The normal thinking would be that family firms are actually not as good as the private firms. But, actual research has shown this as to be counterintuitive. Family firms on an average actually outperform. So, there is a landmark study done in 2003 by Anderson and Reed. And that study examined leading American S&P 500 firms over a 10 year period and then understood as to whether this is right or wrong. What they actually found is that one, more than nearly 20 years ago, percent of the equity held on S&P 500 is by family firms. And actually the result contrary to what the researchers expected, agency theory like we read, predicts that concentrated insider control should be costly to outsider stakeholders. But, in family firms actually showed that their performances are better than non-family firms. The performance was even stronger than family owned firms. The CEO of the S&P 500, also was a family member rather than an outside professional. The performance was even better. There are some well known American examples that can illustrate what we are trying to say from research. Walmart for example, the Walton family, even after Sam Walton's death still a large controlling shareholding is done by the family or Ford Motor Company, which again has, while the shareholding structure is such that the family owns a disproportionate voting control. Both are very large S&P 500 companies and families far better than the index out there.",
