      "transcript_text": "So, why does family ownership help? So, there are multiple theories on how to interpret this Anderson and Re-Finding on why family firms can better or performing better than the rest. The first one is the alignment effect. Agency theory says that once an agent decides to, what do you call, give some of the work to, once the owner decides to give some of the work to an agent, then the interest as long as they don't sink with each other can lead to a loss of value for the owner. But in a family firm is concentrated ownership within the family. The agency theory doesn't work. In a sense that the agent is in sync with the interest of the manager. As a result of it, yes, it works a lot. When the CEO's own family wealth is tied up in the firm's long run value, unlike an undiversified or a professional manager who actually runs it. In the family as a strong incentive, personal incentive to actually do something which can destroy value. In a family business monitoring cost is very, very low because in the case where outside shareholders need to spend less on monitoring mechanism. They have to have incentive contracts, they have to have some interventions. The family's own incentives are already broadly aligned with the actually the firm itself. The family firm also has a very, very long term horizon. They have spoken about this before. They plan for the long term, they ensure that the results are not something they can see only in the long term. They don't work on a quarter to quarter basis. All these things actually help in reasons why the families performance is higher than a non-family firm. The other theory other than we just discussed, discussed alignment theory, it's called the entrenchment theory. There is a study by Willa Gunga and Amit in 2006 which discussed a different kind of risk that a family control can create a type 2 kind of a problem. What they mean is not between the owner and the manager, but between controlling family shareholders itself, those in the management role, those may have board seats because the family controls both ownership and management. It can extract private benefits of control. What do you mean by private benefits of control? Two things actually. One is related party transactions that transfer value within one part of the family to other part of the family. Think of a large family where multiple companies where I purchase from this company, I sell to this company or I get raw material at a differential pricing from some other company. So, all these can cause some issues and therefore as a result of it which suspicion can arise by the family firm is really better than the outside family firm. Appointing underqualified family members to senior roles is very very common. Again, resisting actions which can actually reduce the control the shareholders. For example, the family may decide not to take on an offer of an external acquisition or a fund infusion because they will dilute family control. So, it will benefit all the shareholders which has the family itself even though there is logical benefit. They might refuse to take over it because of the control issues. So, there are two things that we are talking about. What we call the entrenchment effect. They are talking about the alignment effect. How do we reconcile these two things? Literature as well as research says conclusion that the alignment effect actually dominates on an average. Anderson Reeves net finding is that the relationship is non-linear. Performance tends to be strongest at the moderate ownership stakes. The alignment benefits are captured without too much entrenchment effect. But again, the research also says that decline can start at very high ownership concentration levels where entrenchment risk begins to dominate. This is why it is non-linear. So, these are the two theories that we should discuss on why family firms actually better than non-family firms.",
