      "transcript_text": "We have discussed the performance of family businesses on how they are better than the non-family businesses. We have also discussed the impact the family businesses have on society at large. One factor, however, is that these family businesses have continued to perform out the performance of family businesses as well as have this impact for a long period of time. What stands out for family businesses is their longevity. Everyone has at some point has heard that family businesses do not survive beyond 3 generations. There are terms like shirts, sleeves, shirts, sleeves and 3 generations. We will discuss it in more detail in a subsequent module. But usually people think that family businesses do not survive. What actually happens is, family businesses do survive the long run. What does it mean to be a family business? We have to discuss what factors are there that makes these businesses survive the long term. While intent is there, are there more factors that actually look at contribute to these firms surviving the really long run at that level of performance and at that level of social commitment as well. We should look at structural factors in depth in terms of markets, in terms of institutions, in terms of country specific things to understand why family businesses are not. survive the long term. Let us see institutional factors. Factors like property rights, inheritance laws, all these have a very important role to play in the survival of the family firm. Higher survival firms are linked to where there are predictable inheritance and property laws. If you see most of the western world Japan, western world, these are all very predictable. Unless there are some areas in countries where there are periods marked by nationalization or an unpredictable inheritance tax, then it lowers the survival of the family firms. French nationalization is an issue that happened in Wendell family in 1974 when the 250 year old steel business suddenly collapsed when nationalization happened. That is unpredictability. The second thing is about cultural factors that are there. In Japan, for example, we spoke about how a capable sun in lock can also get into the business. Effectively, they have decoupled competence requirements from genetic lineage and therefore, they have a wider pool to choose from. Cultural factors can also drive longevity. The other factor is the capital market depth and the market for capital control. In a market where there are very deep and liquid capital markets, and a good market for corporate control, that means you can have private equity buyouts or hostile takeovers. Family firms will face a lot of pressure either to professionalize or be acquired and therefore, in order to persist, they can have to be very good. Only the fittest can finally survive and remain independent. In economies where there are shallower capital, weaker market for corporate control India has been one for a long time. capital markets yet to be fully developed. They allow for a wider range of family firms to survive including weaker ones. If the capital market is very active and structured deep, weaker performers will not survive and they will get taken over. But in places like India, they survive for a much longer time. Political stability is an important component of it. Businesses thrives when there is political stability or we should not have sudden shocks coming into the system. Suddenly if you have a licensed Raj kind of a thing in India, it will be very difficult for people without deep pockets or those who can't hold on to some of their strengths. It will be very difficult. A sudden liberalization like 1991 can also cause some upheavals. But in spite of all these changes in India, the family firms have survived. India has a large number of family firms and a few people. concentrated ones. Political stability is a driver. Industry level drivers for example, craft and heritage based sectors, they survive always. And right from even olden days from we say from 1300 CE, the Japanese ones that we have identified, they all survived because the kind of area they are in hospitality for example, hot spring ends we spoke about, food and beverage and craft manufacturing. These are all very very very intricate and therefore the expertise of the founder becomes important. Consumer demand underlying production techniques change very slowly and therefore it ensures the longevity. However, we will show opposite pattern are capital intensive industries which can be rapidly disrupted. For example, let us say a telecom industry, the traditional phone that you might have seen and suddenly when technology comes and takes over, those kind of changes can really disrupt and that is where the longevity decreases. Let us also talk about the firm level drivers that lead to longevity of the family firms. These are areas like governance. Do they have a family constitution council, how frequently they meet and discuss and do they have the structures in place, the mechanisms in place which we will discuss in much more good governance. due to longevity. Similarly, the most important thing in many family businesses succession planning, how well they do it, how carefully they display and well in advance so that succession planning is not a cost for trouble is an important reason why family firms are able to survive longer. Ownership structure also drive some amount of longevity. The legal mechanism that is directly connected to HUF in India actually is the highly helped longevity in India. Families that consolidated or consolidate ownership through whether it is a trust or a holding company, all of these have thought through those mechanisms and therefore they preserve their control and their capital and last longer. Profitialization of management is another topic where you separate who runs the business from who owns the business and that is associated with the multi-generation survival because you bring non-family expertise. These are the types of opportunities for operating goals, family retains ownership and they continue for much longer ensure that their requirements are met by the professional board. So, you have seen structures as well as factors like culture which can drive as well as family reasons why family businesses survive much longer than non-family firms.",
