      "transcript_text": "So, what does the family typically value? Within the family members what they will look for is for each other to support them. And they will look for extreme loyalty from each other. Trust is something that is very, very important. That is the most important currency. For the family side of a family business. And even in the business trust matters. Loyalty, trust, support from other family members are very, very important. But at the same time, they will not give importance to skills or how educated someone is, how useful a person can be or skills can be for the business is not something that is considered important. Talent is not again given too much importance in the family system. The main point here is you should accept me for who I am, in whatever form I am, in whatever way I am. And that is, that calls for an extreme level of trust in spite of any of the other shortcomings that is there. That is the premium that the family expects from each other. And this loyalty, support and trust. That kind of also extends as an expectation to other members within the family business. They are kind of an extended family member. Every family member is expected to be like this across multiple branches if necessary. And does not matter what role you have in the business, whether you have a role or not. This is something that is mandated within the family itself. If it is so complex with so many relationships, so many expectations, there should be some kind of a hierarchy. Some kind of a rule that defines who I should listen to. When should I defer to somebody? He is a some kind of a hierarchy within family systems. The hierarchy goes by two things. One by the generation. So, generation is important. You are second gen. I am third gen. Then second gen obviously has a bigger say. It does not matter like I said what that second generation may not be as talented as a third generation, may not have the skills. It does not matter that person is second gen matters. Within a generation, age will matter. The elder brother is always something who is to be listened to. With no questions asked and that is important. No questions asked. There is no discussion. If the elder brother has said that this is the way it has to be done. So, that is the hierarchy. It can create problems sometimes when these are not can turn out to be in a different way in public. For example, in one very big motor company in India, the second generation has about seven brothers. The youngest brother is just a day older than the son of the eldest brother. So, happens in that company. So, these two have played together, gone to school together, did everything together but they are a generation apart. It is expected that the son of the elder brother will still listen to the cha cha. So, they are the hierarchy. So, there is a hierarchy. Well, especially will go by hierarchy. So, they may be a day apart but the cha cha will get disproportionate well as compared to the bhatija. Because of the generation as an issue. And age is one day apart does not matter. All these hierarchy, the way it is decided matters. And then of course, there is the huge levels of informality in how the processes are decided upon, how the policies are enforced or how the communications happen. A lot of it is unwritten in a family system. The rules, the hierarchy, all that is passed on from generation to generation. So, there are no written down rules. There are no written down policies. It is communicated by person to person generation to generation. Understood people function within those rules, informally no questions being asked. So, there is a lot of informality. There is a lot of trust. Therefore, they are able to take things on word and get things done. So, certainly it is a huge thing. Bureaucracy we know can stifle many things. So, informality is a huge plus for the family and the business. Which is where a lot of family businesses make it a point every year to ensure that they all come together. There are certain events when the business itself would go on a very different holiday. Like typically the dualities in the family businesses get together. So, the business is effectively on a shutdown. It is also important the family bonds together. What has happened over time is even in family businesses as families have spread out to multiple businesses. The members are not in one location. They are not in one place. Earlier generations used to be even if you have three brothers, they used to be in one building. Their communities were in spite of four or five brothers. They used to have one kitchen. So, everyone is eating in the same place from the same kitchen one dining room. So, bonding used to happen a lot over the dinner table when every family member used to come together. Today that is vanishing. Even within family families some sense of nuclearities coming along. So, while they still function very cohesively like a family, something has come along. Some distances have come along as a result of the business going wider further. So, this kind of a family bonding is something that they all realize is important. For the new bond, Bathijia to know the eldest cha-cha also they have to figure out a ways. So, all this happens informally very nicely done in many communities. So, participants again this is an activity for you to reflect on these bonding activities that your family engages in. Are there certain events, certain functions that your elders have prioritized that you should attend and interact more. Can you recall these kind of events and compare them with others of your friends with what kind of events or events are happening? And try to ask yourself what value systems have been inculcated in you because of these bonding activities that occur over different periods of time.",
