          "id": 1,
          "question": "Compare and contrast the entry rules, compensation structures, and stock ownership distribution between the 'business first' and 'family first' philosophies in a family firm. Provide a concise analysis of the trade-offs involved in each approach.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The entry rule for business first is merit-based, requiring specific skills and qualifications for the role, whereas family first is relative-based, allowing entry primarily based on kinship with closer relatives having easier access. In terms of compensation, business first follows market conditions and job descriptions, with pay varying by demand and supply, while family first mandates equal pay for the same generation (e.g., cousins receive the same), dependent on age and hierarchy. Regarding stock ownership, business first shares stock based on performance and contribution, and can include non-family members, whereas family first distributes stock equally by family branch per generation, ignoring performance or skills. The trade-off involves a choice between operational efficiency and meritocracy (business first) versus familial harmony and equal kinship distribution (family first). Examiners may test the ability to identify these structural differences and evaluate which philosophy suits a given firm's goals.",
