      "transcript_text": "Is it a common framework that we could use or a simple way to understand how the family dimension or the family system and the business system interact in this family business larger system. Yes, I there is one framework which is extremely popular. It is called the three circle model. Basically, it shows depicts three intertwined circles, each circle representing a certain dimension. So, the three dimensions are family, the business and the owners as a third dimension. So, each all stakeholders in the family business are part of one or two or three of these circles depending on who they are in the family business conundrum. So, can we delve deeper into who these different stakeholders are and where they would be positioned within this three circle diagram. So, there are seven stakeholders if you see from the three circle diagram. Each stakeholder brings their own lens into what they require from this family business and they have their own goals, their own ambitions and their own expectations from the business. Yes. And these are interconnected circles. So, each circle has an expectation from the other circle as well. Yes. How these all play out like we have discussed depends on how good the entire system gets run and how long they last. So, it is important for us to know each circle's expectations, ambitions, thoughts. So, let us start with the most important circle which is role number one. The first stakeholder is the one in the centre. Now, this is usually a family owner who is also part of the business. So, he is a family, he is part of family, he is a owner, he is also in the business. Typically, these are the patriarchs of the business who founded the business and exist in all three quadrants in all three circles. He is running the business. It is part of the family but he also is the largest shareholder or probably the only shareholder sometimes. The second stakeholder, you see number two on your own. on this diagram here. These are family members who are the owners also but not involved in the business. Basically, where you say they are owners means that they have some share holdings. This can be let us say when a company starts, let us say if it is a partnership, the spouse may be a partner along with the patriarch who started the business, but the spouse is not involved in the business. The spouse is very much a family is also an owner because he is outside She is a shareholder. They do not play any active role in the business. They play a bigger role in the family. They are part of two circles. At number three on your screen are non-family owners who work in the business. Now, they are owners and they work in the business. They are owners in spite of being non-family because they probably invested in the company at some stage. They started as co-founders also as possible, but they are part of the business, but they are not family. There could be a shareholding difference between the patriarch owner and this non-family owner because the patriarch or the family owner usually wants the majority stake. They could be a minority stakeholder, but they still make them an owner. But they play an active role in the business. At number four are family members who work in the business, but they are not owners. Listen, they are family members. They are part of business. Two circles, they are not part of the ownership circle. This usually is the next generation. Before they succeed into the business, when the younger generation first joins the business, this is the state they are in. They are family. They are part of the business. They are not yet shareholders. Therefore not yet owners. At all their dreams are saying at some stage, I also want to be a shareholder and become an owner. And that is something that is their end state that they want to be and then grow the business. At number five are non-family owners who do not work in the business. This is again an investor who has invested in the business and outside investor, but doesn't take an active part in the business. It is not on the board. It is not part of the management. And usually let's the patriarch run because there is a high degree of trust in the patriarch and let's the family and the patriarch run the business on their own terms. This is rare usually because families are usually don't try and give business to a shareholder into somebody, especially if they are not going to be part of the business. They usually start off with somebody and that is when shareholders are given. At a later stage, unless it is really forced on them, they would not like to be in this situation or if it is a listed company or if it is listed. At number six are family members who are neither owners nor employed in the business. This can happen when it is a family member too young to join the business. They still are part of the family. They have not yet joined the business. Obviously, they can't be owners at this stage. Most cases, they won't be owners at a young age unless something else has happened. So these are people who are clearly told at some stage you want to join the family business and that is the stage they will become first part of the business circle and later part of the ownership circle but they usually start in the first circle. But there are also family members who are non-owners and remain in this part. That is six. For example, people who have married into the family where their natural transition of ownership does not happen and they are not necessarily involved with the business in any way and therefore, they are still family members, non-owners and they remain in that dimension. Yes. While they may not look to move to the other dimensions at any point later. Correct. What that also is changing size and something for the positive but what you are right, historically, traditionally people marrying into family business, the spouses especially, they do not play any role in the management, they are never going to be owners but yeah. So they will remain in one circle. What is changing is they somehow manage to move to the second circle which is a business circle but they will never make the transition to the ownership circle. At number seven are non-family employees who are not owners. So they are neither family nor owners, they remain only in the business circle. These are professionals who have joined the business usually. From outside they have joined the business, they are recruited for their skills, for their talent. They will continue to serve the management out here. They will continue to serve the family patriarch not necessarily the family but they will they are far more professional and they will limit their work toward the business needs are. So these are the seven stakeholders in the three circle diagram.",
