          "model_answer": "The family member who works in the business but is not an owner (Stakeholder Type 4) occupies the intersection of the Family and Business circles only. Their primary expectation is typically to gain ownership status over time, as their 'end state' is to become a shareholder and grow the business. They are driven by the ambition of future ownership and legacy creation.\n\nThe non-family owner who works in the business (Stakeholder Type 3) occupies the intersection of the Business and Owners circles. They are owners because they have invested capital or were co-founders, but they are non-family. Their expectations are likely focused on financial returns on their investment and professional management of the business. They may have a different time horizon or risk tolerance compared to the family member.\n\n**Potential for Conflict**: Misalignment can arise regarding strategic direction and control. The family member may feel entitled to a greater role or ownership share due to familial ties, while the non-family owner may prioritize financial efficiency or different growth strategies. Conversely, alignment can occur if both parties share a common goal of business growth and professionalization. The family member may value the non-family owner's expertise, and the non-family owner may appreciate the family member's commitment to the firm's longevity.\n\n**Potential for Alignment**: Both stakeholders share the 'Business' circle, meaning they are actively working towards the same operational goals. If the family member successfully transitions into the 'Owners' circle (becoming a shareholder), their interests may align more closely with the non-family owner as co-owners of the enterprise.\n\n**Rubric**:\n- Core conceptual understanding of stakeholder types and circle intersections: 3 marks\n- Clear distinction of goals/ambitions for each type: 3 marks\n- Analysis of conflict/alignment dynamics: 4 marks",
