      "transcript_text": "The third important power is the power to sustain. How do you sustain this business over a period of time? Both the family and the business. You need to get the competent successor who understands these particular dynamics of the business as well as the family. And also, the business is the best. It will be wise enough to kind of have a long term orientation. For this successor has to fully understand the vision of the company, the purpose of the company, what is set out by the founders, and what has been coming along over at least one generation. That is something completely imbibed by the next generation. For that long term orientation to come in, they have to be clear on what this company is up to. We often speak of the Tata Group. Tata's purpose was clearly mentioned that we are nation builders. This is at J.R.D. Tata. I did not say that we are building a business. We are building a nation. And see if you see the areas they went into. They went into steel, they went into auto, initial stages. Those are all which are building blocks for the nation. So, auto employees, so many more people, steals, foundational for so many industries for us. and mining, they went into all of those areas and they said their nation builders. That culture was passed on, passed on. While they have for it, recently into consumer areas. That we only for the last 25 years or so, they have for it into the consumer segments. But largely they were in the infrastructure segments which actually were building blocks for the entire nation, not just a company. So, that culture, that vision, that purpose has to get translated. It is a prerequisite for, when you say for the long term orientation of the company. And the founders have to identify the next generation who are in sync with this philosophy and the vision. Correct. Like for example, look at the Gothraic group. Four generations and the business is in spite of the recent split. Is very stable and align with what the group purpose and vision over these transitions has been. Correct. And it is a lot of effort to actually communicate this to four generations. Generation after generation communicates this. And they have broken the three generation rule clearly and look set to for a couple of generations more. But they have communicated this. Very consistently. That has helped a lot. Similarly, even the TVS group, if you look at how they have been able to manage these transitions across generations. And still maintain that focus. And if you actually look at the extended TVS group, if you so call it today. It is not just the TVS, but because of Malika, you also have the buffet on the other side. Correct. But TVS itself, the entire, they have extended, like I said, they have created so many more new businesses. Correct. So, they have just into auto today. Whether it takes Sundaram Vassana, you take TVS, reach a karateas or they call it. The TVS mobility now is Harita. There are so many more insurance, oil Sundaram, insurance they went into so mutual fund Sundaram mutual. The number of areas, finance, auto, they have gone into so many areas. That's a creation role. Also they have managed to sustain that very well by passing on their tradition. Also developing a clear succession, how what will happen. So they have succeeded in so many more friends. All of these leads to that sustenance. Yes. And this long term vision plays a very important role. Again revisiting the TVS group example. The group companies were among the first Indian companies to win the Deming Award for quality. Yes. And that's only possible in a market like India, where the pressure is on meeting more short term targets. Your continuous investment in quality, which aligns with the family's vision and the longer term purpose that they look at for the organization. That in many ways is what is enabled them to make and stick with that investment. And even after that particular awards have come. Of course, other Indian companies are subsequently won it. But they were among the first. Yes. To show that family businesses from India can really deliver world class quality. And that only comes if the across generations you have that continued investment into what the larger philosophy and vision is. Right. So these three powers collectively are very very important for the owners of the family business to understand. They have the power to destroy very importantly. The power to create they could craft an entirely new entrepreneurial journey. And also the power to sustain what has already been created and how they take that forward. And these three powers span both the ownership dimension as well as the business dimension. And within the three circle diagram it moves across all these three elements. The family, the owners and the business dimension. Yes. So.",
