          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The core dilemma of the right to transfer is that the incumbent generation often resists exiting, treating the firm as an extension of family life and desiring to stay in charge for as long as possible, much like the British monarchy. This resistance is driven by questions of whether the next generation is ready, has earned respect, and can take over authority. If the exit is uninformed or sudden (e.g., death without prior communication), stability is lost, leading to turmoil. Examples include the Sanjay Kapoor incident, where assets fell into turmoil after his death, and Rubai Ambani, who did not inform stakeholders about the transfer process. Conversely, proper planning, as seen with the Max group who stuck to a devolved timeline, prevents surprises. Furthermore, the owner must inform three circles: other owner-family members, non-owner family members, and professionals/external shareholders (as with Reliance and Google/Meta). Even after transfer, the family may retain the family name (e.g., Italian families selling to outside investors) to protect their legacy, illustrating that the 'why' and 'how' of transfer are as important as the right itself.\n\nRubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding of incumbent resistance and stability loss: 3 marks\n- Identification and explanation of the need to inform stakeholder circles: 3 marks\n- Reference to specific lecture examples (Sanjay Kapoor, Rubai Ambani, Max group, Reliance): 2 marks\n- Discussion of legacy preservation through name retention: 2 marks\",\n      \"rubric\": [{\"criterion\": \"Core conceptual understanding of incumbent resistance and stability loss\", \"marks\": 3}, {\"criterion\": \"Identification and explanation of the need to inform stakeholder circles\", \"marks\": 3}, {\"criterion\": \"Reference to specific lecture examples (Sanjay Kapoor, Rubai Ambani, Max group, Reliance)\", \"marks\": 2}, {\"criterion\": \"Discussion of legacy preservation through name retention\", \"marks\": 2}]\n    }\n  ]",
