      "transcript_text": "Let us see how a family business evolves over time. You see, over time, the family keeps growing. Now, as the family grows, it definitely has an impact on the way the business is conducted and how the equations are between the family and the business. So, let us see, what is, what is the value of the business? What are the stages as the family business evolves? And at each stage, let us understand who is driving it, let us understand what are the issues that come up, what frictions can come up, how are they resolved and what are the changes that need to be done at every stage for the business to continue as it goes along. Usually, there are four stages in a family business. The first stage is what we call the controlling owner stage. The second stage is what we call a sibling partnership stage. The third one is a cousin consortium stage and the last one is a family federation stage. What we will now do is go through stage by stage and understand at each stage, each of these stages who drives the business, who controls the family also at that stage and what are the impact on the business, the way it is controlled at that particular stage. So, let us take the first one. The way to see these stages are depicted in this diagram actually. See, the controlling owner stage is only one person who starts the company. He has certain thoughts on his starts the company and that we will discuss in the controlling owner stage. In this diagram, for example, there are three children that he has. The three children when they move in and control the business is what we call as a sibling partnership stage. We are at this stage, it is not important to see who controls what and all. And when they reach that stage, either three siblings are in charge of the business, that stage is called the sibling partnership stage. And then when the next generation comes and each generation might be 30 years apart. When the next generation comes and the third generation, you will have a whole host of cousins who have entered the business. It is quite possible, some do not enter the business also, but for a minute if you assume all of them are in the business, in some capacity or the other and they are running the business together. That stage is called the cousin consortium stage. And then when the family exceeds that goes to the last level, it is what we call as a family federation stage, where the number of family members can be really really high. How does it work as something that you can see at that stage? Before we go to examine each of these stages, let us understand. Does every family have to go through all these four stages? The answer is really no. Because let us take the controlling owner stage. In the previous diagram that we just saw, you saw that there was one founder, right? It quite happens in many families that two brothers have started the business together. One takes care of the market, one takes care of the back end and they have started and grown the business over time. So they have started at a sibling partnership stage. It is usually a partnership. So it does not mean that every family has to go through four stages. Usually it starts off in one of the two first stages, either a single owner or two siblings have started off the business and then it moves on from there. It is rare that somebody and the cousins start off the business in stage one. But yeah, there may be one or two such examples, but usually rare. So let us take the first one, which is the controlling owner stage. In a controlling owner stage, ownership is usually held by one person. It does not mean that it is always only by one person. It is sometimes a couple who have started this. Usually divided in some ratio, but largely control is with one person. So what happens in that stage is the person is really engrossed in building the business from scratch. So he really has to hunt for the customers, understand what the value prop is for the customer, what needs of the customers that he can fulfill. How do you raise funds for the business to thrive? The margins here are between the family and the business are small there. So this person is focused a lot on raising the family at the same time also building the business. He has to raise a team for himself for getting the organization structure rolling. And then he has to also think of as the business grows, what do you do of the next generation? How do you get his successor into the business and more important? How does he let himself out of the business? So these are all the thoughts that the founding owner has to go through. But more importantly, he has to worry about the business itself growing and reaching some size that it is meaningful for both the next generation to view it as something that they will succeed into. And also something that can meaningfully provide for the family as well. And that's the primary concern of the founding owner. How does the controlling owner stage work? See, the decision making can be very, very autocratic. One person holds all the power and it is run pretty much by the whims and fancies of that person. So there is no governance. What that person says works both in the family and in the business. There is no governance mechanism, there is nobody to oversee that person, there is no board of governors. So it works like that. What he decides works as to work at least. So when there is no strategy behind it and you run with whims and fancies, there will be some limitations on growth. And that's something that is built into this stage. Succession is very unclear at this stage. Because of two things, unless it reaches some level where it is meaningful for the successor to come into the business, it doesn't make sense. Secondly, the founder himself usually are very reluctant to let go. Even if there is a successor, they'll wait. So that power for as long as possible because for the founder, his entire identity is linked to that business. Business runs on his name. Letting go would be in loss of identity as well. So therefore the reluctance to let go. There is in this stage, there is a risk of one person holding it all. Because the dependence of that one person is extremely high. The business doesn't make any contingency plan as well. So everything is dependent on the founder and if should something happen to the founders health, well there are issues that will come up. In this stage, boundaries are very blurred. Capital moves between family and business in a free-flowing manner. There is no distinguishing between what is spent for the family and what is spent for the business. Very difficult to account in that way. Also, how are people in this kind of a company? They are usually extremely loyal. They are usually something that the founder has started from when he was started the business in one small room with one desk etc. So loyalty runs very high. Very informal. And they will be aware. The employees will be aware that growth is going to be low for them. Because this is started somewhere. If the company by chance grows very fast, your founder is open to external investment. Then the growth likely to be fast. Else growth will be slow. That is likely to impact the growth of the employee also.",
