          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The controlling owner stage is defined by a single founder (or couple) who starts the business from scratch. Key characteristics include: (1) Decision-making is autocratic, with one person holding all power and running the business by whims and fancies, and there is no governance mechanism or board of oversight; (2) Succession is very unclear and the founder is often reluctant to let go because his entire identity is linked to the business; business runs on his name, and letting go feels like a loss of identity; (3) Family and business boundaries are blurred, with capital moving freely between the family and the business, making it very difficult to distinguish personal from business expenses. This stage is the foundation because it establishes the initial ownership structure, governance style, and operational dynamics that will inevitably need to be restructured as the family grows and subsequent stages (sibling partnership, cousin consortium, family federation) are entered. The founder's early choices regarding control, boundaries, and succession planning set the trajectory for how the business will evolve.\n\nRubric: Core conceptual understanding (5 marks): Accurately identifies the three key characteristics (autocratic decision-making, succession ambiguity/identity link, blurred boundaries). Analytical application (5 marks): Explains why this stage is the foundation for subsequent stages, linking the founder's early decisions to future restructuring needs.",
