      "transcript_text": "So, let us understand the tensions that are there, the frictions that are there in a cousin consortiums, these. The first one, if it sees the active and passive ownership, active ownership would mean that while they are all owners, the active cousins will take an active role in the company. They will contribute to the growth of the business. While the passive ones need not actually contribute to the company, they will still be owners, they will still expect their dividends, but they are very clear that their path lies outside the company and they want to set their own path and do something on their own. So, again, let us look at the expectation. The active cousins may feel that they are very invested in the business, right, and they want the business to grow. So, they will expect that profits are reinvested back into the business so that they can take the next investment and grow the business higher. They will not prefer to take the capital out of the company. They don't want to take dividends and sit on it and let the business flounder. They want to keep investing and growing. Now, those who are passive, the cousins, may expect a higher dividends and payouts because they are less invested in the business, even though they know it is, they hold a shareholding and they know it is important to have capital range. investing is invested. They want a higher dividend so that they can have a quality of life which they have expectations. Like we said, their expectations can be different. The other friction that will come is about rivalry between family branches. Now, they are multiple branches at a cousin stage. There are a finite number of roles in a single company. You can only take up some, one branch can handle finance, one branch can handle sales, all this can be done. can happen. But, there is only finite number of roles. Some of those roles, it is best that they go together. So, then what happens is branches of the family end up competing for the roles. This is a respect to a skills. They are not talking about skills. What they are doing is jockeying for influence. They are jockeying for how much board seat can they get. How much investment will come into the roles that they can get into. Even in a even in a system, a zoom, like I said, by this stage, many families will split up multiple companies or high off into existing business into multiple companies or actually diversify into multiple areas. There is still a holding company which decides how much capital has to go where. In such a case, they will want influence over board decisions. So, how many branches can have seat on the board and how much they can influence is an issue. In the typical HUF family, predominantly what happens is the karta decides. Every branch will are definitely part of the HUF, but they may not have management control. So, this succession law actually which happened which gave equal power to women as well in this. And it is important to note that when the case that earlier much alum launched on the Muruga Pagroupe saying that I should have a seat, I should have a board seat, I should be able to influence the decision. She won that case though I think the family has decided in some way which is not public, but it is an important ruling. So, competition for influence is very, very important at that stage. And therefore, they want each of them will want higher investment from the holding company into their business or their roles. Competency gap is another friction that will arise. Not all cousins will be equally skilled. Lack of skill has no relation to the expectations they will have. Irrespective that expectations will remain the same or sky high. So, what happens is that family harmony has to be still be maintained. See it is worth keeping the family harmony rather than getting to invite and split the family and go your way. Then what happens is in the cousin consortium stage each branches becomes very small. Sheralding is very small and therefore, when you break up into so many parts what each person gets is extremely small. So, it is really important for them to maintain family harmony. So, irrespective of skill gaps everyone is compensated equally.",
