      "transcript_text": "So, let us look at this table where we can really see what is the role of each cousin, each kind of cousin I must say and what is it that each of those cousins want and what their biggest fear is. So, let us take a cousin who is an active CEO in the business, he is running the business very much. invested in the business, remember all the cousins are owners. Now, what would he want? Since he is so invested in the business, he will want to reinvest the profits and grow the company. So, his objective will be I will reduce the dividend, I do not need to take money out and there is no need to increase the lifestyle of the entire family, we are good where we are. So, let us whatever profits we get, let us look at reinvestment either in the same business or let us diverge the same business. or let us diversify into something adjacent or find a way how I can get better returns for the capital, that is the active CEO thought process. What is his biggest fear? The biggest fear that the active CEO will have is one of interference. Now, from whom interference from other branches of the family, who will have a different expectation totally from the business. Some of them will be active players in the business, some of them may not be, some of them may be passive and they may have other business interests elsewhere. So, they would say how can I get capital from this business and grow my business where I have a higher shareholding. So, decision making becomes an issue for this active CEO because the board will have all the branches, all of them will have board seats and they will decide, they can have a say put together, see individually none of them will have a big enough shareholding to influence. But if they can gang up together, they can't put a stop to many of the things that an active CEO might want. The second kind of a role is one of an active cousin. What is the difference? This cousin is also in the business but maybe not a CEO. So, this cousin may hold an important position, maybe younger to the CEO and there is something that usually happens. This is active cousin is a younger one. And he may be heading a division, he may be heading a function, he may be heading having a significant role which really contributes to the business. What is the biggest fear? His fear is that he may be undermined by the other branches of the family. He may be told that the other branches, other branches may tell him that he is not doing a good job. In case this role is given to some other branch, the cousin there can do a better job than this cousin. These kind of questions will come up. So, performance is almost always questioned. The third biggest kind of cousin is a passive owner. Now, this person is passive, he is an owner, does not play any role in the business. He has started out most likely, he has started a path outside the business. He started something on his own. Using some funds of his own and some funds which is taken out of the business and started something on the own, which is doing well, which may require capital more. So, what is this passive owner looking for? Can I get extra dividends from this company? So, I can take out the dividends and reinvest elsewhere. If it is even done for a good cause, it is still worth it. Sometimes the dividends are taken out and reinvested in the cause which will not get in the same kind of returns. And the capital has to get you better returns, you have to put it in a place where returns are more. So, this is an important issue for the active CEO that your dividend, your taking money out of the company. The biggest fear that the business has this good money dividends which could have been invested in the company is being taken out and wasted elsewhere. That is a fear that the other owners are likely to have. Now, look, let us look at the younger cousin. This younger cousin many times what happens is he is given the short shift. That is his biggest fear. He is not taken seriously. He is like the Chout-O-To in the family. To Barajay, I will give you something. It is the kind of thing the active CEO will say. He is never considered grown up enough. This is the person who says that I will give him the role which does not carry too much importance. This younger cousin has a very good chance that he is feeling ignored. He is being slighted. He is not being given due importance. Even though he has the skills, now what does he do? The only option left for me is he realizes there is not much of a role in the company that is ever going to get given there are other active cousins in the family. And we are going to take up the plumber roles. They are elder to them and age matters in a family business. Therefore, I will seek my pastures outside. So, he requests for some investment from the company itself to kick start his business outside or he gets funds from somewhere and start something outside. He has left sometimes a lesser choice than a passive owner. A passive owner possibly even had a choice but wants to just enjoy dividends and take care of whatever lifestyle that person wants. The younger cousin may be left with lesser choice. A senior cousin is somewhat closer to an active cousin. They also seek stability. He may or may not be in the business but he wants stability of both the family and the business. Understands the importance of the business to keep the family also stable. And he understands also that in case the business does not do well, there is a loss of legacy also. The senior cousin is still somewhat slightly more wedded to the family name. He is more emotionally connected to the family name and understand that this is not something they should let go of. So, loss of legacy is something they fear. Then there is a minority branch which has let us say, branched out so much that there are very small shareholding. Even if they have a board seat, they will not be listened to. They want a voice, their voice to be heard. That is what they want. They think they can contribute a lot to the business. They have the skills but they will almost be ever heard. And there fear is, well because of the minority shareholder, I will not be listened to. In fact, I will be told what to do and what not to do. I will be dominated by the rest of the family. Well, I am here to actually provide some genuinely good inputs but I will not be heard. That is our biggest fear. And lastly, let us look at the professional CEO. Now, this is not a family member. He is not an owner. He is not part of the ownership but he is part of the management. This guy or this person, what they want is an autonomy to run the business. They have the skills, their professionals, their work elsewhere, their come with immense experience. They have handled such companies before. They know exactly what to do. You know what does it take? Where should the investment lie? What should be prioritized? What kind of talent is required? They understand everything. Can they can, will they be allowed to do what they want? That is the question that they have. That is the fear they have. They will fear that I will take some very good proposals to the board and it will get shut down. It will get shut down because the family who are sitting on the board seat cannot agree to what is important because there are multiple requirements within that family group itself. So, for him, it is always a task to forge a consensus. How can he work with a few family members who are also keenly invested and can he get their buy-in and therefore force aboard a decision which can be in favor of what he wants and that is may exactly be what the business wants. So, the experience of the professional CEO sometimes does not get utilized fully. These are the kind of issues conflict that may arise in a concert consortium stage.",
