      "transcript_text": "Welcome to this module. In this module, we will be discussing succession in family businesses. We will look at what succession is, who are the actors involved, what are the factors and challenges in succession and the point of view from the incumbent as well as the successor. In succession, the incumbent is the person at the top who is currently managing the family business and is expected to give up their position to the successor. And the successor is a family member or a professional who could take over the family firm and its management and day to day operations. So, we make a succession as a process between the incumbent and the successor. What is really involved in a succession? So, in succession involves the transfer actually as a set of events, process, some developments that happen in which the incumbent transfers both power and management operations, day to day operations to somebody called the successor, who could be either a family member or someone else. In which case, we are talking about succession as the decision making control and influence on the family firm. Yes, so there are two aspects to it. The incumbent transfers ownership control as well as management control. So, once he transfers both of this is when we can call it is a complete succession. And is succession typically an event or is it a more long drawn process? It is a very, very long drawn process actually can take decades. It started very early and for a long time people are groomed into the system and before actually there comes one day, that day is also very vague. It is not exactly one day in which there is an event and something gets done and the incumbent withdraws from the company and doesn't happen that way. So, so there could be a formal event to kind of celebrate the succession or mark the succession. However, there is an entire process that begins well in advance and typically this process is what strengthens this transition between one or more generations. And as a control and influence shifts from one person to the other, the business and the family are still stable and there is a flow, a continuing flow of how the decision making is happening. And therefore, the person both, in fact, we have to talk about both the sides of it, right? The incumbent as well as the successor. So, the successor side while we do talk about the grooming of the successor, the preparation of the successor. It is equally important that the incumbent also prepares himself for the transition. He needs to be able to give up control, he needs to be able to give up decision making authority and take a back seat without disturbing the business after the transition has taken place. So, as Prof. you mentioned two points here, give up ownership and give up management control. Yes. So, these are the two parts of the succession. It can happen that the incumbent has transferred one and not transferred the other in which case it is a partial transfer succession. So, for example, it could happen that I will give you day to day operations, management control, but I am still the owner. Yes. I still have ownership control, shareholding has not been transferred, but day to day control has been transferred. It could be the reverse also, where I have given you ownership, but I am still the boss and I will continue to be a boss for a long time, but I have given you ownership control. Typically, between ownership transfer and management transfer, is there a difference in the extent of power and authority that gets transitioned to the successor? When ownership transfer happens is when the real power gets transferred, management control will be given partially, slowly given to any successor over time, but the final say will still rest with an owner. Yes. In many cases, voting rights are left with the owner. So, the complete transfer will not happen until ownership gets transferred and that is usually the second of the transfers, not the first of the transfers. Okay. But when we talk about transferring control and influence and even ownership, it would seem like a taboo subject in a family that the father is giving up to the son. What happens is succession is deemed something that you are no longer capable of handling it or you are too old or you are past the stage where you can manage it and that's a negative topic. And that is why it is deemed as taboo. Not that somebody is capable now, somebody has come off age, they are in their prime and they can handle it better. They are well versed with new technologies. They are more aware of today's market. All that are positives, but that is not seen that way. It is a negative side saying you probably are very old, you are probably frail, you can't manage it. It is time to give up time to give up. So, those are the reasons why it's deemed as taboo. The succession as I understand it is not happening only in the business dimension. I also need to ensure that succession for it to be effective in a family business. It should also happen of course, other two circles as well. True, so the family dimension also has to see the family has to see that now this is the person who is really taking us forward. This is the future lies in this person's hands. He has taken over and support must be given to this person now to ensure that all three circles are doing well. And therefore, in many ways this person will now be seen as a de facto head of family. Even though there is a earlier generation than come and still around de facto head of family, somebody who is driving the business. So, there is a succession there also. Yes, and therefore, it is very important that it becomes a process rather than a single event. Because suddenly expecting that this network of relationships and exchanges of emotions in the family dimension, for it to transition to another person takes time and effort. And you need to build trust, you need to build cohesiveness, harmony and all of that with a different person. So, it is better that it is a process rather than an event.",
