      "transcript_text": "Can we discuss a little bit more about this difference between ownership transfer and management transfer? Maybe we can do it using an example. Let us take the case of the probe. As in Prem G, the incumbent, if you can call it, this happened a few years ago, but let us say, as an incumbent at that state, still holds far more shares at the time when Rashad Prem G became the chairman of the company. So, as in Prem G, we drew from day to day operations. Rashad Prem G is handling day to day operations. Shireholding is not yet moved. Also, as in Prem G transferred the bulk of a share to the foundation. And the rest of the shares at E holds, it is still, Shireth Prem G holds possibly 10% of what is Father holds today. So, that is the way, that is a transfer of management, not yet transfer of ownership. So, while in case, as in Prem G, wants to over turn a decision taken by Rashad Prem G in the management, they still possibility exists because he still has the voting power which is far more than Rashad Prem G. But day to day, Rashad Prem G does not have to seek approval from a gene Prem G for the day to day operations of the firm. And there are several other examples. For example, consider the Wal-Mart and this Walton family who owns majority stake in the Wal-Mart. After the Sam Walton era, the management passed on to the professionals. Whereas, the family did not very actively stay in the management position. So, there was a different transition that happened on the management dimension. But the ownership control still was retained with the family. So, there were two different directions of succession that happened. On one side, you had the professionals taking over the management. And on the other side, you had the family retaining the ownership control. We can have Indian examples as well as side. So, let us take the Tata group. Tata sense effectively has ownership control of all the Tata group companies. Yes, even today. Tata sense itself is owned by Tata Trust. But see, so Tata sons are where the family is. Yes. In none of the Tata group companies, the family is not there at all. Everything is handled by professionals. Yes. Whether it is TCA or Tata Motors or any of those companies is handled by professionals. And Tata sons is where the family is. So, that is where an example of ownership, shareholder is still with Tata sons. Yes. But the management is with professional professionals. And you also have an international example like Mars group. It is 100 percent privately held by the family. But if you look at it, the management transition has happened to professionals. As well as the family members who are actively staying as the family seabhose of different divisions within the Mars group. And the ownership, however, is still retained within the family itself. And the family has its own board of directors, the family council and that is a very different transition. And on the management dimension, it is again a very different transition. This is true. There are other foreign examples as well. BMW is another example where family has transferred the management control to a set of professionals. And they manage the everyday operations of it. The family still holds a significant share. I think close to excess of 40 percent share in the company in terms of ownership. And they have the voting rights. So, management will follow some of the guidelines set out by the owners. Yes. And by retaining day to day control. And close to India in 2014 when Harsh Marivala stepped down as the executive role from Mariko. The management transfer to the professionals. Shagatha Gupta took over as the CEO. And all the day to day operations and decision making control has moved to the professionals. Whereas at the same time, the Mariko family, the Marivala family continues to retain a majority stake in the company close to 60 percent. So, there is a different transition again. You see in both these dimensions. So, they don't have any money on the executive committee or the CXO level in the company itself. While they return ownership control. And they are able to influence the direction of the company. So, these two dimensions are very important for us to understand how transition and succession happens in a family business. Because when you look at the three circles that we discussed earlier, the ownership family and the business circle. We do not the role of both family members as well as professionals within the organization. And succession can include both the dimensions. So, you may see the family handing over the business dimension on a day to day basis to professionals while retaining ownership control. And family members involvement, you could have a policy about how they are going to be involved. They may be working alongside professional managers, including at the top management level. Or the family members could take a back seat while still being actively engaged with the business. And this entire succession along both these ownership as well as management together combined is what we are looking at when we talk about succession. And both the incumbent and the successor need to have clarity on how each of these transition are happening.",
