      "transcript_text": "In the entire process of succession, commitment plays a very important role, right? Commitment from the successor to this entire succession process. How does it shape or benefit the entire family business and this event or process of succession? If the successor shows commitment to join the family firm and take it to the next level, it really smoothes out the entire succession process, makes it easier on the incumbent to hand over because he sees that the business is now in safer hands. But how does it work out? The successor firm should have the desire to contribute, to show that eagerness to come into the family business and take it over, come with his own thoughts. Should be able to identify with the purpose that the business has, the kind of goals that the incumbent has set for the business or set new goals for the business himself and take it forward. And believe that this kind of purpose and goals are in sync with my own aspiration as to what where I want to take this business. So, if that kind of emotions are there for the business, that desire is there, plus if the person has the requisite skills for it, we call it effective. So, commitment, this is very, very important role that effective commitment can play for the succession process. And this is not just when you talk about purpose and goals and the direction of the business, we are not talking about this only in the context of the business side of it, right? Because for family business, the purpose and values that the family sees that is reflected in how the business is run. So, that kind of an alignment is extremely important. You need to be as a successor committed to how the business is run. How the family wants to run the business, not just how you want to run the business, right? You are only in winning as a custodian at that point of time. But there also been cases where like we have discussed in the primo genitral kind of a model, the eldest son comes and takes over. But is feels that he is duty bound to take over. He feels obligated that I have to take over, I do not have a choice. The family expects of this. That has always been the case in my family. The eldest son has always taken over. How can I break the norms and culture of my family and go separate. So, the eldest son feels an obligation to join the family. And this is kind of reinforced by everybody, right? Not just how the son sees it or the kids sees it. But it is also reinforced in every family function, every meeting with the suppliers, with the business relations. Everywhere it is continuously reinforced that typically in a primo genitral and even otherwise the kids are expected to get into the family business. Especially the eldest son. We call this nomadic commitment where you are expected to follow the norms out there and say that you have no choice but to join the family business. And the successor could also be very calculative about the entire process of succession. In the sense that the way the pros and cons, what is it in it for me? Do I really like this? Is this something that I want to do? What is my alternative that I could do? How much do I stand to gain or lose with this particular decision? And that kind of strengthens a calculative commitment. I have made my choices. I have understood the tradeoffs. I have bought into the tradeoff because I am convinced that overall I stand to gain. And there are sometimes cases where family members fully aware of their own skills, their own abilities and decides and has doubts. And whether he is capable enough to lead the family to the next level, the business to the next level. So at that point of time these doubts can set in. Yes. And the entrepreneurial skills that the person brings to the table may not be high and the person realizes that. And says that do I take a bet on going outside and starting something on my own or do I stick with the family business? At that point the family business appears like a safer bet. Yes. Because it is already established and it is pretty stable by now. Correct. So like we said he may not be so risk-taking. He might be risk-averse saying that fair enough I am not sure I will succeed if I step outside. Yes. This is something that is ready-made for me. I will definitely be welcomed. And things can be stable here for me if I join this place. Why go outside? It is like more like what we call an imperative commitment. So this is the only choice for me. This is only choice. So to some of there are four different types of commitments that we are looking at. Looking at effective commitment. We are looking at normative commitment. Calculative commitment as well as imperative commitment. And any combination of these could actually strengthen the buy-in that the successor has for the entire succession. Thank you.",
