      "transcript_text": "So, I will know that succession is very critical and failed succession can cause problems. Why is it so difficult? Why is what are the kind of challenges that make it so difficult? So, if you look at the entire succession process, there are broadly three elements to it, right? Define and also communicate the guidelines, the principles and mechanisms which will drive this entire process. The second is to plan and actually manage this entire process and the third is to coach, mentor, train the successor to ensure that the succession really becomes a successful process as an outcome at the end of it. And within these broad three dimensions which are also the parts of the entire process, you have various challenges that really pose significant barriers to making it a smooth process. For example, you know, think about the first one of the clones syndrome. The clones syndrome is a term that we used to refer when the incumbent wants that the successor is a literal clone of the incumbent. You want them to have the same competency, the same skill sets, the same way of doing things, the same values that they hold close. And that is very difficult to find even if the DNA has technically transferred, but it is very difficult to find a clone, right? And that can really make succession a very difficult process. But if you find a clone with the incumbent, let go, not necessarily. Even letting go syndrome, again, it is another syndrome that poses a significant challenge. The incumbent holds on to the firm very strongly. In many cases, especially in the founder generation, for them it is the first child or the major sibling among the all the kids. So they have a very strong identity and emotional investment that is interlinked with the business. And they also may not see a path for themselves after the transition has happened. So they want to very closely hold on. She is always doing that. Yes. Which also leads us to another very important part. You know, even if I do pass over, even if I do transfer, right, the hand over is happened, but there is a strong founders shadow that hangs over the organization. And the founder wants that it should be run as if the founder was there. And that founder shadow could even come from expectations of other stakeholders. You know, the employees may want it to work the same way as when the incumbent was there. Or the other stakeholders, the vendors, the suppliers, the clients, they may want to see the founder shadow, maybe even from a positive way, right? You want to see some kind of a business continuity. You want to see some kind of a system continuity. And therefore, the founder shadow is another. another challenge. How do you retain the benefits while also, you know, addressing the challenges of having this shadow lingering. So the incumbent needs to plan a few things to ensure that this goes right. Correct. Correct. And from the successes point of view, the successor may view the entire process of succession as well as taking over the reins of the family business as a kind of a cage. You know, you may feel that you are held within the family business cage. I want to pursue a lot of different paths for myself. a singer, maybe I want to become a movie star. And sometimes, yes, the families we have seen a lot of cases where the families, money is used to help the successor or the next generation meet their ambitions. You know, I will produce a movie or a song album. You know, one of that kind of things we've seen. But when those are not very easily enabled by the family, the successor potential successor may look at the family succession business. as a cage and field trapped. Yes. And that makes it very hard not only for the individuals concerned, but also for all the three systems that are concerned in this entire succession. There's a major challenge for the family businesses. As you know, there's things goes well. The incremental is to do some planning to ensure things go well. Right. He has to involve everyone else in the entire stakeholders in the succession process. And you also need to communicate to them. upfront. You need to send them the right signals. You need to talk to them. Let them know that succession is in process. And over a period of time, you are looking at the successor as going to be taking over. And that is very crucial because all the systems need to work together in order for a family business to try. The vendors and the clients in many cases for family firms are like extraordinary family. So you need to communicate them communicate to them at an appropriate time about all the relevant information. What is getting transferred? When does this get transferred? How is this going to impact the relationships going forward? And that is another challenge that needs to be addressed for a successful transition to happen. It's also transfer is not just the stakeholder relationship. There's a lot of emotion and a transfer involved. Yes. Yes. The founder has to letting go is also an emotional side to it as to because there's an identity issue also there. At the same time, the founder also needs to coach the and mentor the next gen into the role on the cultural side of it. You're talking both about the culture as well as the motivation gaps both at the incumbent and the success. So you can look at these two different points as being very closely linked. And these two challenges are a very significant challenge because of the interplay between all the three different systems. And this becomes extremely important because succession is an irreversible decision with very long term implications. For all the systems in one. But these are the key challenges in a succession process.",
