      "transcript_text": "There are 10 key tensions that need to be managed or eased to ensure transgenerational prosperity in family businesses and these become very important in the succession process. The first tension side is the tension between ability and willingness. The willingness is on one hand you can see the use of resources better for the family business. The ability is how good can they use it? How qualified they are, how competent they are to ensure that resources are invested well. That's the ability versus willingness tension that often crops up. The other tension that comes to mind is the family versus business. Business has a certain need, family has needs, business has a rational needs, family has a emotional needs and there is always a tension between these two. Relatedly is the tension between togetherness versus separateness within the family business. Because togetherness emphasis is a collectivism, shared vision, shared values and how are these influencing decision making. On the other side you have separateness because the business sometimes requires rational decision making and at that level you need to be able to separate the family role versus the business role that's been played. And the other very important tension is between tradition versus innovation. How close do you want to keep the family business with its origins, the past way of doing things versus how innovative do you want to be? What new things do you want to try? The new ways in which you want to do the business, the new business lines that you would want to enter. The other kind of tension side is between transparency and secrecy. How open or how informed can be make the rest of the family. In what the business is happening is a tendency among founders to keep a lot more secret and not share more information. While they have the right to inform as we spoke about this is a tension. How much to share how much is enough and why within the family system and outside the family system. The other tension is between equity and equality. In a family system generally the next every generation will expect equality in the way everything is whether it's true or it's assets, everything is it. But the business and a lot more things make sense when there is equity in a sense. What needs resources at that point of time should be given. It's more and what doesn't need what is going around can do with slightly lesser resources and can still go well is an important decision to be made. And equity of resources is also very important tension that will come up because equality is expected. Yes. And also how much control does the new leader or the successor retain with themselves or the income. How much control do they retain to what extent do decisions always pass through them versus how much autonomy do they give not just to the other family members within the business. But also to the non family members within the business. How does this particular balance be achieved within a family business is also very important. And also you know to what extent do does a successor or the incumbent want that the family business is deep rooted within the original values vision of the family and the founder generation. And what extent will the next generation especially and other family members as well as professionals within the organization to what extent can they you know kind of spread their wings and find you when used for growth. One tension that crops up is between what we call commitment versus competence. The next generation is by rule expected to be committed. Yes. And so the issue of high level of commitment also to the business. But what is also important is their competence and to lead the business and take it further. And this is a constant tension because the people with more commitment expect higher rewards while commitment is seen as good enough to take on roles in the business and take the company forward. And that's one tension that will come up. And probably the last tension is one between liquidity and growth. We may have seen it in the right to value as part of the rights of an owner. So, well how much money can we take out of the business that leaves business with less capital for growth. So that's the liquidity side of it. So if I prioritize growth then liquidity will be less and you have to live in a slightly lesser or slightly more frugal way. So these are the challenges that will come between these two tensions. And these 10 tensions are very key in ensuring that the business survives and prosperous across generations.",
