      "transcript_text": "Let us move on to discuss the incumbent's point of view in passing on the batter. For the incumbent, this is a very emotional stage. He is built the business from scratch. He is kind of emotionally, financially, psychologically, passing on the batter is not something that easily comes to them. So, the entire identity of the person and the company are very closely linked to them. Very emotional attached to the company. That is why it is so difficult to detach himself. Many times, even the social status is because of your role as the leader of that particular organization. All your networks are built around that. And your power and respect is coming from that. position that you hold. It makes it very difficult to leave it. Also, the incumbent starts thinking fair enough. If I leave it, what do I do? This is all I have done all my life. I have built this from scratch. What do I do with my time? Yes. So, it is very difficult for to let go. They realize that they have to let go. Sometimes the market has changed, the successor is probably better suited for today's economy. You need more office, which is more tech savvy. There are many things that have changed over time. It could be a health condition. It could be a health condition. So, but still, it is not easy to let go. It is very emotionally challenging more than the state of the business itself. Even if it is very good for him to let go, it is an emotional challenge for the incumbent. And it is also not uncommon to find many of the family businesses, where the transition has happened. The succession event is our process is done. But you still see that there is an office space for the incumbent after their exit also. The founders office to keep them engaged in some way. The founder does come. Usually comes sits in the office for two hours a day. Maybe it discusses something on the charity or charitable side of the foundation side sometimes. Even if the successor has come, the old employees will remain. They will not go there. Loyalty to the companies, like you said, always very important out there. So, you meet with them and does may not take part in daily decisions. But yes, the corner office remains. And the types of incumbents exit. There are different types of how we can characterize the incumbent based on their desire intent and the circumstances in which they exit. And the most difficult one is the one kind of incumbent who refuses to leave. He says that I am going to be around as long as I can do my work here. I am going to be around and he will take the final call till till the very end. Well, make it difficult for the successors. One or more successors, because he will continue to remain at the helm. As long as he is around and comes to office, none of the employees will directly go to the successors. He will quite overrule the successors sometimes in front of the employees also. It is a very difficult customer to hand direct. I think he is like the emperor. He has the last word. So, he is that kind. These people are typically either forced out or an excellent event like death or the case, most likely. Health, we an issue where when you say forced out is at some point the successors on account of the incumbent's health can force this changes. But otherwise it is only death that can rule out some. And then typically when this kind of incumbent you see, the organization is the one who is really suffers. The successor is not groomed. You are not planned for the transition to happen. You are leaving the firm weaker by not planning out your exit. Very true. The successors in actually in a quandary. He knows that he is capable. He has the knowledge. Can't say the final call. And the employees also sense the situation the successor is in. While he can't decide, yet he can't decide what he says is right, but he can't take it. So, it leaves everyone weaker. Yes, right. The decision takes longer because the successor may not have the final say. And it still has to go back to the earlier generation who may not be always available. So, well, the firm suffers. Another kind of an incumbent is what can be characterized as the general. With incumbent has existed but it is very closely observing what his successor is doing. He is kind of waiting for that first failure when I can immediately jump and come back. And he is already plotting it. His still has deep connect since the organization. He actively maintains them. He knows what levers to pull and is set them all there. Ready to come back right when the first failure. He kind of maybe even hopes that the successor will fail and enable him to come back. I think I would say sometimes the engineer the failure also remotely. They will like you said, they keep in touch with all the people and they will plot as to and literally sometimes engineers to how he can. For a small mistake, he will not sell endangered the organization. But he will use that small excuse to come and plunk himself back in the hot seat and say, okay, now I will take over. For example, it happens in business that some customer might not renew your order. And this happens in business renew your order because they found somebody at a lower price. And that is enough good enough. You may be using all the relations. That is why the business has lost. And he will plunk himself back once small excuse like that. And this leads to a lot of operational chaos. Businesses again weakened and more importantly the new leader is undermined. And if it is a professional still the professional may look outside the organization. But if the successor was a family member and your entire plotting, conniving and coming back, you have basically undermined your successor. So now even the respect towards the family itself would come down. What also such a person does is when they plunk themselves back in the office, one of the first things they do is to overturn the last few decisions taken by the successor. It further undermines the successor and the employees know that, well, is this going to be a permanent feature. Next time he steps out, they will be sure that he is going to come back for sure and overturn. Should we do not do uncertainty, weakened organization again? So what kind of person would be called somebody who can strengthen the organization? Think about somebody who plans their exit will in advance. Sir has a post retirement idea and he plans his exit. Think of you could characterize this person as an ambassador. A post retirement he could serve as a mentor or as a kind of an advisor without trying to take control of the organization. And therefore you have anticipated that I need to make this exit. It is very beneficial for the organization. You have kind of groom the leader, read the leader well in advance and the organization remains healthy. It actually is most stable and stronger possibly at the end of it because it is benefiting not just from the successor but because you continue to engage as a mentor, as a consultant, as an advisor to your successor. The organization is benefiting from both the wisdom of the incumbent as well as the energy enthusiasm of the successor. There is another type called the governor. The difference between the ambassador and the governor is possibly the while in the case of an ambassador, they continue as a mentor after they have formally stepped out for some period of time. The governor possibly does that even while he is in office in the last few months and has a clean break at the end of it. And says that from now on I am not part of this office, I am not coming here to take decisions etc. And pursue something else completely. Take up roles elsewhere for the role of the organization. Some NGOs, some schools, some trust. So the governor probably has done some amount of mentoring but possibly done during the last few months of his tenure in the office and then takes a clean break. These two are good examples of how successor can take over from the incumbent, how the incumbent can hand over to us.",
