          "model_answer": "The three-circle framework conceptualizes a family business as comprising the ownership circle (those who own the equity), the management circle (those who run the operations), and the family circle (the family members involved in or affected by the business). For succession to be successful, the incumbent must assess requirements and gaps across all three circles simultaneously because these dimensions are interconnected.\n\nIf the incumbent only prepares the management circle but ignores the family circle, sibling rivalry or family conflict may undermine the successor's authority. If ownership is transferred but management gaps remain, the business may suffer from operational incompetence. The lecture emphasizes that the incumbent must prepare themselves and the business emotionally, financially, and on governance aspects across all three circles before initiating the exit process. Assessing gaps in only one or two circles creates an incomplete succession plan that is likely to fail due to the unaddressed friction in the neglected dimension.\n\nThe coordinated assessment ensures that the choice of successor (management dimension) aligns with the ownership structure (full transfer vs. co-ownership) and the family's readiness to support the transition, leading to a smooth and coordinated exit across all circles.\n\nRubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding of the three circles: 3 marks\n- Explanation of why simultaneous assessment is necessary (interdependence of circles): 4 marks\n- Concise and clear articulation of the model: 3 marks",
