          "model_answer": "The Parachute path and the Bottom-Up Climb path represent opposite ends of the succession spectrum regarding time and knowledge acquisition.\n\n**Time to Legitimacy**: The Parachute path provides immediate clarity of structure, as everyone knows who the new boss is from day one. However, legitimacy is absent at the start and must be earned through decision-making and test-clearing. In contrast, the Bottom-Up Climb takes a 'whole longer duration' for the process to work out. Legitimacy is gradually built through the acquisition of in-depth operational knowledge and the respect of peers.\n\n**Type of Knowledge**: The Parachute entrant lacks legitimacy and must rely on the strength of the board and advisors to mitigate knowledge gaps. The Bottom-Up Climber gains 'in depth knowledge about how the operations work, how the relationship works' by learning from the ground up. This approach is specifically effective for businesses with 'a lot of tacit knowledge.'\n\n**Example**: The lecture cites the Comcast example where the summer job supervisor made clear that Brian Roberts would be 'judged on merit,' illustrating the bottom-up path. Conversely, the Kumar-Munglambirla example fits the parachute path, where Kumar came in when there was a death in the family, and 'somebody had to assume the mantle.'",
