      "transcript_text": "With a, what are the different responses that we see in family firms to the ascension or the succession process? So, the first kind of response I would say is where the successor is protected and guided and mentored. This is likely to come from someone who is very close to the income and more likely the loyal veteran who has been long in the company, very close allies of the income and some elders in the family. Maybe siblings but still lesser possibility of siblings are more likely closer confidence of the income. What they may actually offer guidelines, some kind of guidance, they can support you where required, give you more chances to succeed. So, these kind of things will happen. What will help in terms of learning, it will be faster for the successor because he is allowed to make some mistakes, there will be some quick learning. The downside is if there is over protection then your legitimacy becomes question saying is always protected for his mistakes. So, the successor also has to use these with caution when to use the mentor when to use the air cover that is being provided and then sometimes stretch himself and let responses flow back. Also, we see other stakeholders. The younger peers from within the family or younger managers or outside directors, professionals within the firm who enable change as their response to the ascension process. And since they are looking at enabling change, what the advantages you can renew, you have a mandate to renew, you can work on strengthening the core business accordingly. And a good approach for the ascender at this support system is to kind of figure out what exactly they are going to try to change, pilot it, then slowly prove and build quick wins and then slowly gradually scale up that change and also equally importantly is to communicate what will not change within the organization. The third kind of responses where there are people who will wait and wait for you to stumble, wait for you to fail and then sometimes be happy about it. So, these are possible rivals for you for the role of the successor. This could be cousins, could be some managers who thought that a professional can take over and not necessarily go back to the family itself. Some non family members, maybe some investor who said, I will have more control now only to find a family member coming in. So, how they will behave is like they will behave as though they are complying with what is required. They are doing exactly what you will do, but they will fall short of something, blame it on variables, blame it on outside circumstances and therefore, hoping that it will lead to a failure. The good side is that if you still manage, the successor still manages to win, create that small early wins, then suddenly you will find many of them coming back to your side. I mean they all want to finally support a winner. So, they will come back to your side and back you to the hilt. But if you fail it will for them will say yeah we told you so kind of thing that this is not a person who should succeed. But the successor from his part should strive for that early wins, stay communique in good communication with everyone, build relationships and therefore, be ready for the next step. You also have stakeholders like relatives who see no future for themselves within the family firm or high capable, high potential, non family professionals within the firm or even the incumbents confidence. Because they do not see a role for themselves after the succession or the ascension is happened, they may even choose to exit the firm. Here what the ascender can do is try to retain by leveraging the organizational design and you provide a role clarity you provide incentives maybe ownership access. But even after all of that if some of the stakeholders do decide to leave then you try to capture whatever knowledge was there. So, that the organization is not left poorer with that particular exit and very importantly treat that exit with dignity because you do not want to harm the system. Yeah while what we said these four are the main ways in which stakeholders will react. There are some other ways which are possible to, one way is possibly to actively rebel or resist the entire move, form collisions and actually open disagreements out some in a slightly other company it could be in a boardroom battle kind of thing. Like what is happening to some extent when Tata-sans want to go public is actually a boardroom battle happening out there. Yeah but from the success point of view best to address the issue not take it personal address the problem build alliances and move forward. Or sometimes there can be people who are hedging and not sure. So, they have one open line with incumbent while also appearing to support the successor and the only way the successor can do is clarify the role of each person and reward where it is due. Also stakeholders can you know kind of show a difference kind of response in which their behavior is to directly go to the incumbent and here the successor should take this step to actively seek out and have this conversation with the incumbent to define the role as to what will flow and how it will flow. Or they could also be a probing kind of a response from stakeholders where you are pushing small deliberate challenges and they are looking at how you respond to these challenges that are facing. Here is where the for the agenda is very important to be very consistent with the response and very importantly also not to overreact. Yes. So, the for major responses from stakeholders can of course be visualized along the spectrum. And another very important way that we can visualize is also to kind of look at a stakeholder heat map who are the important stakeholders. And from these four major responses that we are likely to see what would be their likely response. And we should look for who what is more likely to happen and plan accordingly. The likely ones measured in the chat are more important. The ones that are rare are events that may not occur and the successor did not focus on them as much as what is highlighted clearly as likely responses of the stakeholders.",
