      "transcript_text": "High Students in module 4 will see how Family Dynamics plays out in action in a family business. We will see this in 3 parts. In part 1, we will see how is it to be working together in a family business. What it means is, how our relationships, how our roles, the various branches, our employment policy, what is family's signs and what are the various styles of working together. We will also see how is it for women in family businesses. In terms of how is that it has already been in the past, their invisibility, how is it for spouses or daughters or daughter-in-laws, how is the legal frameworks surrounding women and how is it for working couples. And in part 3 of this, we will see how is it for conflict management in family firms. We will understand how communication is important, what is, where are negotiations that are coming to play here and what are the various sources of conflict that can come up and how they can be addressed. In working together in a family business, one of the first things to look at is family relationships at work. Now, relationships are very hard, they are very, very complex. As you can see in this graph, relationships also compounds in the sense as a way of number of generations past, as the more number of siblings enter into the business and cousin enter into the business. If you see the number of relationships that happen, one to many, many to one, the numbers really compound. And these relationships are essential, very critical in a family business. And the pairs can be different, right? Some can be sibling pairs, some can be co-owners, some can be board members, but each relationship is critical, each relationship is valid. relationships can be broadly classified in three of these areas that you always talked about in the three circle diagram, family, business and ownership. Each one trades in different kind of a currency, right? So family trades in emotion, business trades in competence, skill, ownership trades in power. Logic is very different for each of those, for family businesses or the family or the business and ownership. And the questions asked also very different. how somebody enters or how somebody exits is also equally different in all of these. Questions asked are in a family business, they are asked do we belong in business? Can you do the job? Can you get it done? And in ownership, who designs? See, every big decision, the family expresses emotion, the business expresses logic and the ownership is of all about power. Actually, you have one firm and two people, edges, two rough edges I would call it sometimes. It can be a strength or a liability. The shared history can be a great strength if it's no, it develops deep trust. But it's a liability if there are old, old grievances which keep running around. Similar for emotional bonds or a shared identity, long, the business always wants a long horizon. Patient capital is a big strength when you really look at long horizon. But if you look at a long horizon, there's a reluctance to change or to exit it can become a liability. Likewise, each of these features you're talking of, each of them bring a certain strength to the family and to the business. On one hand, they can also be a liability in a different way. If you see what research always says about relationships and performance, there are multiple researchers that have been carried out in this area. How harmony norms and fairness curves, what we call as a free-to-effect. There's a research that talks about how more sons actually lower performance after the founder's death. So how do you then address it? You have equal of them, equal hires, need some kind of a governance around it. And how in some cases, if a relationship conflict lowers performance of the invest in goodwill. So there is plenty of research. There are actions to how this can be done. In some relationships are very, very important in a family business. you",
