      "transcript_text": "An important thing to note about working in family businesses is that there are going to be a lot of overlaps between the rules that the family members are going to play in the firm because they wear different hats as an owner, as a manager, as well as a family member. And this gets even more complicated when you take into consideration the different branches within a family. For example, if you look at the family access stage and the family firms stages of development, think about when it is a young business family. An important relationship question to us is who counts as family in the firm at this stage. When the next generation is entering the business, it is important to us, is there a fair employment policy. In working together, it is very important to figure out how to siblings in laws and outsiders cooperate. And while passing the baton, it is very important to understand how does anyone exit with dignity. We have already studied the three circle model, the seven roles that happen within a family firm. And working in a family firm, typically, you will notice that several of these roles could possibly collide based on where you sit. And when you wear different hats, it is helpful to visualize it as a four room model with a place for each hat. And when you think about it from an entrepreneurial family-galaxy perspective, the more entities there are within the family-galaxy, the more the number of relationships. Think about a hypothetical family. 21 is the first generation. G2 is the second generation and G3 is the third generation. And you will notice that based on the generation 2, it also leads to three different branches for the same family. Why does this matter? And what are its implications? Think about when the ownership is decided by the branch level. How would it work? The shares are split by the branch first and then among the branches heirs. An important thing to note is that the branch size will definitely drive control. Consider where the seats are split equally between the branches. Each branch then nominates the same number of council or board members. But small branches could get overrepresented. What if I give proportional seats to each branch? And the seats follow the ownership structure. Then the possibility that large branches will dominate and smaller ones could get disengaged. What if I have a branch holding company such that each branch holds its block and woods through one vehicle? This adds an extra layer and politics start moving upstairs. What if you start separating by branch? Where the assets or the businesses are divided among branches? You could lose scale or it might also need to have non-compete and brand rules about how this split works. Why does it matter about where you stand? The implications of where you sit within the family business structure and the complex relationships and role interplay is very important because your stance depends on where you sit. Consider a decision like reducing the dividend to fund a new plant. Think about somebody who is playing the role of a family owner manager. A likely stance is to support the decision because they see growth, legacy and control of the firm. A non-working family owner may hesitate or oppose because they might be dependent on the dividends for income. A non-family manager is likely to support or quietly support this decision because it would bring growth which brings jobs, career promotions and budgets for the firm. A spouse of an owner is very likely to be worried because they are considering the household's cash flow and the children's future. Therefore, it is very important in family firms to avoid an attribution error. Rather than saying that he or she is stubbornly try to understand which circle is the person sitting in and from that perspective try to understand their response to a particular decision. Think about a brother and a sister Krishna and Mehta. When you look at this simple relationship in the generation two, you will note that there are several different roles that play out between these two siblings. As a brother, as a fellow board member, as a chairperson of the board over the husband of the sister who could possibly be the CEO. A trustee, a beneficiary, a fellow foundation director and so on, multiple roles start coming in. One decision can have many, many repuls within a family firm. Firing a brother-in-law may end up touching multiple roles that the brother-in-law is playing. Typically in a family firm, it helps to visualize four important rooms. One is the owner room. Here, power and influence are very important. The owner room elects the board and sets pool may own the firm. Next is the board room where you are looking for wisdom and judgment. The board room is the one which hires the CEO as well as approves the strategy. The management room is where competence and execution are very important because they run the business on a day-to-day basis. And the fourth very important room is the family room. Where love, harmony, unity, development are all important. Who sits in the family room? It includes the descendant, this pouses, the next generation. The family room could have a council of four to seven members elected by the family. It could also opt to have a family assembly, a family gathering every year or two. It could also have its own mandate which prioritizes unity, education, family policies and development of the family members. While this room runs alongside the other rooms, they do not report to the family and this is very important to note. Look at a running example where one family and multiple members can wear different hats either in the core firm or in other ventures within the family galaxy. And the more the number of entities that are there within the entrepreneurial family galaxy, this becomes more and more complex. You simultaneously wear a manager's hat, you wear the hat of an owner where your fiduciary responsibilities both to the firm as well as to the family are very important. And you also wear the hat of being a family leader.",
