      "transcript_text": "While working in a family firm, the principles of fairness and justice are very important. What does fair even mean? And why is it judged twice in a family business? Broadly put, there are four kinds of fairness. Distributive, procedural, Interactional and Retributive. Distributive fairness asks about are the outcomes fair in terms of pay, dividends, shares, jobs and titles. Procedial fairness, the question is about the process. It is about voice, clear rules and whether the same rules apply for all. Interactional fairness is about was I treated with respect. It is about the tone, it is about an honest, experienced explanation. It is about dignity. And Retributive fairness is to ask whether the consequences are fair and the same discipline applies for all family members and for family and non-family members. In a family firm, each judgment is made twice by kids and kin and by non-relations. And therefore, it is important to watch out for this. How does distributive fairness work? Is it about equality, equity or need? Consider the shares split. We are talking about equality would mean equally distributing between three different children. Whereas, equity takes into different consideration and therefore, the split may not work with equality. And when you consider the need of each child, given that child sees families in greater need, you might want to give more to child see. Equality talks about the same for all. It is very useful to build unity but it ignores effort. Equity is based on contribution. It rewards efforts but it may split the family. Need is based on the requirement. It is very kind but can reward dependency. And therefore, it is important to understand which rule applies to which resource before the conflict. Procedural fairness has six different rules. First is consistency. Second is about the lack of bias. Third is about accuracy based on good information. Fourth is correctibility or a way to appeal the decision. Fifth is about representation and voice. And sixth is about the ethics and the process whether it fits with the shared values. Interactional fairness has four important parts to it. First is truthfulness. Second is being respectful. Third is to ensure that there are no insults or hints about other aspects which do not find a place in that particular situation. And therefore, to be proper. And the fourth is to justify and give the reasons. An important family test is to ask would you say it in this way to a non family manager. And then you would be able to figure out whether you are interacting in the role as a family member or as a manager. A good practice is to publish the process before the decision and then deliver the decision with reasons. A fair process in a family business has broadly three different steps. First is to engage. Then explain and set the expectations. These are three important principles of a fair process which need to be applied in five domains of the family firm. First is the ownership domain. Who may own or how shares will be passed on. Second is about the family membership. Many families have different rules about what allows you to count yourself as a member of the family which includes and extends not only to the family of procreation or family of orientation but also to the in loss who are co opted into the family business. Third is the recruitment and careers. How does entry pay promotion and other aspects of the career are impacted with fairness. Fourth is the management transition domain. Who will lead next and how will the decision be taken. And the fifth important domain is family participation. Does the family meet. Does the family meet in a manner that they separate the conflict from the issue. Will everybody find a fair chance to express their voice and his information appropriately and relevantly and adequate intervals shared with everybody. It is important to check for every domain whether people were engaged. Told why and told what happens next. What happens when fairness fails. Imagine if the harmony norms are disturbed or if distribution fairness is not achieved and if role ambiguity is greater. All of this will lead to a relationship conflict. The mediator needs to focus on fairness and clarity which can act as a family impediment through this entire process. If that doesn't happen it leads to a freedom effect. A freedom effect refers to the analogy from the godfather movie and the book where the middle child freedom. You are talking about incompetence, opportunism or where it dubious ethics of a relative which harms the firm. A lever here is to have clear roles visible fairness before the conflict starts.",
