      "transcript_text": "When you are working in a family business, one important thing to understand is what we call a related party transaction. This happens when money moves within the family and the firm and there can be multiple firms and multiple branches of the families as well. How do you understand this? What are related parties? What are the party transactions? What can be the red flags and looks like? Many times when it comes to loans for example, family members have lend loans to the business or the business has lend loan to the family without any guarantee. There is no interest paid, sometimes there is no repayment date, these are all what normally happens in a family business. Because of the interplay between the business and the family. Sometimes jobs are paid much about what is the fair market value as we can see. There is no responsibilities which are linked to the kind of pay. Nothing of this is legally wrong. In law there is an entire section and income tax act on what is related party transactions. But as long as these are transactions conducted at an arms length which are disclosed which are independently verified. You might find sometimes two businesses of the family, two completely business, different business, one is selling to the other, this one is selling back to the other, some of the product. valuation increases if they are listed sometimes. So these are all to be disclosed properly and as long as they are independently approved there is no problem. The other thing that you have to understand is what is tunneling and propping. In tunneling there is a value taken out of the business in terms of like we say business being given to some other party which is not exactly at arms length and value shown differently. So minorities shareholders are the ones which are more affected by either a tunneling or a problem. So dropping is opposite, dropping happens when one firm is in need of funds let us say and they are a whole host of other family businesses which put funds into this at very different valuations at very different risks. So in both tunneling and propping minority shareholders are the ones who are at risk most. There are statutory guardrails in Indian businesses for Indian businesses on family money and family pay. Like I said there are sections both the ones who are in the business. Under the income tax both under the under the CB rules as well as to how related party transactions can be governed. So that smaller shareholders minority shareholders and if it is a listed company even the larger shareholders are not short changed in the entire process. For example let us take this 188 plus rule 15 where relative appointment to an office of profit above a certain pay should need shareholder agreement. Otherwise you could appoint anybody and if you do not have any kind of an approval there is no oversight on anything and finally could affect business performance as well and therefore shareholder value. So there are steps for related party governance. We will see these five steps out here first is to define who is related party this can include not just relatives can be trust also can be the firms of in-laws also. So there are some if I remember right from 27 numbers of who are defined in law as relative. Similarly disclose inform as to what is happening. So these are the related parties to which transactions are happening and it is recorded approved formally and the transaction happens at an arms which is the same price that would give to an independent third party is what they are giving to your related party also and these are monitored. What are important also account is the loan should relatives pay to relatives and lastly which usually happens in most family firms company assets are pretty much used as personal assets. So these are all something that need to be safeguarded properly.",
