      "transcript_text": "Family Science Anxiety travels through triangles and down generations. Second is a circumplexed lens. Here, cohesion, flexibility and communication decide how well a family copes with change. And third is to understand how generations behave. Lineage and age cohorts are different things and both shape behaviour. Why does Family Science matter? can have a perfect governance chart and still fail because of how the family behaves as a system? Think about the family systems lens. In the family systems lens, there are five important concepts. First is differentiation, which talks about thinking apart from the group's emotions. In the family firm, what it means is about your ability to disagree with a parent without obeying or cutting off. Second are the triangles, where a third party absorbs tension. For example, a non-family CEO is drawn into a father's son fight. A third concept is projection, where parents transfer their anxiety to a child and a worried parent can end up over protecting an heir. Fourth concept is transmission, where patterns pass across generations. like few words and favouritism repeat and tend to carry across generations. In the last is cut off, where distance replaces resolution. A branch stops talking and sells out. The aim in the family systems is to stay connected without being controlled. Next is a circumplex lens, where cohesion and flexibility are very important. What is cohesion? Look at this particular table, where you are talking about four different types of cohesion. You could be disengaged, you could be separated, you could be connected or enmeshed. And on the other axis, you are talking about being rigid or how structured are you, how flexible is the family or how chaotic is the family. From a firm's perspective, the firm could be for example enmeshed. And rigid, where you see a lot of founder control. When there are drifting branches, the firm could be very disengaged and chaotic. Communication is the facilitating dimension. It lets a family move towards a balanced center. And also built the model for households. And this can also help us to understand how the family firm can strengthen its body. behaviour. Next is the generations. Within generations, one aspect is the birth order. Is there evidence about this? A popular claim, for example, talks about the eldest sibling being a very responsible and a conventional person. Whereas a middle child is expected to be very diplomatic peacemaker and the youngest sibling is very liberal and a risk-taker. What does the empirical evidence from research tell us? Large samples research find no lasting effect on personality. And there is a very small effect on self-rated intellect. But where does birth order bite? Birth order strikes in terms of the rules, for example, primogeniture succession. The expectations that are said. For example, the eldest child will carry on the business. Or favouritism. But remember that these are scripts and not traits. How do birth order scripts play out in the family firm? Think about four different positions of this child. One being the eldest child, second the middle child, the youngest child and an only child. A common script for the eldest child is that they will carry on the business. A typical hat is that of an owner manager and position in the core firm. Risk over here is resentment from the other siblings or pressure on the eldest sibling. A common script for the middle child is to keep the peace. The typical hat that the middle child is expected to wear is that of an owner or to chair or counsel within the family, assembly and the family counsel. The risk of the middle child is to be overlooked and they may quit or disengage with the family firm. A common script for the youngest child is to be a very protected and at the same time also get a lot of freedom to spread their wings. While they wear the hat of an owner, they may explore their freedom through new ventures or engagement in the foundation. The risk is that the youngest child, if given a lot of freedom, may not take the business seriously. An only child, a common script is to be the sole heir and therefore will all the three hats very early on. The risk is that there are no peers to evaluate them with and the child may feel isolated. How do you fix these common scripts? A good fix is to have a return merit-based path which can be applied irrespective of the birth order of the child and that the common script or the stereotype must not decide the role. Competence is more important. Another very important aspect is about the paternal line and the maternal line. Typically, the descent rules for example property, name, succession often follow one line, dominantly the paternal line. However, the law is changing and daughters have equal rights by birth in the Hindu law. Another dimension is the branch identity. Cozins may see themselves as the father or the mother's line. And in laws, add a new dimension or a second. can family to this entire equation. Many times, the mother acts as the mediator. For example, in the widely reported a mani family split, the mother cochlebene a mani brokered the 2005 split.",
