      "transcript_text": "Let us consider more detailed implications of generations and sibling dynamics. What does generation actually mean? And what makes siblings compete or cooperate as adults? There are multiple different ways. There are four different ways. A lot of these are the same ways of looking at generations and one family across these meanings. First, is to look at it as the descent or the rank in the line. First generation, second generation and so on. Second is to look at the cohort. Where you born in the same time period. Third is to look at the life stage. Are you in the same age phase as your peers? Which was shaped by the same events. Look at this particular example, where the founder is born in 1935. The age cohort is what is termed as a silent generation. The eldest child A is born in 1958 and the cohort is as a label of a boomers. The youngest child B is born in 1975 in a cohort that is the Gen X. Here, the founder is the first generation by descent and A and D are the second generation by descent. However, A and D belong to two different cohorts of generations. One is a boomer and the other is a Gen X. Consider the eldest child of A. E. E is born in 1981. The age gap between D and E is about 6 years. And very likely both of them belong to same Gen X or maybe just overlapping with the millenials generation. So, you see that A and D are in the same generation by descent. Although they are 17 years apart but belong to different cohorts. D and E are uncle and nephew 6 years apart but adjacent cohorts. And these sort of dynamics are very typical in large families and in multiple families especially as the number of branches and multiple generations over years. Therefore, the family business has to be designed for both, the rank by descent as well as affinity by cohort. What are the generational implications for succession and governance? Succession. In this example, uncle and nephew may compete and skipping a generation may fit. From a governance perspective, they represent different branches and age bands and not the generation alone. From a communication perspective, it is important to agree upon the channels and pace for this mixed age meetings where both D and E are very close in age and relatively also the values that they may have grown up with. Because this is where you are testing cohort assumptions within your own family. So, the same generation in a family business is not necessarily the same as the peer group or vice versa. And these implications are very important to understand. While working with siblings, it is not unlikely to end up with rivalry. So, what drives the sibling rivalry? Where two siblings or more are competing with each other. They could stem from childhood roles that never changed or unequal effort or pay between siblings within the family business. unclear authority or even perceived favoritism by a parent. What cools or reduces this rivalry? One, having fair processes and transparent pay and compensation systems. Second is to separate the spheres of responsibility and clear roles. Third is to have a purpose that is larger than any one sibling at the family and the family business level. And having an outsider, advisor or a board can play a very strong role in managing or navigating the sibling rivalry. The sibling rivalry is structural and therefore, the best approach is to work with structure in order to navigate this rivalry and use it for the firm's benefit. In working with siblings, let us revisit fairness. If the rules can be equality, equity or need. In equality, the sibling example is about having equal shares for all. But the risk is to ignore the unequal effort by a particular sibling. Having equity based split will follow the same rules and results for the pay and compensation for all the siblings. But it can split active and passive siblings. If it is need based fairness, there is support for a sibling who might be in a particular financial difficulty. However, it can breed dependency. A common pattern that is observed is that ownership typically follows equality, pay follows equity and hardships, especially when a family member is in difficulty or a branch is in difficulty is addressed through need based fairness. Why are a family fund? What is the role of the parent in resolving sibling conflict? The parent can choose to play different roles as a referee where the parent decides each dispute. Here you are keeping the parent and the central role and the siblings are in the lobby and the parent is the one who is deciding how it will play out. Second is that the parent could take on the role of being a mediator and help the siblings agree. This works only while the parent lives and is respected. Third role that the parent could adopt is to play the role of an architect. The parent anticipates and sets the rules, the forums and exit options well in advance. This kind of a structure typically outlashes the parent and is also better in comparison to the referee role and the mediator role. A fragility test about the role and its effectiveness is if the piece depends on one person being in the room, it is not yet good governance because it is person dependent. So, how can siblings work together? Think about it as a ladder. First, you try to prevent something. There is a shared understanding and better still to have it in writing. Next, is to talk about it in family meetings, in family councils and the family also needs to enable these spaces to have these conversations. And these conversations are typically very difficult. Many times they are run of the milk kind of a conversation but sometimes they can become very difficult but it is important to talk. Third step is to decide. Have a board with a delegated authority which can make the decision. Fourth is to resolve. Mediate before anything becomes formal steps to solve this conflict. For example, bringing in the lawyers. On the last is to exit. Have an exit mechanism. An earlier agreed upon mechanism to sell my stake in the family business and exit. A trap here is the unanimity trap. The unanimity trap could require every sibling to agree on everything and therefore everybody has a veto. How do you resolve this? You need to have mechanics for reserved matters and delegation and this is very important governance mechanism.",
