          "model_answer": "Model Answer (10 marks):\n\n1. **Governance design for dual generational lenses (3 marks):** Recognize that generation operates on three dimensions (descent, cohort, life stage). Governance structures must account for both rank by descent AND affinity by cohort. The example shows A and D are second generation by descent but belong to different cohorts (boomer vs. Gen X), so decisions cannot be based solely on generation by descent.\n\n2. **Communication channel design (3 marks):** Establish agreed-upon channels and pace for mixed-age meetings. Since D and E (uncle and nephew, 6 years apart) are in adjacent cohorts and share values grown up with, the family must test cohort assumptions explicitly. Communication structures should facilitate dialogue across cohort boundaries, not assume shared values based solely on descent rank.\n\n3. **Succession planning that avoids generation-based shortcuts (2 marks):** Avoid assuming that skipping a generation fits simply based on descent rank. Succession should consider that uncle and nephew may compete represent different age bands and cohorts. Governance mechanisms like reserved matters and delegation (rather than unanimity) should be implemented to manage competing interests across generational divides.\n\n4. **Parent role as architect (2 marks):** Design structures in advance rather than relying on the referee role. The architect role anticipates rules, forums, and exit options well before conflicts arise, creating governance that outlasts the parent rather than depending on one person being in the room.\n\nKey structural takeaway: Family business design must integrate both descent-based rank and cohort-based affinity, with explicit mechanisms for communication, succession, and governance that are not person-dependent.",
