      "transcript_text": "One part of working in a family firm is understanding how to deal with siblings, but it is equally if not more important to understand how to work together across family branches. Who holds authority in a sibling or cousin team, and how do families keep branches from drifting apart? Think about five different styles of sibling teams. Let's start with a simple matrix. On when access, let us keep whether the goals are divergent or whether the goals are aligned. And on the other side, let us look at the ownership, whether it is concentrated or shared. When the goals are divergent, but ownership is concentrated, you will end up with a dominant leader and one person decides and the other branches need to follow. When the ownership is concentrated, but goals are aligned, you might end up seeing first among equals and a leadership with team consent. When there is shared ownership, but goals are divergent, the divergent interests are more likely to play out. Here, equal power and different aims coexist. How do you manage and navigate this tension? When goals are aligned and equity ownership is shared, here, you are looking at complete equals where there is equal power and shared aims. Drawing from this matrix, you see this different styles of a dominant leader and the others. You could also have a dysfunctional style. So, let us look at what is required to make it work. So, let us start with the one that is outside this quadrant, a dysfunctional style. Here, you need outside help and then restructure or if required separate so that both the family and the business are stronger. Second is the divergent interests. Here, you want to agree upon a shared purpose or if you cannot agree upon a shared purpose, you may consider splitting it into separate businesses. When there is a dominant leader, having an independent board with a term and a succession plan are very useful and very helpful to make it work. In the style where you have first among equals, here, having a clear mandate, a review cycle and real consultation go a long way in ensuring that the system works stronger and better. When there is a complete equals kind of a setup, goals are aligned and equity ownership is shared, having clear decision rules, delegated authority and very important knowing what the tie breaker is going to be or who that tie breaker is going to be is very important. Sometimes you may even consider pruning their family tree. Most trees do better when you timely prune them. So, when and what should you consider in pruning a family tree? The ownership level. Here, you need to consider things about the buyout or consolidation of branches. You may want to limit who may hold shares. At the governance level, you may want to limit or restrict the seats to different family members or branches. You may want to adequately represent branches but not every individual. At the management level, you may want to have fewer family employees and more professionals. So, across all these three different parts ownership, governance and management, you are trying to reduce the presence of family members while still retaining the important benefits in across all of these three parts. What does research tell us? Simplicity can help both family harmony and business performance. It is not exclusion for exclusion sake. The aim is to have a viable firm and an intact harmonious family. Consider as examples two different split that happened across many many years. One is the 2005 Ammanis and the Reliance Family Split and the second is the 2024 split of the Godridge family. Who is split? In Ammanis, it is about the fight between two brothers who are the sons of the founder. In the Godridge split, it is about two branches, the Adi Nardir branch and the Jim Sheath Smith-Abraj. What was split? In the Ammanis split, it is about the oil and petrochemicals going to one brother and telecom power and finance going to the other. In the Godridge split, it is about the listed companies and the Godridge and boys and the land which becomes important. However, these two splits different. Once split happened after a very public fluid and the mother reportedly brokered this deal. In the other split, it was announced as a very amicable split. The lesson that we can learn from each of these splits. When the rules are absent in when the founder exists, especially when it is an unanticipated exit, it becomes a very messy affair and therefore it is good to have all of this agreed upon in advance. And when separation is a planned pruning, it can strengthen both the branches post the split. The outcomes of both these splits are still playing out and all of us can see these evidence from public news and public sources about how the performance post split for both the family branches as well as the two different groups that got split in the Godridge split. And we can see and observe how timing, how process and how good governance can strengthen the split process itself.",
