      "transcript_text": "In a family business, you must understand that there can be two kinds of outsiders who work in the family and in the business. You must understand what they need and what they bring to both the business and the family. The two kinds of people are in-laws as a well as non-family members or non-family executives. With non-family executives, let's understand what they need and what the family needs. What they need is more clarity. Clear mandate, clear responsibilities, their authority, market link pay and things about what can be their career path, how far up they can view. Finally, the top role is always reserved for the family. Right? So then how far they are and they go. What the family needs from them is skills that the family itself doesn't have. They want loyalty to the firm and not just a one person. Honest advice on how the business is and what needs to be done, what decisions need to be taken. What can facilitate all of this is if we are very clear on their jadeys, their job description, contracts, their path, if these are specified, they will be very happy. For the non-family executives, they have to be very clear that they have to play in their own room. They call the management room. What kind of decisions they can take should be clear to them and where they should not step on the toes of the family and the owners. They have to be quite discreet in keeping what is being shared with them in confidence. They bring skills to the table so competence is a main criteria for them. What they should not do is to play one side of the family with the other or one family member against another or against one brother. branch or another branch. Very important for them to ensure that they pay enough credit to the family's lineage history, values and they should know where their limits are. As long as they can build trust with the family members, they can ensure a long stay in the company. It comes to in-laws, we have to understand in a family business, this is like again like in-any marriage between two families. we call them in this case, one is a business family and the other one is called the shadow family. The family of orientation as we call it. They are two families of orientation put together, they combine and what we call the family of procreation, the couple and the children and the next gen that comes along with it. So what happens actually the spouse who comes into the business family, the spouse actually belongs to two family and then helps raise the next owners of the family. In-laws can also influence the form in some ways. They may be able to bring sometimes the skills at the family lacks. For example, you have discussed how sometimes the CEO is actually an in-laws, a son-in-law for example, happens in some about 10% of the cases. Real skills because the son-in-law is probably worked outside and comes and brings skills to the company and takes it forward. How they can still hear and amplify the skills. and identify the family and the family. They also give us a way to measure the fairness of what we do. And because they are some kind of pseudo owners, there is a little bit of marriage, there will be some inheritance that flows into their side of the family. For example, whatever you say some shares will leak outside the family and go outside. They also are very good at comparing things as to how things are between this family and their own family. Many times they also have a family business and they compare with how their own siblings are performing. So if you want a ladder of how an in-law can be involved in the family business, they are probably a stages to which we can include them in the family business. They can be completely excluded, they will still get the information, they can keep informed but no role, they can be included in the family room. As in, they can be part of our gatherings or get to get to get to the family. They can be included as part of the assemblies that the family is going to have over time. Then there are two other stages where they are inside the business where they can be employed. Sometimes directly in a role or as a consultant as an advisor. And really the last step is to have them as an owner in the family. So these are five stages in which they can be involved. We have to decide as to which person from the in-law side of the family. We have to decide as better for which kind of role. Like we said, 10% can be usually becoming an owner finally. Most of them will be following the first four stages. So when you are defining the in-law rules and welcome new members, let's understand some of the opportunities that we will have and what kind of guardrails we should have. Suppose we are taking them as a successor CEO. It's a good opportunity because we get talent from outside and skills that we don't have within the family. But in which case we have to give a person a clear mandate and what is a plan by which he or she can ever become an owner. Or in case if it is an employee or an advisor. So again what do we need? No opportunities to have someone with different sets of skills and fresh eyes. But it's a job and it's not something that will be ever go to an ownership kind of a role. You have to decide who is family and is important to protect the assets and for all of them whether it's a in-law or a non-family member. We have to clear set boundaries for all of them. Who can go into an owner room at all has to be clearly defined. But if you have to thrive as an in-law in coming into a family firm and taking up roles in the family firm. It's important that you bring skills right finally. That's the biggest currency that you bring. It's best if you have found in your experience somewhere outside. Know the structure how the structure of the family business is the kind the number of relatives who are there in the business. What are their roles. And as long as you can manage this this is very good. Important for you to be very patient. Find someone with whom you can share the kind of navigation that you need to do within the family business and how you can take it forward. As long as you have a certain set of review points after having joined the family firm and you have some path to an exit. I think you can still thrive as an in-law.",
