      "transcript_text": "Here in this chapter, let us discuss the allocation of roles and control. We are discussing two points here, discussing role and control. These are two different things. Many times, these are confused for being the same. Someone gets to decide and someone gets to do this. The role is the job. The control is the decision. We should not confuse these two. Let us see in more detail. The role allocation, for example, is who does what? What does a CEO do? What does a division had to or the trustee do? So, this is decided by competence and some amount of interest that the person also has. But control allocation, if I say who will decide at what level is a completely different ball game there. We talked about three layers of control earlier. I think it was the first module, operational control, strategic control, ownership control. Ownership control is held through shares, through votes, through board seats. These are all decided in the owner room. Control is largely in the owner room. And when you confuse these two things, then you have a role without control or a control without any role can be one of the first stepping stones for a conflict. So, let us understand the allocation across the three layers that we discussed of control, operational, strategic and ownership. In a founder-led firm, all three are with the family. Family will control operational operations, strategic control is the family, ownership is anyway with the family. If you take the fourth one in the table that is shown to you, in a branch-based system, everything is within a family branch. Each branch has its own operational, strategic and ownership control. These are the two, and he is an extreme situation. But when it is a sibling team, which is running a family business, the operational control is usually split. It is decided one brother does finance, HR or something, the other brother does will do marketing, sales, operations, and the split. The strategic control is mixed in a sense that some parts, both of them will together decide, some will be separately. They will have some understanding on how much money can be spent or investment can be done. It is more a mixed case. Ownership is always between siblings equal, more or less, more often than not equal. But when its family runs, family owns and others run, operational is non-family. So, you hire people for specific skills, for specific roles, and they take care of it. The strategic is a mixed sometimes family and their non-family employees get together and decide on how the company strategy has to be run. But in this case, like we said, family owns ownership will remain with the family. What are the tools for which control can be held? One is voting structures. In large companies where you have a board, where you have independent directors, sometimes you can have a dual class shares, where some shares have different, some shares have voting control by which you can control multiple branches of the company, and limit sometimes votes for different branches and therefore exercise control. Sometimes there are trusts and shareholders agreement. Shareholders agreement are important, where you don't, you commit, the owner is not to sell their portion of shares to somebody or can only sell together or there is a first right of refusal for the other shareholder to buy out your shares in case you want to exit. A trust can sometimes be used as a tool, as a trust has trustees and beneficiaries in which beneficiary can get what is decided by a set of trustees. With some rules built into what majority is required, there are some veto rights as well that some constitution scan put inside and say that they can refuse or they can veto for specific decisions. So, you have to have a kind of a balance between all of these between control, between cash flow rights, to ensure that some kind of tunneling is not happening sometimes, where money is ferreted out and given to another family firm, it's easier that way for them because that may be a more more control for somebody and therefore this kind of things can be happening. Governance issues best to be addressed by some of these devices. So, some principles for allocating the rules is first of all separate these layers keep ownership strategy and operations distinct. Use the for room model to ensure that decisions are taken separately in each of these four rooms distinct what can be taken in what room is important. You have to match the role by the competence, the skills and the interest of the person not generally by birth order which is what happens in many family firms, the eldest brother is considered always more skilled or more important given the most plumb role. Have some rules which are written down fair, well in advance and some things can be discussed in the owner room there is a voice for everyone and therefore decisions are being seen as fair. Even better when things are documented and reviewed there is a formal process for all of this records are maintained and then everyone is comfortable that fair process is documented followed that their path is guaranteed and they have a future eye document. You must still plan and exit for everyone in the business in the family at some stage they may want to go and a larger transfer also. So, there are again if you want to have multiple branches and how do you manage that? For siblings there are five styles that we spoke about we have to choose one and understand what can be guardrails for each of those. How do you prove an ownership? How various branches and structures will have some amount of inequality? How can they therefore have some forums, a common purpose or at least clear drawn out purpose so that there is no problem that exists between the branches. How do you manage with outsiders, in-laws? Lastly how control can be allocated in a way that is seen as fair to everyone?",
