      "transcript_text": "In every family business, there is one set of people that deserve special attention. Women. Why does a family business course need a separate lens on women and what does the evidence actually show? Understanding the role of women and family business. businesses requires us to look at it from different lenses and not just one. We are talking about justice and rights, where equal treatment is owed, whatever the business cases. We are talking about talent, we are talking about continuity, we are talking about governance, talking about demographies, we are also talking about systems, where kinships and roles before the business logic does. And in trying to understand these different lenses, we need to understand the lens based on what the audience will actually accept. For example, a founder will hear continuity. A board hears governance, a family hears fairness. Let us look at a few trends of the role of women or the presence of women across businesses in the world. The presence of women in senior management and mid-management roles, as well as at the CEO levels have been consistently increasing over the years. If you look at the board seats, there has been an improvement in the presence of women on corporate boards thanks to a lot of positive affirmation quotas across the world. And in India, it applies mostly to listed firms and most family firms are unlisted and outside these numbers that you see over here. Inside family firms, what does the numbers tell us? Women hold rough and quickly a fifth of top roles, but they are still far from parity. The corporate pipeline narrows at every rung. And what this also means is that women's share by level, if you look at the US corporate data as of 2025, you will notice that the women in the entry roles are significantly higher close to 49 percent. But when you look at the C-suite, it is only about 29 percent. What is the business case? Let us look at multiple differences. Studies from 23 different countries, from McKinsey's study, EY and PIE, study from 91 countries, Amor et al from Italian firms, Nakale et al from French firms. What does all of these studies tell us? Broadly put the findings tell us that, for example, the top quartile gender diversity on executive teams and 39 percent more likelihood to outperform on profit if you have women. The caveat is that these are correlations and not causations. These studies across all of them, you will also find that, for example, family firms give women a better climate as a chair than as a CEO. Women's CEO do better in non-family firms, it does seem to indicate. But the broad rule to keep in mind is never to let the ethical case depend on the financial case. Coming to India, what has been women's labor force participation? This has been steadily increasing over the years. Women now own one in five enterprises but only a tenth of the turnover. This size gap is not a count gap. There are many units that are owned by women but most of them are micro but few of them are large and therefore, this difference between the numbers of firms owned by women and the turnover of women owned enterprises. Five data points on women at the top of business from India. Let us look at women on Nifty 500 boards or the top 500 listed by the turnover or women on the India rich list or the women's leader list. You will find that across all these lists, the representation is not as good as we would really want them to be or as reflective of the important role played by women in our family life, professional life and in every other aspect of our lives. Watch this one. 397 of 573 women executive directors are promoter family members close to 69%. Women, if you look at the evidence, women also start fewer firms and close more for family reasons. And this is important for us, we are talking about family businesses and who takes or who bears the burden. It does seem to be overwhelmingly the women in the family. What does the law tell us? Two thirds of men's rights and lags in enforcement. And if you compare where India stands in comparison to the rest of the world, you will find that we are not as well or as good doing in terms of the legal frameworks or in the enforcement. However, supportive frameworks we seem to be doing a little bit better. What do the numbers say and what they cannot say and this is very important. What do the numbers say? There is an improvement. Women in senior management roles are increasing. And their presence on boards, especially in context where board quota supply is also increasing. Where are they stuck or plateaued? There is a plateau since 2024 in their presence in the sea streets and the pipeline gets narrower at every run. Women owned firms typically tend to be smaller and micro. What is not yet counted? A global family firm series. Or the daughter's success in India. We don't know much about all of this. There are a lot of stories in the popular place. Wonderful examples, but not enough study about these stars. What about the air among women see use and a very, very important and very uncounted work that goes is the unpaid family work that women do. There are five faces of women in business and a different labour market. One is the worker. 49% of entry level in large US firms are women. As an entrepreneur, one in 10 women start a business a year. As an owner or investor, difficulties happen by inheritance or marriage into family firms. As a leader or director, about a third of senior management roles are held by women. As a household manager, most unpaid care work is by women and they tend to link the family and the firm. If you look at non-family firms and family firms across three different dimensions, what is their door? For the non-family firm typically it happens through application and selection. In the family firm you have kinship, birth, marriage and widowhood. Remotion rules in the non-family firm you have formal criteria and HR processes. In family firms, if you have informal decision making, then the decision is made by relatives and it can skew it and not in favour of women. What is a route to ownership? In a non-family firm it is typically through purchase or stock plans. In a family firms, prominent channels are through gifts, through inheritances or in marriages.",
