      "transcript_text": "Let us talk about equality, equity and difference and what a family firm offers women. When is treating everyone the same fair and when does fairness require treating people differently and what does a family firm actually offer women in exchange? There are three important things. and ideas to consider you. First is equality. Equal enjoyment by women and men of valued goods, opportunities, resources and rewards. The question is are outcomes and access equal? Next is equity. Fairness and justice in distributing benefits and responsibilities is equity and typically equity is the root to equality. The key test here is to ask is the process fair given to the people. starting points. The third is difference. People differ in circumstances, constraints and references and some differences are imposed. The test is to ask is the difference chosen or imposed. A good warning to consider here is that equity language can be used to mask discrimination. Is gender blind gender neutral? It is not. and we can understand this from four different lenses. One is the gendered organizations. Here the ideal worker is imagined male and neutrality hides a gendered substructure. Another perspective is to look at it as tokens. Under about 15% of women representation. Here you are looking at visible polarized and women are pushed to assimilate. They are more of a token representation. Another lens is to look at the rural Congruity theory. Here, there is a communal role versus a agenda leadership. There are two prejudices against women leaders because there are certain roles that naturally we tend to ask a woman and it is expected that these roles are congrual to the gender that women play and therefore a gendered role. The fourth is the care and justice perspective. Here you are looking at two ethics. Influential but this lens is critiqued as essentialist and thinly evidenced. What do these different lenses mean for family firms? The one daughter in a family of sons is a token by construction. And therefore it is very important for us to recognize that being gender blind is not being gender neutral. What is the difference without deficiency and without essentialism? What does a research tell us? Women are described as being more conciliatory, attentive and collaborative. And they must prove competence before respect is granted. Stiles may respond to how women are treated, therefore variation within each sex is much larger than the average gap. What are the guardrails that we could use? One is not deficiency. Difference is not an inferiority. Gender is not deficient. Second, it is not essentialist. Style is not destiny. And therefore we should not look at it from that perspective. It should not be a job filter. Good with people must not become HR only. The test we could use is to ask would we say the same for a son? A good design rule for family firms is that roles follow capability and preference and not category. The recommended posture is equitable process. Same criteria for all plus active steps to widen the pool. How would equity in action look like? Think about a five test fair process for the daughter question. First, what are the criteria? Were there written rules before the names or gender were discussed? Second, information. Every child sees the same numbers, plans and people. Third, development. Are you allocating the same budget? Similar level of a mentorship and rotation for each candidate irrespective of the gender. Fourth is the decision body. It includes an independent director and not only relatives and this independent director is also conscious of the fact that you should not be gender blind but gender neutral. The fairness equitable right and fifth is the feedback. Are you providing written reasons and a root to appeal with equal fairness to the daughter as well as to the son? What does a family firm offer women and what does it cost for them? What does it offer? It offers security and assets and protection especially in a crisis. It offers them standing and the founders trust as a family member. It also offers them a lot of flexibility and a better work life fit. It gives them early responsibility, mentorship opportunities and an ownership route. What is the matching cost? You are potentially a minority holder and not having a very clean exit although it does provide the security and the asset. While it provides you protection in a crisis this often these crisis often arrive unprepared. You might have the founders trust but you must prove your competence first. While it does offer flexibility the family and the firm that distinction starts to blur and very early on and the cost is greater for women. While yes there is early responsibility and mentoring this is better climate if you are a chair and not so much if you are a CEO and there you see that the difference does play out. And as an ownership route voting power is often tied to rule. Remember that the benefits are real but these benefits are conditional on how the family manages them and therefore the family's wisdom is very very important in ensuring a wonderful working climate for the women and the family. What is the exchange? Let us look at what the women gains and what the firm gains when there is trust in between. The women gains security and assets, flexibility is standing in the legacy of the business as well as the access to the family capital including trust, patience, what is the firm gain? The firm gains talent. Selected daughters typically have higher human capital because it across a higher bar at the beginning. It also gives the firm a much wider successor pool. It also brings different and diverse perspectives onto the world and you win the loyalty and continuity of knowledge and the benefit of wider networks. But it is key to remember that this exchange holds only if the women is seen paid and given a vice.",
