      "transcript_text": "You will now see how women as an invisible force and how powerful they can be. The question here is, can a woman in a family business hold real power with no title, no pay and no seat on the board or any of the people. of the major councils, can she still wield power? What does literature stay and are there examples which can be corroborated to how somebody who is largely invisible can wield power? What literature establish is that there is a lot of power that women hold, invisible women hold even without titles, even without roles. Inmissibility seen as more structural, not personal. They have heard about CEO's spouses, how they play a role even when invisible. They can give enough information sometimes of softer side which makes decisions sometimes very critical. There are five forms of invisibility. Let's look at each one of them. The first is legal invisibility. What does it mean? There is in the legal status, the woman doesn't have a role which means perhaps no, shadowing, no seat on the board. So these are what legal cases are. The economic invisibility is probably even more severe where it is counted as unpaid or unrecorded labor because they have a role sometimes but still doesn't get paid for it or sometimes paid not at fair market rates. Organizational, there is no title. She is not on the off-chat yet has to contribute, has to be a part of the business. It doesn't have anything to show for it. There is no appointment order or there is no paycheck coming into the month. Ownership, sometimes the name, there will be a partnership with the husband and wife start this company some 30 years ago. She uses a smaller shareholder, maybe some 1910 or 80, 20 or something. It's on paper, they are rich are holding. She is an owner but really doesn't control anything. Her vote matters for nothing. The decisions are taken by the patriarchy, the business is run by him completely. That's the ownership form of invisibility and narrative. In every story of the business, the woman is never mentioned. It's largely how the Patriax struggled and brought about this business to where it is, met customers and got all the stakeholders and started with a small room and built it. Not mentioned ever how the woman had to bear the the family or maintain the family and bring up the kids even when the going was tough the early days of the business. The contributions are well documented. There are some Indian examples as well here as well here. So, the Amurthi for example, is supposed to have given a 10,000 rupee loan to her husband and her book says she wrote it off later and that started off in forces like you know today or Maribhai Tata, how she had to pledge her diamonds to raise funds and Tata steel was going through downturn. These are invisible contributions not very well known but how they can also contribute to a firm. The cycle purses, so the cycle is like these five things. There's unpaid help or having no title, no pay, no documented record of being in the company as a result of it, no voice, no standing there in the company. These can be broken. What happens is daughters, what's their mothers go through the cycle. They have been an invisible contributor with nothing to show for it and they assume that this is what is going to be for them also. And as a result the cycle keeps repeating. It has to be broken at some point of time the places to break it can be either at the title itself, give the title, name the role for the person and what this person can contribute or more importantly. Economic change can be brought, where you pay for them for the role. As a result of it they have a title monthly pay, all this is recorded and people know that this is the role being done by the women. Finally for the kind of shareholding or ownership that the person has there should be a seat on the board. All these are possible to be handled and should be done where women need to be given a voice there. But when you have invisible women there is a cost we paid as well. So in somebody pays for all those costs. For example in the case where there is no pension, there is no shares, there is no standing on exit, there is no monthly pay, the women loses a lot. However if there is succession which happens rather blindly with no inputs being taken by from the women who has a better understanding of all the of the next generation, the family loses out. In some cases the business also is out if there is a key person risk and in the key person risk does come through and there are guarantees being given by the key person or the pattern-arcon such cases which is undocumented. Sometimes known to his wife, it goes on to a different set of problems. It is good to give a seat on the family council so that issues that they think are important especially for the future generations are raised. If shareholding is already there then they should be given a seat in the owner room where as a owner they can record the rights and bring up issues and take decisions accordingly and we know owner room is the most powerful room out here. If they are a director they should be given a board seat, they should be part of the and if they are part of the business as well then a management room where there is a clear role given to them there is a job description, there is a pay well all these are ways in which they can get involved.",
