      "transcript_text": "We will now see the kind of roles that women play and why these roles change? What kind of drivers are there that cause these kind of changes? Are there any sudden events which can cause these kind of changes? We are also seeing what can be a role transition kind of change? So, let us look at these five roles first. These roles can be mapped on this kind of a grid which is against authority and influence. So, on the left side the roles are having low authority or low formal authority, while as we grow up the y-axis we will find the influence increasing. So, these are these five roles. So, an invisible is one kind of a role which has low authority and high on influence but they are low authority. Somebody you can whisper into the CEO's ear and get things done, real influence actually no formal authority. The other side is the figurehead. Formal authority is high but really low on influence. So, these are the five kind of roles we will talk about and really we will see how things can change in this five roles and also what drives these kind of changes. So, let us look at the first one the figurehead and the invisible like we said in the previous diagram, these are the extreme opposites and on opposites sides of it. So, figurehead is there is a title but very little real part like I said influence is low. Somebody like a temporary leader or let us say how do we fix it is how we can add power to this person. There is a title right. So, if you add power to the title in such a way that we can also match both the power and the title there, it will can be a real win out there. On the other hand the invisible woman the CEO's ear whisperer like I get talk about, they have real influence, they have real power but they are not on the chart. Consultation is largely private out here and so, they are missing, missing from the charts, missing from the history. Once they are off the charts, they are not paid as well. What can we do in this year is to add a formal recognition to this. So, we have some manager and entrepreneurial women. Again, these are two other roles that we talked about here. Shobna Barthia today heads HT completely and she is a CEO there. So, there are these firm roles which they have taken over. Pre-apaul and APJ Parh totals from 1990 onwards, entrepreneurial and now completely heads the business. There are these strategic leaders and owner governance like documented cases Malika, Sini was an Taffay, we just spoke or Anisabha Godraj for the Gautraj consumer has been there since for the last eight years. Roasting Adar Malhotra took over as chairman once Shivnadar retired. Let's recap again five roles and let's classify them into three hats. The manager hat, the owner hat and the family leader hat. So, if we take the invisible person, the manager hat is informal. She doesn't have a formal role into the person. She is she has very high influence, remember. The owner hat is not there, very rare that this person also is an owner. But family leader hat is very very often but not very recognized. But if you take a strategic leader, you saw it on the on the 2x2 on the extreme right top. All these three hats will be very very high. It is a very strong manager, he is an owner and is also a family leader. So, each of these five roles that we talked of will fall in one or more of these hats. There is important point here to notice that there is a hat the woman is currently wearing and one or more hat that she must now acquire and then make the transition. So, next point is to discuss what we said, what are the drivers of change. So, these are existing profiles right. How do these transition and therefore, what drives this transition is what we are trying to see here. Once informal roles becomes job when there is there is growth, there is specialization. There is a genuine skills that she brings to the table is when the the roles become more qualified jobs. Family commitments children, elder care, all these leads to a different kind of more informal kind of a role. Certain events and this we have seen more often can really catapult a woman into becoming an unexpected successor. And there is enough examples in this case conflict is one driver which can really redraw the role of a woman because then she is expected to play the mediator, the conciliator and bring things to heal. Sometimes financial or legal issues can create owners unwittingly sometimes not planned like I said some of these cases can happen as a result of which women can become owners. Tax inheritance is an issue is an area how they become owners. There can be some personal factors by which women can really make that transition and change their profile. Ambition is one very important thing, qualifications, their own sense of identity as to what they can become and therefore drive towards that is an important way how which profile of the woman changes. If I were to group this I will call it some structural, some are as a result of shock, some are personal, can be institutional as well. While we will talk about the other drivers shortly let us talk this one, the sudden events change. There are documented cases where unplanned suggestions happen because of a sudden event pushing the woman to the top job. You have seen that in India as well there are quite a few cases like that. This is one, this is an slightly higher probability where every time there is a sudden event happening, invariably the woman gets the top job. We will now discuss is how each of these drivers can actually change and emerge into some kind of a role transition matrix and how the shift happens into what? For example, personal driver where like we talked about ambition for example, can go in any direction. It can be shaped by somebody outside or can be shaped by a family response or can be shaped by an internal drive to reach somewhere. We spoke about sudden event can make an invisible person suddenly into a leader even if temporary. Financial or a legal kind of a driver something changes which is required as a result of an inheritance or a tax or something on the legal side can move somebody from being an invisible to at least a figure head or sometimes even an owner. But family commitments can go both ways that something we must it can lead a woman from a role of a manager being reduced to somebody who is invisible because family commitments have taken the front seat. At some point they can go back and the woman can again enter the business back and become a manager. So this is something that can happen both ways. So this will tell you how a role transition can happen for women based on the kind of drivers that we have talked about.",
