      "transcript_text": "What are the barriers that women face within family businesses? Which barriers are specific to family firms and why do accomplished women in them sometimes feel that they do not deserve their success? If you look at five different issues, what we can call as textbook issues, because these are typically tend to get pointed out and let's look at the evidence against each of them. First is occupational separation. One study connected in Spain talks about 81% of female lead versus 47% of male lead family firms in services tend to see this occupational separation. If you look at the promotion chances, typically women occupy positions that do not carry a formal title or formal role and also get paid. at a lower level than what a male is paid for the same rule. They also tend to have lesser strategic voice, they tend to get left out of the loop and off the organizational staff. They also tend to get overlooked as a successor and there might be a bias against them despite home support and therefore they need to prove their competence much more than males before they can earn the respect. A caution to note is that this five item list is not a summary of all the kinds of issues or barriers but it's just a very general typology that has been put together to talk about broader issues that we tend to come across. If you look at the evidence about a succession within family firms and occupational separations, we tend to note that about 81% of firms tend to have a male successor when both success of the child are available within the potential successor pool. And this segregation tends to narrow in second generation. generation firms. Another very interesting concept to consider is the double bind. Women are expected to be warm and consultative. However, they are also expected to take tough stances and be very decisive. But when women tend to take decisive and tough decisions, they may tend to be judged more unlikeable than a male with a similar decision. And they might not be seen as enough leader material. The same decision or the same behavior, the language is typically used that the son is being assertive whereas the daughter is being very abreast. If you look at this support available to women at home and the resistance at work, we tend to notice a legitimacy gap. At home there is support from the father, the mother and the siblings. All of them tend to bag the girl at the home. And therefore the girl enjoys family legitimacy. But at work, the staff tend to be loyal to the family. The father and look at the girl as the boss's daughter. This results in a building up of rule in congruity that these are male rules. Why are they being given to the daughter? What can help? When the founder publicly backs and sponsors the daughter. And when there is a clear mandate that the daughter will be taking over certain roles, possibly considered for a success position. It tends to ease this legitimacy gap. It also helps when there are early measurable results about the performance of the daughter and also having an HR policy which is aware of the biases against gender. A good first week test is to ask what would signal that the girl's authority is her own and not lent by somebody else? In other words, is the authority that the girl is enjoying in the family firm, her own stems from her role and job descriptions rather than being backed by somebody else, maybe the father or the brother? We can also visualize the barriers in terms of a three-level map. This could be visualized as three concentric circles. At the innermost circle you have the individual. Here you are talking about a blindness or not looking at the differences, a feeling of an imposter syndrome or there is a glass ceiling that they assume at an individual level. At the family and at the firm level, you are looking at issues of separation, fewer promotions, no formal title or the daughter is or the women are overlooked when considered for promotions or roles or a bias against gender. At an institutional level, we can also consider factors such as the inheritance loss property rights, quotas that mandate female representation, the culture that allows or is favorable to the inclusion of women or not favorable to the inclusion of women. And these constitute a kind of a three-level map of barriers that women face within family firms. If we look at and try to connect this back to the employment policy about working within a family firm, the issue if it is occupational separation, the employment policy could use rotation through a core-line function to overcome that issue. How do you overcome fewer promotion? Have very clear return criteria and an independent review before considering the candidate behind the decision. When you look at the issue as being a little strategic voice being available, a good way to work around this and address the issue is to have seats on the family council on the firm's board or give them adequate voice-at-rate rates. When the issue is the potential overlooking of women as successors, a good way to work around this is to plan the names of all the candidates and criteria to be unbiased against the women. And if you talk about the issue about gender bias or the employees or from employees, it is very good to have public sponsorship by the founder or by the family to incorporate 360 degree feedback as well as give a grievance or an appeal route when such biases tend to stem. Where should you have these policy levers? Ideally, you should build them across all the four rooms, but the decision is strongest when it happens in the owner room, motivation to avoid success. Many times it does happen that achievement is coded as being very masculine. So, role conflict tends to crop up for women who are strong achievers. Another issue is the success guilt. Guilt and law coexist. For example, consider a firm, an Egyptian firm where the mother and a daughter are co-leading that particular firm, but when they have success, will they could tend to be a success guilt? They feel guilty, they don't feel that they can fully own and achieve what they lived up to. So, what helps at three different levels for the women, the family and for the firm. For the women, it can be very helpful if you tend to name it and separate the work from the worth or self-worth. Also, keep a law of the results and accomplishments that happen and find a good peer support group who can support you through this entire process. At the family level, it is very important to raise the work or not only the name of the person. It also drop the sibling labels and give a very specific feedback in order to help them to develop better. And you should frame this win not as a loss. It should be looked at as a strength which the family is benefiting from. And at the firm level, it is very helpful to have written down criteria, independent assessment and a 360 degree feedback. Together, all of these three can strengthen the women's role and their feeling of self-worth, especially when they achieve success. And they do not compare themselves as being less self-worth arriving from, let's say, an impostor syndrome or a guilt that they have. How could we achieve so much? Peer networks tend to come when you engage with several of these women leadership, fora or alumni networks that you come from. Here, you tend to find other peers who are also undergoing a similar journey or circumstances. And they can help you to better look at and acknowledge yourself work when you accomplish great and strong results.",
