      "transcript_text": "An important role that women play in family businesses when they do not occupy a formal role is that of being the leader's spouse. There is a typology of six different roles that women play as a leader's spouse. How does the wife of the CEO or the leader share the role of the leader? Succession and Continuity, whether or not she has a formal title. Broadly put, there are six different roles that we can look at. First is that of a jealous spouse, where the women sees the firm as a rival for the husband's attention and time and energies. When does this help or how can you leverage this? If the woman is heard early on and her voice is taken into consideration, it can definitely be helpful in evening out this relationship. And if you do not do that, it can tend to undermine the successor and therefore be harmful for the firm. Especially a succession, what the family should aim to do is to give the leader's spouse a life after the succession role. What will she do? What will she do? What will she do? Succession. Right. Second is as a chief trust officer. Many times, the spouse of the leader tends to be the one who is the closest confidant and holds the leader's trust. Here, the woman can carry messages, can help interpret decisions, make better decisions, strengthen it. However, it can be harmful if it is not accompanied by any accountability. And therefore, at succession, it will be helpful if the family can involve her not solely as a channel, but more actively engage in strengthening that root or the channel of trust. You know, by shaping her also for the role that she is playing. Third is as a business partner for the CEO or leader. Here, she could very likely be a co-owner and therefore also shares the risk. However, it is important that the roles are very explicit and if these roles are made very explicit, it can be very helpful for the firm, but it can become harmful if the roles are not explicit and the distinction between the home and the firm roles tend to blur. What the family should do at succession is to plan the joint exit and finances. Another role that women tend to play when their husband is the leader or the CEO is to be a VP. Now, of HR, of finance and when facilities etc. Here, the women tends to run the back office. It helps when all of this is being run very smoothly. However, it can get harmful when there is a over dependence on the women to do all of these functions and the organization becomes very person-centric. What can the family do? At succession, the family should document her knowledge and also provide a formal role and equitable pay. Another very important role that the leader or the CEO's wife plays is that of a senior advisor. She is like an elder state woman who is wisdom the leader or the CEO leans upon to make the decisions. This is very helpful for the firm when the women actively and wisely advises and helps the CEO or the leader to make the decisions. It can get very harmful if the control rests too much with somebody who does not hold the formal role. The leader or the CEO in this case, what we are talking about is the male. And therefore, the women should not keep the control for too long. What helps for the family at this stage, especially succession, is to agree about the advice versus the decision. How much role will she have in making the decision? And how fair and how does she and is there a formal channel for that advice to get her not only by the individual leader but also by the family council, the corporate board and the rest of the family? The final role is to look at the women, the spouse of the leader or the CEO as a free agent. She has her own career and she is not engaged with the family firm or with the decisions over here. It helps when there is a healthy boundary between the women's free agent role. However, when there is a crisis, it becomes a problem because as a free agent and there was a very healthy boundary which both the leader and the spouse respected, there is no information that was shared which can be very harmful in a crisis situation. At succession, what the family should ideally do is to breathe her fully as well as keeping her appraised timely, well in advance. If you map all of these six roles based on the sense of ownership on let's say the X-axis, a low sense of ownership gradually moving to high sense of ownership. And on the X-axis how it either builds or obstructs continuity? You see that the partner tends to be in the strongest quadrant of this particular matrix. You also have trust officers, senior advisors in the strong roles where the continuity is getting strengthened. Even a free agent partly helps in building continuity. The aim for the family firm should be to move the spouse up and to the right. There could also be an unfortunate event where the leader or the CEO is an unplanned death and the wife is expected to take over. And there are many such cases that we have seen in business history where this widow has stood up and transformed and made the firm very, very strong. So here there are five important questions that the woman needs to answer. One, the advisors for the family and for the family firm do they know them by name. This is where networks and personal relationships ability to call on and rely on their advice especially in strengthening continuity becomes very important. Second, the accounts and the documents does she have access to them? Fully and fairly. Third are the will and agreements who holds the shares the ownership, the votes and the chairperson position. Next is for the immediate 90 days whom will the authority lie with who may take decisions? And lastly, what is the emergency succession plan? Has it been named and agreed upon well in advance? Remember that readiness is very, very important for continuity and readiness is not necessarily involvement. The free agent must know as much as the partner because here we are talking about readiness in crisis and an unexpected and unfortunate circumstances. But readiness is being better prepared for continuity.",
