      "transcript_text": "In succession, we have talked about this rule of primogeniture, where the eldest bond is usually the successor. In, if you read the text as such, it will seem like it is very gender neutral, right? That the eldest bond is the successor, but that is only in words. In reality, it is usually the eldest bond's son is the successor, whether it is in case the son is not the eldest bond also many times, he is the successor. Well, what we are trying to understand is what can we do to help a daughter step up? The eldest or not, and more skilled in how siblings actually split in practice. What are the rules which decide who gets control? Let's look at some of these things. This is a chart which talks about successors in Spanish families. It is a sample size where we know where the first bond can be of either gender, it doesn't matter, but in fact, the successors are male. So, what exactly means? And it is true for many, many things in India as well as we have seen many of these cases. The family says that the eldest will succeed, but what it does in practice is more often than not very different, the son succeeds. So, what can help a daughter step up and what can be done about it? One of the best things that can be done is to name the successor much earlier. Son or daughter name the person much earlier in case a daughter is elder, name her earlier in itself and therefore inputs can be given as to how she can become a successor over time. They must be given a formal role on the chart paid as per market, ensured that she comes with some outside experience, earns credential somewhere else so that when she comes into the system, she is well armed with what needs to be done out here, understands the working relationship having worked outside. Same criteria for everyone and then once you can have some kind of a network built for her, there are these are steps that can be taken to ensure that the outcome is well scripted and fired for success rather than set up for failure, that's something we must do. So, if you see the rules that decide who inherits control, in a male preference, primogeniture kind of is a kind of a system, it's always the eldest son first. Daughters commented the picture only if there is no son at all, there is research, there is documented evidence from UK on this. In India, if you see it is co-personary by birth, the 2005 succession law which the government enacted and is now well enforced, the daughter is equal to the son in ancestral property. Not just that, the doctors also equal to the son in terms of management control and not just strategic control. So, the value of natural MK is a classic case in the Murga Pagrup, how equal rights actually paid out and the court educated in her favor and the family has reached a settlement accordingly. In Japan, in case they have only daughters, the husband joins the family firm, takes the family name, brings in skills from outside and that's something very unique to Japan. What we are trying to say is somebody gets the shares and somebody gets the chair, is there a way we can ensure that women get both. There is some research which shows that what kind of a succession happens and how is performance after succession, if the first bond is a wider gender, start conclusive. Because what this kind of research does not consider is the willingness ability matrix as to how willing is the person who is succeeding and what is the ability of the person who is succeeding. Both these are very important in order for the family succession to be very very successful. There are three legal settings we can compare. Japan, we just spoke about how they can adopt its son-in-law and keep the name and while the daughter in that case only the channel and in complete difference in India where daughters are co-personers by birth and can lead to a partition risk if things are unplanned. In Japan, there is a very different kind of rule where a son-in-law can come and take the name and run the family business. This is unique to only Japan, this is not found in most other countries. Indian H.U.F. can have a partition kind of risk. So let's look at even case of a primogeniture, what are the kind of benefits it offers and what kind of cost it actually brings to the table. There is enough clarity when it comes to primogeniture, everyone knows who is the hair and that's clear on day one. It's usually selected in whichever way, whether it is selected by birth or by gender. Most often than not, it is not selected by ability. Villiness is also not considered. You are almost assumed that you will come and join the family business. The other benefit is that in such cases that the hair can be groomed very early on. What also happens is that the others are not even groomed. It's very clear who the hair is and special care is taken to ensure that the eldest hair is groomed. The benefit is that the control stays undivided. Those who are excluded, well, they hold their shares but there is no role. There is resentment that will build up. That's how it is. For those who are there as part of the largest stakeholders, whether it is employees or suppliers or even other family members, there is some kind of stability. But the key benefit then, we need to consider and key important point that we need to consider is while this ensures clarity and grooming and ensure unity, all these do not need the birth order. Even if it is a woman, all these can be ensured and we can ensure a primogeniture model for women as well. The performance did not be any difference in any case. So, how beyond primogeniture, how are things handled? Ownership should be fair for all children, they are at sons or daughters. But it doesn't mean that all of them can be good leaders or leadership can be chosen in the same way. That need not be fair because leadership should be chosen by a certain criteria. In terms of what the skills they bring to the table, plus important to understand again the willingness and ability matrix. And how they can be chosen is either via a family council and not just one person with a clear written criteria which is applied equally to all the children. And those who are not leaders tomorrow and who are not going to become part of the leadership team can be handled by either a buyout or a dividend. All we are saying is that while the family can be equal in the ownership circle, merit will always take the rule and it comes to business circle. And if the merit so happens that it is going to be a woman who is most equipped to take on the leadership in the future and that should be followed. The rules are the same, there is no reason why it should always be the son. Thank you.",
