      "transcript_text": "Another important question is to look at the air-assess inside and the reasons for them to join. Once a woman is in as a successor, which path does she take? And why did she join the firm in the first place? A research in in 2002, looked at 12 different air-assess and based on the research classified them into three different groups. One is as an invisible force. There is a lot of power, but there is no formal title that is accompanying it. Second is as a professional. There is a title, a function and accompanied by very visible responsibility. The third is as an anchor, providing stabilising support. Here the air-assess is holding the family or the firm together through relationships and very often without a title. We can use and connect this typology with our three life cycle access model that we studied. The first is a professional. If we also look at these different positions as a professional, as an invisible role or as an anchor role, you could look at what kind of events are driving this and where do the typically move into? A professional when there is a personal event, for example, a child birth etc. The family moves into an invisible role. An invisible position could move into an anchor role. If there is an unexpected event, for example, a family crisis and the woman is expected or the air-assess is expected to play stabilising role or an anchor could move into a professional role when there are organizational events such as formalising of a particular role. Another interesting way to look at it is the position is that as an entrepreneur. You are formed a new venture on your own, either inside the entrepreneurial family galaxy or outside it. For example, Ananya Birla formed Sothantra micro-finance very early on. What is important to ask is where the air-ass is now rather than asking who she is? Because the same three drivers and different role authorities could dictate the position that she is in right now rather than who the person is and this tend to change over a period of time along with circumstances. What helps an air-ass advance? Definitely, parental mentoring and support both at home and at the business pay a very strong role in strengthening the air-assess journey. Sometimes even the public sponsorships by the family and the owner group and other members within the family business systems strengthen and enrich the centre of journey. Definitely having outside credentials or work experience and achievements that you can rely on also help. There are different styles that the air-ass could choose being either conciliatory, supportive, collaborative or flexible. And this reported style are not a stereotype to abide by and there is a lot of choice and it varies based on the circumstances and the growth journeys of the air-ass. When the air-ass is looking at the top position they could either be a transparent ceiling or a mirrored ceiling. A transparent ceiling is where the top position is visible from the outside. The policies in a buyer's and closed networks and therefore it is only seen but there is a very clear glass ceiling that exists and therefore she is not able to break through. Contrastred with a mirrored ceiling where the air-ass herself may choose not to pursue the top position. An important question to ask before calling it a mirrored ceiling rather than as a glass ceiling is to check whether her brother, if there was a male candidate as a competing successor, was offered the same options. If they were and the girl was not then it is not a mirrored ceiling. But if the daughter was not offered then it is a transparent or a glass ceiling. Why do women join family businesses at all? There could be two broad categorization. One is to look at it as a push where the air-ass is brought in because she is needed by the firm. Or second there is a pull where she is drawn in into the family firm. The source for a push is typically when there is a crisis or a parent makes a request to the daughter maybe many times waiting. There are a lot of several examples where the earlier generation had only daughters and the daughters are unwilling for a long time but the parents' expectations happened and then the daughter ultimately did enter the business. You know so you see that kind of a push. Typically when there is no brother available. As a pull the source could be that the woman sees this as an opportunity to lead, to contribute and strengthen the family and the family firms legacy. The flexibility of working with the family firm as well as access to the family, capital, first and resources. While there is some evidence that says that few daughters actively choose to be successors this is not hard and fast rule. There are a lot of changes that we are seeing where women are actively seeking out successor roles. And when we look at the evidence for the pull side of it we look at a good combination of instrumental support plus experiences that raises the intention. You see a lot of support both of the family system and the firm system or with the professional circle that you are working with. You might have worked elsewhere either as an entrepreneur or as a free agent either within or outside the entrepreneurial family galaxy or in another firm. And all of this experience strengthens the intention or the willingness to join the family business. What is a risk if either of these two are unmanaged? If the push is unmanaged it is typically treated as a stop gap arrangement. And therefore there is no negotiated role for the errors when she enters. This leads to a lot of blurring of boundaries, no formal authority, lesser respect that tends to come in authority or decision making. All of this are questionable. But when there is a strong pull factor and the woman is willing to enter and is actively exploring succession. The expectations may exceed what the firm can offer and therefore the firm needs to work with the errors. And this is true even for male potential successors. That you need to balance the expectations versus what the family can offer. In the push it is important to ask at succession or transitions. Ask the woman what role title pay she would want. Why now and get clarity on all of this? When you look at the full side of it it is important to ask her about what evidence backs the promise that the family firm is able to make to her about her space within the family business. And what she sees because it is important that we don't have excessive expectations about what the family firm can give. You have realistic expectations that are hopefully gender-dibised. When you think about it from an errors perspective there are five questions that the woman needs to ask herself when she is considering the question of should I join the family firm. First is why? Am I being pushed or pulled into joining the family firm? Would I choose this path if no one had asked me to choose it or take it up? Second is the role. What is the title authority, pay and the reporting line we do I said and can I get this in writing? Third is the proof. What experience and credentials do I bring maybe from outside working with somebody else or in my education achievements etc. What is the proof that I bring in? Next is the path. Am I on the successor list? And what are the criteria that I am going to be measured against? And the fifth question very importantly what is the exit path? What happens if this does not work out? Is there a buyout plan? Is there a notice that I need to serve? When can I make this choice? How do I exercise? And what kind of exit paths will be available to me if I choose to exit? When I find that maybe I am not working out for the family firm or the expectations from the family firm or working with the family firm are not really aligning with my interests.",
