      "transcript_text": "Now, let's look at what law and regulation says and what actually reaches a family firm. Which rules reach a family firm at all and where does the family have to supply its own rules instead? When we consider how far the law reaches an on what aspects, for law, charge or listed firms typically there are mandates around the inclusion of women and female representation on the boards. At every employer level there are regulations about the A and the leaves. And within the family structure there are laws that govern inheritance and property rights. What we must remember is that law is about something that is mandated on paper and there could be variations in practice. It is about how the legal system does the rest of the work. When we look broadly at the different rules that exist at a global scale on boards, pay, leave and inheritance, we find that there are lot of mandates about the presence of women on boards but typically it reaches to large or listed firms. Pay transparency, we do find especially in some of the more developed contexts and differently applicable to the large employers. When we talk about family leave, again there is a difference across countries. In inheritance it does vary both at national level as well as at times. Many of these laws are based upon certain practices for example either Islamic personal law, Hindu personal law etc. Another thing to note especially around board quota mandates is that many times these are comply or explain principle based mandates which means that firms have the discretion about compliance or explain their noncompliance. When we look at the law in India, something to highlight over here is that the companies act does mandate the presence of one women director for the listed firms and firms meeting certain size criteria. And moreover this is about women independent directors. Second, we also can note that when we talk about the inheritance laws, the Hindu succession act was amended in 2005 and women or daughters or co-passionaries. Similarly, even within the Muslim personal law there are provisions to protect women. When we talk about the workplace regulations, we have the maternity requirements, maternity leave requirements, the labor codes, the posh acts and at the enterprise level we also have government support although not a law per se. There is a lot of programs which do provide women with support for example under the PMudras scheme, 3% MSC procurement sub target or 90% CGT MSC cover if you register as a woman owned unit as an MSC. But this is a moving target right for example a corporate laws bill or a sharia that challenge and labor code rules are all pending. So, we are in a transition but we are moving I would say in a very good direction hopefully we will get stronger and more protection and representation for women on a legal platforms. What should a family business do about all of this? 1. Understand the scope which rules reaches at all at the size of our firm as well as the aspirational size that you want to grow into. You are listing aspirations on the jurisdictions that you fall under. Second is about the pay and leave plan for the pay gap reporting and labor code compliance wherever the family form operates. Next is about the inheritance audit each family members personal law use and make strong use of wills gifts or trusts where the shares are in equal and get the understandings in writing. At the HR floor level ensure that there are maternity safety and a posh committee which are not optional especially as the size of the firm keeps increasing. And within the family constitution right equal treatment of daughters and sons into the family constitution because this is what the family does definitely control. But you should keep in mind that the law only sets the floor. The family sets the promise right for example the law may mandate certain board seats pay leave or a daughter's legal share. The policy that the firm has in practice can strengthen this further with written criteria pay bans review etc. And the family constitution is the promise what the family is promising to the daughters and the sons is their equality is their equity is their fairness do they recognize the differentials all of this is very important. The part that the family controls is the policy and the promise which is the practice and the family constitution.",
