          "model_answer": "The three models of working couples in a family firm are: (1) Primary-secondary model, where one spouse is the primary carrier and the other is secondary, typically running the back office; the governance challenge is that conflict always resolves in favor of the primary carrier, making the secondary role supportive. (2) Turn-taking model, where priority alternates on a schedule; the key challenge is whether the turn is ever honestly honored, as one person may decide to continue forever, effectively breaking the agreement. (3) Double-primary model, where neither spouse yields and both are ambitious with significant growth paths; the key challenge is conflict management, requiring an arbitrator to ensure clashes do not get out of control. A real-world example from the lecture is the scenario where it is the wife's family firm and the son-in-law is the CEO: this can ensure family harmony but risks nepotism creeping in as in-laws generally do not question the son-in-law. Another example is Infosys, with over 2 million shareholders holding over 4 billion shares, illustrating why owners cannot run daily operations and must delegate authority to professional managers, which relates to the delegation challenges faced by working couples.\\n\\nMarking Rubric:\\n- Core conceptual understanding of the three models: 4 marks\\n- Identification and explanation of the key governance challenge for each model: 3 marks\\n- Integration of a real lecture example (Infosys or son-in-law/CEO scenario): 2 marks\\n- Clarity and structure of explanation: 1 mark",
