      "transcript_text": "In this section, we will discuss conflict management in a family business. It is great if family businesses can continue from generation to generation without any conflict. That is great. But invariably, it is probably inevitable that some kind of a conflict happens. It is great if it is something that can be resolved. There are tools to resolve it. But here, what we will try to understand in this case is more an introduction as to why conflict can occur and can there be some kind of a method in which conflicts can be avoided. What we will try and see over this section. So, first what we will see is how, for example, what I am taking of a sale board case is to how a family business is. ended. You see here, there is dispute over a sale board between brother and well, each brother brought a different view. Some said it is funding racing. The other said it is helping a mandatory in customers. It is about a boat and some expenses over a boat. But in reality, the boat was getting discussed at the surface. What there was was a deep seated resentment over roles and contribution to the business. The trigger was just the sale board. What actually caused the business was the business went down as a result of this infighting and sold at a discount and the family permanently lost an identity. It was never about a sale board. It was about some deep seated conflicts that was there and which was kept bubbling, kept building up, building up till it there was a trigger and that trigger led to the closure of the company. There are enough research papers which talked about how disagreements actually caused families to lose trust among themselves and lead to some kind of a breakdown of it. There is enough research to say that they occur fairly regularly. The owners have to take a step back and understand if these can be resolved and how to resolve this and independent referee sometimes you should help. So, there are three kinds of conflict, what can call stages or how hot they are or how cold they are and what stage of the conflict they are and how they can be resolved. If the conflict is too hot, it means it is gone public. Everyone seems to know about it. It probably reached the courts as well and the warfare is being fought in the public domain. In which case such kind of company is growth stalls, it is better off for the company to exit the business, split between the litigants or sell it. Nothing much is going to come out of it. It is very probably too late in the day to save it and trying to find some kind of mediation around it and try to preserve it. Then there is this Goldilocks zone. Goldilocks is typically measured when spoken about when it is either too not so bad or not so good either or it is usually like earth is said to be in the Goldilocks zone. We are neither too close to the sun or not too far away for water to survive. So, similarly there is a Goldilocks zone for conflicts in a family business where it is not too hot. There are difficult issues which are raised. They are addressed may not be immediately but there is some path towards addressing it and they get addressed in some reasonable amount of time because you cannot address everything on day one or quickly enough some need some time. But the intent to address the issues that are being raised is never in doubt. There is still some amount of trust left in the family and trust is the currency of the family. So, as long as there is still some notion of trust left issues can be resolved and that is the Goldilocks zone. Too cold is the other hand is there is enough bad blood in the family. You see as an outward unity but actually there is not it is it is fake. Internally everyone is bubbling boiling but well they don't bring it out to the open. There is an elephant in the room it is it is never name it is never called out nobody speaks about it. People are silent and one fine day you will find that the company is collapsed or it is sold. It will surprise everyone but that is not surprising because it was always there. It just showed up in public did not show up. So, we spoke about these three zones right. So, this is a three question quiz which will tell you which zone is your current family business in. And there are three parts to it. So, if you go through these and get your answer for these three questions. Get a good enough idea. Where is your family business and therefore if you are in either of these three zones hot, cold or the Goldilocks zone. What needs to be done to ensure that family business is still stable and can be continued. Generally these are questions on the direction of the family firm. The way the decisions are being made in the family firm. For example, are critical issues being addressed or there is nothing that is not left unsaid in the room. Everything is spoken about honestly transparently or there is nothing left unsaid. Nothing being carried back after the meeting without being spoken out in the room. And how are relationships in the family? How harmonious they are. Are you able to still work and celebrate together? You may not be extremely close with each other, but you should have a very good working relationship and more importantly. They should be enough trust in the system. That trust is an important currency. And as long as intent of each of one of the family members is not questioned, the relationship can always survive. So with these three questions you can arrive at a decision on where your family business is. And more importantly how can the family business still be managed well and continued across generation by preserving that trust?",
