      "transcript_text": "Let us now move to the different types of conflict. Once conflict is coming, what shape does it take and is every type of conflict do equal damage? Broadly put, there are four types of conflict. One is a relationship conflict, second is cognitive conflict, third is process conflict and fourth is succession conflict. While the first three are not very specific to family firms, they have a unique flavor when applied to family firms. And the fourth, the succession conflict is a family business specific type of conflict. Let us go into each one of these in more detail. Relationship Conflict, this relationship conflict manifests when the conflict is about the person and not the issue. Some symptoms whether the conflict is about a relationship. You know you tend to see a personal attacks which can tend to get disguised as business feedback. I am not like the person, but I say that the decision is bad. Why am I saying the decision back? Because I am trying to push against a person. And the dispute tends to persist even after this stated issue is settled. Because this was always about the person. And arguments returned to how someone was treated and not what they did. and the tension is felt even in unrelated meetings. Relationship conflicts are almost uniformly harmful. Research finds that it reduces satisfaction and performance regardless of the task. And this has been confirmed multiple times and in family firms this often worsens because you are moving the relationship conflict, especially if it is between family members or between family conflict. and in finance world like an extended family. We are taking it beyond the management circle or the business circle into other dimensions as well. Think about when one person wanted to buy a sailboat and wanted to show it as a business expense since they wanted to treat or better receive customers. The board was the stated issue. But it is about the roles and contributions where the relationship conflict was certainly lying underneath the entire system. by dispute. The second type is cognitive conflict. We are the disagreement is about ideas and not about people. We may be having a disagreement about the idea, the the the interpretation of facts. This is very functional and can strengthen decision quality when retained as a cognitive conflict. You could also have effective conflict where the disagreement that has become now very personal and emotional. This is very dysfunctional because it tends to reduce and lower trust over a period of time similar to what a relationship conflict can do. What is a tendency to watch? In a family firm, cognitive conflict can often slide into effective conflict. Once old history starts to get pulled in and therefore it is very important to keep the conflict where it should be. If it is about the idea or the judgment or interpretation of facts, it should be kept over there rather than shifting it into an effective conflict. The next one is a process conflict. Here it is about how and who not about the what? What does it look like? For example, who has the authority to decide? The family firm example is if there is a capic decision that needs to be taken and it is crossing the threshold which the family had originally agreed upon. Do you need a board sign off or can the CEO approve it alone? Who has the authority to decide? Second, who reports to whom? Consider two siblings. Both of them are vice president within the same firm and there is some bit of clarity missing about who should the plant manager answer to. Therefore, it is about the process in terms of the reporting line and the hierarchy. Next is how information should flow? The family room here is about a strategy shift after the board room or before the board room. Where should the decision first be placed? It is about a process and the process should be consistently followed or there should be a criteria about which decision should go to which room when? The fourth and very family specific type of conflict is a succession conflict and this again has a cognitive layer to it as a relational layer to it and a process layer to it. At the cognitive layer, there could be a genuine disagreement about who is the most capable among the pool of potential successes. And the relational layer, there could be old for example, a father's son or a sibling rivalry that tends to resurface during a succession. At the process layer, not having agreed upon criteria, timelines or planning the entire succession process will in advance or the decision maker to make the choice. All of this can make succession a very conflict-redun journey. And this can be the worst of all the types of conflicts for a family firm. Why? Because a succession dispute is a family question, a management question and an ownership question all at once, which is exactly why no single room can resolve it alone. Which type of conflict helps and which type harms the firm? Cognitive or process conflicts can improve decisions when more assumptions are tested because you are able to challenge why an idea is good or not or the judgment backing it. Which type of conflict is helpful and when can it harm the firm? It is good to consider this along two different aspects. One is the effect of the conflict when the family harmony is high and second is to consider this effect when the family harmony is low. When family harmony is high, cognitive or process conflict can improve decisions because you are able to challenge assumptions or the decisions being taken on very solid grounds. Relationship conflicts even if the family harmony is high are still harmful and succession conflicts when the family harmony is high are manageable with an agreed pre-built process. When the family harmony is low, cognitive or process conflict typically tend to spill into relationship conflict. And relationship conflicts are definitely harmful and very severely when the family harmony is low. And when the family harmony is low, a succession conflict is a very high risk of this becoming a full-blown relationship conflict. Often ending with very bad outcomes for the family level as well as at the family firm level. The practical implication of this is to protect family harmony first because that is what keeps the task conflict from turning into a relationship conflict. For example, take a particular conflict and you could use four classifications over here. For example, in one classifications two cousins disagree respectfully on whether to enter or not into a new export market. Or for example, a son feels that his father praises his sister's ideas and dismisses his own in every single meeting. Another case is where nobody can say whether the CFO or the family council approves grants to family members. And the last one, consider where the founder has not named a successor and three children each assume that it will be them. You could ask yourself which type or types each of these fit into and will family harmony push this towards constructive or a destructive outcome.",
