      "transcript_text": "He spoke about how conflict is inevitable, what are the sources of conflicts, but how does actually conflict manifest itself in a family business, and how do they get worse? We spoke about how conflict is not overnight, right? It takes time before it gets worse and before it gets worse and before it gets worse. So, what are those kind of stages? There are actually seven stages in which the conflict goes through, and spirals out of control. And even then, it is not that it starts with two people and the conflict remains with two people. It sort of divides the entire family. You can find various interest groups, alliances being forming, and then pulling the entire family in different directions. And that's how the conflict plays out. So, stages, alliances is how this entire thing plays out and we will see all those stages in alliances. First, let's understand actually there is how the conflict is seen visible outside. Many times, publicly, the leaders are saying that there is no conflict. We get along perfectly. But beneath the surface, we will actually find like an iceberg beneath the surface is usually much bigger than what is visible outside, right? That's what they say of an iceberg. And that's how it is in many cases. What's happening below and in the business is what is not often getting discussed like unequal contributions, unequal pays, decisions being avoided are not being made. There's no voice for people who have resentment. There is no structured governance mechanism. So, in the end, when there is a rupture, there is a breakup or there is a split. You will be searching for reasons. But the reasons to always there, it just never came out. It was never publicly acknowledged or never discussed across. So, to the outside world, it will appear sudden. But those in the family, we will say, if you see the stages in which a conflict starts from mere disagreement to an outright split or a war, there are seven stages. So, we call them how first interest, diverge, positions being hardened, communication breakdown and so on, till it becomes a full-fledged family or war in the public. All of them are avoidable. Every stage, the conflict is avoidable and at every stage it's a choice. So, you must also understand at what stage your family business is in. And therefore, how can you ensure that it doesn't go to the next stage? Is what is the final outcome of this session if we can understand that we, there is a way to stop it at this level itself. So, let us understand which level you are and try and stop it at that level and not let it proceed down. Intervention may be required for you to do something to stop it at that level itself. Let's see this seven stages in detail. What starts is very simple, interest diverge. It's not personal. Each one, each role creates a different kind of an interest. So, if I am in finance and I want to ensure that I cut costs and not spend in resources, somebody else is in a different role in marketing which the interest is to do more branding for the future. So, they want more money. So, the interest here can and diverge. There is no set agenda or set strategy which clearly decides what role can do what yet. So, in which case roles will create different interests. Ideally even rational interests also like the finance guy, racially wants to control money right. That's nothing irrational about it. This is still at this stage if they are discussed can be stopped. But if we let that, what can you say, breed and things are not resolved at this stage, it goes to the next level which is where position hardens. We have specific demand starting to win. Resource, decision starting to feel zero something either he gains or I gain. There is no way both of us together can help the company gain. That's not the thinking that starts forming. Everyone is thinking that I have to find a way for my demand to get met. The larger company picture or the larger family picture is not kept in mind. So, negotiation if it starts at this rate on positions, the family and the business will not win. Like we said earlier, we have to get it back to a stage where interests are kept in mind. We negotiate on an interest is fine and then find some path for around interest is still okay then negotiating on positions. What happens when positions breakdown is leads to the next stage where communication breaks down. And most time when communication breaks down it is not that they are fighting, it probably goes silent first. They don't talk to each other. Or if there is a fight that fight will lead to a longer period of silence at best. But silence is more often than more often prevalent than an actual fight which is still the same a communication has broken down. What happens is when all the first these stages are breached is when all the allain starts forming. So far it is between let us say two people in the business who are disagreeing on their interest or hardening their positions. But when allain starts forming it is a larger splinter. So, literally what I would call a splinter where you are involving allain's is some of which some of whom are family members, some of whom are outsiders across generation. So, the larger play being out there this is like then you are starting to figure out how you can outsmart the other allain's being formed and the same thing positions are now being taken by an allain's. Communication breaks down between these allain's. And now they will also take the help of non family managers and employees. How they can be used to further their own agenda each allain will want their agenda to be taken forward. And how a subordinate can do it, how an employee can do it and how what rewards can be given to employees who further their agenda. What incentives can be given. This kind of game start getting played and the non family members start realizing this that there is this group and there is this group. Quite possible that they start playing one again another also. Next stage is when advocates are called in then the it moves from the four rooms that we talked of all within the company to the legal rooms goes to the court rooms. So, then well it's about how each one of them really increase the conflict to further their own ends and they all see a good bargain all this the lawyers and the consultants and they will figure out a way to make money out of this. And well the lawyers lead it to a litigation and we have enough companies which went to litigation and there is value drop on the family finally. The entire family and all the allain's may not gain anything. So, these are the seven stages of the conflict. But if you see the fault lines along which these alliances form are usually five they they form along the family branch or the owner is forming one allain's in which all of them are shareholders and they want to have their say it can form by generation or gender. Which is almost like rebellion by one generation against the previous generation or something. Yeah, these are ways in which the fault lines can occur and some kind of dispute starts conflict starts and starts spreading. Towards the end let's see these are the kind of examples we know where conflict has been big. Money is a case where we know there are other kind of conflicts that happen. Interesting that you know the kind of conflict that happened between the signaniya family between father and son. In the unique case again advocates were called in the son finally one he threw out the father of the behalf having inherited he threw out the father. So, this is really the final step where advocates are called in. So, in this section what we have seen is how the seven stages are of a conflict in a family business while inevitable can be stopped at every stage with with some good intervention. And we have seen in detail how these stages pan out. The fault lines along which these alliances form and how at every stage we can prevent this conflict.",
