      "transcript_text": "Let us delve into the professionalisation of family businesses. What professionalisation looks like and what does the global evidence say? If we put two family firms or two firms side by side, one is run informally and one is run professionally, what differs between these two and how common are they really? So look at this particular table. If you look at a non-profitionally run firm, the decisions are typically made very informally, usually by the founder or by the family, whereas in a professionally run firm you have very clearly defined rules, rules, and a management team. And this also tends to play out even in the hiring and promotion decisions which typically tend to happen through family and personal networks, whereas in professional firms this would happen against stated criteria and you are also more open to non-family talent. In a non-profitionally run firm, the information is typically held very closely by the family, whereas in a professionalised family firm, information is shared with the managers who need it. In a professionalised family firm, succession is typically planned ahead, sometimes even years in advance and this is a very good process with very defined and explicit criteria which guide how the succession process will take place. What does the global evidence say? While yes, many of the family firms do have a board to large extent because of regulations and regulatory frameworks in each of these countries and the operator close to about 15% of these boards are independent or non-family members. You also see variations about whether they are likely to have a formal board or whether there are variations in the presence of next generation family members participation in the development of strategy. What these numbers also point to is that board is common, however an important caveat is about whether voice is adequately represented in these boards or not. When typically the family tends to hold and it is the family's informal structures or the family system that is governing and making sure about how the board is run, how decisions are being made. The voice tends to be lower because family systems are taking a higher priority over business systems. And therefore while board is common, real outside voice is not. If you look at what Indian evidence or when you compare that with global evidence, we can look at surveys for example from the PWC surveys and we do find that there are many areas that Indian family firms seem to be doing better but also areas for improvement. For example representation of cross industry board experience inclusion of non family members onto the board or also how the transition can take place. All of this needs to be decided at the board level. The real barrier by what is stated by these respondents themselves is that there seems to be a lot of resistance from the senior generations and it is not the absence of talent or rules themselves, which again reinforces the earlier point that I've made that it is not about having the board or populating the board but about ensuring that voice and diversity are adequately represented on the board. Why is professionalization becoming an urgent requirement and not optional? One, when a firm is run proficientally, it allows the firm to navigate the complexities of competition. Markets tend to move faster than any one single firm or a person can track by themselves and therefore having a professional structure can enable you to navigate that complexity. Second, in raising external capital or bringing external resource persons on board many times in fact more often than not external stakeholders beat banks, private equity or corporate partners, they expect the firm to be professionalized. And the penalized when they see that the firm is not being run proficientally and there's going to be associated cost to just ensuring that particular transition. Another important reason why professionalization is very important is to ensure successful transition across generations. More family owners need a process that they can all trust for fairness, for equity, for equality and decisions are not based on any single person's judgment or discretion. And lastly, professionalization is also required because of talent considerations. Does the family firm's structure permit enable and strengthen merit based advancements? Because these keep both the family and the outside talent within the firm and provide adequate scope, especially for women also to find their place and to grow and to that growth contribute to both the strengthening of the family as well as the family firm. It is important for us to keep in mind that professionalization is not about removing the family. It is about changing how decisions get made as both the firm and the family grow.",
