      "transcript_text": "When we talk of professionalisation of the family business, what exactly does it mean? Can we define it? Actually, what changes when a family firm refresionalises? And why does it usually require a formal top-down direction? And just intention is to be able to change the value of the family business. So, in this research, it was done in 1984, we discussed the transition to a professional management. From an entrepreneur management to a completely functional or a professional management. Let's see how these two differ. The entrepreneurial or the professional management is the same as the professional management. To differ, the entrepreneurial or the family business relies usually on one person's judgment, one person's relationships, his or her decisions, which are often centralised many times informal. But in a professional management, it relies on a specialised role and is a defined process on how decisions are taken. They are taken at the right level, they are taken at the right delegated level and not left to just the founder alone. The family business scales by the founder working harder and longer, because he's a single person who is driving the business and taking it to the next level. However, a professional management company would scale by adding capacity. We've been talking of capacity building. Systems are another important thing that are there in a professional business. That's how they scale by. And when do they break down? The family business breaks down and the business out grows the potential or the capability or the bandwidth of the single person in charge. The professional business on the other hand, breaks down and systems are broken and systems outrun that trust needed to use them. So this is basically the difference. This is genuine transition. It is not something a checklist that you can move from here to there, ticker few boxes and then you have achieved a transition from a family business to a professional management. This is serious stuff. Flambot's pyramid of organization development gives a structure for some of these framework for some of these. This is a six step framework where when you professionalize, you first look at the market side, then you see whether your products or services, when you see market means you see what your customer side is. What is the customer need? What is the size of the market? Then you move to the production services wherein is your product or service fulfilling the need of the customer? Are you able to ensure that the customers are satisfied? Are you able to ensure that their requirements are completely met? Next you go to resources which means the financial resources, human resources, factory setup, capital, all that comes under the resource bundle. What is generally advised is before you go to the upper layer which is now the step 4, 5, 6, you focus a lot more on first three steps because these are the basics actually first and second are the very, very basic where you are ensuring that you have a product or a service which meets a customer need. And that is the basis for any business. It is later I do go to step 4 where you have put up and put in operational systems, talk about your accounting systems and marketing systems and at step 5 you have the management systems and finally, focus a lot on the corporate culture. So this is how when you transition you focus some of these steps are followed. So in working definition for what we call as professionalization, we are calling it the deliberate replacement of informal person driven decisions with formal rule based criteria based ones across the firms markets resources operations management systems and culture. And carried out by people who meet the standard of a professional manager. This is not a switch, this is a complete process, this is something something like a journey. This does not mean that the family has no role to play going forward. It still means that a family can be a professional manager tomorrow too. This changes how decision gets made, does not change who makes the decisions as long as the person who is making the decision meets the criteria of a professional. This is a process like we said not an event not to be taken lightly. So why do family firms need a CEO or a talk management team when there is a person? There are 4 reasons. One is a decision bandwidth. How much can one person handle? How much can that person contribute to the scale of a business? Second is this is a succession readiness. It is a role that a successor can step into. The role is defined, the skills are defined, the need is defined, this is something that can be easily taken in as a succession process. This is external credibility. When you have a professional at the top, the other stakeholders, external stakeholders whether it is a bank, an investor, your suppliers, all of them understand that this is a firm that is therefore the long run will outly one person. And therefore this is a stable company to do business with. Lastly, specialized judgment. There are skills which require very very specialized knowledge. Nobody in finance or an operations leader. Rather than somebody who is a generalist who is a sort of jack of all trades and can handle everything.",
