      "transcript_text": "What are the benefits that accrue to a firm through professionalization? If professionalizing is hard and sometimes also resisted even actively, what exactly does a family firm stand to gain from doing it anyway? You can look at 9 different benefits that can broadly be categorized into 3 different aspects. First is the people and talent. The way that you select, train, the pay promotion systems to ensure that all of this runs on merit. And when that happens, you are providing adequate opportunity for women, outside talent, and also the family members within the family business system itself. This strengthens non-family commitment and contributes to strengthening the family system as well as the family business system. Second is the governance and discipline. Professionalizing a firm brings independent evaluation and incentive systems, and these are very important to consider, especially with the when you look at the value that they bring in considering which strategic options to pursue or not. Furthermore, there are institutional pressures for transparency and disclosure requirements, especially when the firm crosses particular size thresholds. And relatedly, the rules that bind the family and the non-family alike are stronger and better understood between all the members when there is a professionally run structure rather than an informal structure, and this strengthens the family firm. Third is the strategy and capital. Professionalization enables the handling of competitive complexity. It also brings the advantages of having diversity of viewpoints while considering growth options, as well as choosing between strategies and making trade-offs. This also makes it easier when the family firm needs to access resources, typically capital from external parties such as banks, private equity, and the broader capital markets. What we need to look at is that these 3 clusters that we see are not independent. People systems make governance credible, and credible governance unlocks the benefits of resource access from external markets. And therefore, these different benefits are very important reasons that should drive and motivate a family firm to professionalise the way that it is run. Let us now get into each of these in more depth. First, people and talent. Within this, we can talk about the benefits of high-commitment work systems. Having merit-based selection and performance linked to merit rather than to other considerations also makes the family firm stronger and rewards better performance, which has a payoff, especially in the longer run. Relatedly, professional structures also enable the development of in-house training and development capabilities. And therefore, there is a continuous pipeline of talent that is available, and these talents know that their work and their merit will be rewarded, which will drive high commitment. Second, a real path is available for merit-based talent, including women, because an informal structure tends to create artificial glass ceilings or mirrored ceilings, which we have discussed earlier. But when there's a professional structure and there are clearly laid out criteria and rules about how talent will be rewarded, everybody sees a path for ensuring that they will deliver their best and utilize their talent to strengthen the family firm. And third, it also strengthens the non-family commitment. Because the presence of merit-based pay and promotion exists, non-family members have a strong reason to stay within the family business. They know that they will be equally rewarded for similar levels of talent and performance outcomes in comparison to family members. And this strengthens the family firm by giving it access to a wider pool of talent beyond the family members alone. For example, look at Godrej Consumer Products. There is a non-family CEO, Vivek Gambhir, who leads the firm for nearly over a decade into 2020. Market capitalization rose from about 700 million dollars to about 9 billion dollars before another family member, Nisaba Godrej, succeeds him. So, you see that having a professional structure can enable these benefits to play out, and you can move between having a family CEO to a professional outside CEO to again a family CEO, and these transitions work to strengthen the firm in the presence of professional structures. Second, let us delve deeper into the governance and discipline, the rules that bind everybody within the firm. Why does independent evaluation and incentives matter? When you have the same appraisal targets and processes for pay and promotion, irrespective of whether the employee is a family member or not, everybody knows that it is the outcome and the process that matters. This serves as a very important disciplining mechanism, and that rule also binds the non-family staff as well as the family members. If a rule binds only the non-family staff, then it is a double standard. It is not a rule, right. Because the family consideration is what is coming in. But having a professional structures ensuring that everybody in the firm can see and understand that there is a fair process, a fair discipline approach towards how talent will be rewarded. Relatedly, there are also external pressures from institutions such as banks, regulators, large customers, as well as within the family, the next generation itself. Because many times the next generation also increasingly expect governance that reflect how their peer firms will behave. A tendency that is termed as isomorphic pressure, where firms tend to behave like each other over a period of time. And because the next generations, when they're going through their coursework, when they're studying, when they're interacting with their peers, they start encountering the advantages of professionally run firms. They also tend to expect the same from their own family firm. This push to professionalize from institutional pressures can arrive from very different sources, sometimes through a loan covenant. Sometimes the vendor expects that there is professional reporting and clear transparency of information flow, right. And all of these lead the family to change and professionalize. The third is strategy and capital. Competitive complexity is very difficult to handle when the firm is being run very informally. But having a professional structure enables you to organize the way of doing business through specialized functions, also with partnering with credible partners externally and other professional firms. Second, it also brings diversity of viewpoints into decision-making and consideration of growth options. Outside managers and directors bring experience and insights that the family members by themselves may not sufficiently have. And this strengthens the firm by enabling it to look beyond the confines of the thought processes and resource pools of the family alone. It also eases up access to external resources, especially capital, because good governance and clear professional governance lowers the perceived risk for external parties such as banks, private equity, and capital markets. Remember that opacity by itself is a risk, and all of the external audiences are in the business of pricing risk. And therefore, having a transparent professional structure enables you to address their  concerns about perceived risk associated with the family firm.",
