      "transcript_text": "Who is a professional manager and what precisely makes someone a professional manager, whether that person is a family member or not. Because remember that professional doesn't mean non-family. So, what makes somebody as a professional manager? If you look at 5 criteria which is being listed out, right. Firstly, are you looking at general principles or are you basing your decisions based on case specific intuition? Which means that are you drawing the reasons from transferable knowledge which can move across case situations, or are you relying on gut feeling alone? Next, should the client base their judgment on what you know, should they trust you or should they just obey you? And where does this trust come from? A professional manager is an expert who knows what is good for the client, right. And this expertise comes from professionalizing rather than leaving it to be a very informal structure. It is very difficult to establish expertise and convey that credibility in the presence of informality. Third, professional managers are basing themselves on helpful and objective relationships. There are and their decisions are not clouded by, let's say, personal or emotional ties, how they feel about somebody else. It's guided by very objective criteria and that enables and strengthens the firm. Fourth, is the person's status driven through achievement or through family ties? Remember that authority rests on what is accomplished or what was done. It is not based on what or who someone is related to. Because authority that draws from relational ties can evaporate over a period of time. But authority that is built on accomplishment tends to be longer lasting. And lastly, does the person belong to a voluntary professional community? Voluntary professional communities set standards, and being a member of such communities ensures that you are abiding by those standards, and these standards tend to get reinforced by the peers beyond the employer or beyond the focal family firm itself. A crucial clarification here that is required is that professional does not mean non-family. You can have multiple combinations. On one axis, let's look at whether the person is a family member or a non-family member. And second, whether they're behaving like a professional or not behaving like a professional. Let us look at a family non-professional. What would this person look like? typically they are placed in that position based on their birthright or ties to the family, and they tend to defer to more informal family judgment and intuition over solid data and evidence when taking decisions. Next, let's look at what a non-family, non-professional would look like. Here you're looking at somebody who is not a family member, but they have been placed in that particular position for reasons other than their track record. Here, the person is being rewarded for their loyalty to the family and therefore will very expectedly defer to what the family says or wants. Now, let's look at the professional. What would they look like? A non-family professional is brought in based on their track record or academic accomplishments. Typically, they are brought in to serve as experts because their judgment is respected and trusted because of the merits of the process that leads to that particular judgment. The track record, the academic accomplishments, their past performance, all of this strengthen that particular credibility. For example, consider the case of Vivek Gambhir, who was heading Godrej Consumer Products. What does a family plus a professional look like? Here you do have a family person at the helm of affairs, but this person is making their decision and reasoning based on data and evidence. They may have inherited the role, but they're earning that role over a period of time through their accomplishment repeatedly while they're discharging the duties and responsibilities associated with that particular role. They're not dependent upon the family tie to retain their position, and they're able to hold every employee to the same standard as their peers as well as their subordinates and even if required, the seniors. For example, look at how Harsh Mariwala was able to, as a family member, professionalize Marico and how the firm was run. It's a beautiful case where you see a family person professionalizing and ensuring that the firm will run as a professionally run firm and not as an informally run family firm.",
