      "transcript_text": "Let's look at professionalization inside the firm from the perspective of a 3-circle diagram from the  perspective of the 4-room model, and let's understand where the professional manager will sit inside the circle, inside those rooms. Why are we doing this? Because it's important to understand where the professional manager sits and therefore what is the authority that that the professional manager will get. Let's see where we can place the professional manager on this 3-circle diagram. In case the professional manager is also a family person and also has an owner, is also an owner, in which case he will he will sit in the center. So, this is a case of a family founder who's also CEO, who's also shares. However, we must understand whether he meets in all these 3 cases that we are going to discuss here and where he will sit is only if he is professional. And we have defined  who is a professional manager earlier. If he is a business only then he will only sit in the business circle. He's not part of family. He's not part of ownership. He's a non-family manager. Somebody who's external, who will come  and and join the business in a professional role. He doesn't have even ownership, even ESOPs kind of a thing. The third kind of position let's take is a family plus ownership only, which means he is a professional he is a family shareholder who does not work actively in the firm on a day-to-day basis. Can be  a passive participant  in the company or at best can be once in a while called in as an expert for something. He's not a full-time employee within the company. Let's see from the 4 room lens. In the family room, the main triggers are love, unity, development. In this kind of the room, a manager's authority where does it come from? Manager's authority cannot come into this room. In the owner room, the owner room relies on power and influence, right. These are the people who own shareholding. This does not come under a management kind of an authority. They are people who own. They will set the way forward. Third is the boardroom. The boardroom is the seat for wisdom and judgment. These are the people who  will hire and evaluate the people in the management room. They mean they are likely family members. There will be some 1 or 2 independent directors, but these are the people who take the decisions for the company, for the management also. Management room is where we find competence and execution. These are the people who are hired from outside, brought in to fulfill certain roles basis their skill set. And this is where the criteria we have to see whether the person coming has the meets the criteria for professional and therefore this is the room where the professional will actually sit within the 4 rooms And let's understand the common failure point where you don't match authority to competence and and then how do you fix it? Suppose in the management room if the authority is granted  on to family or the owner room standing alone, it's a very difficult one for the management room to handle. How does it happen? Because the socioeconomic wealth and the we talk spoke about the type 2 agency incentive, both favor it, and as a result of it,  this is a failure that can happen. How do you fix it? You have to grant authority to whoever builds it. Based on the  5 criteria we discussed in the earlier section, we have to understand that whoever is a professional has to have the relevant authority. And once the authority is granted to the management room is when this will really work. Otherwise, if you don't if the competence lies in one room and the authority lies in another room these problems are going to happen.",
