      "transcript_text": "What constitutes the process of professionalization? Given that a family firm wants to professionalize, what actually determines how hard, fast, or disruptive that process will be? Let us look at 6 variables which shape how professionalization unfolds. First is the environment. Here you need to consider factors such as the competitive context, regulatory pressures, as well as the capital market complexity and the pace of the external environment. Second, family characteristics. What is the size of the family? How many branches are involved? Which generational stage is the family firm in? And all of these play an important role in shaping how professionalization unfolds. Third are the characteristics of the business itself. For example, the size and the complexity within the organization. Next is the managerial approach. Are you being very entrepreneurial about all of this, or do you already have, for example, a functional leadership style already in place? Fifth is the performance outcomes. How the current performance plays out, what it looks like, its current results are they doing better, worse compared to peers' historical performance? All of this shape both the urgency as well as the appetite for change. And last are the stakeholder effects. Some important stakeholders include the banks, the customers, employees, as well as the family expectations. Let us look at the family characteristics in depth. First here is the controlling owner. Who owns? Is it one person, a couple, siblings? Is it multiple different family members? All of these affect the professionalization process. Because the closer it is to a personal choice, then you don't need other owners to agree to ensuring that a firm will be able to professionalize. Second is a sibling partnership, and the third is a cousin consortium. The more the number of people are as co-owners and the more distant they are within the family tree, it only complicates the decisions in professionalizing a family firm. Because you need to get multiple people on board with the decision, and they all need to agree on a professional structure, and this invariably does lead to certain costs that different members need to absorb as owners, some may need to take a back step. Not everybody's egos may be satisfied. Not everybody's financial considerations or non-financial considerations may be satisfied. And therefore, the more the loosely held ownership, the harder it is to professionalize. Next, the environment, business, and managerial approaches together. A hard combination between all these 3 is when you have high complexity, weak building blocks from the family's business characteristics, and if the firm was already running on an entrepreneurial style. When you combine all these 3, it makes it harder to professionalize the firm because you're trying to address multiple issues simultaneously. Next, let us look at the performance outcomes as well as the stakeholder effects, which is like a  feedback loop in this entire process. Performance outcomes are a definite necessary feedback loop to consider in any decision. In professionalization, the performance outcomes in comparison to peers, in comparison to the firm's own history, shape how urgently and is the family firm willing to go through the entire risk of professionalizing. Stakeholders are carefully observing the performance and the governance together, and they will adjust their expectations accordingly. And these expectations become the next round's pressures on professionalization.",
