      "transcript_text": "Why is it often argued that a family business should be more like a non-family business? And if professionalizing means that, you know, you have to behave, you have to act more like a well-run non-family company. The research shows that actually Stewart and Hitt in their research argue that the framing is a mistake. What Stewart and A. Hitt actually ask is that family business cannot claim to become more professional or that they should not try. What they should actually claim is that professionalizing is actually not something which is a straight line. It's not something which is something that can be transferred like any other company or replicated like any other company. It's very, very dependent on the context. It's very dependent on it's actually multimodal, as we'll call it. And it's not one template that will satisfy everybody. What they argue is that they should have a blend of an agency theory and socioeconomic wealth theory, and use these both to come to their model, their template of professionalization. Professionalization lacks a single meaning, and if you want to treat it like one template for every family business, it would be a mistake. And that's the argument that Stewart and Hitt actually make. These 2 domains that we talked about kinship and commerce, and how they apply their logic and what is the strength of each of those domains. Let's understand where they fail or what is the limit in which they run into. The kinship domain the logic is more of obligation, trust emotion, reciprocity, relationships. This is what something which is there on the family side. Kinship, what I refer to is on more the family side. What are their strengths? Their strength is they can be fast. They're very informal, patient and long capital. They build something for the really long term, and this is a feature that you'll be seeing across this course that we'll be talking about. There they reach their limits. Can they alone run a competitive business? They  need the help of other stakeholders to run a cooperative business. That can be management, that can be a very, very professional board. They need some professionals in the company. Family alone is neither large enough  nor will have all the skills to run a professional company, to run a large company. Commercial domain, the logic is very different. It has formal contracts. You  go with the what the market wants. You understand customer needs. Credentials are important. Discipline, market discipline is equally important. They are scalable. They are impersonal. They go with economic logic a lot more. And there's something that is understood well by outsiders. But on their own, they also reach their limit. They can't generate the family firm's own advantage that, that will get and we spoke about strengths of the family business. That is not something that the professional alone can generate. So actually, where opportunity lies for a professional is at the interface of these 2 domains. Exactly where a professional will really be able to dominate is where there is the trust, there is  the speed, there is the patient capital  of the kinship and how it can survive the commercial domains, processes, scalability and something  where there is lot more legible being to outsiders  and all that matters  where they too can work together is excatly what we wanted. Let's talk about this  aspect of principal-to-principal conflict, where many times is where a breakdown occurs. What do you mean by a principal-to-principal conflict? Let's understand  we have spoken at length about the principal and the agent conflict. But what if in a  family firm there are 2 principals? Let us take there are 2 brothers who equally own the family business. Both are principals. They can hire others who can be called agents, but these 2 are still principals, right. In that case, let's  see the kind of conflict that could happen. A Type 1 conflict is the classical conflict where there is a conflict between the principal and  the agent. Well, that's how non-family governance addresses it. Yes,  it was built for this. In a Type 2 case, where the family principal and their own entrenchment and altruism we spoke about where because you can't remove somebody and they feel entrenched out there and their skills don't matter, just being in the family matters. So, can the non-family governance, can it address it? To some extent, not fully, partially at best. But let's take a, a principal-to-principal kind of a conflict. In which case these are active family members and others who are maybe  passive family owners or even another active family member fighting on the direction of the business, on the strategy for the business. This is not something a professional management can ever solve. This is this is a completely different ballgame. What will happen is that the employees will then take sides one of each principal, and  it's a free-for-all. It's  not something that will end neatly unless the principals can come together. So in summary, what I want to ask is: an advice like to a family business saying behave more like a non-family business, is that the right decision? Is that the right advice to be given? Let's see from four different angles whether this is right. Let's talk of socioeconomic wealth. The non-family kind of a company will we never be able to recognize the socioeconomic wealth and the non-financial goals that a family business will have. Can a non-family business management, can it resolve a principal-to-principal conflict? This is an agency kind of a problem which non-family governance was never designed to solve. They can solve it when it's an agency when it's a principal-to-agent conflict can be solved. There are mechanism. A principal-to-principal conflict is beyond them. If there is a tacit kin-based judgment that is there within the family, we have seen that these kind of family decisions are fast and they can  outperform formal routines, which is in the case of a  non-family business, and they can take some very good decisions under uncertainty. So, the fourth criteria is about the domain interface. So, if you have a situation where professional advice can be given to a family firm to clean up and get their act together, that is the best scenario. Where you have someone, someone an advisor who can help clean up the family business is a real win. So in summary, it is not that every family business needs to go professional. Some of these criteria that we just discussed are equally important, and there's no one template for also when a family business has to go professional.",
