      "transcript_text": "In the entire professionalisation journey, Vivek a very interesting question to consider is how much control should the family retain? Because when we've been speaking about professionalisation and even otherwise, a general thought that crops up is professionalisation means outsiders. Although we have made the point that professionalisation includes how family members themselves behave professionally, right. But still, you know, there is the element of control that the family needs to or should it and to what extent maintain over the firm. Sai, when we talk about control, we must clearly define that what is the kind of control we're talking about here when we are moving the professionalisation space. Yes. We have talked about operational control, strategic control, and and ownership control. In professionalisation, ownership control is ruled out. That's not for discussion out here. What usually happens is is some amount of strategic control is given to a professional, insider or outsider, and a lot of management control is actually what is given first. Yes To either an outsider or another family member first. So, there is evidence which is both ways. Evidence when the family cedes a lot of control to an, a professional and evidence on what happens when the family cedes very less control to a professional. We have to examine both in this case. And when we are talking about control, we are also talking about balancing control with accountability, right. And especially that becomes important at both these two levels. One is at the operational control, the day-to-day running of the business. Who is accountable and to whom? And what is driving that accountability? Is it family ties or is it the business logics? Because we keep emphasizing this multiple times, and I think that's extremely important to, again, emphasize over here that a family business is a mix of the 2 systems, right. Right. And therefore, it does matter about the extent of control and for what it is coming and why, where and for what reason the accountability is being held. What evidence actually shows, Sai, is that with increasing professionalisation, the value of the firm is actually increasing. Performance increases to some extent, but after a certain point of time when greater control is given it the, the curve tends to flatten a bit, and this is really what research is showing. So, to some extent, professionalisation, as long as it's combined with accountability, will continue to increase the value of the firm. And here we are also talking about the twin goals of a family business, right. The accountability is not only in terms of the financial wealth. We're also talking about accountability for the family's non-financial wealth goals as well. What facilitates professionalisation within a family firm? Are there certain factors that we would want to specifically consider? I would say the first factor is the willingness factor. The family may have enough ability inside the term. Again, it's a willingness ability matrix out here. Too, for the firm, not to the individual in this case. But while the firm may have the talent inside, which represents the ability side of it, the willingness is the question that needs to be addressed, which is a huge facilitator. Correct. If the family understands that professional talent is needed from outside or some specific skills which is needed for professionalisation is needed, and they are able to bring in that kind of professionalism, there can be systems, processes can they can bring all of this. That is a clear facilitator for professionalisation. And another important one to note is the pace of professionalisation itself, because professionalisation in itself is a very interesting journey, and you have to map that onto where exactly or what stage the family business is in both in terms of the business stage, as well as in terms of the family. Is it in the founder generation? Which generation is running? What are the other requirements that are going on at that particular point of time? So, it's very important to also pace it out. Also, when you  talk of professionalisation, we have to understand that there is competence now. It has to be matched with authority. In which case, what decisions can be taken by which institution within the company. Or whether it's which room who takes. We talked about the four-room model. So, which room can take what kind of decisions, given the competence that now exists in the business will actually facilitate. If the decisions are at cross-purposes within rooms or somebody overrides the decisions that needs to be taken in the other room, this will not facilitate professionalisation. And I think another very important aspect which we are not covering right now, but we need to get into more detail is the governance element. True. Because the entire professionalisation right from the intent, the process, and the subsequent strengthening of professionalisation within a family firm, in many ways depends upon the strength of governance, and that is a very important thing. So, what we have seen is what can facilitate professionalisation and how is the importance of family control. On professionalisation, and both, I think are very important factors in the, the family's journey towards professionalisation. Very true.",
