      "transcript_text": "In this part, let us understand innovation and the family business. Does the family business logic help or hinder innovation? Or what facilitates innovation in a family business? Innovation seems to be sometimes a paradox that decides a family business survival. The very things that family business is constructed around, right. Concentrated control or very long and patient capital and protecting what already exists. Things like these are almost like an opposite of what innovation seems to require. So logically, family firms should struggle to innovate. Do they? Still, we find that not many struggle. Why is that? A family firm that cannot renew its products processes, or business model leaves the next generation less reason to be part of it. If the next generation sees that this business is not coping up with the economy of today, with the times of today, with the technology of today, therefore, its products or process or goods, services are not suited for today's generation, then they may not want to be part of that business. They'll say, \"Okay, fair enough. You run it the way you want. I'll go and look outside.\" But for a family founder, for the patriarch, transition across generation is an important thing, and what they want to do is to ensure that the business is passed on to the next generation. So, they have to make it suitable such that the next generation is ready to in to inherit it. So in many ways, while we are seeing innovation and succession  are 2 separate topics, they may actually be not. Family firms have to innovate sometimes to ensure succession. So, if you see the tension that is there between what the tendencies of the family firm are and what really innovation requires, let's see some of those tension  that happens. A family firm is very concentrated in its decisions, in its rights. But innovation will require a willingness to get in some outside expertise, because for innovation to understand what the market requires today may not be that skills may not be available within the company, which has been in one format for quite a long time. What a family firm tendency is to have a multi-decade horizon. Patient capital we talked about. But innovation is not like that, right. Innovation is one, it is it is willing to take risk, has a very quick turnaround, willingness to accept failure, tolerance, and move on to the next state and see  and try, keep trying. Failure is something that is very, very not looked upon very favorably by  a standard family business because they are worried if they fail, what will, will the business last the next generation? While innovation requires just the opposite. Say, from the family tendency, socio-emotional wealth or continuity, identity is important. While  in a innovative firm, they should be willing to even cannibalize the product that the firm has been known for because they are willing to take risks, build something that provides a better experience, a service, something to the customer, even if it means some, some existing product dies because of that. Because  they are ready for the future. They're not living in the past and get annihilated completely. What the family business brings is a lot of knowledge in one business. But what an innovator will require  is openness knowledge and the models that the outside world has and how they can capitalize on it and build a better product, a better service,  and take this forward. So in many ways, these are these 4 tensions that exist between the family business the way it's run and what an innovator require. But why is this so important now for family business? There are 4 reasons why today family businesses must think about innovation or run the risk of sometimes getting left behind. The first one is technology pace. There are very less room now to define hard innovation choices than what earlier generations had. The less time also in hand. The pace at which technology is changing can leave a family firm behind by a long way, and as a result, one of their stated objectives, the next generation to come and take over, there'll be nothing left for the com- next generation to come and take over. Second thing is the multi-generation overlap. When you have 2 or 3 generations active in the same time in, in a family business, what happens is each of those generation view come with a different view of risk. They come with a different tolerance for risk also. So, some the next generation, they'll want to they are more adaptive to technology perhaps. They want to experiment something. They want to take it forward in a different way. So, this is an important thing that the family has to understand, saying every generation will not come with the same set of views like what was handed over. The next generation has grown up in a different way  with different kind of use cases, different technology, et cetera. The third point is global competition. There was a time when the family business was started, the competition was largely inside the country or inside their area. They were trading  in a limited area or working and providing those goods and services in a limited area. Today, the world has gone global. Competition is not even visible. It can come from e-commerce, can come from Q-commerce. So, the challenges can come from any quarter today, and the challenges are well-funded. Those,  the competitors are not coming from patient capital. They are coming from  very risky capital and people      well, what can you say? Well, understanding of risk and well, they come with well-funded, well-networked and they can really throw us out of gear. The last point is talent expectations. Employees are increasingly expecting the company to invest in their future as well. Invest in them and groom them and also skill them and make them ready for the future.",
