      "transcript_text": "Before applying innovation or trying to understand innovation in family firms, let us look at and try to understand four very popular ideas on innovation. And this vocabulary can help us as we go further in trying to understand innovation and family firms. First is the concept of creative destruction. This was given by Schumpeter. What creative destruction says is that typically what happens is industries go through a continual process such that new ways of organizing, new ways of doing things continuously keep emerging and that emergence destroys the existing way of doing things. And a firm's survival, this entire process, you know, new way of doing things emerge and old ways of doing things die out, this process is called as creative destruction. In this context of creative destruction, a firm's survival depends on being willing to destroy its old base or old ways of doing things first because it needs to create value based on the new order. Second is the distinction between incremental versus radical innovation. Incremental innovation is about improving what already exists by a few degrees. In contrast, radical innovation is about creating something qualitatively new. The third understanding that we will find helpful is to look at exploration versus exploitation. These are two very important skills that any good business or any good professional also needs to carry with them: exploration and exploitation. Exploration is about searching for new possibilities. In contrast, exploitation is about refining and executing the existing ones and doing it better. So firms need to do both. They need to keep searching constantly for new ways of doing things, as well as doing what they're currently doing in a better manner. And the last one is disruptive innovation. A sustaining innovation is where you are serving your existing markets, your existing customers whom you are already serving, you are trying to innovate for them. But disruptive innovation, typically it comes from Clayton Christensen and colleagues and it is very specific on what it does. You know, so when firms, the established big players in the industry, they start finding their place in the market and target their customers. Typically, what happens is there tend to be overlooked customers or overlooked segments. A disruptive innovator enters by creating something and serving those unaddressed segments of the market. And once they stabilize and build in those markets, they use that as an inroad into the mainstream market over a period of time. And that disruptive innovation can change and alter the way of doing things and the industry structure also. All of these are important for us to understand also in terms of some of the concepts that we have been discussing. For example the tensions within the family business systems or the generational patterns or external environment, right. These concepts, while they are not developed specifically for family firms, they're also applicable for family firms.",
