      "transcript_text": "Let us examine family firms and innovation from an ability and willingness perspective. We've discussed how family firms spend less but often end up getting more. Let's try to understand why, and by separating these two questions of ability and willingness also what happens when these are blurred and what happens when they both look two important questions, right. Ability and willingness. What do each of these ask? First is the ability. Can this firm marshal the required resources to drive innovation? What typically drives ability? You need access to resources, including capital. You need decision-making discretion. You need freedom from the short-term pressures of external investors. And all of this can give you an advantage and drive the ability question: Are you able to drive innovation? Second is the willingness. Does the firm's leadership want to take the risk? Here we're talking about issues about the control identity of the family, the continuity and other aspects of the socio-emotional or the non-financial wealth of the family. That typically tend to drive the willingness of the family firm in driving innovation. What is important for us to also keep in mind is that the variation between family firms lies actually in the mismatches between these two and not a direct combination of both being high or both being low. Why does this matter? Think about why the ability of family firms to innovate is often higher. Because of concentrated ownership and control, family firms have the advantages of patient capital, and this enables them to make multi-year commitments. When patient capital allows you to make multi-year commitments, you are in a position to take bets which have a payoff much further down the road and therefore invest in innovation, which will tend to pay off much longer time frames or time horizons. In contrast, look at when you have a lot of external investors or you're a professionally run firm with every year the AGM, the board pressures, investor pressures, market pressures. You may not, although you may have the required money, you may not be able to do it because you don't have the patience required to drive such innovation. Willingness is often inconstant. Why? Because the same control creates the socio-emotional wealth stakes which can block action. The family firm is not only looking at financial outcomes from the innovation that it drives but is also worried about other aspects, you know, the prestige, the identity, the commitments. We have always been known to be this kind of a firm. Why should we innovate and exte- enter into new markets? Or I've always been employing these 100 people and supporting their families. Why should I invest in digitalization or automation? The willingness may often come from the non-financial wealth dimensions of the family business, and this can block the drive for innovation. As a combination of both ability, which is typically higher for family firms, and willingness, which is very inconstant, what happens is that we tend to see lot greater heterogeneity across family firms in comparison to non-family firms as a group. Right. The same advantage that gives family firms the capacity to innovate is the same feature that can also make them choose not to innovate. For example, consider the carbonated soft drinks industry. For a very long time, it was a classic case that has been used in MBA classrooms across the world that the carbonated soft drinks has a space only for two, the Coca-Cola and the Pepsi, and doing trying to break that duopoly will require a lot of resources and a lot of time. And therefore, it has always explained a way that there will not be a presence of a third major player. You might have small players, you might have local players, you might have, and they might capture their market or do well in their market, but it's very difficult to beat this particular duopoly. But look at what Reliance was able to do with Campa Cola, right. Because of the family ownership and control, you are able to exercise the advantage of having patient capital and that ability to make multi-year commitments and to back your bet over a longer period of time with deeper resources. This advantage suddenly challenges many of the conventional wisdoms that we have heard about what is required to be broken cannot, in reality, play out because markets want short-term results, market wants quick results, market cannot wait for so long. You need to show immediate results in order to raise funding. Whereas that doesn't work with family firms. They have greater ability. On the flip side is the willingness, right. And therefore, it is important for us to consider both of this, ability and the willingness. If you look at that as a two-by-two matrix, on one side ability and on the other willingness, you have four different combinations. Let us look at the first quadrant, low ability and low willingness. Here, the family firm may not have the required resources to invest in innovation, may not have the right mindsets possibly, may not have the required financial capital possibly, may not have the network capital possibly, right. So the ability itself is low. And add it to the complexity, low willingness. We don't want to change the way that we are doing it. This is the way that my grandfather did it, my great-grandfather did it, my father did it, and therefore we will do it. And if the business survives, even our children will do it. Survival risk is the greatest in this particular quadrant. Because business environment keeps changing. Innovation sometimes becomes a necessity and not a strategic choice. Second, let us look at high ability. Next, let us look at low ability but high willingness. The firm may want to innovate. The next generation possibly wants to do innovation, wants to find a new way of doing things, delivering or creating new value for their customers or new ways of organizing the business. However, they might have genuine resource constraints or capability or capacity constraints, and therefore they need to be able to look beyond and address that particular challenge of ability. The third quadrant, let us look at high ability but low willingness. The firm may have the required resources, let it be money, let it be networks, let it be technology, let it be customer access, supplier access. The firm may have the required capacity. However, they might be held back by perceiving too high a risk to the socio-emotional wealth. We have always been known to be this type of a company. Our identity is linked to this way of doing things. Why should we change now? Or the younger generation wants to innovate but the senior generation is not willing to let go of the reins of the firm, and they have another 5 or 10 years before that shift is going to happen, by which time it becomes too late. You had the ability, but low willingness. And the fourth is the strongest one. You have both high ability as well as high willingness. You have the required resources, capital, networks, capacity, managerial talent, and the discretion to act. The willingness is where the discretion comes in and the appetite to use them. Here, the family business has an opportunity to be an innovation leader, right. You have the willingness and the ability to back that particular willingness. Family firms can fall into any of these four quadrants. However, they are not limited to being or they are not forced to stay in that particular quadrant for long. They need to be able to manage innovation in any or multiple of these quadrants, and they may even move across these quadrants as circumstances change, either with financial or with their non-financial wealth.",
