      "transcript_text": "How is innovation suppressed in family firms? And does family control can actually, can it suppress innovation that is possible  in a firm? What we will try and understand the reasons why suppression actually happens. We'll explain the suppression side in little more detail, and we'll also understand how the push towards more control also plays a role in some of these. So, let's understand it from the socio-emotional wealth angle first. We spoke about this olive mill example, where a family firm had a chance to join an olive mill cooperative, which could have kept them in better standing over time, but they declined the membership. Now, let's look at this from from the angle of the olive mill trade. Also look at it how innovative decision-making would have looked at it. Let's take the choice. The choice in the olive mill trade was to join a risk pooling cooperative. In which case you the risk is not something you alone will bear. It's a pool that will bear and it'll hold you in better stead. What would mean in terms of an innovation analog is to fund a risky R&D project. Since there is risk involved, but would have risk could have petered down over the long term but it is still  a risk that you are not willing to look at. What it offered was a lower risk of failure  for yourself if you had joined the cooperative. And it would have what would have given you is actually a much better expected long run, long-term return. But you declined it. Why? Because you would have to shed some control of it. It would have gotten you some certainty of performance, but it would have meant that not all decisions could be taken by you. You would lose some control, even though it brought a risk, a certain amount of risk. So, your aversion towards losing control and your identity overruled your aversion over some amount of money investment in R&D. It's not aversion over money as much because you are not worried so much about money today. Control overrode that. So that is one reason why innovation fails. We also talked about R&D spends in the same way. If there is an above aspiration pattern when things are fine usually we'll be more cautious. See, the default form for most family firms is they are not near a survival threshold. Behavior changes that if you have to if you are at a survival threshold, which means fundamentally you are not able to the question is whether you will be able to pass on your business to the next generation. When that question comes, everything is you know, thrown  at the problem because then the longevity is very, very important  for the founder then the questions are very, very different. What it means is that lower R&D spend than what a non-family firm would spend at the same time. Let's also look at it from the entrenchment  and the legacy kind of an angle or a type 2 agency applied to innovation. In case of a, let's say, a parental altruism and what happens in a,  in this case in an innovation specific version. In a parental altruism kind of a scenario, there's an underperforming family-led project, let us say. It is not evaluated the way it should be. Because the family member is never subject to the same standards as an outsider. The second thing is entrenchment. Somebody whose pet project is something that, that can be started off with whether even if it is not performing and somebody else is there who is performing can never be promoted above you. Can never, you can never be evaluated for a replacement to take over your role because you're a family member. As a result, the project in whatever state it is, will continue to be in that state. Innovation will never happen. The last issue is one of self-control. Many times the person who's championing the project is also the same person who's to decide whether to continue investing in it, whether to kill it or move on from there. And because sometimes the identity of the person is sometimes linked to that project, saying, \"I started this, and I am experimenting with this,\" he or she would hate to kill that or he or she will never bring it to a stage where, you know, the name or can be delinked from that person.",
