      "transcript_text": "In an earlier section, we talked about how family control can actually suppress R&D spend. Actually, the same set of features under very different conditions has the potential to do the opposite, actually enable innovation. We'll look at how that can happen. Well, let's look at some of those. The first condition is patient capital. Well, this is actually very powerful because the family business wants to stay in the business for a really long time and invest such that the business can be transferred from generation to generation. So, if there were to be a project  which has a longer payoff, a decade-long payoff, in which case family business will probably be happy to do something like that because it pays off in the long run. It's fine. We have the patient capital. But if it were  a venture capital kind of a thing which wants a quick turnaround something that is more risky, you will figure out faster and set it on that path, but the results will be known very early and may go either way. Patient capital may not be so much  a bet there, but something which is long-term can enable the longer tenure of the business. Patient capital will help. Similarly, the human capital involved, which has very deep and good knowledge of the business, of the industry can help because there can be some kind of investment in R&D done using these kind of human resources and you'll probably spend less, be far more efficient with that. Family business has social capital, which means, for example, suppose you are going to innovate something, let us say. You have a wide network where this new product can be tested. Product, service, feature, whatever can be tested because it is coming from a position of trust, you can give it to trusted people outside your ecosystem in your larger stakeholders. They'll give you very honest feedback on how this is or what is needed. You can ascertain needs much better from a very, very good tight kind of a network that you'll have. Social capital is very important. That's something that family businesses have. Imagine a non-family business, you'll have to literally go and test it in the open market. And take feedback from customers like that and figure out whether this is right. And you may not be even sure whether the feedback is right, wrong, genuine. In this case, you'll get trusted feedback. Also survivability capital. In a sense that what the this kind of a capital can   provide is is kind of a buffer through any kind of an R&D or a project's uncertain tenure, right. As it goes through ups and downs there will be hard stretches that it has to cross, and this kind of  a buffer is well, well important to ensure the projects reaches a certain milestone. Now this kind of patience, again, is there in a family business. So if used well, patient capital can be a big strength and can enable innovation rather than the other way around. Second thing is long-term orientation and the importance that is given to continuity for the family business. So, there  are 3 things we're talking of. We're talking of futurity, continuity, and perseverance that goes along with this. So futurity, we basically here we're talking of an extended horizon into which ordinary decision has been taken, and we don't like we say, we don't go QSKT. This kind of a thing gives importance to things that will change over a longer period of time and invest today and not wait for immediate results and have a very long-term horizon but people are willing to invest in innovation that can see results over a much longer period of time. So suppose, let us say the ideal time for some project to for some product to come and become mainstream is 7, 8 years away. Many non-family businesses will not invest today. We'll invest closer to horizon, is what they will say. Family business will say, probably say that, \"Okay, I have it, let me invest now. Start  small, keep building. It'll come, at that time it will be very useful for us.\" Continuity is another thing in terms of how the longstanding values  of a family business. It's actually  a very important strategy. It's not just a, it's not a bet. Values when they carry over a long period of time and comes with that orientation, it can really ensure that the thought process of innovation, investment, future patient capital, all these  are also as part of values carried down. So, the investment in R&D continues actually. Lastly, in terms of perseverance, the family business knows that come what may, some of these small steps that they are taking towards innovation must sail through, must continue, even though there are business setbacks, even though there can be some temporary setbacks, some economic recession maybe the that the business industry is going through. But some of these innovation are continued as a result of  the thought process of the family business, which is not so much towards a  near-term payoff, but something which has a long-term payoff, and as a result, the projects are kept alive. Third factor we spoke about is tacit knowledge that the team has. This is more an efficiency thing in a sense that the same amount of R&D spend can give a lot more, can last a lot more longer as a result of the knowledge that is existing in the team, which therefore assemble the right kind of team for that R&D project that  we they know who is one who has this kind of a mindset. Get the right kind of capital and which can be deployed in a much more efficient way. How resources can be combined into one capability they have pulled in from various parts because knowledge they know exists in various parts of the organization. And they will leverage where social capital is required in terms of the customer, suppliers, so that some of these resources can be leveraged from there often at a far lower cost than what a open market  would probably get, and put together this R&D kind of a project. So, these the social capital, the knowledge that exists within the team can actually enable innovation.",
