      "transcript_text": "When we look at the multiple stories out there, one thing that definitely stands out is whether exploration and exploitation within a family business or the path that it seeks to choose, to what extent is it explained by generational differences? You must first explain exploration and exploitation Sai. Yes. Exploration is more about seeking innovative paths outside the, the current firm today. While exploitation is finding something within the firm itself on the, and how we can, within the firm's locus today, to see how we can get innovation from the existing firm itself. So we are what we are asking is which of these is something that family firms are more used to, and what would they first turn to? Correct. I think in most conservative family businesses exploitation is something that comes very naturally to them because they are more efficiency-seeking. They're more how can the focus on cost is very high. So exploitation comes very, very naturally. But exploration is something that is more sometimes forced on them. They see the need for that kind of a change is more when exploitation actually happens. And this is therefore something that changes with generations. Plus you can also add to this the fact that family businesses are seeking some bit of stability of income for the family, right. So which is kind of putting it at risk if you're continually being very explorative. But exploitative, you're trying to maximize the outcome from the existing business and therefore that also is what could explain this balance between exploration and exploitation. But then there's this other very interesting thing that we tend to often see the younger siblings being more explorative. True. But we also must understand that the family is not, the family and the business are not in isolation. By the time the second generation, the sibling comes or the cousin consortium comes the family probably over a period of a generation has probably done the exploitation part quite a bit. They are operating at whatever efficiency they can. To now innovate further, exploration is important. Secondly, the environment also has changed over time. Over the next thirty years, things have changed. We need to adapt, and the family which is focused on, on exploitation may not have quite adapted to the environment that is changed over time. And, and therefore, exploration becomes important when you touch the second generation. Yes, yes. And also with the younger sibling, right. The elder sibling is typically the one who is kind of expected to move in to running the business which kind of gives more freedom and flexibility to the younger sibling to pursue different passions. And partly also explained by the fact that ownership or even strategic control in many cases naturally moves to the elder sibling, leaving the younger siblings to figure out their place. And it's not very uncommon especially in some of the European context and the other with the Western Hemisphere of the world, if I could so call it, where a lot of family businesses are family incubators, where they look to create a space within the entrepreneurial family galaxy for the newer firms or the newer exploration opportunities to find place within. While we are seeing exploration as a change that happens over generations, we must also see another aspect where this happens due to increased professionalization in the firm. So when professionals come into the, come into the firm, when outside talent come into the firm, they bring fresh ideas. And the family also realizes that these are important for the company to keep up with the market. That's one more reason. Just generation and professionalization are reasons how exploration is actually furthered in the family firm. And lastly even the formalization of strategic planning itself as a routine also strengthens exploration. Because, you know, in, in many ways, you're talking about formalizing what you're going to do. You're formalizing the search process. You're formalizing the multigenerational involvement within the family business. And therefore, you're kind of systematizing how the entire multigenerational effect, the professionalization, how do all of these really fire both these engines? You need the firm to be ambidextrous, do both exploration and exploitation, right. I think you're talking of one important aspect here. What the family and the business need to be is to have that necessary willingness for this kind of a change. Once you have a willingness, then they'll explore ideas within, which is the more exploitative version, and also outside. Outside by either because of professionals, either insiders or outsiders, fresh ideas, fresh talent. As long as willingness is there this kind of a change can happen. And therefore, when you put all of these elements together, we are kind of talking about can family businesses institutionalize ambidexterity, both exploration and exploitation? And the role of multi-generational involvement if there are multiple family branches or cousin consortium stage, different family members' involvement, professionalization, Professionalization and its effect, formal strategic planning, kind of bringing all of this together in trying to explain how, Innovation can actually take place in in a family business.",
