          "model_answer": "Family businesses naturally default toward exploitation because they are efficiency-seeking and focus heavily on cost minimization, which maximizes outcomes from the existing business. This comes very naturally to conservative family firms. However, exploration is often forced upon them. Key generational factors include: (1) By the second generation, the family has likely maximized efficiency from the existing business through exploitation, making exploration important for innovation; (2) The external environment has changed over time (over the next thirty years), requiring adaptation that an exploitation-focused family may not have achieved; (3) Younger siblings are often more explorative than the elder sibling, as the elder is expected to run the business, giving the younger freedom to pursue different passions. Additionally, ownership and strategic control naturally move to the elder sibling, leaving younger siblings to figure out their place. In the European and Western context, family businesses often act as family incubators, creating space for newer firms or exploration opportunities. The family also realizes the importance of exploration when outside professionals enter the firm, bringing fresh ideas needed to keep up with the market. Formalization of strategic planning systematizes how the multigenerational effect and professionalization fire both the exploration and exploitation engines. Ultimately, the family and business need a willingness for this kind of change to institutionalize ambidexterity.\n\nReal-world examples from the lecture: Infosys illustrates why owners cannot run daily operations and must delegate authority to professional managers (illustrating the need for professionalization). Gautam Adani and Mukesh Ambani illustrate net worth as a stock variable versus profit as a flow variable. Tyco International's CEO spending $2M on his wife's birthday party and billing $1M to the company illustrates direct agency costs, which relates to the friction between exploitation and exploration.\n\nThe core thesis is that family businesses must balance their natural inclination toward exploitation with the forced need for exploration to survive across generations.",
