      "transcript_text": "What facilitates innovation in a family firm, right. Are there certain things that can help create a more facilitative environment for innovation to thrive within a family firm? Let us look at firstly the professionalization. Can professionalization help in addressing the ability, willingness, and what aspects can it help? For example, think about the type two agency problem where you're looking at the family as a controlling shareholder. You might have minority external shareholders but also what happens is because of this, that you are being more kind, more protective of other family members, maybe the next generation, and protecting them. Can professionalism address some of these issues? First is parental altruism, right. The parent is very kind understandably to their children and wants them to succeed, therefore maybe more patient or maybe protective of their initiatives. But professionalism can address the negative aspects of such altruism. By having an independent board or an independent outside member who is loyal to the firm and not to the family alone and therefore is willing to express their voice and evaluate and judge the possibility of the opportunity and hold the family member to the same standard that will be applied to every project is very crucial in facilitating innovation. Because then what happens is a judgment is based on the merits of the possibility or the opportunity rather than on the family loyalty and ties. Second is the deep entrenchment that we also see with the type two agency problems. Here, what tends to happen is that there is a family director, and they, they are associating their identity or their role in the family business and their legacy as being this particular legacy product, and therefore, they're not very willing to call for a review of it. Again, here, professionalism plays a very strong role in addressing this limitation. Because a non-family director or an independent director on the board with real voice or an independent peer or a non-family peer can actually help take a call and evaluate what exactly is happening with this legacy product. Because they do not have a particular stake that is tied to that legacy product. The third is the self-control problems that arise again from type two agency problems. Having professionalization enables a staged review process. This separates the project's champion from the kill decision. There is, which means that there is an independent evaluation of the opportunity, which is separated from the person who's trying to champion that particular idea. What typically happens is when ideas begin to fail, family firms, because they're also not only looking at financial wealth but also non-financial or socio-emotional wealth, they may be more protective about against innovation when you see multiple attempts to fail. So having an independent evaluation or separating the project champion from the kill decision is important because it limits and restricts your losses, which enables and supports new ideas when they come up or their time is due. And therefore, innovation can address and therefore, professionalization can act as a strong facilitator in innovation within family businesses. The second important aspect or element which can help family firms in their innovation is governance. Here you're looking at the role of formal strategic planning, right. Having formal strategic planning is very useful because it channels generational energy into real innovation activity. Why? Because you are being asked to abide by and describe your role, describe your presence and contribution within the family firm through formal strategic planning processes rather than a very informal gut and intuition-driven way of doing things, and this forces you to evaluate better. Second, having a chartered innovation committee. This committee or a subcommittee or maybe a group, is one which also has real authority. It includes the presence of outside expertise. Maybe the outside expert may not necessarily have a formal role or a position within the family firm but they definitely have a position within this chartered innovation committee. The purpose of such an innovation committee is to provide outside expertise and a very unbiased, broader scope of doing things, and this forces the firm to regularly look and explore new ways of doing things. The third is ring-fencing exploration budgets. Many times what happens is innovation budgets tend to balloon. You end up spending much more than what you originally planned for, because once you put a little bit of money, it is only natural to try to chase bad money using good money. Because I've already put 1 crore, why don't I put another 20 lakhs? Maybe I will recover that money, right. Ring-fencing as a good governance practice, ring-fencing the exploration budget helps to protect the exploration from being crowded out by exploitation-safer returns. And it also gives you the discipline of working within certain budgets, and that budget becomes dedicated, right, and therefore ring-fence the exploration budget. You are having a dedicated budget and you're trying to work within that budget, and you're able to pull the plug, the kill switch at the appropriate time. A common thread across all of these three is that each choice targets a very specific failure mode that we have been identifying in this particular module. And this is not about a general good governance advice A third way to do this is to engage in open innovation, deliberately looking outside the family firm itself. There are three ways to do this. The first is sourcing in. Sourcing in is a practice that can broadly or easily be explained as deliberately bringing in outside ideas, technology and/or partnerships. In other words, you are questioning, which is true about open innovation itself, why should all the innovation happen under my roof? Can I source it in? The second is, can I source it out? Can I let a promising internal idea leave my doors or leave my fence via maybe a licensing agreement or a spin-off where some other people can develop it better and find more success for an asset that I currently control but I'm not able to get the maximum out of it. Why family firms need it to be named. It works as a direct countermeasure to the insularity risk that we have discussed earlier, and therefore, open innovation enables a firm to look outside, either as a source of new ideas, new possibilities right at the seed stage, or to commercialize an asset that already exists within the firm.",
