      "transcript_text": "In part 3 of module 5, let us look at internationalization. And let us look more closely at how ownership and control can shape a family's expansion beyond its home markets. Let's look at why would a family firm already which is already comfortable at in their home market and wary of risk when going outside would go abroad at all. And what is the cost-benefit analysis that a family firm would do as against a non-family firm when they try and go abroad? There are 4 reasons that we can think of. The first reason is probably the most obvious one is market seeking, in terms of there is a large market outside which seems to be growing faster or not served, and there is something specific to this family's strengths that can be well marketed outside. Now, you also are in India, which is probably also an area which is as a low-cost market for production, etc., for example, and that could be something useful when you're marketing abroad. So, market is one reason why you would look to internationalise. Secondly,  it could be efficiency especially if you're going to scale or you are going to replicate an existing footprint  for the purpose of growth or specialization, you could look at internationalization. The third would be something like a strategic asset which you will seek. You could be acquiring a brand, a technology build some kind of a different kind of a network. Maybe a stakeholder network usually via an acquisition. We will see examples of all of these somewhere later in this section. But that could be a reason, again, strategically buy an asset and with that get some strength and maybe use it not just in that market, but also in the home market as well, that's the third. The fourth would be resource seeking. It's a possibility that natural resources are unavailable at home or low-cost products of some kind are unavailable at home, then you really have to go outside and seek it. So, for example, some of the commodities that that industry seeks are not available at home. For example, we know the story of magnets and China. We want to make magnets. We want to make semiconductors, chips, any of those sorts, or even mobile phones. Magnets have to be imported from China. So, resources  may force a co-company or sometimes think of a company how they can go outside, actually set up something and from there export. So, these are 4 classic reasons why a firm would internationalize. What  does internationalization actually cost? Or what it does mean in terms of financials for a family business? The first is the easier one, the capital commitment. So, which means every move will mean that there is some capital which is to be required and something that will need to be tied up for the overseas market, which means what could have been used at home, what could have been what is probably sometimes needed at home will get tied up  in the export. The second part is managerial complexity. It means that the skill set required will be very different out there and you may not be able to staff the same way that you're staffing at home. Coordination is something that's demands a lot more when you are in different markets against a different time zone. So yes, managerial complexity  will increase. Integration risk will also increase, especially if you have an entity in an acquisition which is supposed to deliver what it is what is the promise  which it was, let's say, an acquired or an acquisition. When we may realize that some of them, they may not deliver the way  they are thought to, and things may actually go haywire. Also what happens is there's a liability  of, can I say foreignness because there are extra costs just for being in a unfamiliar territory, just for being in a foreign land. The  newness of it, the lack of familiarity there. There's a misjudgment as to whether there's a right fit distance, time zones, all these can play a role in not exactly things going to plan. And therefore, there is a, so all these are when I talk about whether it's a capital or managerial or integration or just being abroad, these are all the kind of costs that an internationalisation will bring when a firm goes abroad. This is so far it's irrespective of whether it's a family business  or a regular business. Now what's the twist that happens if it's a family business as well? Family business, again,  we'll discuss this from the willingness and ability matrix, okay. And that's an area that  we'll see being repeated everywhere, the willingness and the ability of the family firm to do many things. Ability, ability is often high. What it means is that the family has the financial mettle, has patient capital and a very long-term horizon, and therefore can absorb the costs that come with internationalization. So, that's the lesser challenge. Willingness is not exactly that something that is constant and well, not necessary that is there equally with every family member. Ability is something we can see with the family as a whole, the family firm here as a whole. Willingness is often measured in terms of individuals out there. The family individuals are, they are part of it. Now, some may be saying that we'll go ahead, some will be saying we'll not go ahead. What it also means is that some amount of control the family has to shed when you go abroad. Now, this is a question. Once it comes to a control issue, the way the family can react is very different. So, that's one area which will be constantly tested. The ability is there, the willingness questionable is when we really have to decide whether to go abroad. So, the willingness ability matrix is appli-applicable to internationalisation also.",
