      "transcript_text": "Sai, is there any available research and evidence on what actually acts as a trigger for some of the internationalisation of the, of the family firms? Is there prior research that can throw some more light on what parameters within the family actually contribute to internationalisation? So this is a very interesting question because the empirical evidence when we look at it, goes in different directions. We have studies pointing to a positive relationship between family versus non-family, as well as a negative relationship on the impact on internationalisation. It essentially comes down to three broad things. One is the extent of control that the family has. This you're looking at the ownership. You're looking at their position in the upper echelons or the CEO basically, or, or the board presence. Second, you're looking at internationalisation related decisions. These, for example, could be about the speed of internationalisation, the degree of internationalisation, the scope of internationalisation, which and the entry modes, because that determines how much of the family firm's resources are you putting at risk. And the third are the home country conditions. So broadly, we are looking at questions within this. So when you talk of family control what kind of control leads to what kind of internationalisations or what kind, higher or lower the control and its relation to the family going international. Is there a link? There, there is a link. While earlier for a very long time it was argued that family firms in comparison to non-family firms would internationalize lesser, mainly because they want to protect their resources against any risk of internationalisation. However, there are studies which do argue that if the family firm survival itself is at risk, they may look to internationalize. On the other side of it, there are, there are also the upper echelons theory which talks about, for example, the younger generation, the kid has gone abroad, done his studies over there, built a professional network over there and come back. Or there are other families running their own family businesses who have internationalized in your social circle. And you're looking to them as a peer effect that you're following. Right. So does control by itself explain? Not fully. Okay. It's a mixed empirical evidence, right. Similarly, with the type of decision, speed, degree, control scope, or FDI versus export. And this matters because it's talking essentially about how many resources or how much resources of the firm and the family are you putting at risk, right. And therefore, there is a variation that we expect. But if we were to boil it down, you know, aren't we talking about a very fundamental thing? Is it really a family versus non-family question? So Sai, I wouldn't say it is very different for a family or a non-family firm. The evidence doesn't point to any one direction conclusively. What it really means is family firms go international under certain conditions. Yes. So the conditions will determine the way forward for the family business, and conditions will determine how much risk to take, yes, more than the ownership itself more than the institutions that are in the home country. The situation that the family sometimes finds itself in, the circumstances may also dictate the path that the family has chosen to go. True. And if you look at emerging markets, yeah, because we are in India and we are kind of bucketed in the emerging markets, right. If you look at the evidence from the emerging markets, increasingly we have become more FDI-oriented internationalisation. What are your thoughts on that? In the case of emerging markets the FDI is more an incoming FDI rather than something the emerging markets is not going and, and putting money outside and investing globally. That would represent a greater deal of risk for the family firm. But what the family firm will likely use this FDI as an incoming stepping stone to perhaps go outside and export their own, their own goods over to the international markets. Export is considered a low risk as compared to investing globally. Correct. And if money comes inside, family firms are are all for it. So, yes, that way it's a, it's a welcome shift. And India falls in this bracket, right. Where India, yeah, a lot of FDI is, is coming into India. Firms are the family firms of, of global family firms, which again contribute to 60, 70% of the global economy, right. They invest in India, in conditions like India where the market is growing. So they, from their perspective, the global family firms are investing in markets where there is future growth potential, and they are so sure of the growth potential that they're willing to take the risk. And the currency also helps them in this kind of a shift. And also if we take for example, more prominent sectors like the automobiles in India, we've seen the, when inward FDI was restrictive, we've seen foreign companies come in and get into joint ventures with Indian family businesses. And slowly, over a period of time, these family businesses have started separated from the joint venture, but they've also started independently exporting. And you're right that there is a big difference between inward FDI and outward FDI. But broadly across emerging markets, including India, the numbers, I'm not saying that we are outward FDI is exceeding inward FDI, but we are seeing a more positive trend even towards the outward FDI. There are increasing number of family businesses which are adopting this route across emerging markets, and that is a very welcome sign. Think about the Samsungs of the world, the Hyundais of the world. These are all family businesses in many ways, and they are doing FDIs across the world. And India is no exception. We have a lot of stories of Indian businesses which are doing outward FDI and very actively doing that.",
