      "transcript_text": "Let us look at the advantages that a family firm has when it comes to internationalization. We saw in an earlier section that the evidence is somewhat mixed, right. What we're trying to see that if conditions are right, what are the few things that actually work in the favour of a family firm? Let's look at that one by one. The first thing and the most important perhaps thing is the outlook of the family. We have, we have seen this multiple times. Patient capital and a very long-term horizon. These are very useful when a family firm has decided to go step out of its home market and go abroad. How? They know that there is years of investments to be done without expecting the same kind of return that they will expect from home, because it's a completely new market and, and before it turns a profit that requires patient capital. And what family firms have is plenty of patience in that area. Again, let's see it from the willingness ability. They have the ability to sustain that investment and they don't need external approval. Because of the kind of control they have in the existing family firm they don't need 20 outside approvals for getting things done. There's only one thing that can be a catch in, in some of these kind of situations, and that catch is capital can be patient but at some time there's a thin line between being patient and being ultra cautious. So patient should not convert into caution. And we have seen earlier that it can possibly become something like that. So there should be a lot of focus in the internationalization. There should be metrics being demanded, performance being monitored very closely so that patient capital does not become very, very cautious in terms of results and therefore you're not forcing or not driving it hard enough. The second thing that works in their favour for going international and sustaining out there is what I can call the reputation and relational capital that a family firm possesses. Let's understand what this is. First is the family name can be a very good substitute for lack of a, a local track record. When a family firm goes abroad, nobody knows them there, right. So they're going to establish themselves there. But in some way, what happens is that there is a family name which can be tracked and stakeholders there will see, okay, you've been, you're new in this place, but you seem to have a 50-year-old record in your home territory. So there is a name that brings some amount of trust. So let's look at it this way. If, if TCS goes to Latin America, they will still acknowledge that yes, they are big there, right. So while I know size matters out there and longevity does, longevity actually, TCS is smaller than many other firms. But yes, the reputation that a family, the, the family firm brings matters a lot. The second is the firms often will follow an existing relationship abroad We discussed it in earlier section what we call as a network model. What it means is that they will not start from absolute zero or without, without a, without a scratch. So usually what happens is there is an existing customer that they are servicing. Let's say I am exporting to a, a company somebody in India who is in turn exporting abroad, or the, the company's branch in India and exporting abroad. Now you say, \"Why can't you set up there and do something for me there?\" So there is a relationship that exists with some firm out there, what I would call an anchor client kind of a thing. So you're following an anchor client, an existing relationship, that from there you branch out and, and start farming your business to a lot more other clients, hunt for new clients, and establish a business model out there. So the network connection is something that is matters. These have limits. In a sense relationship built in one context it doesn't mean that you can automatically transfer it to something, some completely different. You're servicing, say, multiple industries. Something in one sector will not transfer somewhere. Something in one market will not abroad, will not transfer to the second market abroad. So there is going to be starting trouble. But as long as you are, there is trust, there is a network effect that you are trying to build on, and from there building it on and becoming bigger in that market family firms can still succeed. So these are unique to family firms again because trust matters and network matters. The third thing that that would matter when a family firm goes international is the stewardship and unified decision-making. Let's see what they mean. Research has also thrown up that this is something that, that matters a lot in a, in a family firm. The first one is stewardship We've talked about some part of it in terms of patient capital. But what is also when we talk of stewardship is the, the management, the family is fairly patient in their reach towards this foreign market. There is a committed expansion plan, and this is something that is can, confirm out over multiple quarters. This is not a family business is not something that will look at results in the next couple of quarters. Correct. So there's no short-term financial engineering that the family will look for. That's the kind of thinking that the family business has, and this is the kind of stewardship that matters a lot. The second is unified decision-making. What it means here is that the commitment is important. And once the family itself is aligned and there are fewer stakeholders after that to convince beyond that. See, we've spoken about it much earlier, saying that unity in the decision sometimes is far more important than the decision itself. In this case, if the family is united in the decision that we have to go abroad, we have to go to a different market, we have to stick our neck out there, we have to stay patient out there and ensure that this succeeds and we, we, we lay the seed down there might take a decade, two decades to come out. As long as there is unity behind this decision, resources will be committed for this decision, and the family will ensure that this project actually succeeds.",
