      "transcript_text": "What are the factors that influence the internationalization of a firm and internationalization of a family firm? There are broadly put four different levels at which we can classify these factors into. One is the firm level, next is the industry level, third is at the country level, and lastly, the upper echelons level. At the firm level, we can look at what are the resources that the firm has. Do they give the firm a unique advantage as the firm seeks to internationalize? Maybe, for example, a unique intellectual property or a unique way of doing things, or even the firm size because sometimes it does lead to slack resources being available which enable the firm to take risky decisions such as internationalization. At the firm level, you could also consider the product transferability. This would refer to how easily can this product move across international markets and find acceptability and similar levels or maybe even greater levels of willingness to pay in the host country. Will the advantage that the product related advantage in the home market carry over to the host market as well? Related is also the capital intensity of the decision. How intense is the commitment requirement based on the entry mode? And this differs. Export is a low commitment, whereas FDI is a very high commitment mode. Again, at the firm level is the ownership aspect. Who are the owners? Because the internationalization also depends on the extent of family ownership as well as the presence of blockholders. For example, if you have foreign institutional investors as blockholders, they could bring unique networks, perspectives which could positively shape the internationalization of family firms. And when you consider family ownership, how dispersed is it within the family? Who owns? How many members own? How many branches own? How is the decision going to be in terms of ease of moving within the ownership dimension? All of this, combined with the family's objectives of both financial as well as non-financial wealth, together shape the internationalization decision of family firms. At the industry level, we will look at the competitors within the industry, and when we consider that, are there industry level advantages that the family firm can leverage? And secondly, am I following my competitors abroad? Sometimes what tends to happen is when a lot of the competitors within the industry or a lot of the peers are internationalizing, the family firm may face with peer pressure and reflect an mimetic isomorphic behavior. In other words, the firm may tend to start reflecting the behavior of other competitors within its industry. If they start to internationalize, the focal family firm may also internationalize. A key thing to  keep in mind over here, when we're talking about peer pressure from the industry, at the family firm level, it could also include other families who are owners of different firms in other industries but who are close in my family network. And that could also put a pressure or an advantage because I can leverage those networks as they seek to internationalize, and these could also shape the internationalization of a family firm. At the country level, we are considering the institutional environment. There are two aspects here. First is the institutional environment at the home or the host country. At the home country level, you're looking at whether the institutions, be it regulatory, cognitive, or normative, are they supportive of internationalization or does the firm seek to escape a more constraining or weak institutional environment by internationalizing? The family firm or a firm may also seek to springboard and gain access to strategic assets to compete in international as well as strengthen its competitiveness in its home country. And therefore, the home country institutions matter a lot. Host country institutions also matter. There are two layers here. One, when you look at the host country institutional environment in isolation, are they conducive of inward internationalization? Second is the institutional distances between the home and host country. Are they similar in terms of the regulatory environment? How similar or dissimilar are they in terms of the policies, in terms of the culture, in terms of how they think and what they perceive to be a good quality product or not? All of these distances, including, for example, even the geographic distance between the home and host country, are country-level factors which shape the internationalization decision. The fourth level is the upper echelons or the upper echelons perspective. The upper echelons perspective focuses on the attributes of the decision maker at the top. This is typically the CEO, the managing director, or the chairman of the board. For example, consider the foreign education of the CEO or a lot of foreign country travel by the CEO, or the CEO's prior foreign market exposure as a manager before they moved on to that particular role. Or consider the family member as the CEO. Here you're looking at the family member because they're the CEO, they're also cognizant of both the financial and the non-financial goals of the family firm. Or when you look at the family firm and the family member as the CEO, also consider the networks that the family CEO has access to. The routines and practices about how deep or how stringent is the strategic or rigid is the strategic planning process within the firm. All of these different, different characteristics can explain different internationalization modes. For example, having a prior foreign exposure, either through education or work experience, or having greater foreign networks, international networks through either family networks, professional networks, or business networks, all of these could shape the export versus FDI decision. They could also shape the speed of internationalization decision, the degree of internationalization as well. These four levels do not work in isolation. They tend to interact, and this interaction happens for all firms: firm, industry, country, and leadership. However, there is an added layer for a family firm, and this is the family. For example, at the firm level, also consider familiness as a resource. When we spoke about industry and the peers in the industry, we also considered that other family firms within the family's social network as peers. At the country level, we're looking at institutional factors that uniquely shape how a family firm will tend to behave. For example, there are many norms which are very unique to a family's behavior. And at the leadership level, we also considered how whether the family member is a CEO or not, or whether the chairman is a family member but the CEO is a professional. This could have a very different implication because the family is seeking to protect both the financial as well as non-financial. And a family firm sees this in its integration of the family systems and the business systems. And therefore, the interaction between these four levels is very nuanced and different and therefore, we see different outcomes for family firms when we compare their behavior or their internationalization behavior with non-family firms.",
