      "transcript_text": "Let's look at the challenges and obstacles a family firm is likely to face if and when they go to a new market or a market abroad. We spoke about the kind of advantages earlier as to what happens when a, what aids the family business when they go abroad. Let's look at the flip side of it. Let's look at the obstacles. The first and probably the foremost obstacle that a family firm will face is probably internal. It's about control, dilution, and resistance to capital coming in from outside. So let's face it. First, when you have to go abroad, you have to go to a completely different market that is far away. The requirements are very different. The kind of what do you call, infra setup there might require some capital. Such capital may not be something that is available from within the home country itself, which you can go and deploy out there. So that's not something that every family firm will have. And if you don't have capital from within, from let's say internal accruals, then where does the capital come from? That kind of capital can only come from loans. You may have to look at new kind of lenders maybe some kind of investors, some kind of a private equity some kind of whether HNIs or some, some new investors into the company. Or you may have to have a, a partner who will put in a substantial investment in the company in return for a certain stake. Now, whether it's a minority investor like a, a private equity firm all of them are going to have some, some kind of a stake in the family business. The moment you talk of a stake, then you're talking of a, a control being shared with someone. Well, you might find structures. You might say, \"I will, I will create a new company. In that company, these people can can put a stake. I will not let them touch the parent main company.\" You can figure out structures. Remember, you have the right to design as owners. You design the structure of your company. So you may do all of that but still there is somebody to whom today you are also answerable because that person has lent some capital to the business, and that capital is very important for you to go outside and, and spread your business. This is a question that is that will bother the family. Again, let's look at it from the willingness and ability matrix. Is there ability in the family firm to actually go out and get money? Is the ability of the firm is something that others will be willing to put money? Yes, they will be. The family has the ability to attract capital. Does it have the willingness to attract capital? That's the question, because willingness often mean that you have to shed some equity, shed some control, and this is where the collision starts. So this opportunity is weighed not against the potential gains first, but potential risk of control loss. That's the first thing the fam-family will always look at. The next thing that the family firm will face as an obstacle is what I would say an insularity. What do I mean by insularity? Insularity is people, especially when it comes to talent within the company, not willing to understand that this is requires a different playbook. They are, they are full of what they have learnt in their home market and how they can go and apply it there itself. So they have to recognize that the needs need, the needs of that market are different and we need to adapt. This happens with people who have been long tenure in the system. They're used to a certain style of functioning. They'll they'll go and try and adapt the deploy the same playbook in a completely different market. They're going to have problems. So this talent, which is likely to have a closed mind, is what can be a problem. How can you avoid it? You can actually avoid it by having a, a local partner who understands the local market extremely well. He who knows, and because that local partner has probably spent decades out in that market, understands what works, what doesn't work, how do you go to market, what are the kind of relationships you need to build where to invest, where not to invest. These are things they'll come with some local knowledge. The tenured employees in the home market may not have the same kind of insight for deploying themselves the product in the, in the overseas market. So a local partnership can still alleviate this kind of an issue. Lastly suppose assume that there is an issue of a succession timing and something that we are going abroad. Suppose you're doing a commitment, at the same time there is also a leadership transition happening in the home market. Now, these are two large things to, to grapple with. Succession itself, we've spoken about it, it's a journey that is happening. It takes that its own time but at some stage it reaches a critical point and more and more decisions, more and more responsibility is given to the next generation. At that time, if suppose a, a foreign commitment is to be done, and because it's going to take even more longer for that, it is best that the decision be taken by the person coming into the business rather than somebody who's you know, leaving the business. So these two are, are heavy duty kind of work that cannot, it's very difficult, very demanding for them to go side by side and they, they will collide. So in which case it's best to figure out and see what is the best way forward. There's no one, one size fit all here, but all we are trying to say is that the, these can be both heavy duty and, and heavy on the load of the, of the successor in the business.",
