      "transcript_text": "Earlier, we have discussed control dilution as an obstacle or a challenge in internationalisation. But why does that happen? Think about the family firm and its consideration of two goals, right. One is a financial goal and the second is a non-financial goal. In a non-family firm, the internationalisation decision is only looking at the risk-return trade-off on one dimension, which is the financial alone. But in a family firm, you're looking at this risk-return trade-off on two different dimensions. On one side, you're looking at protecting the financial wealth and trying to grow and preserve that financial wealth. On the other side, you're also looking at the socio-emotional or the non-financial wealth of the family firm. What is the implication of this? Many times, the family firm may not be very open to internationalising. It may tend to prefer domestic growth, domestic diversification over internationalisation, largely because it looks at internationalisation as carrying a socio-emotional cost, which also comes from the loss of control which also comes from the need to raise external capital. And if the financial capital itself takes a downturn, the family's wealth is threatened. And there exactly is also the catch. If the firm is not doing well in the domestic market which means that the firm is carrying a threat to its financial wealth, it carries also an associated threat to the socio-emotional or the non-financial goals, which the family firm wants to very closely protect. Because it needs to take care of the lifestyles, the income stability for all the family members. And therefore, when faced with a genuine threat to its domestic survival or when faced with underperformance, a sustained underperformance over a period of time, the family firm decision to internationalise may arise not only from the financial need to look at new options of growth, but also strengthened by the non-financial objectives and goals of protecting and providing for the income stability of the family members. And therefore, while typically family firms may not be very risk preference display in terms of internationalisation or a risk-seeking propensity with internationalisation, when there is a genuine threat to their survival, that threat becomes costlier on the non-financial dimension, and therefore, family firms may flip their risk preference towards a more tolerant approach and internationalise. This is what is referred to typically as a mixed gamble of internationalisation by family firms.",
