      "transcript_text": "Let us now look at the governance of family firms. Within a family firm, governance is about 3 different aspects. One, we're looking at governance of the firm. Second, we are looking at governance at the family level. And third, we're looking at governance at the ownership dimension. Without governance, there is a huge risk that the dinner table becomes the boardroom, and whoever is the loudest or the most senior wins that round of discussion, regardless of which circle the decision actually belongs to. And therefore, let us delve deeper into governance of a family firm along these 3 different dimensions: family, business, and ownership. When we look at the family firm using the 4-room model, there are 4 different rooms. In the owner room, there is a power and influence. It is about who can own and what decisions the owners retain the decision-making rights to themselves. Second is the boardroom. Here, what matters is wisdom and judgment. What is the strategy? Who makes the strategy? Who hires the CEO? Who oversees the risk? And so on. In the management room, what matters is competence and execution. This is about running the business on a day-to-day basis. And in the family room, what matters are love, unity, harmony, and development of the next generation. And therefore, each of these rooms require a different type of governance. Why do family firms tend to under-invest in governance? And what does it cost? Typical objection from the senior generation would be sentences that in other words, sound or mean that we have been managing for so far without this. Why do we need it? True. Very often for a small firm, or maybe a 4, 8-person organization or for a very young firm, it's fine if you don't have a very strong governance in place. Things may be working for some time. But no, if you need to scale it, if you need to sustain it, governance is very important. Why does things change with scale? A 40 or a large 80 person, 100 person or more family system as well as a business system cannot run simply on handshakes and dinner table conversations. These consensus, unless they are in writing, will not work. What is the cost if you don't set or establish governance well in advance? Establishing governance or thinking about or correcting failures that arise from the lack of poor governance and building it especially after a crisis, is very expensive, and it often arrives too late in order to prevent the damage, and therefore the family and the firm take a huge hit. Therefore, what we are trying to do here is to understand how the persuasion of the system of governance works, what is governance's purpose, the value, and why it is required to be established well in advance rather than to wait for a crisis which necessitates it. This is broadly organized around governance within these 3 different circles: the business circle, the family circle, and the ownership circle and each one is centered around a different question. In the business circle or the boardroom, we are asking a central question of how does a board actually work and who should be on it? For the family circle or the family room, the central question that we are picking up is what structures let a growing family stay uniformed? What structures let a growing family stay unified, informed, and harmonious? And in the ownership circle or the owner room, we are looking at the question: how is ownership itself governed, documented, and transferred?",
