      "transcript_text": "An important mechanism and structure in the governance of firms is the board of directors. Let us begin the governance of the business circle by focusing on the board. What is the purpose of the board? Most jurisdictions require a board by law. Why should a family firm want one that does a real work? The board is positioned between the owners and the managers. Typically, the kind of decisions that go to a board are those which pass a litmus test. You could ask, \"Is this decision significant enough that a bad choice could place the business at substantial risk?\" If yes, it belongs at the board level. What are some of these decisions? For example, hiring or firing of the CEO. Many times, the directors consider this the single most important decision. Second is succession planning. Within family or non-family firms, management succession should be planned well ahead. Third is executive compensation, including the separation of pay for management from returns to the owners. The fourth is the strategy and material decisions. These involve approving the directions the acquisitions, as well as significant resource access, including loans. Fourth are regulatory and legal compliance requirements and ensuring that the firm meets its statutory obligations. And last is the annual dividend, where the board sets against the owner room's policy and the year's performance and the business needs. There are 4 types of family business boards from informal to very fiduciary First is an informal advisory board. The legal authority for this is nothing, but the advantage of having an informal advisory board is that it's very easy to implement, very flexible, and they also have very limited liability. A formal advisory board is also lacking legal authority, but it adds the advantage of being able to give a structured advice and maintaining and building institutional memory. The third is a fiduciary board. Here the legal authority is full and binding. The advantage is that such a legally authorized fiduciary board is empowered to make tough decisions and has legal responsibility to all the shareholders. Fourth type is a hybrid of a fiduciary and an advisory board. Here the legal authority is partial. Only those members who have the fiduciary responsibility, they have the legal authority, and it kind of mixes the advantages of both the types of a fiduciary and an advisory board. Remember that whichever type you choose, members should understand the owner's interests and very, very importantly be willing to challenge them and not just agree. Because here you're not looking at buddies, you're looking at business. Your responsibility is to the business as a member of the board and not about your personal relationships. The purpose of the boards are to oversee the business. Their purpose is not to run the business. Typically, they  offer 3 broad types of services or functions. The first is purely service in nature, where they're providing advice and counsel to the owners, to the management, and this is where the firm is drawing on the directors' prior experience. The second type of function that they perform is that of resource provision. Here you're providing legitimacy, you're bringing networks on board, you're providing on enabling access to capital, talent, and customers. And the next is control, where you're monitoring the firm's performance and the risk, and this is also what we are very commonly associating with what a board's function is, the classic oversight function. Each of these 3 roles in turn shape and strengthen the effectiveness of the board.",
