      "transcript_text": "Let us delve a little bit deeper into the board roles. Let us begin with the service. What exactly does a good director give a family business beyond showing up at the board meetings? First is the board capital. Every director or member of the board brings what is called as board capital. This is the resource which is backing the role that the member is playing. And there are broadly 2 types of board capital that a board member brings. One is the human capital of the director, and the second is the relational or social capital of the director. The human capital typically consists of their prior experience, functional expertise, industry knowledge, reputation, and prior governance experience. The social capital that the director brings to the board include their ties with capital providers such as banks private equity and other sources of capital. Networks and relationships with customers, talent pools, regulators, and with other firms. Why does this matter for family boards? Typically, if the family's board is only populated with relatives, it might tend to be rich in either human capital or social capital alone. And the gap in the other aspects of the required capital for the board and for the firm to perform well is a common fixable gap. How does the service role take place in practice? Here we're talking about the board providing advice, counsel and mentoring. The board acts as a strategic sounding board. It pressure tests the strategic plans expansion decisions before they are finalized, before the firm is actually making a hard commitment and putting the resources at risk. Second is the succession mentoring. The board can play a critical and much required role in coaching the next generation leaders, adding credibility that the family alone cannot supply. And third is that they can provide an outside and a less attached or a more detached view. They're able to see a new hire, a partner or a risk that the founder is too close emotionally to judge very rationally. What makes this service role distinctly useful? The service role is available even on an informal advisory board, which has no legal authority at all, which is typically a family firm's first governance step. However, formalizing it with a fiduciary board strengthens the governance mechanism. What does an outside service give a family firm that only insiders cannot? Let us look at 3 different aspects. First is the firm-specific knowledge. An inside director or the family member builds a deep in-depth knowledge about the firm over many, many years. An outside director, while playing the service role, builds this firm-specific knowledge over time, and they start much later in comparison to the insider director. Second is a willingness to challenge the founder. This is typically constrained for a family or an insider director. For example, think about the difficulty in challenging the father if they are sitting on the board. In comparison, an outside director is structurally more free to say the harder truths. What is their stake in preserving the status quo? For an insider director or a family member on the board, it is very high because not only are the career, but also the identity and relationships are very deeply tied. In contrast, an outsider director, the stakes in preserving status quo are much lower because if the firm performs poorly, their reputation also is at stake, and it is not only personal relationships that they are looking at. If you're considering your own firm's board, what could be a service role self-check? One: does the director's background match this firm's actual strategic questions? In other words, is the director bringing relevant expertise to bear when they discharge their role? Second: has a director shifted the CEO's mind on something material this year? Have they changed a real or influenced a real decision? You don't want purely yes people on the board, that's bad. Third: are they capable and able and willing to mentor the next generation? At least one director should actively develop a rising potential family leader as a successor. Fourth: is the environment safe for the directors to raise hard questions, including a family member's own performance?",
