      "transcript_text": "How big should a family firm's board be? Bigger boards sound safer. But what does the empirical evidence say? And if there is a tipping point beyond which size is actually not beneficial, where is that point? If you look at the empirical evidence or research on the board size, there is something called as Jensen's rule of thumb because it draws from that particular research. That effectiveness tends to decline once a board grows past roughly 7 or 8 members. But why is bigger not automatically safer? As a board grows, individual accountability shrinks. There is an increase in coordination costs, and genuine debate gives way to polite diplomatic consensus. Exactly what a controlling family least needs from a board meant to challenge it. So, the family firm-specific pressure towards oversized boards. Common trap that family firms tend to fall into is to ensure that every branch gets a seat. This treats the board as a representation body rather than a decision-making body. And a 15% or a 20% representation body cannot do a 7-person board's job. And therefore, it is important to give voice to the branches, possibly through a family council or an owner council, which is a family-level governance rather than the business-level governance, where you should rather seek to reserve the board seats for competence and what the business demands. Within India we have legal minimums which are specified by the Companies Act as well as SEBI-related thresholds. A practical guideline for looking at what is an effective board size. Typically, 5 to 9 members for most family firms is a very good number If you are seeking to create a new role or a new board seat, look for a specific gap that you can name. In other words, add a seat for a specific expertise or oversight need and not for entitlement, right. Relatedly, you need to revisit this at transitions. Many times firms do the first and the second very well, but they forget to revisit the board size at regular intervals. For example, when there is a generational handoff or a major acquisition has taken place or outside capital is brought in or a very significant decision is at stake or another transition within the firm. Therefore, it is important to revisit the board size at relevant transitions. And lastly, test the effectiveness directly. Some important things are: can everybody speak candidly at the board meetings? And is it the board that is actually deciding or is it the owner room which is actually deciding? This tells us whether the board is really effective in discharging its function or not. The board should not be too small that it cannot play its role. It should not be too large that it becomes ineffective due to the liabilities of largeness and therefore cannot discharge its role effectively.",
