      "transcript_text": "Why do family firms resist independent directors and how effective are they? While these benefits are very well established, then why is it that so many family firms still avoid bringing outsiders onto the board? Broadly, there are 5 reasons that family firms resist independence on the board. First, confidentiality. You want to keep financial and compensation details within the family. The loss of control, the loss of freedom, limits to the tenure, you know, a long-term, fully trusted relationship with one particular outsider, and you want to maintain that. Secrecy, you want to keep family conflict and succession doubts private. You don't want any outsider to know that. What do we know about the effectiveness of independent directors on the board? They work best when appointment and reappointment  are genuinely independent of the controlling family. Second, they work worst when this is a pure symbolic compliance or compliance only for the statutory reason. But they are functionally selected and retained at the family's pleasure. Somebody the family knows and therefore the family is very comfortable and therefore that person is going to tow the family's line. That is when independent directors work worst. What does evidence tell us? Evidence tells us that effectiveness tracks the appointment process more than the director's individual credentials, right. What was the process that was followed? And that is where effectiveness actually comes more often. What happens when independent directors fail in doing their jobs? Think about cases like what happened with Satyam, what happened with IL&FS and many others, right. Typically, when we look at governance failures, there is a shared lesson, especially about independence and independent directors. Independence on the roster is not necessarily independence in practice. Structure, information, and culture decide whether a family board or a family firm's board has symbolic independence or substantive independence on its boardroom. When a family trust or a family governance mechanism or a family ownership structure effectively controls the board appointments, how independent can any director's vote realistically be? And therefore, while independence does have its benefits, it is important to ask whether the independent director on the family firm's board is played in a symbolic manner or the firm is looking for a substantive role.",
