      "transcript_text": "Another aspect about the board is the committee structures. Not every committee the board creates actually decides a lot or the influence that they exercise. So, how can you tell which is which? Typical board committees within a firm and also a family firm include audit committee nomination and remuneration committee, stakeholder committee, risk management or strategy committees, IT committees, for example. Some of these are mandatory, some are not. And therefore, there's a lot of variation, especially in the non-mandated committees, whether boards have them or not. And each of these committees have a core job that they're expected to do. What is important for us to understand is the difference between the power that each committee actually has, which also is where a lot of scholars say that there are certain committees, what are termed as power committees. Power committees have real delegated authority. They meet substantively, and their findings and their recommendations significantly influence and change decisions. A symbolic committee, in contrast, exists simply to satisfy a checklist. And they ratify the decisions which are made elsewhere, right. So, power committees typically are those that are mandated, like, for example, your audit committee, your nomination and remuneration committees. In India, you also have CSR committee, which is mandated. However, how they're able to perform their function is a very different question. A good one-year test is to ask, has this committee influenced or changed a real decision recently, or has it only been ratifying one that has already been made? This question is very difficult to answer as an outsider who does not know what exactly is happening within a board. But when the family member gets onto the board, it is very important to keep asking this question at the back of their mind because it tells you whether your board is really performing the functions and roles that they should, or is it only a symbolic presence. You also have management committees where you're delegating without losing oversight. Which discipline matters here? There is a written delegation of authority with very clear limits clear reporting structures and anything above this line still goes to the board. What is very important for us to note is that committees should be designed deliberately and not out of habit. You should periodically review whether each of these committees still have a real and a current mandate, how effectively they have been able to do that. You need to staff each of these committees for substantive roles rather than symbolic presences, right. You want to have a committee chairperson who insists on real agendas and not simply ritualistic ones. And you want to have fewer committees which are better than having many, many hollow symbolic committees. Few well-run committees any day beat having multiple symbolic committees.",
