      "transcript_text": "Now let's consider family members on the board across generations and with variations in firm size. How family presence on the board naturally shifts as a firm grows older and larger, and how the board must interact with the family itself. Family board presence across generations, right. Here, when you look at the ownership stage, you're talking about controlling owner stage, sibling partnership, cousin consortium, and a family federation stage. And there is a variation in the board composition or a typical board composition across these ownership stages. And the implication is that governance requirements change with each of these ownership stages. What should be the family presence on the board across firm size and organization structure? One, small and simple structures work very good, and all family board can genuinely work at any generation. Second, it is also important to consider the size and how many businesses the firm is in operating in and how many geographies it is operating in. Do you need outside expertise that a family's own career paths rarely produce? In other words, do you need access to board capital, human or social, which the family alone cannot provide? What is very important to note here is when do you need outsiders, and to what extent should family fill the board seats? You should carefully track the growth of complexity in the firm's operations. You should not give in to considerations of generational pride. \"My father had a board seat, so my generation also, I should have a board seat,\" or every branch should get an equal representation on the board across generations. Generation pride is not the reason to populate a particular board's seats. And you should pace the change of the board with the requirements of the business. How do the board management and family council actually interact? Think about 3 very important aspects that are required for a family firm: the succession, the strategy, and the family constitution. These 3 play a very important role in helping us to understand how and when or what kind of decisions requires and who plays what role between the board of directors, the top management, and the family council. When does somebody validate? When does somebody approve? When does somebody generate? Where does this question originate? Who needs to validate this, right. Who advises on this? And that variation changes with different decisions. But a central theme underlying that interaction is that none of the 3 can work in isolation. You need them to interact What is a good self-check at a generational level? One, does it match the complexity? Does the board's composition reflect today's business environment's complexity? Second, is representation answered? Do you have a written answer for how you're going to manage the growing shareholder numbers, especially at the family member level, right. And are you looking at or are you looking at every branch having a representation on the board? And third, is there a real council relationship? Here you're considering whether the family voice actually reaches the governance through a proper channel. Correct, because you're trying to separate family governance or the ownership governance from the business governance.",
