      "transcript_text": "What are some key governance questions for family members serving on the board? Before any family member takes a board seat, it is good to have a shortlist of questions and have them answered honestly, preferably in writing. What are they? One, questions about readiness and competence. Here, it is very important to have honesty. Does this person want the responsibilities of this role or the title and standing that comes with it? What is this person really looking for? Are they cognizant of the accountability and the responsibilities? Next are questions about process and accountability. How is this evaluated and by whom? You want to have a written process which is applied to family directors as rigorously as to anyone else. Second, can it be removable in practice or does family standing make non-renewal practically impossible? In other words, you are looking at entitlement and therefore you are overruling process and accountability. And third, are the boundaries being respected? Board disagreements do not quietly go away. They tend to resurface as family grievances. And there you mess up the entire space, both the systems, business and the family. Next are questions about compensation and exit. For example, is pay set and disclosed the same way for family and for non-family directors? Is there a defined term with a real decision point? And is there an honorable way for them to step down when their tenure ends? And each of these matter in order to maintain both the business continuity, strengthen the business, as well as strengthen the family harmony. The next element of governance in the business circle is the board of advisors, which is typically a lighter weight first step. Not every family is ready for a fiduciary board. What does an advisory board then actually offer and who serves as an advisor? What is the case for starting with advisor before moving on to fiduciary authority? Service and resource provision value is brought in, but in the absence of fiduciary exposure and with no binding authority ceded, it serves the family to as a genuine lower risk first step towards a more formalized governance. How does an advisory board actually work? One, provides regular cadence. You know quarterly is very common, and also you want to meet frequently enough to build real continuity in that advisory role that the ad board plays. Second, be very clear about the topics which the advisory board is going to give or provide their counsel to the family. These could include strategy, major decisions succession progress, as well as external market developments. Third is that there is no binding vote. Because that the because there is an absence of a binding vote, the advisory board can provide candid inputs to the owner, and the owner is free to accept or decline it. And there is a light charter or a much lighter responsibility of the advisory board in terms of the scope, in terms of the frequency of the meetings, the confidentiality and the modest compensation that they get. And therefore, the advisory board is kept very simple. They meet as required. There is no binding vote, and therefore, this is typically the first step towards formalizing governance, and many firms retain both an advisory board as well as a fiduciary board. Many many times the same person serves on both. Who serves on the advisory board and what do good advisory members look like? Typically, they are either retired operating executives who bring in relevant industry experience or have run something of comparable size or in the same industry before. They could also be functional specialists for example, domain expertise in finance or technology or something like international expansion or digital transformation, if that is what the firm is looking for or they could also be family business consultants. The advantage of having a family business consultant on an advisory board is that they're able to recognize patterns across many different family systems and tell you that this problem is not unique to yours alone, and how can it be solved by learning from other families' experience. What are the benefits, challenges, and when should an advisory board graduate to a fiduciary board? The important benefit of an advisory board is that it brings low risk and adds real value, right. There is of course, a service and resource roles provision without the fiduciary exposure, and therefore the owner feels more comfortable with the advisory board rather than a binding constraint if there is a fiduciary exposure. The challenge is that because the fiduciary exposure is not there, the advice of the or the counsel of the advisory board can be ignored. It's a non-binding advice and can quietly be set aside whenever it is not convenient to the founder. When should a family firm graduate to a more fiduciary or a hybrid board? Typically, this should happen once the stakes and outside capital justify real binding accountable oversight. Business complexity has grown. You're managing a lot of geographies, a lot of different businesses. Multiple people are getting involved. You've brought in outsiders. You're responsible and accountable to more people, and it cannot be run in the ownership circle alone. It now requires to be run as a professional organization, that is, when you need to grow and graduate from an advisory board to a fiduciary board.",
