      "transcript_text": "We have so far looked at governance in the business circle. Now let's look at how governance looks like in the family circle. The first question is why do you have to even do governance in the family circle at all? There is really no law behind it. Unlike in the business circle, you're supposed to have like say, a board of governors, it's mandatory. There's no such rule when it comes to the family circle. Then why do you really do it for? And how do these two go together in terms of family governance and firm governance? Is there something shared between them? And what happens if you stay informal? So, these are some of the things that we can look at in the next section. So, you look at the firm governance. The firm governance, we spoke about having a board of director, a vision, some strategic decisions being taken, and there's a top management team which is operating the business, and that's the, the business circle kind of things. For family governance, there are structures like family council, and we'll look at all of them  in more detail. What is a family forum for? A council for? What does, how does What kind of decisions does the family council take in terms of educating the next gen or transfer of legacy, nurturing family harmony, unity, the values and culture of the family also? These two have an interface. One is a clear communication between the owners and  the members of the firm. The second one is ensuring that the family's entrepreneurial ability and the family's prosperity stays across multiple generations. We remember this family is something that they look for  really long-term. So, the governance on both sides, the shared goal, longevity and continuity is an important goal for both of them. So, we have 2 different kinds of role on the on the business circle and the family circle. Different aspirations, but where they meet is ensuring longevity and carrying forward the legacy culture across multiple generations. But then what happens if you stay informal? If the family firm is in the early stage, let us say it's the founder started first generation, the founder himself or herself is deciding a lot of things. At that stage, you , really don't need this kind of governance. The single person takes all the decisions, and this can continue without a formal governance kind of a structure in the early stage of the business. At the same time if you start it when there is a crisis, it becomes a different kind of issue, even if it is in the early stage. Building a governance mid-crisis which means that emotions really run high there will be a trust deficit and the lack of a structure can really things can spiral out of control. So, there's a cost of deferring this decision of putting in family governance, saying that, \"Okay, we are still too early. Let's not do it.\" Instead of waiting for a crisis to build it, the cost is we don't have clear decision rights and easily conflicts can get triggered as the family grows and you don't have these systems in place. So, what we're trying to see over the next few sessions is let's look at some of these institutions, if I can call it. The forum, the council, the constitution, the family office. All office, let us see how important they are and how we need to take them as seriously as what we discuss the institutions in the business circle.",
