      "transcript_text": "Now let's also see what a constitution does. You've seen the council. One of the most family council's most visible output is a document that's called the family constitution. We have to understand what kind of document it is, what goes into it, who creates it, and is it legally even enforceable? And why it's not so important whether it's legally enforceable or not. And how does a constitution differ from a shareholders agreement? So let's see all these points over the next few slides. The family constitution. First is a written document. It's a written document of the family's values, rules, policies. How it is created is actually drafted by a working group. This working group is nominated by the forum, overseen by the family council, or sometimes the council itself is a working group. And this is a, a set of rules, policies which are agreed by the whole family. So nominated members and discussed policies all go into a written document. That's the family constitution. It's done by a smaller group. Why should everyone in the family agree with it? It's because, one, the, the entire, the way it works is the forum nominates the, the members who will work on this constitution. The rules and policies are discussed with all the family members before they are drafted into the constitution, and they are brought back to the family forum. So this is again discussed in the forum. Everyone agrees. There is a, there is some amount of dissent sometimes happens. A common ground is found. There is consent by everyone for the final draft, and then it is framed as a family constitution. However, this is not a legally enforceable document. No, no company in case of a, a dispute between, let's say, branches, they can't point to the family constitution and go to court. This is not, like I said, not, not legal. Then why do we even do it? It's done because there is huge advantage in the process than in the final outcome. Because so much has been discussed usually when the council drafts it, brings it back to the forum. This is not something that the council drafted once and it's, and it's approved in one shot. There's huge iteration that happens between the council and the forum, and it's worth the time because this something once said it can last a couple of generations. So like, like they say, the journey is more important than the destination. This is a very similar kind of analogy out here. The process in making the, in creating the constitution is far, far more valuable than the final print. What gives it the value is one, the process, and secondly, there is a moral commitment that every family member has given to it, and there's a social cost to breaking it. The family wrote it together, and if one person breaks the constitution, it is visible to everyone else in the family as to who broke it, and that carries a certain social cost. And the socio-emotional cost of the family business is something that we have discussed. It's very, very important. So everyone will think twice before breaking it, something that they have jointly agreed upon. Again, let's see the difference between what a family constitution can do and what a shareholders' agreement can do. First thing, the legal enforceability, we have spoken about it. A family constitution is not legally enforceable. But a shareholders' agreement is very legally enforceable, especially if it is also mentioned in the articles of association of a company. So what can a shareholders' agreement do? For example, it can say as to what happens if one person wants to exit and sell the shares. Is there a, for example, a first right of refusal or is, are there tag-along rights? Or what can be sold? What stake can be sold? So there are various clauses that can be put in the shareholders' agreement, all legally enforceable. What does it cover? The family constitution will cover the ground rules and the family-level policy, values, culture. The shareholders' agreement is something talks only about ownership. Ownership, transfer, voting rights. How do you buy and sell? How do you exit? How do you purchase more? So these are kind of things that the shareholders' agreement will discover What is the kind of validity each of them have in terms of their power, in terms of their enforceability? Family constitution has a moral commitment and a social cost of breaking it, like we discussed. But a shareholders' agreement, like we said, is legal, and therefore you're going to break contract law, you're going to break company law. So that is something that is enforceable in court, and court will really can look into the details. A family constitution cannot be taken to a court.",
