      "transcript_text": "Now let's look at another such institution, which is the family office. Let's understand what a family office is. What are the functions of a family office? And why is a family office needed at all in the first place? What does it do? When do you think in a family business is a family office even required? And let's also understand what a family office cannot do. First thing is a family office is required only when the family wealth grows to an extent and grows in complexity in terms of the nature of investments that any one member finds it very difficult to manage. And that is when we need to create this institution called the family office. So let's understand the core functions of the family office. There are three core functions that the family office will do: investment, support, and governance. The first is investment. This is about professionally managing the family wealth, which at a stage has diversified. So there could be multiple assets. There could be movable assets, there could be immovable assets. The movable assets could be every, anywhere, for example, because it's movable, it's not necessarily even constrained to one geography. You could be financial wealth sitting across borders in various kinds of investments. The movable, the immovable ones also could be hard assets which are sitting in, again, across borders, or could be something which are somewhere in, in between, which is like, say, a, a family charter flight or a yacht or something like that. So all these need to be managed. They're all family wealth. Who will manage all of this? That's where a family office first comes into the picture. The second thing is support. Support in, in terms of the entire, and especially at this stage when a family office is required and wealth becomes huge, there are tax implications, a lot of legal implications, insurance required. The family would have got into something like a family philanthropy, administration, families tax services and a lot more other things that are required. Now, to do it for the entire family in, in a centralized way, the family office plays a very crucial role. And lastly in terms of governance, is administering the family council, constitution, family meetings. And I use carefully this word, is administering. This is not deciding. The family may not be able to decide what goes into the constitution, what goes out, or what gets decided in a family meeting. It's the administration of these, something that the family office will look into. With these three functions, it can be said that the family office is very much a governance institution except that it lot more looks into the financial side of this, and that's where its ambit stays. But it's very important institution too. The typical trigger for us to when you should have a family office is when the wealth outgrows the family business or the operating business. This usually happens when the family business has had a partial sale or there is an IPO and some-somewhere there is a large infusion of capital coming into the, into the family and the business is when suddenly there is a huge amount of wealth that is sitting in the business. Or with individual family members who have, who have sold their stake to an external party or something. That is usually the trigger when a family office is required. Till then, as long as the family's wealth is in a, in a way which can be managed by individual members or is largely getting reinvested back into the business, a family office may not be required. So let's look at some signals that the family and the business may be ready for a family office. Signals like diversified wealth assets have grown well beyond the operating business, or there is complexity. There are more branches, more generation, informal coordination of handling the wealth of the family. Again, this is not coordination for the business sense. This is coordination of the wealth of the family in terms of its investments, insurance, tax handling. So all these becomes complex as more and more branches, especially if there are five branches which are cross-border. So if there are other institutions structurally, like multiple entities, cross-border holdings, trusts, when, when you have all of these philanthropy then the complexity has increased to a level where any one person finds it very difficult. There are some recurring unanswered questions that keep on coming back the administrative questions that that keep on coming back. Who will manage all of these? And there is no clear owner. Nobody has been ever told that you are the head of family office per se or you will manage the, investments of the family. In which case it's a signal that is clearly seen that we need a family office. Lastly, we must also understand what a family office is not. This is an administrative setup. It supports the council and the board. While it's an important governance setup, this is not something which is a decision-making authority, and that must be very clear to all of us. This is a support role. This is a support function. It does what the family wants. It does what the board wants. It, it's an administrative thing that takes the administrative operating overload away from key decision-makers, but goes with what the decision is taken by the administrative mechanism, by the decision-making authorities. So let's be clear on what the family office is also not.",
