      "transcript_text": "Let us now look at the various types of family offices that are possible. There are 4 or 5 types of family offices that are possible. And what is the kind of family office that suits any family business? It usually depends on size. There's no single best fit method for any family business as to what kind of family office it should choose. Depends a lot on the stage of the business, on the scale, and the amount of wealth that is it is serving. So, let's look at all these kinds of family offices. The first one, and probably the most simplest couple of versions, are the embedded family office and the virtual family office. The embedded family office is more like a team which is sitting inside the operating business itself and is usually run by 1 or 2 members of the CFO's team. And they take some amount of input from the CFO. They also listen to the family and operate the business. The virtual is an outsourced setup, outsourced to somebody who is most importantly very, very trusted, or somebody who's even if we specialize, needs to be trusted by the family, has their own dedicated staff. In the embedded one, the staff are still part of the company. In virtual, it's a completely different set of staff, outsourced. What happens and the risk that we run in an embedded setup is that the lines are very thin between  the family and the business finances and the family offices, how they operate. Because it's the same team under the CFO, these can cause some amount of confusion and because even sometimes because of the transfer of funds between the family office back and forth b- and the operating company. It's a,  it's a blurred line, so that is something that the family must be very careful about. The next type is a single-family office. A single-family office  is a separate team which works as a family office for only one family business. So, as compared to a multi-family office, which means that this entity is a separate office by itself, has controls the family office for multiple companies, multiple families. So, there are differences. If I'm exclusive with one family or I also have multi-families. So, what can be the differences between a multi-family office and a single-family office? A single-family office,  like we spoke about, serves one family exclusively, while a multi-family office can serve multiple unrelated families usually. The cost structure is something that the family office is supposed to have. A single-family office, the entire cost is borne by the same family. In a multi-family office the fixed costs are shared across multiple clients. To some extent, therefore, each family gains because the costs are a fraction of what would it would have been had it been a single-family office. The single-family office is able to customize a lot for the one family that it serves. It's able to serve multiple branches in a different way of the same family. Each member of the family can have a different version of what they want, and the single-family office can do that. However, this can't be done by a multi-family office. It is okay, but at the same time it will have some standard templates as to how what it can do in, in, you know, when it takes care of their investments, their insurance, all of that. It will have some amount of standardization. It can't be customization at that scale that the single-family office can do.",
