      "transcript_text": "Next, we shall also see about family holding companies and their investment arms. What is really a holding company? And why do you need a holding company? At what stage do you need a holding company? And what purpose does the holding company really serve? This question usually arises of having a holding company when the enterprise has diversified well beyond one business and the family needs to build a structure to govern the whole set of companies, the whole portfolio of companies and not just one company. That is where a situation is where you will need a holding company. What a holding company will do it is a very, very important governance vehicle too. What it does is it ensures that capital reallocation for sometimes can happen between multiple branches of the companies. So when you have different entities, let us say, and you have a holding company on top, holding company will have ownership in each of the entity companies. Each of these entity companies have a different profile, a different growth profile, a different risk profile. The holding companies in between can manage both the risk and the requirements of the strategic requirements of each of those companies, which are likely to be different. Let us say a holding company, and let's take the example of Tata Sons, for example, is a holding company of the multiple Tata group companies. All of them are not growing the same way. Corus is perhaps struggling. JLR is okay. Tata Steel is goes through the cycles that that the steel industry mandates. Titan, on the other hand, is doing very well. TCS has been on a roll so far and has been the, and the main funder of many of the new enterprises that Tata goes to. But what is the, what is the risk profile of TCS when AI comes? So this is where a holding company comes in and manages a lot of these things. But why does a diversified business group need a holding company precisely for capital reallocation between different kinds of companies having different risk and business growth issues? But it also is because capital markets are not present so well developed in emerging markets. So you'll find a lot more importance for holding companies in emerging markets than in well-developed markets. The holding company effectively becomes a bank which gets capital from one and, and provides capital for other, for other of the holding companies. Lack of developed labour, labour market. So what can be done in other companies can be shared. Can we have a centralized support system that can in, can emerge for all the companies under the holding company? Contract enforcement is can be sometimes weaker in emerging, in emerging economies. So these are the reasons why a holding company seems to exist in the emerging markets a lot more. Family-backed investment and venture arms is another area that is growing in India. You can, you see examples like Premji Invest, which is belongs to the Premji family. It's the PE, is the PE more and investment arm. It's, it's completely separate from Wipro Software or Wipro Limited. This is, it has its own management, has its own team, and can take its own decisions irrespective of Wipro. It can even invest in companies that can tomorrow compete with Wipro.",
