      "transcript_text": "Let us look at the ownership choices. There is variation in the types of family ownership that the family business can choose from. Owning a family business is not one single unitary thing. There are different models. Broadly, you can categorize them as four different models. Each has its own governance demands. On one side, look at whether you're looking at exclusive ownership criteria or more inclusive ownership criteria. And on the other axis, look at whether it is a unified decision control or it is a shared decision control. Using this, you can populate the matrix into 4 types, which are sole owners, partnerships, concentrated, and distributors. Survey evidence among family firms points to a variation and a little bit of even, not so even ownership distribution across these 4 different models. And there is a reason for that, because each family firm has to choose an ownership model or an ownership type that works best for its unique needs. How do sole ownership and partnership look like in practice? As a sole ownership model, look at the House of Camus. Ownership and control have been concentrated in one successor per generation for over 150 years. On the other side, as a partnership model, consider Kofra or the Brennenstuhl C&A. Here, ownership has been shared but very exclusive. Roughly a 15 year apprenticeship is required before being admitted as an owner. How do distributed and concentrated ownership look like in practice? Consider the Bacardi Limited as a distributed model. Roughly 7 generations, inclusive ownership, and typically the stakes are near one percent and there is a shared decision-making. You can look at Vitamix as an example of concentrated type. Here again, you have inclusive ownership, but decision control is unified via a collaborative leadership model. I invite you to consider and classify Indian family businesses that you can encounter either through internet or in your interactions into one of these 4 types and try to understand why is that choice being made. Remember that this choice is not permanent. There is a difference between the legal versus enacted type. In other words, what the documents say the ownership is and how ownership actually functions on a day-to-day basis, and there can be significant divergence between these two. Closing that gap in itself is a governance act. Moreover, the firm's needs change over a period of time, and as the firm's needs change, either on the business dimension or on the family dimension, the firm may choose to move across these different types along these 2 dimensions.",
