      "transcript_text": "There is another way that we can try and visualize this from an ownership alternatives matrix to examine what the ownership strategy is. Choosing an ownership type is not enough by itself. Owners also need an explicit strategy for what they want from their ownership. De Massis and Rondi have provided a good framework to try and look at this. On one side, you have the ownership competencies, including the matching competence, timing competence, and the governance competence. And you also have alternatives along the other dimensions, which is the ownership approach. Do you want to look at yourself as a custodian or as an entrepreneur? Ownership rights difference between voting and non-voting rights, and the ownership activity. Are you being an active owner or a passive owner? And this gives a variation in the possibilities of alternatives for being or becoming an owner in the family firm. How would custodian versus entrepreneur and voting versus non-voting mean, and what would it change? The choice between custodian versus an entrepreneur separates preserving what exists where you're a custodian versus actively growing and reshaping what already exists where you're an entrepreneur. A kind of an exploitation versus exploration. Are you focused on preserving what the current wealth exists or are you looking at entrepreneurial ways to create new vehicles within the family galaxy? Second is the presence of voting or non-voting shares. This is about separating economic ownership, which is where the dividends and the valuation stands versus more formal decision controls. Voting shares bring formal decision control along with the economic ownership. And the third is passive versus active owner. Are you playing a role of an owner by simply holding a stake? Or as an active owner, are you showing genuine engagement in the ownership and the governance of the ownership? Why do owners need an explicit ownership strategy? How do we weigh financial return against control, legacy, and family employment, and over what time horizon? It is very important for every owner or owner group to address this particular question about what matters to them, the financial wealth, the non-financial wealth, and how will they balance them over time and with changing complexity, both in the business dimension as well as in the family dimension. It is also very important to match ownership strategy with the ownership structure with the family's actual goals. A good sequence is to first agree upon the ownership strategy first. This will change to reflect the realities, both in the external environment on the business dimension as well as the family's dimension and what the family members need at a particular point of time. So, the first step is to agree upon the ownership strategy. Then choose the ownership type and structure to serve those strategy. It is not the other way around that you take the structure first and try to fit in the strategy to align with the structure, no. You choose the strategy and deliberately, consciously design the structure to meet and realize the strategy.",
