      "transcript_text": "Within the ownership circle, the shareholders agreement plays a very crucial role because it is a legally enforceable backbone. The family constitution expresses values The shareholders agreement does the opposite. It binds in law exactly what happens to the shares. What does a shareholders agreement typically cover? It covers the decision rules about majority rights, super majority on certain decisions, or if it is unanimity thresholds, maybe at the branch level or by the decision type. Second are transfer and exit mechanics. What can a shareholder do with the shares that they own, and how can they exit? Is there a right of first refusal? Is there a specific process to be followed when you want to give up your ownership? Can it go outside the family? Should it stay within the family? How does this work? The third is the valuation methodology. How are shares going to be priced whenever an exit or a transfer is triggered? Who values? What methodology is going to be used? Is there going to be an external party involved, and how will this entire mechanism work? What are the decision rules and how do these vary? You're looking at things like a simple majority where for example, one share, one vote. And here, typically, a simple majority works when you are considering voting on routine operational matters. Second is super majority, often 75%. You may require super majority to hold for very consequential decisions, including amendments to the shareholders agreement major decisions such as acquisitions by the family firm, or when you're trying to create new share classes with differential voting rights. The third is unanimity. Here it is about the most fundamental matters, such as the core purpose of the firm, the nature of control itself, right. And therefore, the decision rules may vary, right. Even when we're talking about simple majority, the share class matters. Certain share classes may carry additional voting rights, or certain share classes may carry weaker voting rights. Next is a transfer and exit in more detail. Typically, what you see along here are things like the right of first refusal, where existing shareholders get the option to first be offered, and unless they refuse, it cannot be sold to an outside party. Then you also have things like the put right where the minority can force a buyback under very specified conditions. This behaves very similar to a put option but with specifically to triggering an exit or a transfer. The third is a drag along provision. Here, the majority can compel minority to sell alongside them. Relatedly is a tag-along provision. The minority can join a majority sale on the same terms. However, there are enforceability traps of all of this. For example in India, there is a very good case about V.B. Rangaraj versus V.B. Gopalkrishnan. What happened in that particular case and what a practical lesson for us from that particular case is that a transfer restriction only in the shareholders agreement and not also in the articles of association may be unenforceable against the company. In other words, very simply put, the articles of association need to reflect and agree with what is there in the shareholders agreement. Or even simply put, articles of association are more important and therefore the firms need to be carefully consideration of both the shareholders agreement and the articles of association and that they are aligned.",
