      "transcript_text": "Next, let us consider the shareholders or the owners' council. The four-room model named an owner council as the owner room's working body. What does it actually do and who sits on it? There are variations across, but broadly, let us try to understand what the owner council in the four-room model does. How does it differ from the family council? There is a core distinction. In the owner's council, membership follows the ownership stake, not family membership, which is exactly why the decisions of the owner's council carry legal weight in comparison to the decisions of the family council. So, who sits on the owner's council or what who constitutes the owner's council? This again varies based on the ownership type, sole owner, partnership, concentrated and distributed. Typically, in the sole owner model, there is no requirement for a council because there's only a single owner, and that owner's decision directly carries. In a partnership model, the admitted partners or the partners on the shareholders agreement as well as articles of association, they themselves are the constituents of the owner's council. In a concentrated ownership type, the branch, the family branch or the group holding matters and along with the unified decision control. In a distribution type of ownership, the owner's council could vary based on maybe it is on an elected basis or a rotating owner representatives because you have so many owners, right. Ownership is distributed across many owners. So,  and the owner's council is supposed to be a subset of all of these distributed owners. And therefore, you look at mechanisms which can determine who serves on the owner council, and therefore, it becomes a subset of the owners. What does the owner's council actually decide? Think about 3 different types of decisions and look at the owner's council's role versus the board. One, let us consider when there is a major acquisition or a sale. While the board may decide it, it is the owner's council which needs to approve it. When you're talking about selecting or removing board members, the owner's council decides, and the board only recommends. Typically, when it comes to hiring and firing CEOs, the owner council approves while it is the board which decides. And therefore, you see that there is a variation and separation of roles in terms of the owner's council and board, and when does the board decide and when does the board support and execute what the owner's council decides, and what does it recommend and send to the owner's council. How does the owner council differ from the family council? This is about keeping the rooms distinct A good practical test is to ask, \"Is this decision being made with owner council authority or has a family council conversation quietly been treated as if it had that authority?\" Right. If there is a carryover from the family council conversation into the owner council, then you're blurring and removing the line which separates these 2 rooms, and that is bad governance. Each, the owner council and the family council, have their respective duties and purposes that they should serve effectively in order to strengthen the family business.",
