          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain the core distinction between the owner's council and the family council in the four-room model, and describe how the membership basis of the owner's council varies across sole owner, partnership, concentrated, and distributed ownership types. (10 marks)",
          "model_answer": "**Core Distinction (3 marks):** The owner's council represents ownership interests and its decisions carry legal weight, derived from economic stake. The family council represents family relationships and harmony; its decisions are advisory/relational and do not legally bind the business entity. The practical test for good governance is determining if a decision uses owner council authority or if family conversation has been incorrectly treated as such.\n\n**Membership Variation (7 marks):**\n- *Sole Owner Model (1 mark):* No council is required because a single owner's decision directly carries authority.\n- *Partnership Model (2 marks):* The admitted partners, as defined by the shareholders agreement and articles of association, constitute the owner's council.\n- *Concentrated Ownership (2 marks):* The family branch or the group holding matters exercise unified decision control within the council.\n- *Distributed Ownership (2 marks):* The council becomes a subset of all owners. Selection mechanisms include an elected basis or rotating owner representatives because ownership is spread across many owners.\n\n**Governance Implication (2 marks):** Blurring the line between owner council and family council authority leads to poor governance, as each has distinct duties and purposes essential for strengthening the family business.",
