      "transcript_text": "How does over ownership governance in the entrepreneurial family galaxy pan out? Most of the time that we have discussed the family circle so far has been about one company and how governance vehicles are there within one company. And how does the single family controls all the vehicles out here? When we are discussing the galaxy, it is no longer about one company, right. There's a whole host of institutions that are  inside the, the family galaxy. So, how does governance happen in the galaxy and not just in one company? So, let's understand that. The family galaxy metaphor, in that case, it rarely is about one company. It's a constellation. It's a constellation of can be a firm, can be offices, foundations, holding companies, trusts, and so on. The family is only at the center. And so far, we have been discussing what is center. We need to discuss a lot more about the galaxy also and the go institutions that can govern that. There are dimensions to the family galaxy. Let's take the shape of the family galaxy and what it captures. What it captures are how entities are arranged relative to each other. When we talk about the mass dimension, it's about the capital or strategic weight a given entity carries. Let's take the case of the Tata's galaxy, if I can call it. The TCS weight that the TCS carries is a lot more than, let us say, Tata Automation today. Tata Automation is capital guzzling today. TCS is the one which is funding a lot of that. So the weight that TCS carries in Tata Sons is a lot more. Luminosity, visibility to outsiders. The listed firms, for example, within a galaxy is far more visible than a private trust, for example. Continuing with the same Tata example you will definitely have TCS  far more visible. It is listed but there are some, let us say, Dorabji Tata Trust. I don't know how many have even heard of it. So it's a very private trust, which is also a  owner on Tata Sons, but it's far hardly even known. That is still part of the galaxy. Distance from the center. It's not a it's not an empirical distance we are talking of. It's how about how family controls it. It's a control distance we're talking of. How closely controlled or do we let the entity run independently? You have professionals managing it, and we are okay with it. The last is about regeneration rate  as a dimension, is how quickly are entities created or retired as the family keeps changing. An example of the family galaxy, let's talk of the Premji galaxy. You have Wipro. Wipro is listed very, very visible, highly luminous, like we talk about as an operating company,  which is where the as in all this  has a significant higher capital weight, mass as we talk about. Luminus also mass and very close to the center. We have PremjiInvest. It's the family office  and the investment vehicle which invests in many new companies as a PI visible to some extent. Is it how closely is it? It is a bit, a little bit distant from the center. And it does carry some capital weight, but it comes from Wipro, it's where it is. And what it does is  ensures that it is part of many new entities that are created or closed. The foundation,  on the other hand,  is a philanthropic entity. It  operates at a fairly good distance from the center. It is not so much family controlled and it is it is left to run independently. Its vision, its mission are clearly defined by the family, and then it is left to run by itself. It is not so luminous in sense. It is not so visible to outsiders. It is not listed. It is still a foundation, cannot be listed as a result of it. And then there is a controlling trust which controls the entire Wipro stake  and very, very invisible. We hardly know about the controlling trust. Why ownership governance has to be different across the galaxy is because the four-room boundaries are very different and has to be different for every company. And every node in the galaxy will have a different set of boundaries that have been built. As a result of it, the galaxy has a different four-room structure. Otherwise, if you're gonna have one common room, it    can result in a very, very, very messy room.",
