      "transcript_text": "Let us consider how families engage with the external environment in which they are embedded. As a first part of this, let us look at family firms and community engagement, right. When we think about firms, they are also stakeholder managers, right. We look from the lens of what is the theory of the firm or why does the firm exist, and you know, gradually move from there to asking a question: What makes a family firm's relationship with its external stakeholders different from an ordinary firm? Because every firm is dependent on its stakeholders, and it has to manage these stakeholders very well. When we think about and revisit the stakeholder theory, we had agreed that, you know a firm is every firm is dependent, and their performance and legitimacy depend upon how they manage the relationship with every stakeholder group. Because these stakeholder groups can either affect or are affected by the firm, its objectives, and its operations. This moves beyond shareholders to include other stakeholder groups such as employees, customers, suppliers, communities, regulators, the broader public, alongside these investors. The stakeholder theory looks at strategy and reframes strategy as a web of relationships with these multiple stakeholder groups, which need to be managed deliberately, and not as a series of arm's length transactions. Family firms also sit inside the same general framework, but the family itself is both an internal actor and very often a visible external stakeholder in its own right. Why are family firms distinct from other types of firms as stakeholder managers? Right. A couple of reasons. One is their longer time horizons. Many family firms plan and invest across generations and not just fiscal quarters. This changes how they weigh the long-term payoffs of community relationships. Second, their identity or the family firm's identity is very often fused with the family name. The family's brand and the family's personal reputation are often the same asset, so stakeholder missteps carry both personal and corporate cost. Third, due to the presence of concentrated and continuous ownership, a controlling family can commit to a community relationship for decades in a way that a dispersed ownership structure of a publicly listed company can rarely rely on. These traits are very important when we examine their impact on how families manage and balance their socio-emotional wealth. Earlier we had discussed about the FIBER framework, which includes the family control and influence, their identification, the binding social ties, emotional attachment, and the renewal of family bonds through dynastic succession. Relatedly, we also spoke about the socio-emotional wealth or the construct which is used to capture the non-financial wealth that family firms are very interested in protecting alongside their financial wealth. Now, look at both of these, right. The socio-emotional wealth and also relatedly, the FIBER framework, and consider the family firm's distinctive mechanisms, characteristics that impact how they can interact with their communities and stakeholders, and the story gets more interesting, and it also gives a different flavor to the community engagement that family firms engage in.",
