      "transcript_text": "Do entrepreneurial families actively engage with their community? Is community generation, in other words, a first-generation founder's gesture, or does it persist and even deepen across generations? What does the evidence tell us from India and also globally? Yes, it does tend to deepen across generations, both with Indian as well as global business families. The oldest, most multi-generational firms tend to have the longest running and most institutionalized philanthropic vehicles. India offers a very unusually rich set of second, third, and even fifth generation cases like the Tatas, the Bajajs, the Murugappa groups, Godrejas, Mahindras, and the Shivnadars and the HCL group, right. Globally, you have multiple examples. Just to name two is Walton and the Mulliez family, which show the same multi-generational pattern. Similarly, the Ford Foundation also shows what happens when a family disengages from its own foundation and later reconnects. We've also especially in the Indian context, seen a lot of examples in the popular press of first-generation wealth creators, like the Premjis, the Nilekanis, the Mazumdar-Shaw, you know which illustrate how new Indian wealth is institutionalizing giving and not necessarily only as a multi-generational continuity. Let us look at and compare Indian evidence with the global evidence. If you look at India we have very old establishments like, for example, the Tata Trusts from 1892 onwards, which hold close to 66% of the Tata Sons. We have the Jamnalal Bajaj Foundation 1977 onwards. It's a fourth generation line Jamnalal to Ramkrishna Bajaj to Rahul and then Shekar. We also have the AMM Foundation of the Murugappa Group, founded in 1922-24, which recently marked its 100th year in Jan 2024. We also see the Shiv Nadar Foundation, which has been ranked as number 1 on EdelGive-Hurun 2024 list with a record donation of 2,153,000 crores. Globally, you see the Walton Family Foundation, established in 1987 has a family board, a third generation and over 5 years has redirected close to 2.2 billion dollars and also declined the giving pledge. You have the Ford Foundation 1936. The family exited the board in 1976 and returned later in 2019. We also have the Mulliez family from France, about 700 odd family members and also very strong case on values transmission and ownership design. Moving a little bit deeper we see these several different examples that you can note over here. And from these, let us look at a few of them in more detail. The Tata Trusts, right, tata Trusts are a network of philanthropic trusts. It's not a single entity. These date back to the J.N. Tata Endowment of 1892, the Sir Ratan Tata Trust 1919 the Sir Dorabji Tata Trust 1932. Dorabji Tata died without heirs and left his Tata Sons stake to the trust, the origin of the trust's large equity position. The trusts today collectively hold more than 65% of the Tata Sons today. Recently, Noel Tata became the chairman of the Tata Trusts in October 2024, succeeding Ratan Tata. And this continuity of trust leadership has moved into a different generation. Tata Trusts have been very actively funding healthcare, education, livelihoods, nutrition, rural development, water, and research across different areas, geographies through both grant making and direct implementation. Let's look at the Bajajs and the Murugappa groups. Jamnalal Bajaj was a close associate of Mahatma Gandhi. Some studies also point to him as adopted as his fifth son, right. He founded the family's philanthropic identity, the Jamnalal Bajaj Foundation, which was inaugurated in 1977 by the Prime Minister Morarji Desai. The chairmanship of this foundation has passed down the family line from Jamnalal to his son Ramakrishna, to his grandson Rahul, and currently Shekhar Bajaj today. In 2024, the Bajaj group was ranked third on the EdelGive Hurun India Philanthropy list, donating 352 crores. Now, an increase of 33% on a year-on-year basis, and was one of the top donors to arts, culture and heritage in that year. Murugappa Group's AMM Foundation traces its history to a 1922 hospital foundation stone and recently marked its 100th year in 2024, January. It is still run by a member of the founding Subbiah or Alagappan lineage, and actively the group uses this as a vehicle to engage and contribute to the community. When we look at international cases, we look at we see many examples. Let me talk about the Walton Family Foundation, which was set up in 1987. The entire board are family members. In 2016, the board shrank from 22 members to 5 members as third-generation grandchildren took control. That generation redirected roughly $2.2 billion dollars over 5 years toward a K12 education, the environment, and Arkansas economic development, and chose not to join the giving pledge. They said in Rob Walton that we have chosen to go our own way. Another example is the Ford Foundation, set up in roughly 1936. The family board presence lasted until Henry Ford II resigned in December 1976. From then, for a very long time, there was no family member actively sitting on the board until Henry Ford III's election in February 2019. When you look at the Walton Foundation and the Ford Foundation, there is a contrast between these 2. One family doubled down on hands-on control at its generational transition. The other seemed to have disengaged for over 4 decades before reconnecting. Keep in mind that this disengagement, what I'm talking about, is only through the presence on the board as a mechanism.",
