      "transcript_text": "Why do business families care about impact? Is it about wealth? Is it about identity? Is it about spirituality? And what are the drivers of family firms giving? What is a family actually protecting when it gives, and is it only about reputation? When we look at family firms, we have pointed this out earlier that for them, there is not only the concern about the financial wealth, but also the non-financial or socio-emotional wealth. And this typically is off the balance sheet, right. It's beyond the balance sheet. It cannot be captured on the balance sheet. And family firms optimize for both of these distinct, sometimes even possibly competing forms of wealth. In this course, we have deliberately avoided the informal term dual wealth, right. It is not a very distinct, separately named construct. And therefore, we have kind of tried to avoid that. Philanthropy is one of the few activities that can build both at once. On one side, it can protect reputation and reduce regulatory, political and other forms of risk to the financial wealth, while also reinforcing identity and belongingness, which are a part of the socio-emotional wealth. What drives or what are the motives for a family firm to engage in CSR? Broadly put, you could classify them into moral, instrumental, and relational. Moral is about doing CSR because it is the right thing to do. It's based on morality. Second is an instrumental perspective. Here it is about using CSR as a mechanism to build and protect reputation or legitimacy, which in turn strengthens the family business. And third is to look at it as a relational approach. Here, this is about the family using CSR as a vehicle to serve its own social ties. What does evidence and research tell us about these different motives? Research points out that instrumental and relational motives often tend to outweigh pure moral motives. Family firms do tend to give substantially to protect their socio-emotional wealth, and not necessarily only out of altruism. This, however, does not remove the element of a moral grounding for why they're doing CSR. But it just points out that the family firm, irrespective of whether it is moral, instrumental, or relational, still tends to end up benefiting from instrumental and relational CSR activities. Philanthropy or the CSR protects the family's identification and reputation, which in turn reduces the risk to the family's binding social ties. This in turn gives the next generation members a values-aligned channel of engagement and supports the renewal of family bonds. And therefore, irrespective of what the underlying motive is, we do find that family firms actively engage with their communities and using CSR or philanthropy as a vehicle. And yes, the motives are different, but the beneficiaries still tend to benefit as a result. From a visibility point of view, the family name is on the door. Because the family's name is embedded in the firm's brand, in many cases it's eponymous, right. The family firm's name with the family's name. Reputational consequences tend to get attached to living, named individuals and not to an anonymous corporate entity. What this means in practice is that a family firm's community missteps and its community wins are read by the public as the family's own personal record. It is a reflection of the family and not closely tied to the family firm's identity. It is a family identity. Another very important aspect of family philanthropy and CSR is their spirituality Beyond reputation and SCW, many entrepreneurial families tend to frame giving as an expression of shared spiritual or moral values, which are lived out as a family practice rather than as a corporate program, right. For entrepreneurials families, these could tend to manifest in 8 different recurring pillars which reflect a shared sense of family spirituality. What are these 8 pillars? One is the connection and transcendence, which gives a sense of meaning beyond the business itself. Second is a deep motivation for spiritual growth and development. There could be ongoing personal and moral growth of the family members, which is reflected in how they tend to behave and give to the community. Third is nature and art experience. There could be a shared appreciation of nature and the arts, and that also is spirituality, right. Next is emotional sharing. This often tends to be an expression of feeling within the family. The family feels that emotional bond with the community or it could seek to nurture relationships and deliberately invest in the well-being of family ties. It could also be because of learning and education where you're valuing knowledge and lifelong learning. Your spirituality could also be reflected in services and acts of kindness. You know, things like everyday generosity, which need not be necessarily, you know, formal giving mechanisms. Spirituality could also be reflected in shared practices and rituals, recurring family traditions and observances like, you know, celebrating a particular festival, and around that festival, you're making donations, charities, supporting the community in celebrating that festival. So, there are different pillars which support and link the family spirituality and how it shapes the family finding meaning in engaging in community development through CSR or philanthropy or other vehicles. When we look at the Indian value systems, there are 2 which I would like to highlight here. One is a sense of a Gandhian trusteeship, and the other is a communitarian roots, right. The Gandhian trusteeship is. A good reflection is the Bajaj Group or the Birla Group, in which you see Gandhi's, Mahatma Gandhi's trusteeship doctrine, where the wealth is held in trust for the society and not as pure private property. Jamnalal Bajaj and Birla both built their philanthropy as a reflection of this doctrine. Khadi, support of women's education upliftment of marginalized communities, and funding the independence movement as well. The communitarian roots is reflected in several of the communities that we discussed earlier, and of particular note is the Parsi community and their communitarian roots. Tata's philanthropic identity predates and is less directly tied to the Gandhian trusteeship. Instead, this is rooted in the Parsi community service tradition and Jamsetji Tata's own reformist vision. These are two very distinct moral roots. Communitarian roots are not only about the Parsis. It could be about  the Jains, it could be about the Chettiars or any other community. The trusteeship need not necessarily come from a pure Gandhian doctrine, but a sense of trusteeship that is reflective of the spiritual roots of the family. But these distinct moral roots have or manifest in a common institutional outcome, which is that the family wealth is routed very often at scale through trusts.",
