      "transcript_text": "Another very important element of family businesses and their engagement with the community or philanthropy is value work. Can a family's values survive a generational transition without a deliberate mechanism to carry them forward? Because philanthropy is the family's mechanism for articulating, transmitting, and renewing its values very often. Family business owners often don't separate work from values. Family business owners typically don't separate a real self from a work self. Personal values carry directly into the business conduct, including their giving. And because family firms plan across generations, they feel an explicit need to pass on these values and not just assets to the next generation. Giving often reflects local roots and a reciprocity, giving back to the communities that helped build the firm's wealth in the first place. Philanthropy also gives non-executive family members a continued channel of involvement in the enterprise and can lift employee morale and also aid the recruitment of external talent. Happy families are very important for the success of a family business. Can philanthropy serve as a unifying force? Scholars drew on a 30-country, 70-interview study of family philanthropy and noted that philanthropy does function as a unifying mechanism across geographically and generationally dispersed family members. A shared family foundation doubles as a training ground for next-generation financial and managerial skill building. Hands-on philanthropic involvement deepens the younger family members' appreciation of how the family's wealth is actually used. Delving deeper into value work, there are three moving parts that are very important to understand. First is articulation. Articulate, right. The family names its shared values explicitly. Sometimes they can even formalize this into a family creed or a family constitution. The second is transmission. How do you transmit this, right. Values are passed on to the next generation through joint philanthropic decisions and shared service experiences. The third is renewal. Values are refreshed at each generational transition using philanthropy as a lower-stakes, emotionally resonant vehicle than the operating business itself.",
