      "transcript_text": "How can a family firm organize for impact? Once a family decides to give strategically, what structure actually carries the decision forward? Are there models, governance, and legal vehicles to help in doing this? Broadly put, there are four different ways that families organize their giving. One is as a standalone foundation or as a trust. Second is through a corporate CSR arm. Third is through a family office impact desk. And fourth is to partner with a third-party intermediary or a platform. And each of these work very differently and have a different requirement from the family firm as to what they need to do in order to create more impact and better impact through these particular channels. There are different examples for how family firms have engaged through each of these four different models. Which model fits which family? A large multi-generational group with an established public identity, such as the Tata's, the Bajaj's, the Birla's, the Godrej's, the Murugappa's, tend toward a standalone trust or foundation. These tend to be the most durable, legible vehicle for a century or a multi-generational scale identity. Any sufficiently large Indian company carries a CSR arm by law, regardless of the family preference. The real strategic choice is how tightly it integrates with voluntary family giving. For example, the Murugappas' AMM Foundation does by serving both the roles at once. A family with strong in-house investment capability, like the Dandekars or the Premjis, can extend that capability into a dedicated impact desk, which bridges grants and impact investing under one roof. A family that wants strategic philanthropy without building in-house capacity can route giving and philanthropy through an intermediary. Look at an external agent, an NGO, a partner institution, or a platform instead of trying to do it by themselves. And all of these models work depending upon the strategy that the family firm agrees upon about how they will engage with their community and their giving roles. When you look at the generational handoffs in practice, think about  the Bajaj Foundation, which was inaugurated in 1977. In 1994 the grandson Rahul Bajaj becomes the chairman, and in '22, Shekhar Bajaj becomes the chairman, which is the great-grandson of Jamnalal Bajaj. So, you see that there are also generational handoffs, not only in the family business or the business side of a family firm, but also on the way that they practice giving and engagement with the community. And this is taken very seriously by family firms. There are different legal vehicles for how Indian philanthropic capital can be organized and structured. For example, you have a Section 8 company, you have a public charitable trust, or as a registered society. And family firms have a choice between these legal forms. Just as they have a choice in the legal form of how they should structure their firm, they also have a choice about how they should legally structure their philanthropic activities. Moving ahead, it is very important for us to take a step back and ask ourselves and revisit the question as to why families and why family firms actively engage with the community. Why does philanthropy and altruism play such a strong role? Not only for altruistic reasons, but also for strategic reasons, instrumental reasons. Not only to strengthen the value system of the family, but also strengthen that with the next generation during generational handoffs. Philanthropy, giving, and engagement with community. Multiple examples we have seen. I invite you to go online to look at public records, to interact with family firms that you are familiar with, and ask yourself: What role does this play in their progress? Is it purely altruistic? Does it also serve unintended or intended, strategic benefits, instrumental benefits? How does the next generation get in and get involved? How are these built over a period of time? Are these coordinated at the family level or at the family firm level, or are these independent episodic initiatives that tend to happen? Why and how does family spirituality build and strengthen the family firm's giving and philanthropy over a period of time? This field is evolving and a lot of changes are happening. It is very important for us to understand how family firms engage and how such engagement strengthens the family firm.",
