      "transcript_text": "Here is a simple question to start things off. Is seeing believing? Can you always trust what you see? Most of us would probably answer yes. After all, we know the very, very popular saying, seeing is believing. Let me ask another question. Can you always trust what you read and hear, say in newspapers or television, in business reports or big data analytics? If you witness an event with our own eyes, look at a graph, read a news piece, examine a dashboard or watch a video, we tend to assume that what we are seeing, reading, and hearing represents reality. But does it? Imagine you are working as a business analyst for an online retailer. One Monday morning, you receive a dashboard showing that website traffic has increased by 30% over the weekend. At first glance, this seems like excellent news. More visitors usually suggest greater customer interest, right? Would you immediately conclude that the company's marketing campaign was successful? Perhaps. But what if the increase was caused by bots or a technical error in the analytics platform? Or perhaps a viral social media post that generated curiosity but not purchases. The numbers are real, the graph is real, yet the conclusion may be entirely wrong. This illustrates one of the most important lessons in critical thinking, one that is essentially important for students of the BBA in Digital Business and Entrepreneurship program such as you. Data never speaks for itself. People interpret data. People ask questions. People also make assumptions. People construct explanations too. But because people are involved, errors in reasoning become inevitable. So, let's return to our original question. Perhaps we should revise the question to ask, do we believe everything we see and hear and read? Okay now, let's ask another question. Have you ever been in an argument with your friend and you were absolutely convinced that you were right? Only after a little while you realize that you have live, overlooked a very important piece of evidence. Now, most of us have done that. In fact, psychologists tell us that human beings are remarkably poor at evaluating information objectively. We only notice evidence that supports our opinions. We ignore evidence that challenges ours. We jump to conclusions with very little information. And we rely on heuristics or mental shortcuts that are often useful, but sometimes misleading. Sadly, the smarter we become, the better we are at defending our existing beliefs rather than questioning them. We rely on mental shortcuts that are often useful, but sometimes misleading. Sadly, the smarter we become, the better we often become at defending our existing Critical thinking is therefore not simply about intelligence. It is about intellectual discipline to question our own existing beliefs. Let's see how a lack of intellectual discipline might work on a large scale. In 2012, statistician Andrew Pole and his team at Target Corporation, one of the United States' largest retail chains, built a predictive model that was designed to identify which customers were pregnant, so then Target could send them targeted promotions at exactly the moment they were buying new things and forming new buying habits. These subtle shifts in purchasing behavior of a customer, for instance, from previously purchasing regular scented lotion, makeup, et cetera, to suddenly buying unscented lotion, calcium supplements, and magnesium tablets. These were the subtle shifts. Target, the productive model men picked it up as that the customer was likely in her first or second trimester of pregnancy. And then, Target assigned each customer a pregnancy prediction score and sent coupons timed to match each stage of the pregnancy. Great model, right? Well, it was, until a man walked into a Target store outside Minneapolis and asked to speak with a manager. What happened next became the subject of a now-famous New York Times investigation. The man was furious. His teenage daughter had been receiving promotional mailers featuring baby clothes, cribs, and maternity products. A teenage daughter. He demanded to know whether Target was actively encouraging his teenage daughter to get pregnant. The manager apologized. He had no idea what had triggered the mailers. A few days later, the manager called the man at home to apologize again. This time, the father was also quieter. He was not as angry as before, and he said, \"I had a talk with my daughter. It turns out that there have been some activities in my house I wasn't completely aware of. She is pregnant, and she's due in August, and I owe you an apology.\" Target's predictive model had known about the man's teenage daughter's pregnancy even before the man did. Here is a question I want us to sit with. The model was technically correct, the data was real, and the analysis was rigorous. The predictions were all accurate. So, what exactly went wrong? What went wrong wasn't the analytics. What went wrong was the thinking around the analytics. The team asked a technical question, \"Can we predict this with great precision?\" What they neglected to ask were the human questions. Should we? What are the second-order effects of being this intrusive into people's lives? What does it do to trust? And what does it do to the relationship between a company and the people it serves? Target's response once the story went public or through The New York Times investigation was telling. They quietly began inserting irrelevant ads, tools, beer, lawnmowers into the pregnancy mailers, so the targeting wouldn't look so surgical. The predictive model stayed, the precision stayed. They just got better at hiding it. The analytical thinking that built the model was sophisticated, but the critical thinking around it was absent. Many students assume that critical thinking means criticizing everything. It does not. Others think it means being skeptical of every claim. Not exactly. What critical thinking is actually a careful process of evaluating information before reaching a conclusion. It involves asking questions like, what exactly is the claim? What evidence supports it? Is there another explanation? What assumptions am I making? Could I be wrong? Notice that these questions are not limited to academic settings. Managers ask them. Judges ask them. Consultants ask them. Scientists ask them. Journalists also ask them.",
