      "transcript_text": "So, this is the framing section. We are going to be looking at how logic and critical thinking are the foundational elements of reasoning. Earlier, we looked at Target's predictive model, a technically sound model that still ended up creating problems. Let's move now to the first pillar of this course, logic and critical thinking in reasoning. Before that, it's important to understand something more basic, how reasoning actually works. What does it mean for a conclusion to follow from evidence? What makes an argument valid in a technical sense? And maybe most importantly, is it possible for an argument to be logically correct and still lead you to a wrong decision? The scholars Michael Scriven and Richard Paul, 2 of the most cited names in the field of critical thinking, describe critical thinking as, quote, \"Self-guided, self-disciplined thinking which attempts to reason at the highest level of quality in a fair-minded way.\" Let's slow down on that word, 'reason'. Reasoning is the process of moving from what you already know or believe to a new conclusion. You start with some information, you do something with it, and you end up somewhere new. That's the whole activity. The part that matters is the something you do with it. 2 people can start at the exact same information and reason their way to opposite conclusions. The information isn't what makes reasoning good or bad, the process is. Let's take a simple example. A founder sees that signups doubled after a price cut. One founder reasons lower prices drove the signups, so should, we should cut prices further. Another founder reasons signups doubled, but let's check whether this was the price cut or a marketing push that happened during the same week. Same starting fact, but different reasoning processes. Only one of them is actually checking whether the conclusion is earned. That difference is exactly what logic and critical thinking are each responsible for. Logic checks the structure of the move from premise to conclusion. It asks the question, does the conclusion actually follow from what you started with? Critical thinking checks the material that's going into that move. Are your starting points actually true, complete, and relevant? Reasoning with both logic and critical thinking checks is the whole activity. This is why reasoning can go wrong in 2 different ways. It can go wrong structurally. The conclusion doesn't follow from the premises, even if the premises are true. Or it can go wrong at the source. The structure is fine, but the premises were incomplete, biased or false. So, the argument is standing on nothing. Same activity, 2 different points of failure. One more thing worth naming before we move on. Reasoning isn't something that only happens in formal arguments. It's running constantly in the background every time you draw a conclusion from evidence. For example, reading a number, a metric, sizing up a competitor, deciding whether or not you trust a claim that someone has just made. But you have to remember that it happens very fast and without you consciously checking it. That's exactly why it's worth for you to s, learn to slow down and perform these 2 checks to test your reasoning deliberately. Logic is a check on structure. Does this conclusion follow from these premises? Critical thinking is the check on the premises themselves. Do they deserve to be trusted, first of all? Most of the time, nobody checks these 2 elements. The conclusion seems to just arrive and it feels normal that of course it is the same, right? Of course, this is what the conclusion should be. Learning to notice that moment and run both of these checks, logic and critical thinking, before accepting the conclusion is the actual skill that this course is building. Reason needs both: logic and critical thinking to check whether the argument is solid. That's why we treat both logic and critical thinking as 2 expressions of the same underlying discipline rather than as separate skills. What this ultimately means is critical thinking is not a checklist, nor is it a personality trait that you were just born with or some people are born with. It's a skill and it can be learned.",
