      "transcript_text": "Let's now see what happens when you don't do a logic and critical thinking check, and ultimately reasoning goes wrong. Let's start with an event from 1986. On the morning of January 28th, 1986, the space shuttle Challenger was sitting on its launch pad at Kennedy Space Center in Florida, the United States. It was an unusually cold morning for Florida, colder than any previous shuttle launch, and overnight ice had formed on parts of the launch tower. The evening before, engineers at Morton-Thiokol, the company that manufactured the shuttle's solid rocket boosters, held an emergency call with NASA officials. Their concern was a part called an O-ring, a rubber seal roughly the thickness of a shoelace that sat between segments of the rocket booster. Its job was to keep hot gases from escaping through the joint during launch. The engineers weren't speculating. They had data. On a previous comparatively cool launch, 53 degrees Fahrenheit, the O-rings had shown some erosion and blackening, which indicated that hot gas had partially escaped past the seal. The forecast for the next morning was 29 degrees only, colder than that earlier flight and colder than any launch before it. One of the lead engineers, Roger Boisjoly, argued that the launch should be delayed. His reasoning was straightforward. Cold temperatures had been linked to O-ring damage before. Tomorrow would be colder than any of those previous cases, and so the risk of O-ring failure was higher than it had ever been. But NASA management pushed back on this. That pushback was reasonable on its face, and also in a narrow sense, it was also logically defensible. Managers asked the engineers to prove with data that cold temperature caused O-ring failure. And so, the engineers made a chart. It plotted every launch where O-ring damage had occurred against the temperature at launch. But that chart had a problem. Can you guess what that is? The chart only included flights where damage had already happened, and it left out every flight where nothing went wrong, including several warm weather launches with no damage at all. Without that comparison, the pattern looked weak. If you put them back in, the pattern becomes obvious. The colder the launch, the more likely the damage. So, the chart understated that risk and the room with management made its decision based on that chart. But notice what actually went wrong here. It wasn't the logic. Given the evidence on the chart, the engineer's conclusion followed reasonably enough. The problem was the evidence itself. It was incomplete and nobody caught that in time. This is the distinction I want you to really understand. Logic tells you whether the conclusion flows from your premises. It says nothing about whether those premises are the right ones or the complete ones. You can reason your way to a wrong answer with a completely valid argument if what you started with originally was flawed. Here's what happened next. The launch went ahead the next morning. 73 seconds after liftoff, an O-ring failed in more or less the way Boisjoly had warned it might. The Challenger broke apart and all 7 crew members were killed. Let's take a minute here to learn from this example. It would be easy for us to say the management ignored the engineers and that's what led to the tragedy. Somewhat true, but it's not a very useful way to understand what happened. A more precise way to think about this is, what evidence and what kind of questions were left out of the thinking process?",
