      "transcript_text": "The distinction between logic and critical thinking is really what this first pillar of the course is about. So, let's slow down and define our terms properly. Logic refers to the structure of an argument, the formal rules that determine whether a conclusion flows from its starting premises. For example, if I say all recorded O-ring failures happened below 40 degrees. Tomorrow will be 29 degrees. Therefore, there is a meaningful risk of O-ring failure. That's a logically valid structure. Given those premises, the conclusion does follow. But logic on its own can't tell you whether the premises are true, complete or even the right ones to be considering. Remember, logic is structure. You need critical thinking to examine where your premises, the starting thoughts actually come from. What is missing from them and importantly, asking yourself whether you are even asking the right question in the first place. And this is why, throughout this course, we are going to be treating logic and critical thinking as 2 parts of a single skill rather than 2 separate topics. If you have logic without critical thinking, you can end up with an argument that holds together internally but is resting on a shaky foundation. The argument might flow logically, but it may have faulty assumptions, selective evidence and even vested interests. The reverse is also true. Critical thinking without logic is like that vague sense you have sometimes that something is off, but you don't exactly know what. Now, you might have good instincts about an argument. You might know this is the right argument or you might feel that this mi, is the right argument. But if you can't explain where the reasoning comes from, where it is going, and where it actually breaks down, it's hard to make the case to anyone else, and people are not going to be convinced. And it's also easy, if you are not convinced to be talked out of a correct instinct by someone who simply sounds more confident than you do. Remember what Scriven and Paul had argued about reasoning, that reasoning happens at the highest level of quality, and it's just not about building a valid argument. That's the logic half. It also means being self-disciplined enough to keep questioning that argument's foundations and fair-minded enough to follow the answer where it leads, even if it leads to a conclusion that you do not desire. Put together, logic and critical thinking are what good reasoning actually looks like in practice. It's both the ability to construct an argument that holds together combined with the discipline to keep testing whether it actually should. And we saw how this worked in the Challenger story. Now, let's see some more examples, and let's see how the same underlying failure of logic and of critical thinking shows up in other settings. In 2018, one of India's largest infrastructure financing groups, Infrastructure Leasing and Financial Services, defaulted on roughly 91,000 crores in loans. Right up until shortly before the collapse, 3 major credit rating agencies had the group rated at their highest possible grade, even though the group's debt had grown by 44% over the previous 3 years. This wasn't a case of hidden information. The relevant numbers were sitting in public filings. What seems to have been missing was someone with the authority to ask an obvious follow-up question. Why a supposed line of credit meant to cover overdue payments hadn't actually been used for that purpose? In 2022, the Indian edtech company BYJU'S reached a valuation of $22 billion, making it India's most valuable startup at that time. The underlying growth was genuine. Millions of new students joined during the pandemic, and the company's reasoning that this growth justified aggressive ac, acquisitions to compound it further followed reasonably enough from what was actually happening. By late 2023, however, that valuation had been written down by 86% to under $3 billion. The growth trend itself wasn't fabricated. What appears to have been missing was a rigorous ongoing check on whether pandemic era growth and the losses that were being incurred to chase it could realistically continue once the conditions shifted from the pandemic conditions. Now, it's worth noticing that these 3 examples, a space agency, a credit rating process, and a startup, what they have in common. In each case, the reasoning on its own terms was defensible. However, what was consistently missing was someone stepping back from that argument to test whether its starting assumptions actually deserved the le, the level of confidence that was being placed in them. At some point, most of you will find yourselves in a version of that decision room. It might be a founder under pressure to launch a product earlier than the data really supports. Might be an investor deck where the projections look solid in a spreadsheet, but depend on one optimistic assumption that nobody has actually stress-tested. It might be a team debating whether to ship a feature. What you can do is to ask these question. The data clearly shows, but also ask the question, which data? And more importantly, is there some data that also got left out? Let me reinforce the main point here. Calling a decision data-driven doesn't automatically mean it's been reasoned through carefully. Data can be arranged into a perfectly logical case and still be missing precisely the piece of evidence that would have changed the conclusion. So, here's a question I'd like you to carry for the rest of this session, and even through the rest of the course. Not just, does this argument s, make sense, but what would I actually need to see or hear in order to know whether it's right? The second question isn't one logic alone can answer. It needs, and you guessed it right, it needs critical thinking. In every case that we've looked at today, capable, well-informed people had all the tools at their disposal, but still made some very faulty decisions. It's because the human mind has some default settings that actively work against it, and we are about to look at 3 of these settings: confirmation bias, motivated reasoning, and anchoring.",
