      "transcript_text": "Let's now talk about the common obstacles to critical thinking. We saw how critical thinking is a way to check whether your premises deserve to be trusted. We also saw how the check often doesn't happen, and it's not from carelessness or from a lack of tools. Every case that we saw and studied so far involved very capable, well-informed people who just didn't do the checks that they were supposed to do. Now, what got in the way? What got in the way were a few default settings that the mind runs automatically. Let's look at 3 of them: confirmation bias, motivated reasoning, and anchoring. Let's start first with confirmation bias. Confirmation bias is the tendency to favour information that supports what you already believe, and it has 3 working parts. First is selective search. That's where you go looking for evidence, but you only look in places where you are likely to get what you already think of as true, and skip the places that might not align with what you want to hear and see. That is selective search. The second is selective interpretation. Now, when the evidence that you find is ambiguous, you choose to read it in a way that fits your existing belief. And the third is selective memory, where you only recall the instances that supported your view more easily than the ones that didn't. Let's see how this works in a concrete example. A founder believes their product's core advantage is speed. Now, when they start searching for evidence, they read only the reviews that are mentioning load times and skip past the ones about pricing. When it comes to interpreting evidence, a customer comment that says, \"It's fast but a bit expensive,\" gets filed away by the founder as a compliment and not as a pricing complaint. And when a month later customers say about the product that it's fast, it comes to mind immediately. However, the word expensive, which the same customer might have said, doesn't come up at all. So, when you are choosing evidence and selecting evidence, ensure that it is not selective. Before reviewing this evidence, you need to write down what would actually change your mind. Then you check whether or not you looked for that specifically, and not just for more supporting evidence of your view of something. So, the second obstacle to critical thinking is motivated reasoning. Motivated reasoning is reasoning that is shaped by what you want to be true rather than by what the evidence supports. It has 2 parts. The first is asymmetric scrutiny, where the evidence that supports the preferred conclusion gets accepted quickly, while the evidence that is against what you want to see, it gets picked apart. It's questioned and held to a higher standard before it's even allowed to count. That's called asymmetric scrutiny. The second is rationalization. Once a conclusion is reached this way with asymmetry, a story gets built afterward that makes the process look neutral, even to the person who did it. Let's see how this works in an example. A team has spent 8 months building a feature. In the launch review, a metric showing strong engagement in the first week gets accepted immediately the, as proof that the feature is working. A metric, on the other hand, that shows that engagement dropped off sharply in week 3 gets challenged. Maybe the tracking is off. Maybe it's a seasonal dip. Maybe it needs another month of data. Both the numbers are coming from the same dashboard, but only one gets cross-examined. So here, is what you need to do. Ask who benefits if this conclusion turns out to be true, and whether that person is you. Then ask whether you'd have applied the same level of scrutiny to evidence which does not align with yours. The third obstacle is anchoring. Anchoring is the tendency for the first number or claim that you hear to shape every judgment that follows, even when that first number has no real basis. It also has 2 working parts. The first is the anchor itself. That is an initial figure that enters the conversation, often with little justification behind it. Say, for example, the cost of a pen is Rs9.99 paise. Now, that is your anchoring number. The second is insufficient adjustment. Later estimates move away from that anchor, but not far enough. The anchor keeps exerting a pull even after new better information arrives. For example, if the actual cost of the pen is 10 rupees, the first anchoring number, 9 rupees 99 paise, continues to exert a pull even after the Rs. 10 information arrives. Let's see a concrete example from behavioural research. People are asked to spin a wheel that lands on a random number, then asked to estimate the percentage of United Nations member countries that are African. People who spun a higher number gave higher estimates than people who spun a lower number. The wheel has nothing to do with the answer. It shifts the guess anyway. Concrete business example. An investor opens a negotiation at a specific valuation. Every later conversation adjusts up or down from that number, the initial number, rather than being rebuilt from the company's actual numbers. The original number may have been picked with very little vigor. It could be an approximate number, but it still becomes a reference point everyone else negotiates around. Here's what you need to do. Notice the first number that entered the conversation and ask whether it was ever actually justified or whether it's simply the first thing anyone said. How do these 3 obstacles combine and come together? The 3 obstacles being confirmation bias, motivated reasoning, and anchoring. These obstacles, please understand, don't operate independently. Anchoring, for example, supplies the starting number of belief. Confirmation bias directs your search toward evidence that supports it, while motivated reasoning supplies a reason you want that evidence to be right and screens out evidence that it isn't. All together, they can produce a conclusion that feels fully earned, built solidly from real data, but still be wrong because the process that's assembling it was never neutral. However, this does not mean that every conclusion deserves suspicion. It means the moments that deserve the closest scrutiny and analysis aren't the ones where the conclusion feels uncertain. What deserves scrutiny are the ones where a conclusion comes very fast, it feels obvious, and it looks very convenient. So, that's where your focus needs to be. Now, the combination of these obstacles is usually assign 1 or more that these, 1 of these 3 is doing quiet work underneath all of the, the, the entire process.",
