      "transcript_text": "So, let's now go on to talking about evidence, interpretation, and framing when it comes to critical thinking. We've talked until now about how reasoning well means running 2 checks on logic and critical thinking. We saw how logic checks whether a conclusion follows from its premises, and critical thinking checks whether those premises deserve to be trusted in the first place. And we've seen what happens when that second check gets skipped, whether it's in a courtroom filing, a hiring panel, a startup metrics deck, whether it's a video, an election season statistic, a COVID-19 pandemic statistics. And now we realized that in every case, the structure of the argument wasn't the problem. What it was built on, the premises, that was the problem. Now, here is what we are going to ask next. If the check is about premises, what exactly are you checking? You have to remember that the premises don't arrive already labelled as trustworthy or not trustworthy. What they come to us is as evidence, whether it's a number, a report, a claim or a chart. This evidence or premises doesn't just sit there neutrally waiting to be read. Think about the data, right? Think of all of this as data. Once it comes to you, you have to interpret it. And here's the part that we often miss. This data, this evidence gets framed even before it reaches you, and it already shapes what conclusion it seems to point towards. That's what we're going to be talking about next. We're going to look at evidences, we're going to look at how evidence is interpreted and how it is framed. So, in the last session, we talked about the different kinds of obstacles that occur in, in critical thinking. We talked about confirmation bias, motivated reasoning, and anchoring. And we also talked about how they don't just operate in thin air. They operate on evidence, and this evidence is interpreted. Let's look now at how to take this process apart and look at what actually counts as good evidence versus evidence that only looks credible. Now, there is a set of facts, but we saw how this set of facts gets interpreted differently by 2 different people, and 1 interpretation might seem more defensible than the other. Now, how does that happen? Framing. What kind of words can you use or what words or the order of these words, what is included into a sentence and what is left out can all change what a reader or a listener concludes without a single fact being altered. When we looked at Patrick Hurley's material, we saw how the difference between cognitive and emotive meanings can be described either as a headcount reduction or a gutted workforce. Now, each of these words have a different kind of a meaning, and therefore they lead to certain kinds of conclusions. Framing is that idea. It's scaled up. It's not just a word choice, but a whole architecture of how this evidence gets presented, whether in a chart or a headline or a slide or a policy summary. There are 2 reasons why this matters, the same as we did the last time around, both professionally as well as in a civic life. For instance, professionally, you will spend your career reading other people's evidence. You're all going to be investors and entrepreneurs. You're going to be reading investor memos, market research. You're going to be looking at competitor analysis, and you're also going to be producing your own for other people to read. In a civic sense, you'll spend your life encountering evidence that's been selected and framed by someone else with an interest in one particular conclusion, and they want you to reach that particular conclusion, too. Now, in both the cases, what you need to do isn't cynicism, disbelief in everything. It isn't just assuming that every piece of evidence that you see is manipulated. What you need to develop is a way in which you can look at the evidence and ask, \"What if this was presented differently?\" And whether that different presentation would change what you believe. This is what we are going to be looking at next.",
