      "transcript_text": "Let's start with what makes for good evidence. It begins by distinguishing evidence from assertion. Let's ask a basic question first. How do you know something actually counts as evidence rather than just being a claim that someone is asking you to accept? Here's a distinction. An assertion is a statement that is offered with no support attached. It asks you to accept based on the speaker's authority, confidence or how many times it is repeated. Evidence, on the other hand, is something that's offered in support of a claim. It should be something that can be checked, traced, and verified other than by that person who's making this evidence or the claim. Now, please remember that the difference isn't about how the sentence is framed or how it is phrased. Both an assertion and evidence can sound equally confident. The difference is what's standing behind the sentence. Let's take 2 statements that sound almost identical. The first one is, \"Our product is the fastest on the market.\" The second one is, \"Independent benchmarking by a third-party testing firm found our product loads pages 40% faster than our 3 closest competitors.\" Now, both of these examples sound equally confident because I'm saying it confidently, and it is written very confidently. Now, only the second one, however, the second sentence, gives you something to check. It gives you a source which is named, it gives you a method, and a specific comparison. The first one is an assertion: Our product is the fastest on the market. The second one has a source, independent benchmarking by a third-party testing firm. Third-party testing firm is the source, independent benchmarking is the method, and a comparison is that the product loads pages 40% faster than our 3 closest competitors. Both confident, but it's only the second one which gives you the source, a method, and a specific comparison. The first one is merely an, an assertion. The second one is the evidence. Both seem persuasive though, right? But are they truly persuasive? Confident, unsupported claims are often persuasive sounding. They're fluent, they're emotionally compelling as claims, and they're also backed by real support. Sometimes because they don't have the evidence, they might look and feel like they're being supported even more. Because a real study that supports evidence comes with several caveats and margins of error. A bare assertion does not have these, right? The caveats and the margins of error. So, you have to remember that sometimes confident, unsupported assertions or claims are just as persuasive sounding and sometimes even more emotionally compelling than those claims or as or evidence that are backed by real support. Here is what I want you to do. When you hear a claim, ask what would have to happen for you to check it yourself. And if the answer is, there's a specific source, method or data set I would go look at, then you're looking at evidence. If there is no source, there is no method or a data set, what you are hearing and looking at is an assertion. I'm now going to talk about something that is relevant for all of us, numbers. When we look at a number, we think that numbers speak for themselves, and they feel like evidence by default. But we have to remember again that a number is only evidence if it comes with a traceable source and method. For example, 9 out of 10 professionals recommend this approach. It's a number, but it's also an assertion. Unless you know who these 10 professionals were, what kind of source it comes from, and how many people actually participated in the survey and who conducted the survey. A specific sounding figure can carry all the persuasive elements of evidence while functioning exactly like an unsupported claim or an assertion because the specificity is doing the persuasive work that verification should also be doing. Now, this shows up constantly in professional settings. Let's look at a market sizing slide. Now, this slide might say this is a $50 billion opportunity. That's an assertion unless it cites where that figure came from and what it's actually measuring. Civic life also runs on the same distinction, often with much higher stakes. Think about any major news event. A lot of times you'll see claims that are stated with total confidence and zero attribution. Many people say, many people think, but who are these many people? What are they thinking specifically? This kind of many people kind of rhetoric circulates alongside claims that cite named sources, specific data and methods that can also be checked. Now, the unnamed as well as the named sources both can sound and look identical in a social media post. The confidence of delivery tells you nothing about what is supported and what is not, what is real evident, what evidence and what is not real evidence. The only way that you can tell them apart is ask the same question every time. What is standing behind this claim? Can I actually go check it out? A claim can come with a citation, a chart or a screenshot attached, and it looks like you can go and actually test that source. But it can still fail the test. If the citation doesn't actually say what it's claimed to say or if the chart's axes are misleading or if the screenshot has been altered or taken out of context. All of these things happen all of the time, and we know that. Now, they look like evidence because they have the citation, the sources, but is it actual evidence? Is it real evidence? So right now, the check that you have to perform isn't about does this claim have a source attached? So, you see that a source is cited and you say it's done, the source is cited. So, I go on with what I'm doing. But what you need to do now is once you see a source that is cited, you have to ask the question, have I or someone who's equally credible actually gone and confirmed that the source says, what it's being used to say? And that second step is the one that people skip constantly. As soon as they see that there is a source, they think that the job of checking stops there and that is enough due diligence. We now have to come to an agreement. Let's not think that every assertion needs to be distrusted because people do make well-informed assertions all the time. Take, for instance, a doctor with many decades of experience. The doctor might make a well-informed assertion based on what the patient is presenting with, the symptoms that the patient is presenting with, and that based on also several decades worth of experience, can contribute to the doctor making a well-informed as-assertion or a clinical judgment that does not have to be accompanied by several blood tests or a CT scan or an MRI. A doctor's clinical judgment based on the medical history of the patient as well as the symptoms that they're presenting as well as their own many years of experience, can contribute to the doctor's assertion and that does not have to come with CT scans and MRIs or several b-blood tests. The doctor's experience is enough. So, here's the habit that you need to build and this is something that we will continue to bring up in the future too. Alright? So, before you evaluate what a claim means or how it's been framed or what might be missing from it, ask the basic question first. Is this evidence or this is, or is this an assertion wearing evidence's clothing? Everything else we're about to cover, facts versus interpretations, framing, selective omission, even the idea of what's called an objective record only matters once you ask this basic question: is this evidence or is this an assertion?",
