      "transcript_text": "Now, look at evidence and see what is it is actually made of, because evidence is rarely just one thing. Most claims that you will encounter in your professional as well as your civic life blend 3 different components: facts, inferences, and interpretations. And people tend to confuse them and treat all of them as if they're all the same, and therefore deserve the equal weight. They don't, and learning to distinguish one from the other is what we're going to do. A fact is a verifiable state of affairs, something that is true regardless of who's looking at it, and it is checkable and verifiable by anyone. For example, quarterly revenue was $2.3 million. Another sentence, unemployment was 4.1% last month. The video was recorded on March 3rd. These are facts which don't argue for anything by themselves. They just state what is. Now, here is where we take a fact and you do something with it. This is called an inference. An inference is a conclusion drawn from facts using reasoning. A step beyond what was directly observed. What, one that follows from the facts through the specific chain of logic structure. Here is what an inference looks like. Revenue rose in each of the last 3 quarters, so it will likely rise again next quarter. It is an inference. It's not stated just in the data itself. You are adding something crucial to it. Think of it as an insight. It's built from the data using a reasoning process you can inspect and challenge. A good inference is disciplined, follows a visible path from the facts to the conclusion, and that path can be checked for validity or strength, in the same way that any other argument can. An interpretation, on the other hand, is different from both. An interpretation assigns meaning or significance to facts, and it's shaped by the perspective, values or framework of the person that's making this interpretation. Which means that 2 people can look at the exact same facts and produce 2 different, equally reasonable interpretations. For example, this revenue growth shows our new sales strategy is working is an interpretation. So, is the competing claim. This revenue growth reflects a market-wide seasonal upswing that would have happened regardless of our strategy. Now, both start from the identical fact that the revenue is growing. However, neither is forced by that one fact alone. Choosing between them requires additional evidence, judgment and context, and not just the repetition of the number or the fact. How do we keep the 3 apart? A fact, an inference and an interpretation. Remember, they're not the same. Here is how you can keep them apart. A fact answers what happened. An inference answers what does that likely lead to. An interpretation answers what does that mean? Now, you have to remember that confusing these categories is not a minor error. A sharp reader should be able to ask at each layer, is this the fact itself, a reasoned step beyond it or a judgment call about what it means? Unfortunately, most people never separate the 3 and most people deliberately or not benefit from it. In civic life, an identical confusion also leads to identical issues and usually at a higher volume. Take crime statistics for instance. There is a reported incidence of a particular crime that rose 15% in a city last year. Now, if that reporting methodology is sound, that becomes a fact and reflects an actual increase in the underlying crime rate rather than a change in reporting behaviour or policing focus. That this reflects an actual increase in the underlying crime rate rather than a change in reporting behaviour or policing focus is an inference that you're making. Now, this is a real and sometimes reasonable one, but one that depends on ruling out those alternative explanation. Is the increase in crime rate because of more reporting or is it because of fo, policing focus has increased? Now, if you leave out those alternative explanations and you are only focusing on the increase in underlying crime rate as if there are suddenly more criminals running around, then that is a problem. What is the kind of inference that you are making, right? That's what we're discussing now. The increase in crime rate could also mean that a particular policy failed or a particular approach to public safety should be abandoned. Now, that is an interpretation that you're making, and it is shaped by the values of who is making that interpretation. If you are inclined to believe that it's a policy failure, then you are going to claim that the increase in crime rate is because of a particular policy. Or if you are inclined to believe something or your values support a particular stance, then you might argue that a approach or a policy to public safety should be done away with. That's an interpretation, and interpretations are shaped heavily by the values of who is making the interpretation. All 3 facts, inferences, and interpretations routinely are used in the same breath with the same tone of certainty and the listener or the reader, they walk away treating this interpretation which is shaped by values and attitudes and ideologies as if it were on the same footing as the fact itself. Crime rate has increased, but is the interpretation that crime rate has increased therefore a particular policy needs to be abandoned? That connection a lot of times is not questioned. This does not mean that interpretation is not a great process only because it is shaped by beliefs, values, ideologies or that only facts need to be trusted. Interpretation is necessary. Facts rarely tell you what to do on their own. Crime rate has increased does not tell you what we need to do about it. And refusing to interpret at all just means someone else's interpretation will fill that space instead. We can't eliminate inference and interpretation either. What we need to do is to know which one you're doing at any given moment and to stop letting that certainty of fact quietly influence how we inference or interpret something. So, here's a practical habit that you can inculcate. When you encounter a claim, pull it apart into its layers. Ask what part of it is simply a fact, is it checkable, and is it in dispute? Ask what part is an inference. A reasoned step which is beyond the fact, which can be evaluated the same way we evaluate any argument for validity or strength. And finally, ask what part is an interpretation. A judgment about significance which deserves scrutiny and also is open to reasonable disagreement rather than treating it as something that is settled and it is the absolute truth. Keeping these 3 separate, facts, inferences, and interpretations, is going to be the basis of what we are going to be talking about next: framing, selective omission, and the idea of an objective record.",
