      "notes_markdown": "# Video\n\n## 1. Executive Mental Model & Core Intuition\nSelection bias is the sharpest form of framing because it operates before evidence is even presented. The mind constructs coherent stories from whatever information is available, automatically filling gaps without flagging what is missing. This means a conclusion built on a selective subset of facts can feel just as solid as one built on a complete picture. The critical question shifts from \"Is this framed favorably?\" to \"What population dataset or set of cases was excluded before I even saw the data?\" If the missing group were included, the conclusion might change entirely. Even the raw record, video footage or data sets, is not neutral. A camera has a specific field of view; everything outside that frame simply does not exist in the record. Similarly, a data set only exists because someone decided what counted as a data point worth recording. That decision itself is a choice, not a neutral fact of the world. A well-organized data set feels authoritative simply because it is clean and legible, but that feeling of clarity is not the same as the guarantee that nothing relevant was left outside the frame.\n\n## 2. Concise Point-Wise Conceptual Mechanics (Cause & Effect)\n* **Selection Bias Mechanism**: The mind is built to construct a coherent story out of whatever information is currently available (`WYSYATI` - What You See Is All There Is). This habit is automatic and affects everybody. A confident conclusion built on a small, selective set of facts feels solid, even if crucial evidence is missing.\n* **The Gap Detection Problem**: The mind does not naturally flag the gap between all available evidence and only the evidence seen. This is why selection bias is more powerful than framing mechanisms (word choice, chart scale), which can sometimes be spotted by a careful reader.\n* **Missing Data Point Blind Spot**: If a data point is absent entirely, it cannot be caught by checking an axis or scale. The only defense is the prior question: \"What wasn't I shown that might have changed the conclusion?\"\n* **Survivorship Bias (Abraham Wald Example)**: Military researchers studying bombers returning from combat mapped damage on wings and tail, planning to add armor there. Statistician Abraham Wald pointed out the flaw: the data only included planes that survived. Planes hit in engines or cockpits never returned. The absence of damage in those areas was the signal, armor belonged where the data showed no damage.\n* **Business Example (Startup Accelerator)**: A startup accelerator showcasing successful alumni companies excludes every company that failed through the same program. The sample seen by a prospective founder is not representative of actual odds.\n* **Civic Life Example (Risk Perception)**: Kahneman's research found people overestimate dramatic, vivid causes of death (plane crashes, shark attacks) due to disproportionate media coverage. These are more \"available in memory.\" Availability gets mistaken for frequency. Slow, undramatic causes (heart disease, diabetes) are underreported and invisible in public risk sense, even though they are statistically more common.\n* **Raw Record Neutrality Fallacy**: Even video footage or data sets feel objective but reflect choices made at capture time. Video has a specific field of view; data sets rely on someone deciding what counts as a data point. Clean, legible organization creates a feeling of authority, but clarity ≠ completeness.\n\n## 3. Conceptual Distinctions & Versus Matrix\n| Dimension | Concept A | Concept B | MCQ Diagnostic |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Scope of Attention | Evidence currently available | Evidence excluded before presentation |\n| Mind's Natural Habit | Constructs coherent story automatically | Requires active questioning of gaps |\n| Detection of Bias | Can spot misleading chart axes | Cannot spot absent data points visually |\n| Kahneman's Label | \"What You See Is All There Is\" (WYSYATI) | Survivorship bias / Selection bias |\n| Real-World Consequence | Public policy shaped by dramatic, visible risks | Actual common risks (heart disease) underfunded |\n| Examiner Test | Identifying the missing dataset that would change a conclusion | Distinguishing between framing mechanisms and selection bias |\n\n## 4. Key Formulas & Numerical Application\n*Conceptual Session: No mathematical formulas introduced in this lecture.*\nThe lecture focuses on qualitative mechanisms of evidence selection, omission, and the perception of raw data neutrality. The core learning is conceptual: understanding how bias operates through what is included or excluded from a presented frame.\n\n## 5. MCQ Trap Radar & Examiner Gotchas\n* **Trap 1: The Availability Heuristic Trap** - Examiners test if students confuse \"availability in memory\" (dramatic media coverage) with \"actual frequency.\" Students may incorrectly conclude that plane crashes are more common than heart disease because they are more visible.\n* **Trap 2: The Survivorship Bias Assumption Trap** - Students may assume that showcasing successful alumni accurately represents program odds. The trap is failing to recognize that failed companies are systematically excluded by construction.\n* **Trap 3: The Raw Record Neutrality Trap** - Students may treat video footage or clean data sets as complete, neutral truth. The trap is failing to recognize that field-of-view choices and data-point selection decisions occur before any presentation bias is applied.\n* **Trap 4: The WYSYATI Automatic Conclusion Trap** - Students may not realize that a confident conclusion on selective evidence feels solid but is potentially flawed. The examiner tests whether students can identify when a story is constructed from incomplete information.\n\n## 6. Summary Flashcards\n* **Front**: Why does a conclusion built on selective evidence feel just as solid as one from complete evidence?\n* **Back**: The mind automatically constructs coherent stories from available information (WYSYATI) without flagging missing gaps.\n\n* **Front**: What did Abraham Wald conclude about bomber armor placement based on returning planes?\n* **Back**: Armor should go where data shows no damage (engines/cockpit), because those planes never returned; the absence of damage is the signal.\n\n* **Front**: In the startup accelerator example, why is the founder's sample not representative?\n* **Back**: The showcase excludes companies that failed, by construction, making the seen sample unrepresentative of actual odds.\n\n* **Front**: What creates the feeling that a well-organized data set is authoritative?\n* **Back**: Clean, legible organization creates a feeling of authority, but this clarity is not the same as a guarantee of completeness.",
