      "transcript_text": "Let's Look at a real paragraph from a business report and annotate it based on what we have learned about implicit positions and explicit claims. Here is the paragraph. It reads, \"Our platform helped thousands of small businesses grow this year. As always, we remain focused on the merchants who make a community what it is.\" Now, what's the explicit claim? The explicit claim is a claim of helping thousands of small businesses grow. What is the implicit position? As always. The phrase \"as always\" implies consistency the paragraph never actually demonstrates with evidence. There's another implicit position here. There's no mention of businesses that left the platform this year. It's an absence, but not a denial. There is a common trap here. Let's not mistake a position for a fact. An implicit position, when it is repeated often enough, can start to feel like it is a fact rather than a position or a stance that someone has chosen to take. What do you need to do here? You need to conduct a check, as we have been doing for all of the lessons before. Ask yourself, could a reasonable person like you read this same text and hold the opposite position? And if yes, what you found was a position, not a fact the text simply reported. Another common trap, which is overreading. This is called the opposite error, where you find an implicit position that isn't actually supported by the text but it's only supported by your own assumptions. Now, we have to remember that close reading isn't just speculation for speculation's sake. Every implicit reading needs a specific textual hook, and if you can't point to the word, the omission, or the structural choice that produced this, then the reading isn't good. Now, what do you need to do? You have to inculcate discipline. You need to understand that every implicit reading needs evidence. So when you start to speculate and you start to unpack the reading, you have to state the implicit position, then name a specific word, an omission, or a structural choice in the text that supports it. Now, if you can't do it, you only have a hypothesis and not a close reading.",
