      "transcript_text": "Let's see how one reads an actual report at work. I'm going to show you something right now. It's an excerpt from an actual report. Read it for a few seconds and read it quickly once without stopping. Think about what you have been left with. Probably something like solid quarters, some challenges, management sounds confident, et cetera. Hold on to that impression. We're going to come back to it at the end, and I have a feeling that your idea of what you just read might be different. Now let's do something differently. Let's do this slowly. Like an analyst, a student like you, a journalist or an auditor might actually read it slowly, not fast, and one sentence at a time asking what each phrase is actually saying. So let's start with this phrase broadly in line with expectations. My first question here is very simple. Whose expectations? Set by whom and when? Now this phrase sounds very precise. It sounds like it is referencing something real, but there's no baseline given anywhere in the report. You can go back and check the report if you want. It can actually be checked against anything. That's something that is worth noticing. Remember what we talked about earlier. Confident sounding language or numbers that carry no verifiable content is suspicious. Now let's look at this sentence. Footfall in physical stores declined 8% year over year. And also this other sentence, online engagement metrics trending positively. Let's look at this pairing of these two sentences. One side of this sentence gives us a hard number, 8%. It's checkable and it is specific. On the other side, the phrase trending positively with no number attached at all is vague, and this comparison between a precise and a vague sentence is rarely an accident. It often means the vague number isn't good news either. Now, if it were strong, wouldn't they have given us a figure? Let's look at another sentence. A phrase, optimize the store portfolio. In the sentence explore opportunities to optimize the store portfolio. Again, worth pausing on. Because it's a very common piece of corporate language. In most business writing we see optimize the portfolio as a euphemism for actually closing stores. The report never actually says that it is closing stores. It lets the readers supply this hidden meaning themselves without actually committing to it in writing. Let's look at another phrase on the cost and investment claim. The sentence reads, \"Operating costs increased 6% and these investments position Northgate well for future growth.\" Here what we have is a causal claim. These investments position us well for future growth, but it is asserted, it is an assumption, and it is not being demonstrated. Nothing in the report shows us evidence for that claim. There's no metric, no comparison, and there is no timeline. It's stated with the same confidence as the revenue figure above it, 8%, but it doesn't carry the same kind of support. Here's another sentence and the phrase, \"The team has worked tirelessly.\" This sentence is doing emotional and reputational work and not really informational work. It tells us nothing about the performance. The team has worked tirelessly. Not really anything about the performance. And notice where it is placed. It's placed directly after a paragraph  about rising costs. Now, that's a common pattern. A warm human sentence placed right after the least comfortable numbers softens the transition. Let's look at the closing paragraph. We anticipate Q4 will present similar dynamics committed to delivering value for stakeholders. And the report ends on its vaguest note of all. Similar dynamics commits to nothing specific. Committed to delivering value is a claim almost no company would ever contradict, which is exactly why it carries so little information. Now, on the other hand, a strong close would give us a number, a target, or a timeline but this doesn't give us anything at all. Now let's look at a side-by-side rewrite There's the same underlying information but rewritten so every vague claim is either backed up by a number or clearly flagged as an unproven assumption. Let's take a moment to read both versions. You'll notice two things. First, the rewrite is shorter. A lot of the original's length was doing persuasive work, not informational work. Second, it sounds far less confident. That gap between the two versions is exactly what close reading shows. Not that the original report is dishonest but that it is written to feel more informative than it actually was. Okay, now that you've seen how a rewrite looks, it's your turn now. You already have a short report, one that you really haven't seen or read closely. Read it twice, once quickly and once closely, and then we will use the four questions that we just practiced on it and make a rewrite. Now that you have seen  how to do a rewrite, it's your turn. You have a pre-read, which is a short report. You may not have done a close reading of  it. Now that you know what to do, let's apply the four questions that we just practiced on it to make sure that it is fully factual and informational. Let's start with the question: What's the comparison point for this number? Second is: What is an adjective doing? Who is the hidden actor in this sentence? What question is this sentence really answering? Work through it on your own, then check it against the answer key. The goal isn't to catch the lie in a report. It's to build the habit of noticing how merely confident language is different from real evidence.",
