      "created_at": "2026-11-06 08:57:17",
      "transcript_text": "Good communication isn't just about telling a compelling story. It's also about making a convincing argument. In our previous sessions, we saw how stories persuade. We looked at the SCR framework, which is situation, complication, resolution. We saw how that mapped to context, conflict, choice, consequences, and we also saw how all of these elements come together to create business narratives. A very important lesson from that session was that business narratives are not just a collection of facts, right. What business narratives or business storytelling is about is a carefully constructed account that guides audiences towards a particular way of seeing a problem, and therefore a particular solution One particular point that I want to talk about a little bit more in detail today is resolution, and that is where the actual work happens. This stage, the resolution stage, is where someone moves from describing the world to actually recommending what should happen next, and that is where the storytelling becomes an argument. Think about it, right. A startup founder doesn't simply tell investors that a market exists. What the founder does is to argue that the market is worth entering. A consultant doesn't just describe a company's challenges. What a consultant does is to argue for one strategy over another. A product manager doesn't simply present customer feedback. A product manager argues that a particular feature of a product deserves funding. Even when you're writing an email asking your manager to approve a proposal, what you're doing is making an argument. Remember, stories capture attention but arguments justify the decisions, and in businesses, you almost always need both stories and arguments. Today, that distinction between stories and arguments has become even more important because many of the recommendations that we read are no longer written entirely by people. Can you guess what else is happening? Yes, it is AI. AI tools such as ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude, Microsoft Copilot can now generate business reports, strategy memos, marketing copy, and investment summaries in seconds. And this writing looks very, very polished. It's very organized. It is to the T and can even suggest recommendation. But here's an important question that you need to ask yourself. Now, if an AI recommends that your company should enter a new market, launch a new product, or invest in a new technology, how do you know whether that recommendation is a good recommendation? Now, remember our lessons from before with logic and critical thinking. You need to ask yourself whether beyond the writing, which looks very polished, you need to ask yourself whether the argument that is underneath it is a solid one. You have to ask yourself questions like what's the claim? What evidence supports it? What reasoning connects the evidence to the conclusion? And just as importantly, you need to ask, what are the assumptions underneath the argument that make it look and read solid? Now remember Technology may have changed the speed at which these recommendations, arguments, stories, anything can be produced. In a matter of seconds, we get polished looking writing, including arguments and stories and assumptions. Technology has changed that. But what has not changed is how they should be judged, the standards by which they should be judged. And that is what we are going to be looking at today. We're going to look beneath the persuasive, polished looking writing and examine the structure that holds it together. Now, to see this in action, let's return to a company that we have discussed before, Netflix. Remember the time 2006, that crucial time when Netflix began a story that we now see as successful. So when we tell the story of Netflix today, we see that in hindsight that the story seems inevitably successful, right. Now, in 2006, we saw how Netflix recognized the future, whereas its competitor, the big giant Blockbuster, did not. And then going on, we saw how streaming replaced DVDs, and that is the end of story. But again, this is a story that is told with hindsight. But if you go back to 2006, the story seems very different. Netflix DVD-by-mail business was already thriving. It was profitable. It was growing steadily and well understood. And streaming, by contrast, was an expensive, very unreliable technology for many people, many households, and also the broadband infrastructure wasn't yet very widespread. So in 2006, Netflix was profitable. DVD-by-mail business was profitable, and streaming was yet unexplored territory. Now, for Netflix to ask for investment in streaming meant that a lot of resources would have to be put into a business model that wasn't making money and risking at the same time the profitability of the business that already it was. Imagine presenting that recommendation to Netflix's board at that time. You couldn't simply say to the board, \"Streaming is the future.\" That wasn't evidence. It wasn't even an argument. What you needed was to persuade people on the board that this uncertain investment in streaming was worth making. So what did that argument look like? Now remember the claim evidence and reasoning structure that we had talked about earlier. The claim in this regard was straightforward that Netflix should invest aggressively in streaming technology. The evidence included rising broadband adoption, falling data transmission costs, changing patterns of media consumptions, and also the projections about future consumer behaviour. But again, evidence alone isn't enough, right. The company needed reasoning. They had to ask and answer these questions. Why did those trends justify making such a costly investment in streaming at that point of time in 2006 instead of waiting another 5 years? That logical bridge, which we call reasoning, which connects evidence to a claim, is what ultimately persuaded the decision-makers on the board. Now notice something very, very important here. That famous story we tell today that Netflix saw the future only emerged after the decision succeeded. The argument came first, the story came later, and that is very, very often true in businesses. Remember, successful decisions are eventually retold as stories, and in these stories we have clear heroes, clear villains, the obvious turning points, and of course, the nice neat resolutions or endings. Whether you're proposing a new product, evaluating an AI-generated strategy, recommending a marketing campaign, or even simply deciding whether you want to enter a new market, what you as a future manager with digital business experience will be expected to do is to communicate, yes, clearly, but do something more important than just communicate clearly. What you will be expected to do is to justify your resolutions, your conclusions, recommendations, and that's why you need to understand how arguments matter.",
