      "subjective_questions": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "question": "Based on the lecture transcript, explain the critical difference between a business narrative (story) and a business argument. Why is the distinction important when evaluating AI-generated business recommendations? Provide your answer with reference to the Netflix 2006 case.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "A business narrative (story) is a carefully constructed account that guides audiences towards a particular way of seeing a problem and therefore a particular solution. Its primary purpose is to capture attention and shape perception. A business argument, conversely, is a logical structure consisting of a claim, evidence, and reasoning that justifies a decision or resolution. Its primary purpose is to provide justification.\n\nThe distinction is critical when evaluating AI-generated recommendations because AI tools can produce polished, organized text that looks like a story or a recommendation, but this polish does not guarantee a valid underlying argument. As the lecture noted, AI can generate strategy memos and investment summaries 'in seconds' that are 'very, very polished,' but the speed of generation does not change the standards by which the argument should be judged.\n\nApplying this to the Netflix 2006 case: The argument came first. Netflix had to persuade its board to invest in streaming while the DVD-by-mail business was profitable and streaming was 'expensive, very unreliable' with limited broadband infrastructure. The claim was that Netflix should invest aggressively in streaming; the evidence included rising broadband adoption and falling data costs; but it was the reasoning, explaining why those trends justified the investment *at that point in time* rather than waiting, that connected the evidence to the claim and persuaded the decision-makers.\n\nThe story we tell today, that Netflix saw the future, emerged only after the decision succeeded. In hindsight, the story has clear heroes and villains. But at the time, the argument was risky and uncertain. This illustrates that successful decisions are eventually retold as stories, but the argument must come first to justify the resolution. When evaluating AI recommendations, a student/manager must look for the claim, the evidence, and the reasoning bridge, rather than being swayed by the polished narrative style.",
          "rubric": [
            {
              "criterion": "Core conceptual understanding of story vs. argument distinction",
              "marks": 4
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Application to AI evaluation context",
              "marks": 3
            },
            {
              "criterion": "Integration of Netflix 2006 case evidence",
              "marks": 3
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flashcards": [
        {
          "front": "Why does polished AI-generated writing not guarantee a valid argument?",
          "back": "Technology has changed the speed of producing recommendations, but what has not changed is how they should be judged. The standards require examining the claim, evidence, and reasoning underneath the polished text."
        },
        {
          "front": "In the Netflix 2006 case, what came first: the argument or the story?",
          "back": "The argument came first. The story emerged only after the decision succeeded, as successful decisions are eventually retold as stories with clear heroes and villains."
        },
        {
          "front": "What is the role of 'reasoning' in the claim-evidence-reasoning structure?",
          "back": "Reasoning is the logical bridge that connects evidence to the claim. It explains why the evidence justifies the claim at that specific point in time, rather than just presenting a list of facts."
        },
        {
          "front": "What is the primary purpose of the resolution stage in business communication?",
          "back": "The resolution stage is where someone moves from describing the world to actually recommending what should happen next, and that is where the storytelling becomes an argument justifying the decision."
        }
      ],
