      "transcript_text": "So you learn why some arguments feel convincing even when they are weak, and why others, even having good evidence, sometimes fail. Very importantly, we'll look at how experienced professionals test the strength of a recommendation before actually acting on it. We'll explore five ideas. First, we'll look at rhetorical appeals, and then we'll look at the basic building blocks of every argument. That's claim, evidence, reasoning. Then we'll distinguish between deductive and inductive reasoning. We'll look at a model called the Toulmin model. And finally, we'll compare how arguments work in different professional contexts. Let's start with rhetorical appeals. Again, let's get back to our favorite company, Netflix. Remember the recommendation that we ended with to the board? Invest heavily in streaming even though DVDs were still profitable. And suppose in that boardroom you and I were sitting in 2006. Now, what would the executives, imagine you and I being executives. What would the executives have told the board to persuade them beyond just spreadsheets? Now, would they have just been persuaded by the numbers? Imagine they're looking into the future. They're speculating about the future. The board is probably not going to be persuaded by that, right. The data was solid, and it matters, of course, but data rarely speaks for itself. The people on the board, the board members, have to decide whether the data is trustworthy, whether it matters, and whether it's justifying the recommendation that you and I, the executives, are making. That means persuasion begins before anyone has fully evaluated the logic of an argument. Now, more than 2,000 years ago, there was this very famous person called Aristotle. He was the first one to ask the same question about persuasion: How do people become persuaded? Writing in ancient Athens, Aristotle saw that citizens regularly argued their own cases in court and even debated public policy in the assembly. Aristotle observed that some people were able to argue their cases in court very successfully, and some others were able to also debate public policy in the assembly. And what Aristotle noticed was that successful persuasion, whether it was for a case in a court or for public policy in a assembly, what successful persuasion almost always depended on was three things. People listened because they trusted the speaker, because they cared about the issue, and because the reasoning made sense. He wrote a treatise and in that treatise on rhetoric, he named these three modes of persuasion as ethos, pathos, and logos. Now, even though this language of ethos, pathos, logos is ancient, the ideas still are surprisingly modern, and we continue to use them even today. Every business presentation, investor pitch, marketing campaign, or a product launch still relies on some combination of these three appeals of ethos, pathos, and logos. Let's take a look at each one of these in detail. Here's the thing with ethos. Ethos is about credibility. It asks the question, \"Why should I trust you?\" And before an audience actually evaluates your evidence, they often make a much quicker judgment. They ask, \"Now, can I trust this person who's making this argument?\" And the credibility there about trusting this person making an argument actually goes on to influence your own decision. So ethos is about trust and it is about credibility. Now, let's look at this in an example. There are two people who recommend the same investment. One is a close friend who has no financial background and who is very quick to judgment, who says things like, \"Hey, this is a good investment. Just invest.\" Right. Whereas the other is a very experienced analyst who has, say, about 20 years of experience studying the industry. Now both of them, even if the way they say the argument or tell the argument, are making the exact same argument about what to invest in, right. Now which one, whether your close friend or this professional analyst, are you going to believe and trust? Even though the evidence is the same, the source is not, and that is what ethos is. Now, if you were given the choice of making an investment based on the argument that your friend has made versus an argument that a professional has made, which one would you choose? The evidence is the same, but the source is different, right. I'm guessing that you probably would go with the professional investment expert, right. I would. Now, this is what credibility means. It is experience, it's expertise, reputation, and even transparency. Sometimes even admitting uncertainty leads to credibility, credibility can be earned, and it can be earned through your behaviour. Say, for example, a company that acknowledges mistakes, often it becomes more trustworthy than the one that insists that it has never made any mistakes. Patagonia is a great example. The company Patagonia started a campaign called Don't Buy This Jacket. Can you believe it? A clothing company which said, \"Don't buy this jacket.\" Let's look at how they used ethos. On Black Friday, a day which is associated with consumer spending, the company published a full-page newspaper advertisement with a headline, \"Don't Buy This Jacket.\" Surprising, right. Now, at first glance, this message seemed so silly and absurd. Like I said before, why would a clothing company discourage its own consumers from buying its products? But looking at the advertisement, you will see why. The ad explained that every jacket production carries environmental costs that are hidden, that the consumer does not see. For example, manufacturing uses water, energy, and raw materials, and unnecessary consumption by consumers actually contributes to environmental damage. The advertisement itself contained very little technical evidence. Very few statistics were included about environmental impact, and they were not very detailed. What made the ad for Patagonia persuasive was because it strengthened Patagonia's ethos. So Patagonia is a company which was committed to environmental causes, and their advertisement, Don't Buy This Jacket, even if it was at the risk of reducing sales, it still upheld its commitment to environmental causes and therefore was consistent with its values, and that is what credibility or ethos looks like.",
