      "transcript_text": "So far, we've looked at reasoning as if it moves in a single direction, but that's not true. Some arguments begin with general principles and move towards a specific conclusions, whereas others begin with individual observations and build towards broader patterns. Those are two very different ways of thinking, and understanding the difference between these ways of thinking is essential if you're going to evaluate business arguments effectively. Let's look at two types of reasoning, deductive and inductive reasoning. So far, we've looked at the building blocks of an argument, which include claim, evidence, and the reasoning that connects them. But reasoning itself isn't always the same. Arguments can move in different directions, as we just talked about. Sometimes they begin with a general rule and apply it to a particular case, and other times they begin with a series of observations and use those observations to draw a broad conclusion. These two approaches are known as deductive reasoning and inductive reasoning. Understanding the difference is incredibly useful to us because each type of reasoning needs to be evaluated differently. One can offer certainty and the other can only offer probability. The trick is knowing which kind of argument you're dealing with. Let's start with deductive reasoning. It starts with a rule. A deductive argument starts with a general principle and applies it to a specific situation. If the general principle is true and it's applied correctly, then the conclusion also has to be true. Here's a very simple example. Let's imagine a company where all the employees must complete cybersecurity training before accessing the company systems. There is one employee, Priya, who has not completed the training, the cybersecurity training. Therefore, Priya cannot access the company systems. Now, if the first two statements are true, then the conclusion follows automatically, right. There's no room for interpretation. That's what makes deductive reasoning powerful. All employees must complete cybersecurity training to access company systems. Priya has not completed the cybersecurity training. Therefore, Priya can't access the company systems. This is deductive reasoning. You're not asking whether the conclusion is likely. You're asking whether it necessarily follows from the premises. Another example, think about a company policy. Every expense above 50,000 rupees requires board approval. The proposal requests 75,000 rupees. Your proposal, let's say, requests 75,000 rupees. Therefore, the proposal requires board approval. Again, assuming the policy is accurate, the conclusion is not debatable. Above 50,000 needs board approval. Your proposal is 75,000 rupees, so therefore you need board approval. This is deductive reasoning, and the conclusion here is not debatable. It's built into the rule itself. Deductive reasoning actually appears quite frequently in businesses and business decision-making. It is seen, for example, in compliance, financial reporting, tax regulations, employment laws, internal company policies, et cetera. With deductive reasoning, there's very little interpretation that's involved. Similarly, many AI systems also operate deductively. A fraud detection algorithm might be programmed with rules like transactions above a certain value from an unfamiliar location require additional verification, and once these conditions are met, the system follows the rule Whether it's a human manager or an AI system, the logic works in the same way. Inductive reasoning or induction actually works in the opposite direction. Instead of starting with a rule, it starts with observations and from those observations it builds a general conclusion. For example, sales of electric scooters have increased every year for the past five years. So you might conclude that demand for electric scooters is likely to continue growing. Now the word here is likely and that's the key. Unlike deduction, induction never guarantees its conclusions. It simply makes a conclusion more or less probable. A sudden technological breakthrough or a policy change, whether it's a government or a company policy change economic recession, all of these could completely change the trend. That doesn't mean that the reasoning was poor. It simply means that inductive arguments are always open to revision when new evidence comes along. Most business decisions are inductive. This is one of the most important ideas that I would like for you to actually sit with. Almost every strategic business decision is based on induction. Companies can rarely speculate about the future. What they look for is patterns, whether it is through market research, customer surveys, economic forecasts, consumer behaviours, industry trends, all of them are based on picking out patterns. All provide observations and managers use these observations to predict what is likely to happen next. Now think about a retailer who's deciding whether or not to open a new store. No one can predict what the future looks like. The manager does not know whether the store is going to be successful or how many customers will walk through the doors next year. What the manager and the company will do instead is to study similar locations, population growth, income levels, traffic patterns, purchasing behaviour in those areas. And those observations make success more or less probable. They can never make it certain, and that is inductive reasoning. Let's look at an example. Let's look, for example, at consumer trend forecasting. Imagine that you're working for a supermarket chain and over the past six quarters you've noticed something very interesting. You've seen that the sales of plant-based foods have increased steadily. Customer surveys that you've done suggest that there is a growing interest in sustainable diets. Social media conversations are also showing rising awareness of environmental issues  And based on all of these observations, you decide on behalf of the company that plant-based products deserve more shelf space. Now, is that conclusion guaranteed? Is that going to be a successful decision? Perhaps not. Perhaps a temporary fashion trend explains the increase in plant-based diets, or inflation could change the purchasing habits, or consumer preferences themselves might shift, right. And they might shift again next year. And the company and you are making the best prediction it can using the evidence that you currently have and is currently available. And that's exactly how induction works. The conclusion is reasonable, it's just not certain.",
