      "transcript_text": "AI also works inductively. Let's look at an example from generative AI. When ChatGPT predicts the next word in a sentence, it isn't applying rigid lexical or logical rules. What it's doing is making probabilistic predictions that are based on patterns learned from the enormous amounts of text that it has in its repository. Now, if millions of examples suggest that one word follows another or one phrase follows another, the model predicts that continuation. You see what is happening. The system isn't reasoning deductively. It's reasoning inductively. It's identifying statistical regularities, and that explains both generative AI's strengths and its weaknesses. Because it has learnt from so much data, it often produces very fluent, very quick responses because the amount of data it has is a lot, right. But because it predicts probabilities rather than certainties, it can also generate very convincing looking statements that unfortunately could be incorrect or even worse, could be completely hallucinated. Now, the model isn't lying. It is not deliberately trying to cheat you. What it is doing is basically engineering design, right. It's extending a pattern beyond where the evidence actually supports it. It is actually one of the risks of induction itself. Let's look at the case of UPI and digital payments. In India, over the last decade, we've seen that digital payments have grown dramatically, and UPI transactions have increased year after year. On the other hand, smartphone adoption also has increased and expanded rapidly. More merchants accepted digital payments. Consumers became increasingly comfortable using QR codes. Now, suppose you're the CEO of a retail chain and imagine it is 2021. You might reason like this. Digital payments are growing consistently. Smartphone ownership also continues to increase. Customers increasingly expect cashless transactions. Therefore, our future stores should be designed around digital payment systems rather than cash-heavy operations. What kind of a reasoning or argument is it, deductive or inductive? It is speculating about the future. It's based on a pattern, so it is inductive. You're identifying this pattern and making a prediction about the future, right. And as it turned out, this prediction of digital payments was largely correct. That's why business strategy always involves uncertainty. So why should we spend any time distinguishing between inductive and deductive reasoning? Because people often expect the wrong level of certainty. Imagine someone says, \"The data proves customers will buy this product.\" That language should immediately make you pause, because business data rarely proves anything. It usually suggests, it supports, it indicates, it makes likely, and those are inductive words. Using deductive language for inductive conclusions makes the argument sound stronger than it actually is, and you'll hear this a lot of time in business presentations. You will see people argue that, \"Our research proves,\" or, \"Our evidence clearly demonstrates. This guarantees.\" These statements are based on inductive evidence, but the confidence comes from language. It's not really from the logic. And one of the easiest ways to become more critical and a more critical reader or a thinker is for you to ask, \"Is this actually certain or is it simply probable?\" This distinction between inductive and deductive reasoning matters because the two kinds of reasoning should be judged differently. With deduction, we should ask whether the logic is valid, and with induction, we should ask how strong the evidence is and whether other explanations are possible. We've seen how business world lives in a world of induction. Inductive reasoning is the most common type of reasoning, especially when it comes to decision-making. Managers are often making decisions without all the perfect information. Entrepreneurs launch products whose success cannot be guaranteed, and investors place bets on companies they believe are likely, not certain, to succeed. Recognizing that uncertainty does not weaken business thinking, but in fact it could actually make it more honest, is an important thing for us to do.",
