      "transcript_text": "So we talked about how to structure an argument, to identify the different components of an argument, and also how to analyze an argument. What we're going to be doing in this session is learn how to evaluate an argument. Let's start with a statement. All fish live in water. A dolphin lives in water. Therefore, a dolphin is a fish. That argument feels like it's working. The logic moves along very nicely, and yet the conclusion is false. Can you tell me or think about why it might be false? Because, yes, dolphins are mammals. So what went wrong here? To answer that question, we need to separate two things that people constantly keep mixing up. Whether an argument is put together correctly, a polished looking argument, and whether it's actually true. And that's the difference between validity and soundness, and that is what we're going to be learning to do today. So let's start with validity versus soundness. Let's define validity first because that is what people get very confused about. So an argument is valid when its structure is solid and if the premises are true, the conclusion must be true. Validity is entirely about the relationship between the premise and the conclusion. But it doesn't say anything about whether the premises are true in the real world. Validity is about form. It's not about content. You can also test it by asking, is there any possible way for the premises to be true, but the conclusion to be false at the same time? And if you say no, then it is valid. If you say yes, it is invalid, no matter how true the premises happen to be. The second layer is soundness. An argument is sound when it meets two conditions at once: it is valid and also all its premises are actually true. Sound arguments are the gold standard. The structure is airtight and the material going into that structure is accurate. Now let's go back to our dolphin example. So the argument that a dolphin is a fish is actually invalid even if both the premises were true and the conclusion wouldn't follow. It is not a solid conclusion because plenty of non-fish also live in water, like otters, submarines sometimes they also live in water, right. In some sense. So it fails the validity and therefore it can't be a sound argument. Now, here is a good way for you to remember the relationship. Every sound argument is valid, but not every valid argument is sound. Validity is like the, think of it as the minimum bar and the reasoning is what needs to hold the entire argument together. Whereas soundness is the full package. It is correct reasoning plus the correct facts. You must be wondering why this distinction between validity and soundness, why does it matter outside a classroom? Why? Because people use this validity but unsound argument constantly, whether it is in marketing, whether it is in casual arguments with friends, political rhetoric. A lot of persuasion works by building this structure that feels like it is logical, but it is false or unproven premises, and that is a problem, right. Whether in the classroom or outside of it, it is a problem. And here you are in a classroom on critical thinking. So we need to know what these layers of validity and soundness are, and that is what makes your evaluation of an argument tight. So here's the thing. If you only check does this conclusion follow and never check are the premises actually true, you're only doing half the job of evaluating an argument. All right, so here is part number two, where you are looking for strength versus cogency. The first part was validity versus soundness. We are now going to be looking at the strength of an argument versus cogency. Now, validity and soundness work very well for deductive arguments where the claim based on a conclusion follows with certainty. But a huge amount of everyday reasoning isn't like that  Think about arguments like it's rained every day this week in Bangalore during monsoon season, so it will probably rain tomorrow too. That conclusion isn't guaranteed. It's a probability claim, right. Now, those are inductive arguments. Remember our session from earlier where we looked at deductive and inductive arguments? So this argument about Bangalore that every day of the past few days have rained, therefore it will rain tomorrow is an inductive argument, and we need to use different evaluation toolkits for these kind of arguments, inductive arguments from deductive arguments, right. Now, this is what brings us to strength and cogency. An inductive argument is strong when, and if the premises are true, the conclusion is probably true, not certainly, but likely true. So strength comes in degrees. It is somewhat true, very true, most true, that kind of thing, right. Strength comes in degrees. So, for example, let's look at a moderately strong inductive argument. Here is the argument. Every crow I have ever seen is black. Therefore, all crows are black. Again, every crow I have ever seen is black. This is a moderately strong inductive argument. It's not a guaranteed conclusion, but it's a reasonable inference from this large, big pattern that you have seen that every crow that you have seen is black, right. Now compare this to a slightly weaker argument. We are talking about strength of arguments here, right. This is a weaker argument. I saw one black crow yesterday. It's a weaker argument. In fact, we should be saying it is a very weak argument. You see one crow which is black, and therefore you make the conclusion that all crows are black . It's the same conclusion, but it's much weaker because the evidence base is much thinner. You saw one black crow versus every crow that you have ever seen being black, right. So strength, just like validity, is still about the relationship between premises and conclusion. You can have a strong argument built on false premises. For example, in this statement, all lottery winners publicly announce their win within 24 hours. Nobody has announced winning last week's lottery. Therefore, probably nobody has claimed the prize yet. That's a reasonably strong inference structurally, even if the first premise turns out to be false, that all lottery winners publicly And this is where cogency comes in. It is the inductive equivalent of soundness. So here is what you need to pay attention to. An argument is cogent when it is strong and its premises are actually true, or at least they're well supported and reasonable to accept. So just like sound arguments are the deductive gold standard, cogent arguments are the inductive gold standards. Okay. Good reasoning plus good facts equal inductive cogent arguments. Now, here's a parallel structure that is going to be useful for you. All right. With deductive arguments, aim for validity. Look for structure and soundness, which is structure plus true premises. For inductive arguments, you want to aim for strength, which is structure, as well as cogency, which is structure plus true premises. It is the same underlying logic, but two different families of argument. Because deductive arguments claim certainty and inductive arguments claim only probability.",
