      "transcript_text": "In our earlier module, we saw how an argument is made up of 3 parts: the claim, that's what someone wants you to believe; the evidence, that is the data or the facts that are given; and the warrant, which is the reasoning that actually connects the evidence to the claim. We also talked about how evaluating arguments differently depending on whether they are deductive or inductive, which is  different types of reasoning, right. Deductive, which is valid, sound arguments; or inductive, which is strong and cogent arguments. We looked at all of them and how they can go towards evaluating arguments. Today, we're going to see how that framework works in the real life. See, here's the uncomfortable truth about businesses, and you might already know this. Most bad decisions that are made don't happen because someone is missing evidence or because someone makes a false claim, okay. Bad decisions happen because there's a break in the warrant. You know, the third part of the argument? The warrant is this reasonable link between the evidence that we have and the conclusion that we draw from it. And when that link is not strong enough, what we have is called a fallacy. And this matters a lot more in business than it might in any other classroom. See, because in a boardroom or an investor pitch or even a Slack thread, internal Slack thread about last year's numbers, nobody is going to say, \"Hang on, let's map out this argument.\" Because decisions get made really quickly, right. No one looks at all the different parts of the argument and then lead to a decision. Decisions are  made really quickly and on the go. What is often driving the decision-making is gut instinct, confidence and persuasive language, and that's why we have to pay attention to some problems that go into this gut instinct or confidence or persuasive language, and that is what we are going to be doing today. We are going to be looking at fallacies and why it is important for us to recognize these fallacies when decisions are being made so quickly and on the go. Because they might sound like they are right, but they are not always right. What they do is these fallacies tap into ways of thinking that feel natural and intuitive. So, a hasty generalization sounds like common sense. An appeal to authority can sound like humility. Say, for instance, if someone says, \"Who am I to question the CEO?\" Because the CEO is the ultimate authority, right. So, if you don't question it, there might be something that the CEO is also doing wrong, but we don't question the CEO because of the authoritative position that the CEO occupies. And a slippery slope, another fallacy that we will look at today, can sound like a sensible risk management. If we allow this, where does it stop? The problem isn't that these arguments are not right or that they sound ridiculous. The problem is precisely the opposite. The problem is that they sound very reasonable, even when we see that the evidence doesn't actually take us where the argument says it will. Take a very ordinary management decision, working from home. Imagine a manager looks at the numbers and notices that one team's productivity has fallen by 10% since the team moved to hybrid work. And now the manager says, \"There you have it. Remote work is hurting productivity. We need everyone back in the office.\" That sounds familiar, right. Now notice what happened. The evidence is real. Productivity did fall by 10%. But what does the evidence actually show? Does the evidence prove that it is remote work that caused the decline? Not necessarily, right. Maybe it was because two key employees left. Maybe the team took on a very difficult project. Or maybe the performance metric itself has changed. Or maybe there were other delays somewhere else in the process. The problem isn't in the evidence. The evidence shows that there was a 10% decline in productivity. Now, here is the problem. The problem is the warrant, that which links the evidence to the claim. The assumption that connects productivity fell after we introduced hybrid work to the claim that hybrid work caused the productivity decline. That's where the problem lies, and that is what a fallacy is. That is why fallacies are so important and so useful to study in management. The conclusion may be perfectly plausible, and the manager may even turn out to be right, but a conclusion being right doesn't make the reasoning that got us there sound. And that's the distinction we need to keep coming back to. Good evidence isn't always enough. We have to ask whether the evidence actually supports the claim that we are making. So, this is what I want you to do from now on. For every fallacy that we cover, is the evidence true or false? Instead, ask where exactly does the warrant break? Which means what is missing or the broken link between the evidence and the conclusion.",
