      "transcript_text": "Now, let's look at other types of fallacies that might be of interest to students of the Digital Business Entrepreneurship course like yourselves. These are fallacies about cause and effect. We are looking at 3, slippery slope, appeal to authority, and post hoc. These are types of fallacies that you see in reasoning. These are called causal reasoning fallacies. Now, this connects directly to what we looked at earlier when we talked about inductive reasoning and causal claims. Now, how do we know that one thing actually caused another. This is where things can get really tricky because our minds are very good at spotting patterns, and they jump to conclusions about what these patterns mean. So, we see 2 things happening together, and we assume that one caused the other. We hear an expert make a confident claim and take that as proof because we know or we assume that experts are confident, and when they make confident claims, they are necessarily telling the truth. Or we imagine that one decision will inevitably set off a whole chain of consequences. The problem is that seeing a connection isn't the same as establishing a cause, which means that one thing made the other thing happen. And that's what make these fallacies particularly interesting. They often look like good reasoning. There may be data, a trend, an expert opinion or a sequence of events behind the argument, but a conclusion can still be a leap. Now, that's what we are looking at with slippery slope, appeal to authority, and post-hoc reasoning. Let's start with post hoc ergo propter hoc, which basically means, it's a Latin phrase. It sounds more complicated than what it is, but what it means and what it describes is, \"After this, therefore because of this,\" and that's the Latin phrase. Let's break it down. It basically means this happened after that, so that must have caused it. And honestly, this is something we do all the time. Now, say a startup launches a new Instagram campaign and the website traffic goes up. Now, what are we thinking? That it is because of the Instagram campaign that the website traffic goes up. Now, it's very tempting to connect these dots. We did X, then Y happened, so X caused Y. Now, is that necessarily true? There's a stronger way to think about cause and effect. Now, how is that? Whenever you see a neat story in your data, say for in your analytics classes, you see data and you see that there is a neat story. You see that we changed some, changed something and then something else happened. Now, pause for a moment. Ask what else changed at the same time. That is where you are looking at the evidence in a much more critical way. So, you see that the causes could actually be something else which lead to a particular effect. Let's look at another fallacy. It's called the appeal to authority. It happens, this is also a shortcut. It happens when we accept a claim mainly because someone is important, successful or highly qualified. Now, when they say it, it is very tempting to kind of believe it, and this can be particularly tempting when you are working in a startup. Imagine your founder says, \"I've built 3 companies. Trust me. Customers won't pay more than 499 rupees for this.\" Now, that's certainly worth listening to. Obviously, that's the founder. But is the founder's experience evidence that a product that is you know, priced at 499 rupees is the right product? But is the founder's experience evidence enough that the price that they're suggesting, 499, is the right price? Not necessarily. Maybe the founder has excellent market experience. Maybe they're absolutely right. But you still want to look at the customer data. You need to look at willingness to pay that research. You need to look at competitor pricing, conversion rates at different price points or even conduct an actual pricing experiment. So, if we were to look at this, say with analytics say someone says, \"Our senior data scientist says this model is accurate, so we don't need to question it.\" But we know that even experts can make mistakes, right? And expertise in one area doesn't mean that they have expertise in another. So, the important question isn't, is this person impressive? Do they sound confident or are they authoritative? The important question is, is this person an expert in this particular question, and what evidence supports this claim? So, what should you do when an experienced person, an entrepreneur, founder or senior manager says, \"Trust me, this strategy will work\"? Now, don't dismiss their experience. Use it and ask, \"That's interesting. What makes you think that?\" \"Can we test that assumption against the customer data?\" Now, that's what you will do when you are offering a fallacy-free response. Now, expertise can tell us where to look, but it should not stop us from actually critically looking at evidence. Now, let's look at another fallacy, a causal reasoning fallacy. It's called the slippery slope fallacy. A slippery slope happens when we take one relatively small step and assume it will inevitably lead to a whole chain of increasingly serious consequences. So, it sounds like this. If we allow this, then the next thing will happen, and then something else will happen. And before you know it, everything will fall apart. The problem isn't that those things could happen. Now, they might. We can never know. They might happen. But the problem is assuming that they must happen without showing why one step necessarily leads to the next. Now, if you go back to all of those debates around remote and the hybrid work after the COVID pandemic, there were a lot of arguments like this. If you let people work from home 2 days a week, next, they'll want to work remotely all the time, then productivity will fall. But if you look and look at the chain of events, look at the links that are connecting these events. Does allowing 2 days of remote work necessarily mean people will demand fully remote work? Does fully remote work necessarily mean productivity will fall? Or does lower productivity necessarily mean company culturally will collapse? Not really. Now, each of these things is possible, but possible isn't the same as inevitable. And that's the connection that we discussed earlier about those conditions, about necessary and sufficient conditions that need to exist when we link cause and effect.",
