      "transcript_text": "So, now let's move on to sampling fallacies. We'll look at two, hasty generalization and anecdotal evidence. Until now, we looked at fallacies where the problem is how the argument is constructed. Now let's look at something slightly different. We'll move to the evidence itself in much more critical way. Let's look at how much evidence we actually have and what kind of evidence it is. Now, when we are making inductive arguments, this is especially important. Remember, inductive reasoning doesn't usually give us certainty. We are asking how strong the evidence is and how confidently we can generalize from it. That's inductive reasoning, and that's where sampling becomes important. Now, you can have perfectly reasonable logic but if your evidence comes from a very tiny or a small unrepresentative sample, your conclusion can become very misleading. Let's look at hasty generalization fallacy first. Now, the basic mistake is that we take a small or unusual sample and make a much bigger claim than the evidence can support. Now say, let's take the famous example of New Coke. The popular version of the story is that Coca-Cola made one terrible decision because it tested this new product, it was called New Coke, on very few people. But, and that's the popular story. But that's not actually what happened. Actually, Coca-Cola conducted enormous blind taste tests. About 200,000 people were tested, and the sweeter formula that is the New Coke actually performed really well. But New Coke actually failed. So what went wrong? What was found was that the test wasn't actually measuring. The interesting part is what the test wasn't measuring. People were essentially being asked, \"Which taste do you prefer? The new Coke or the old Coca-Cola?\" And the new Coke did well. 2 lakh people were actually tested, right. And the consensus was, according to taste, the new Coke taste was better. The new Coke did well. But Coca-Cola then had to make a much bigger prediction. Will people want to replace the Coke they already know and love with this new formula? There are two different questions that were being asked. A taste test can tell you something about taste preference. Which one do you like? This one, the old Coca-Cola or the new Coca-Cola with more sugar, it was sweeter. Now, it does not tell you about something that is very important to brands, and that is about how people feel about brand attachment, about nostalgia, identity, or what people will do when a familiar product suddenly disappears from the shelves. So when Coca-Cola had to reckon with this question, people did not want to lose that old nostalgia, and that is why the new Coke, even though they liked it more, failed because the nostalgia for the old Coke was bigger than the slightly sweeter taste of the new Coke. And Coca-Cola, obviously, as we know, went back to the old formula, right. Okay. So the lesson isn't simply don't trust small sample. It's more subtle. Make sure the evidence you're collecting actually represents the behavior you're trying to predict. So the question that Coca-Cola should have asked is, will you be okay if we replace the old formula with the new formula, okay. And I'm sure Coca-Cola would have found that most of their sample would have said, \"No, we like the old formula.\" Now let's go on to this next fallacy that's called anecdotal evidence. Anecdotal evidence fallacy is when we take one person's experience or perhaps a handful of experiences and treat it as evidence of a much broader pattern. Entrepreneurs are particularly vulnerable to this one because many startups begin with a personal experience. You might have heard founders say, \"I had this problem and I couldn't find a good product to solve it, so I built one. If I needed it, other people must need it too.\" There's nothing wrong with starting there. In fact, that's how many good ideas begin. The problem comes when we treat the founder's single experience as evidence that a large market exists for a particular product. Your experience is real but it's still a sample size of one. Now, this anecdotal experience, that single sample size shouldn't be mistaken for proof. So here's the takeaway. And a useful rule for both hasty generalization as well as anecdotal evidence fallacies. A story can give you a question, a sample can give you evidence, but neither will give you automatically a conclusion. As entrepreneurs of the future, you'll constantly be surrounded by dashboards, customer feedback, reviews, surveys, A/B tests, and stories from individual users. The critical thinking move for you is to ask, \"How much can this evidence actually tell me?\" and not, \"Is this evidence true?\" You need to ask, \"Is this enough evidence, and is it representative enough to support this claim that I'm making?\" Now, when you ask those questions, you'll be able to see how fallacies show up when we resist change or rush towards it. Now, this is what we're going to see in action.",
