      "transcript_text": "So far, we've looked at fallacies that can distort evidence, claims, or the connection between them. Now let's look at a very human problem, how we think about change. Sometimes we assume that the old way must be better simply because it's familiar. Sometimes we assume the opposite, the new way must be better simply because it's new. And sometimes we give something extra credibility because it sounds natural. All 3 are shortcuts in our reasoning. Let's look at some of them, right. Appeal to nature. The appeal to nature fallacy is the idea that something must be better or more correct simply because it's natural, organic, or the way things that were originally developed. This sounds convincing because natural has such a positive association. You might hear, for example, in the startup world, organic growth is always healthier than growth through acquisitions. There is a reasonable idea behind this. Organic growth can mean building a customer base gradually, understanding your market, and developing the business from within. But always is doing a lot of work here. We've always done it, so we have to continue doing it. Acquisitions can also be a very effective growth strategy. Now think about Facebook's acquisitions of Instagram and WhatsApp, for example, which became very important parts of the company's broader growth story. So, the question shouldn't be, is this growth organic? It should be, does this particular growth strategy at this point of time make sense for the business in this market? Now, natural doesn't automatically mean better. Let's look at another fallacy. Appeal to tradition. This one is probably the easiest to recognize. It's essentially, we've always done it this way, so there must be a good reason to keep doing it. We've seen this, right. Maybe in college, maybe at an internship, or maybe in a student club that you've been part of. Now, in businesses and management, think about annual performance reviews. For many years, many organizations treated the annual review as simply the way performance management was done. But eventually, companies like Adobe and Microsoft started questioning whether once-a-year conversation was actually the best way to help people improve. And remember, their career trajectories also depended on this once-a-year conversation. Now, both Adobe and Microsoft changed their approaches to performance reviews during the 2010s. The important point here wasn't that annual reviews were always bad. It's that we've always done it this way isn't, by itself, a reason to continue doing it. Now, Adobe and Microsoft saw that tradition can tell you what has been done, but it doesn't always tell you whether it works. And at that point, they found that they needed to change and do things differently. So, when you hear, \"We've always done it this way,\" a useful follow-up question for you to ask is, \"Okay, but is this still working?\" Right. Now, let's look at another fallacy. Appeal to novelty. Now this is the opposite. It's the mirror image. Instead of saying old is better, we assume that new is And we see this everywhere in digital businesses, right. A founder says, \"This is the newest AI-powered platform, so it must outperform the system that we're using now.\" But wait, what does new actually tell us about performance? By itself, it's not telling us anything. We need to see benchmarks. We need to compare results. We need to see the problems that this, the new technology is solving and whether it actually solves better than any other alternative. So, whether someone is defending something because it's traditional, promoting it because it's new, or praising it because it's natural, the critical thinking move is the same. You don't judge the idea by the label. You ask what the evidence is actually telling you.",
