      "transcript_text": "Welcome back. We're going to be talking about cognitive biases and the psychology of bad reasoning today. Last time we looked at how arguments can go wrong, especially the fallacies that exist in arguments, and how you can spot these fallacies in other people's reasoning. Today, we're going to turn that lens on ourselves because spotting flawed reasoning in others is very easy to do. However, catching it in our own thinking is so hard because you know what? Our brains are always taking shortcuts all the time, and often these shortcuts we take without even knowing that we are taking these shortcuts. And even if we are being smart or well-informed, doesn't make us immune to flawed reasoning. So, let's take a look at some of these shortcuts today and how they shape the way that we think. Let's ask this question: Why do smart people make bad decisions? Have you ever made a decision in a case competition, an internship, a negotiation that you may have felt was completely rational in the moment, and only in hindsight later on you realize you had fooled yourself? Now, that is not a character flaw. There's nothing wrong with you, and it's also not a lack of intelligence. What this  is human cognitive architecture, the shape or the structure of the way that our brains process and understand things. And understanding this human cognitive architecture is one of the most underrated skills. And it's a really important skill if you put in the effort that you will carry out of this classroom and into a boardroom, a client meeting, or a negotiation table. See, professionals rarely fail because they can't read a spreadsheet. They fail because they trusted a gut feeling that felt like certainty, and when it was actually a bias that looked like certainty. What we will do going forward is cover 5 ideas, and 4 of them come from the work of the psychologist and Nobel laureate Daniel Kahneman. And the fifth of these ideas shows what happens when organizations and institutions deliberately engineer these very same biases, but at scale. First, the architecture itself, what Kahneman calls System 1 and System 2 thinking, or like we just said, thinking fast and thinking slow. Second, we look at confirmation bias. That is our tendency to seek out and favor that information that agrees with what we already believe in. And the third is the availability heuristic. Why the examples that come to mind when there is a problem or a solution that you're trying to find for the problem, there's some examples that come to mind that most easily feel like it's the most likely truth, even when they aren't. Fourth, anchoring. How an early, often arbitrary number that comes right at the beginning can silently control every other judgment that follows it. And fifth, building directly on these in-group biases and social proof that the first four ideas are about, we'll continue to look at the fifth idea. That is the propaganda and manufactured consent. Now, this is what happens when organizations, brands, and institutions learn or create these same biases that exist in each of us very deliberately but at scale. So, let's begin.",
