      "transcript_text": "One, thinking, Fast and Slow: The 2 Systems. Let's look at the system one versus system 2. Daniel Kahneman's central metaphor, which he lays out in the opening chapter of his book, Thinking, Fast and Slow, is that we don't have one mode of thinking. We have 2, and they operate almost like two different characters living inside the same head. And he names these 2 different characters system 1 and system 2. System one is fast. It's automatic. It's the part of your mind that reacts before you've had time to even think. Say, for example, if I show you a photograph of an angry face, how do you know it is angry? You know. You don't calculate it. You don't think about it. You don't unpack it. You just perceive it as angry instantly. If I ask you what 2 plus 2 is, the answer, four, arrives before you've consciously solved any of this, right. I mean, it's not a big problem. Immediately you get the answer that it is 4. So, this is system one, and system one handles things like, you know, if you're on a empty road, you're driving automatically, right. You can read the tension on somebody's face, say a client's face across the table. You're not perceiving things in a very deliberate fashion. Or say, for example, if I give you a phrase, bread and What is it going to be? Yes, butter, right. Bread and jam also maybe, right. But what you are reacting to is the sound more than the actual meaning of the words itself. Now, System 1 thinking requires almost no effort, and this is a very important part. It never feels like a choice. It feels like you're simply seeing the world as it is. That was System 1 thinking. System 2, on the other hand, is the slow, deliberate kind of thinking according to Daniel Kahneman. Now, it's this part of you that engages when you multiply a large number, say, for example, 17 by  24 in your head, or when you're comparing two job offers across multiple criteria, or working through a, say, a discounted cash flow model, or even trying to park a car in a very tight spot. This is all very, very deliberate, very strategic, a very slow kind of thinking. So, System 2 is full of effort. It gets tired, and very importantly, it's also lazy by default. See, System 2 will only switch on when System 1 can't handle a problem or senses that something has gone wrong. Now, here's the question that matters for us all. Now, which of these 2 systems do you think is running the show most of the time? In your day, in your decisions, whether it's on the job, in class, or anywhere else Yes, it's system one. Most times it is system one, right. So, almost always we want to do things which are not too much effort, which don't get you tired, which are somewhat instinctive in nature. So, Daniel Kahneman's argument, and this is the crux of his entire book, Thinking Fast and Slow, is that system one is not a minor background process. It is the default architect or the author of most of our judgments. System 2 likes to think that it is in charge, or we like to think that System 2 is in charge. We want to think that we are always putting in  the time and the effort that is needed for rational executive decision-making. But in reality, System 2 often just rubber stamps whatever System 1 has already concluded. And in the end, we create a nice, neat, confident-sounding justification which we are very happy with. So, why does this matter in a professional setting? Because the corporate world, businesses, they run on uncertainty. There are market signals which are ambiguous, incomplete data, a client who won't tell you what they actually want, and that is exactly the environment in which System 1 loves to take over. So, when there isn't enough information, System 1 does not stop and say, \"Hmm, I don't know. I don't know the answer,\" or, \"I don't know the solution.\" What it will do is, it is kind of dangerous, but what it does is it fills in these gaps that \"I don't know,\" it   erases, and it creates very confident-sounding answers. Now, Kahneman describes this as the mind's tendency to construct the best possible story from whatever small, limited evidence that we have available. And then the mind actually believes the story completely. Even if we don't have much evidence, we tend to believe the story that we have constructed from the System 1 thinking. And that is why managers will walk out of a single client meeting  very enthusiastically and declare, \"We've cracked this account.\" And this is on just the basis of one conversation, right. System 1 thinking doesn't wait for big statistical significance. It wants a story, and it wants it now. Now, there are 2 implications for students like all of you, 2 implications for business decisions. Now, one is that intuition itself is not inherently wrong. So, experienced professionals like a seasoned investor  an operations head with, say, 15 years of experience, all of them have genuinely well-calibrated system 1 instincts. This is because they have, through their many years of experience, built up something called pattern recognition. And the problem is not that for them, fast thinking. What they have been able to do is to recognize patterns, and those patterns are always successful in nature. So, they have been able to recognize successful patterns. And so the problem, again, is not that fast thinking exists or that someone is doing fast thinking. The problem is not knowing when to trust it and when to override this fast thinking. We were just talking about the seasoned  investors, right. So, they are able to recognize these successful patterns, and they know when to trust their fast thinking and when to say something is not quite right and to override their own fast thinking. The moments that require slow, deliberate System 2 thinking, that is in, say, pricing decisions, hiring decisions, or whether to walk away from a deal, are exactly the moments when  you know, most of us are very tempted, whether it is out of fatigue or time pressure, to let System 1 quietly make the call instead. I think we are all guilty of doing that. Now, keep that tension in mind, right. When we are fatigued, we tend to kind of gravitate towards System 1 thinking. So, every bias that we discuss going forward is really a variation on the same underlying story. System 1 taking a shortcut and System 2 being too tired, too rushed, or too willing to go along with the whole process of critical thinking.",
