      "transcript_text": "Lets, talk about the first and arguably the most business relevant of these cognitive shortcuts that we take in reasoning, confirmation bias. Confirmation bias is our tendency to search for or notice, interpret, and remember information in a way that confirms with what we already believe, and to overlook, dismiss, or explain away anything that is contradicting this kind of thinking. Kahneman connects this directly back to System 1. Once System 1 has made this initial impression, whether it is about a person, a proposal, or a business opportunity, System 2 thinking doesn't usually go looking for reasons that critically unpacks this particular proposal or a person or a business opportunity. Instead, it goes looking for reasons to actually say that your first impression is the right impression. So, what Daniel Kahneman describes this is, it's as a broad pattern, as a kind of built-in bias toward believing and confirming rather than doing the critical work of doubting and disconfirming. So, our default setting is to accept a story as true first, and only question it if something forces us to. Now, let's turn this back to you. Think about the last term you were genuinely very excited about a business idea or a strategy recommendation. Did you spend more time searching for reasons that it would work or reasons that it might fail? So most of us, if we are actually honest, spend far more energy kind of looking for reasons to see if the idea will actually work because we are emotionally invested in it, and again, this is not laziness. It is actually efficiency. It is cognitive efficiency in a narrow sense, but it's still cognitive efficiency because confirming something is actually easier than disconfirming it, right. So, it requires actively imagining how you might be wrong. When you're disconfirming something, it requires a lot of work on your part. And remember, System 2 by default is lazy. Confirmation bias exists across the business context. Say, for instance, in a corporation or a corporate context, confirmation bias shows up everywhere. In market research, where the team asks very leading questions, almost guaranteed to produce answers that validate the questions they're asking. Say, for example, a question like, \"Wouldn't you love a product that does X, Y, and Z?\" Rather than asking neutral questions that could actually go against the idea that they're proposing. In hiring decisions, say when an interviewer forms a snap judgment about a candidate in the first 90 seconds, and then spends the rest of the interview unconsciously collecting evidence to kind of justify the first impression. We've seen these same examples even in previous modules, right. So, these  biases continue to exist everywhere,  in every kind of reasoning. So, our minds are remarkably good at building a confident, coherent story out of whatever limited evidence happens to be in front of us. And unfortunately, we're kind of bad at pausing and asking us, \"Hey, what is the evidence that is missing?\" Now, this is what you need to do. You need to find a counter. You need to find an antidote to what your mind is asking you to do, because you're the professional, right. And you are going to be the critical thinker, so you need to find an antidote. What's the antidote? It's not willpower. You can't simply say, \"Try harder if you want to be unbiased,\" because the bias operates before you're even aware that you have actually made a judgment, right. So, this antidote that you need to skill up to is actually structural. So, you need to build a habit, and even better, you need to build a process of actively seeking out evidence that is  disconfirming in nature. So, before you commit to a big decision, ask yourself very deliberately, very strategically, \"What evidence would prove me wrong here? And have I actually gone looking for it?\" You can actually ask one of your friends or someone on the team formally to play devil's advocate on every major recommendation.",
