      "transcript_text": "Our second bias is that we are going to be looking at is the availability heuristic, and it comes from a very simple mental substitution that Kahneman describes System 1 as constantly making. When we are asked how likely or how frequent something is, we don't sit and calculate probability. Actually, nobody does that intuitively. Instead, what we do is System 1 thinking, right. System 1 substitutes an easier question for this question of how likely or frequent something is. So, the simpler or easier question is, how easily can I recall an example of something? And then it treats this ease of recall. We immediately remembered something as if it were the answer to this original question about frequency or likelihood. So, the question of how likely are you to do something becomes a question of what can I remember easily and what can I recall from recent times. So, the problem, of course, is that ease of recall and actual frequency are two very different things, and there are all sorts of factors that have nothing to do with the real-world probability, right. How recent an event was or how vividly we remember it or how much media coverage something received or simply how memorable a story was is not the same time as how likely or how many times we you know, that event occurred, right. I'm going to ask you another question. It's a little macabre. Which of these questions feels like a bigger risk to most people, dying in a plane crash or dying in a car accident? Almost everyone feels that plane crashes are most dramatic and salient danger. Most people will answer dying in a plane crash is a bigger risk to most people than dying in a car accident because plane crashes dominate news cycles, they generate hours of coverage and leave very vivid  images in our minds. Yet when we look at the data, a completely different story emerges. The bigger risk is a car accident, and the data shows that most people die by car accidents than by plane crashes. But we still keep thinking that the plane crashes are the more terrifying, more frequently occurring instances. Car accidents are common. They're dispersed, and news outlets rarely treat them as big national news. So, there is that gap, and that gap between what is vivid and easy to recall and what is a very common thing is the availability heuristic. Now, let's look at this in a corporate world. A manager reads about one spectacular success story that is a vivid, widely covered, endlessly discussed at every conference kind of story. Say the story of Zerodha and Nithin Kamath, right. It's a very spectacular success story, and that story becomes available, constantly available in their mind whenever they think about the likely outcome of any kind of a similar strategy. Now, the other very quiet, unremarkable failures that Nithin Kamath has probably had never made the headlines, and therefore they're not available to this manager's memory. Nobody even wrote those small case studies about those many failures, right. So, the result is a distorted sense of the actual odds. Now, how do we correct it? What is the antidote? The antidote here is deceptively simple, but it is quite hard to practice. Here is what you need to do. When you notice yourself judging something as likely or unlikely, common or rare, stop and ask yourself, \"Am I basing this on actual data or on whatever or whichever example that comes to mind fastest?\" Whenever possible, you have to try and replace this vivid anecdote or an example with actual base rates. What does the broader data actually say and not what the loudest or the most vivid actually is suggesting.",
