      "transcript_text": "Let's now look at a third bias that is anchoring. Now, anchoring is one of the most well-replicated findings in all of behavioural science. And Daniel Kahneman devotes a lot of attention to anchoring because of how strange and how strong this effect of anchoring really is. Anchoring occurs when an initial number, even when that is a completely arbitrary number and does not have any logical connection to the judgment at hand, ends up influencing a subsequent numerical estimate. You would expect or you'd want your mind to simply ignore an irrelevant number that comes after this initial number, but it doesn't. What it does, your mind, is it clings to this irrelevant number, which has anchored at the very beginning. So, it clings to it. So, Kahneman uses a very, very important  and quite a famous example. Daniel Kahneman describes this famous sort of example. Participants were shown a spinning wheel that was secretly rigged to stop on either a low number or a high number, and nothing more than chance. Low or high. They were then asked an entirely unrelated question, say something like, \"What is percentage of countries or estimate of a percentage of countries in a certain region of Africa?\" So, participants who had actually seen a high number on the wheel gave very high estimates to the number of countries in the  in a particular region of the African continent. And those participants who had seen a low number on the spinning wheel gave a lower number on the question about how many nations are there in a particular How random is that, right. A spinning wheel number that you see gets fixated into, in your mind, an estimate of the number of African countries in a particular region of the continent, or produces different estimates.",
