      "transcript_text": "So, let's bring it all together for a moment and connect all five ideas, because they're not really 5 separate biases. They're part of the same underlying story, but they're playing at very  let's say 2 different scales. At the individual level, system 1, wants a fast, coherent, confident answer to almost every question we face. Confirmation bias then helps protect that answer or reinforce that answer once we have it. Availability heuristic gives us very vivid, memorable examples that help build the answer in the first place. Anchoring sets the starting point, and from there we begin to make a particular judgment. Social proof allows the people around us to reinforce the whole thing. A shaky answer can start to feel solid simply because everyone else seems to believe it too. Then we begin to move to the larger scale, right. We looked at propaganda. Propaganda is what happens when someone else learns how to pull all four of these levers for you, and they're doing it deliberately and repeatedly. So, before you've even stopped to ask whether the answer you're given is actually your own answer, propaganda is already at play. So, here is a question that you need to ask yourself. When was the last time you found a business narrative that is a pitch, a report, or leadership announcement completely convincing? And once you looked at it, did you check what was in it, and very importantly, what was left out? Remember, none of this means that you should distrust your own instinct or every piece of communication that you come across. You have to remember that expert intuition that is built over many, many years of actual genuine experience. Remember what we were talking about, how to recognize successful patterns. That's very real, and it's very valuable. Daniel Kahneman himself says so, right. However, what this also means is the higher the stakes of a decision, the greater the uncertainty or the persuasive pressure that is surrounding it. So, you have to more deliberately need to build in those moments of saying, \"Hey, let me pause here. Let me play a devil's advocate,\" or, \"Let me write this pre-written independent estimate. Let me look at a genuine search for what is missing.\" You need to do all of this, and you need to pause, right, to do all of this. Precisely because neither your own mind nor the messages that you will receive will give you that pause. So, you need to take that pause in order to do things critically, right. You need to think critically. So, in the real, this professional edge that we're trying to get you to develop via critical thinking, you have to remember  that professional edge is not about having better instincts than everyone else. But that professional edge comes from knowing exactly when not to trust these own, what you feel naturally, right, those instincts all the time. And to know exactly when you see that a story is a little too familiar or a little too convenient to be entirely true. So, that is where your real professional edge comes from.",
