          "model_answer": "The difference between an epistemic bubble and an echo chamber lies in the nature of the missing or discredited outside voices and the subject's responsiveness to new evidence.\n\n*   **Epistemic Bubble (Maya):** An epistemic bubble occurs when relevant voices are simply left out of a person's information diet. In the lecture, Maya is an example of this; her Instagram feed, friends, and habits never brought up mainstream news. Because the information was merely absent (missing), not actively forbidden, the bubble is fragile. When Maya is shown the missing news or evidence, she is open to changing her mind because she was not pre-disposed to distrust the source.\n\n*   **Echo Chamber (Manan):** An echo chamber is not merely a lack of outside voices, but a constructed environment where outside voices have been discredited in advance. Manan's example illustrates this; his family and friends taught him that anyone outside the close-knit group is not to be trusted. When shown the same evidence as Maya, Manan does not change his mind; instead, he digs in deeper and rejects the evidence.\n\n*   **Effect on Questioning Expertise:** This distinction is critical for a critical thinker. If one assumes a person is in an epistemic bubble, the strategy to help them is to populate their information diet with the missing voices or evidence. If one assumes a person is in an echo chamber, simply presenting more evidence will likely fail and may entrench the person further. Effective questioning requires diagnosing whether the person is missing information (bubble) or actively rejecting it due to group affiliation (chamber).",
