      "created_at": "2026-11-06 08:57:17",
      "transcript_text": "Let's now go on to discussing the social construction of value. Let me tell you a story. In January 2007, a man in jeans and a baseball cap stood in a Washington, D.C., in the US, metro station and played violin for about 45 minutes. Do you know who it was? Nobody knew. People were walking around him. They just heard the music. Very nice violinist. But who was actually playing was one of the best violinists in the world called Joshua Bell, and he was playing on an instrument which was worth millions. In fact, a few days earlier, he had sold out a concert hall where some of the good seats cost well over $100. But in the station in Washington, D.C., Almost nobody really stopped. They heard, like I was saying earlier, they heard the music. They kept on going about their work. Now, Joshua Bell collected a little over $30. The same man, same music, same violin. But the difference, where was it? It wasn't in the sound. It was in the setting. The hall, the ticket, the reputation, and the crowd all around was telling Joshua Bell that he was worth all of this money and the attention, and that I would like for us to talk a little bit about today, right. We, when we talk about value, we tend to talk about it, value of things, as being inbuilt into things, like it is some weight or a color of a particular object. What we need to think as critical thinkers is that the value is actually not just inherent, it's not intrinsic to something, some art, but it's a lot to do with the outside elements like institutions, markets, brands, and reputations which give an object its value. So, it is kind of built, right. Not all of it, but most of the value of things is given by or is built by institutions, markets, brands, and reputations. Let's look at five things, right. Intrinsic versus extrinsic value, how institutions create value, what price actually measures brand equity and reputation and legitimacy. Let's start with intrinsic versus extrinsic value. When we say something is valuable, we typically mean 2 different things, which are quite different actually. Philosophers use intrinsic and extrinsic in a couple of ways. The first is about why we value something. Extrinsic value is value as a means to something else. A hammer by itself may not really do much, but when you put it to a nail, it becomes valuable. Intrinsic value is value for its own sake. Say, for example, happiness or friendship or a person's life. All of these are valuable on their own. Let's look at the idea of the diamond. How do we now assume that the diamond means love, right. And it's typically  in the US sense, I think it's two months' salary, or here in India it might be an entire year's salary. Now, how did that diamond get that particular value? In the 1940s, the De Beers company and its ad agency ran a campaign that was built around this tagline, \"A diamond is forever,\" and it reshaped the engagement ring market. The carbon, which is what diamond is made up, was always there, but the meaning and much of the price was added. Or take the Mona Lisa, for example intrinsically is it more valuable than a skilled copy that looks very, very identical to the eye? If we looked at it side by side, the original and the copy, we might not know which is which, and they might look very, very alike, right. But when we remove the copy and we only have the Mona Lisa, the original, we tend to see a lot of value in it because of its history, its authenticity, and its place in a story. All of that is relational. So, much of what we call a thing's value is actually a fact about its relationship with  the viewer, the listener, the receiver. So, it is socially constructed, but it does not mean fake or it's arbitrary. For example, money is socially constructed, but it's still very real and it buys things for us, right. The question is who builds value? How is it built? And whether we notice that it is built. Let's look at how institutions create value. A museum doesn't only display value. It actually confers value. When you put an object in a glass case with a light and a label, you've told every visitor, \"Pay attention, this matters.\" The sociologist Pierre Bourdieu argued that cultural fields run on a kind of currency that isn't money, and he called this symbolic or cultural capital. Critics, curators, all of them decide what counts as good taste. Let's look at another example of how value is created via rankings. When we see that institutional rankings, for instance, actually don't just measure schools, but rankings actually change the schools themselves. For example, rankings change the way in which people work within an institution. When you look at certifications, things like organic labels, Michelin stars, fair trademarks, academic accreditation, all of these are really good solutions because we can't often judge the quality of something by ourselves. So, we can look at  a vegetable which is marked organic and know that it is a good  object for you to consume. However, the label stands in for knowledge that we don't have, and that's useful. But the label also creates a line, and we might think that we have to trust the label than we trust ourselves and our judgment. So, just as a reminder, let's think about how value is created. As we've discussed, value is constructed, but just because it's constructed, it does not mean it is not valid or it is not authentic. All right, you can build a lot with a particular story and an endorsement of value, but you can't build it forever. And if particularly a claim that this value is resting on is a false claim, then value tends to also fall apart, and that is your job to do as a critical thinker. It all comes back to trust. So, reputation and legitimacy are also the social forms of the credibility that we talk about when we talk about experts. They're what lets strangers rely on each other without having to check everything. Imagine the amount of work you'd be doing, right. So, a label which says organic And if you've trusted it in the past, you've consumed that object in the past, looking at the same label you can say, \"Okay, it's organic and I've eaten it in the past, so I can trust it.\" So, reputation and legitimacy  and credibility, all of these are important when we start to talk about the experts, expertise, and how value itself is socially constructed. Let's go back to the metro station. Joshua Bell's music didn't change when he walked from the concert hall, which was packed, and they, everyone knew that it was Joshua Hall, to the platform where he was incognito and no one knew who he was. What changed was everything that was around it, the ticket price, the venue, the reputation, and the crowd. That is the machinery that tells us what to value, right. The outside, the trappings, if you will. So, the lesson isn't that value is fake, it's that value is made, it's socially constructed, and it's made by people like experts and the institutions with their own expertise, with some vested interests. Museums, critics, experts, rankings, prizes, brands, reputations, all of these are useful, but each one of them is also a lens which shapes what we see and how we see.",
