      "flashcards": [
        {
          "front": "Why do engineers, product managers, and designers become territorial about their work?",
          "back": "They have put in significant time and effort into perfecting their specific contributions and take pride in their work, making them attached to their output."
        },
        {
          "front": "How does a researcher establish credibility when their findings are questioned?",
          "back": "By referencing statistically significant sample sizes and methodological rigor, such as a 95% confidence level from 100 respondents, rather than relying on authority alone."
        },
        {
          "front": "Why can't researchers always rely on historical benchmarks for novel products?",
          "back": "For truly novel products (like the automobile or iPhone), customers cannot imagine what does not exist; historical data from unrelated products (like soap) is insufficient, so researchers must use proxy behaviors from adjacent functions."
        },
        {
          "front": "What is the researcher's recommended approach when facing a novel innovation with no customer benchmarks (e.g., AI summarization)?",
          "back": "Look at the closest immediate behaviors or case histories from adjacent functions (e.g., how HR, admin, and IT currently summarize issues) to build a proxy for adoption."
        }
      ],
