      "transcript_text": "This is also very important when you are launching a new product and you're forecasting, right. So, you could say we'll sell 10,000 units next quarter. That sounds very precise, but precision isn't the same as accuracy. So, think about this in another way. It's the same estimate. Our best estimate is about 10,000 units, but there is a meaningful chance that we'll sell less or more than this 10,000 units. Now you've got a range of possibilities rather than one number that looks more certain than it really is, and that changes how you make decisions. If there's a reasonable chance that demand could be much higher than expected, you may need enough inventory and capacity to respond, right. And if there's a significant chance that demand could be much lower than those 10,000 units, you might avoid locking yourself into some commitments of inventory or supply, right. That decision isn't based on one precise number, 10,000 units. It's based on the distribution of possible outcomes. It could either go higher than 10,000 units or much lesser also than those 10,000 units, and that's the important shift that you need to make as a thinker. See, remember, you're not asking what will happen. You're asking, what are the plausible things that could happen, how likely is each of these possible things, and what should we do given that there is uncertainty? And why is that important for us? Or this isn't just something that we do in the classroom, right. Businesses make these decisions every day, as we've been talking about, whether it is inventory, staffing, marketing budgets, pricing, product launches, supplier commitments. These judgments are being made every day by decision-makers. So, you need to become really good at doing this. Now consider the opposite situation. A business makes a five-year plan based on one forecast that everyone starts treating as facts. Revenue will grow at this rate. Customers will behave this way. This technology will develop at this pace. This market will look roughly the same 5 years from now. Now, the further that you're getting from the present, the more assumptions you're making, right. Customers will behave this way, all of those. Those are assumptions that you're making. And these assumptions are written sometimes into the spreadsheets, and they will start looking like facts. And that is where miscalibration becomes dangerous. The problem isn't that the forecast might be wrong, that yes, that revenue will grow at a particular rate. The problem the bigger problem is that the organization  make large, very difficult-to-reverse decisions as though that forecast was certain. And unfortunately, that's something that we've seen a lot of in businesses. Now, there are companies that commit heavily to stores, to warehouses, inventory, or other fixed costs just as consumer behavior is beginning to change. And that issue isn't that they fail to predict the future perfectly. Nobody can predict the future. The issue is whether they recognized how uncertain that future actually was, and that's the kind of mindset that we have to build here. So, when you hear a forecast, don't just ask, \"What's the number?\" Ask, \"How confident are we in that number? What assumptions is that based on?\" Right. \"What happens if we are wrong?\" And also ask this very important question: \"What would make us change our minds?\" And that is what calibrated judgment looks like. You don't need certainty to make a good decision. You need to know how uncertain you are, and make sure your confidence doesn't become bigger than the evidence. Let's try and make this concrete, right. Imagine there's a founder who's sitting across the table from an investor and saying, \"We will hit $2 million in revenue next year.\" Sounds very confident. And for the investor or anyone who's listening, confidence can be very reassuring. But what does that number actually tell the investor about the risk? Not much, right. Now, imagine the founder says instead, \"Our current estimate is that there's a 60% chance we'll reach somewhere between $1.5 million to $2.5 million.\" There's about a 15% chance we could exceed $3 million if our enterprise pilot converts, and about a 10% chance that we could fall below $1 million if that pilot stalls. Now, that's a very different conversation, right. The second statement isn't pessimistic as compared to the first statement about hitting a particular number very definitely. What the second statement is doing is simply being more honest about what is known and what is not known. So, ask yourself which version, right, the first or the second, gives an investor more useful information and why that first statement about very definite numbers, very optimistic-sounding numbers, are actually hiding risk rather than reducing risk. Because $2 million looks like knowledge, but actually it might be just a guess. Again, you can't predict the future, right. Now, the second version of maybe $1 million or maybe $3 million, depending on the pilot, right. The second version makes the uncertainty visible, and once you can see the uncertainty, you can actually make decisions around it. So, this is a calibration habit that I want you all to kind of practice. How do you do this? Before you make a business conclusion, pause and ask yourself these 3 question. First, how confident am I? Don't just say likely or very likelier. Try putting a rough number on it. I'm about 70% confident. And even if that number isn't really perfect, it you know, it forces you to quantify your confidence, and therefore it makes you think differently. Second question to ask yourself is what would have to be true for me to be wrong? What assumption am I relying on? Is there something that could change? What evidence would make me reconsider my conclusion? And here's another question, the third question. Have I been right this often before when I felt this confident? And this is the part that most of us skip, right. We don't wanna ask ourselves, have I been right this often or this confident before, right. So if you regularly say, \"I'm 90% sure,\" but you're wrong about half the time, your confidence level is not calibrated. And that's important because this connects directly to what we were doing all of that, right. And  that's an important question to ask yourself because this connects to what we were doing earlier in this course with inductive reasoning. Remember, an inductive argument isn't simply a strong or a weak argument. You're making a judgment about how much confidence the evidence deserves. You are actually evaluating the evidence. And if you are a calibrated thinker, which of course you're going to be, a calibrated thinker gets into this habit of asking, how confident should I actually be given that this is the evidence that I have? Then with practice, over time, you can look back and see whether your confidence matched reality. And that's how judgment improves. So, calibration gives us a way to talk about how confident we should be. But there's another trap that we have to be a little careful about. Sometimes we see a very complicated situation and think, \"I just don't understand what's going on.\" But you need to learn to tell the difference, right. I mean, that is what we need to kind of learn. The difference between complexity and confusion. Just because something looks complex, you can't let yourself get confused and say, \"I don't understand this. I don't want to deal with this.\"",
