      "transcript_text": "So, let's look at some practical frameworks for structured analysis in professional context. We've looked at some of these basic habits, the 3 basic habits of analytical thinking. We are you know, we talked about generating competing explanations, being honest about uncertainty, breaking down complicated problems into manageable parts, 3 habits. Now we need something that helps us put all of this into practice. That's the question you're probably asking, right. How do I do this in a practical sense? What you have to do is to learn to apply frameworks. A framework is basically a structure you can use to organize your thinking instead of starting from scratch every time. You probably already come across some of them. So, here are a couple of frameworks. A framework like MECE, which is where you are breaking a problem into parts that are mutually exclusive and collectively exhaustive. That is what the acronym MECE stands for, mutually exclusive and collectively exhaustive. Then there is the pyramid principle framework, where you're putting your main conclusion upfront and then organizing the evidence underneath it. There are also other frameworks like the hypothesis-driven analysis, where you are starting with a possible explanation and then looking for evidence that can test this hypothesis. There is you know, there are other frameworks like the OODA loop, which is observe, orient, decide, act, which is useful when you're making decisions in situations that are constantly changing. Now, these frameworks which help you structure information can be very useful. A framework is a simplification. You have to remember this. That is both its strength and its weakness. It is useful precisely because it takes something that is messy with many, many interacting paths and gives you a structure that you can work with. But the moment that you forget that you're looking at a simplified version of reality, the framework can become misleading. So, I'm going to use an example of this really wonderful essay about ChatGPT. Ted Chiang, the writer for The New Yorker, wrote in his essay in 2023. He writes in his essay, \"The blurry JPEG that is ChatGPT.\" He describes ChatGPT using the analogy of a blurry JPEG of the web. So, think about what happens when you compress a huge high-resolution image into a much smaller file. You preserve something important. You can still recognize the picture, but what you lose is the detail, Ted Chiang argued. So, fine textures disappear, some small features get flattened, and some information is simply no longer there. So, what Ted Chiang does is to use that idea about the blurry JPEG that ChatGPT is supposed to be to think about what happens when a huge amount of information is compressed into a system that can produce something that looks coherent and convincing, and that gives us a useful way to think about business frameworks too. A framework takes a messy business situation with hundreds of variables, conflicting signals, different stakeholders, historical context, and compresses it into a few categories. There are 4 boxes, there are 5 forces, a 2-by-2 matrix, a neat hypothesis, and that's why frameworks are so useful. You can actually use them in a meeting, but you can't put the entire messy reality of a company on one PowerPoint slide, right. But here's the catch. The framework didn't make the underlying problem less complicated. What it did was to simply compress that complexity into a structure that is easier to work with, and when you compress something, you do lose information, but that's not a problem. The problem comes when you forget what was lost. Think about SWOT analysis, where you're looking at strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats. Now, this analysis is useful because it gives you 4 simple categories for thinking about a company's situation. But here's the interesting part. Suppose a competitor suddenly cuts its prices. Is that a threat? Obviously, but could it also be an opportunity if it allows your company to position itself around quality or differentiation? Possibly. So, which box does it belong to? The framework doesn't necessarily tell you. Two analysts can put the same piece of information in different boxes. It could be a strength or a weakness. Now, that's not because they can't use the SWOT analysis framework properly. It's because the underlying business reality is actually much more ambiguous than these 4 boxes, that is strength, weakness, opportunity or threats suggest. So, let's take this other example. Imagine you're analyzing falling customer retention, and you decide to use a hypothesis-driven approach. You look at the data and come up with a nice, clean hypothesis. Retention is falling because we recently increased prices, great. You've got a hypothesis. You've got a framework, the hypothesis-driven framework. You've got an answer that looks presentable, but before you accept it, you need to stop. Remember the blurry JPEG? Remember what you've compressed out of the picture? Did you actually consider other explanations? Maybe onboarding has become more difficult, or maybe a competitor has included other features, or maybe a competitor introduced some new features that customers really wanted. There are other maybes, right. Did we test those possibilities, or did we simply pick the first hypothesis that gave us a neat story? And that's the question that I want you to keep asking when you use any analytical framework. Is it true or it just looks like it is the truest looking picture? So, here's a practical  habit for using any framework. Use these frameworks. You need to use them. Don't throw them away just because they're imperfect. Start with the framework. Let it give you a structured way to go through the problem. That's a very valuable skill to develop and to organize your thoughts, identify gaps, communicate quickly, and move a decision forward. But don't forget to take a second look. You have to ask yourself, \"What did this framework leave out? What got flattened? What didn't quite fit into one of the categories?\" Treat the framework as a first draft of your thinking and not the final truth, and that's kind of similar to the lesson that we should be taking away from the blurry JPEG analogy of Ted Chiang, right, in the New Yorker essay. A compressed version can be useful. It can also be very good, but it is not the full resolution original. So, let's kind of get bring everything that we have covered in this module together. Learn to develop competing explanations so you can stop grabbing the first nice, neat story. Learn to calibrate because that forces you to be honest about how confident you should actually be. And learn the distinction between complexity and confusion, which will stop you from giving up on a problem just because it looks messy, and also stops you from pretending that a genuinely complex system has one simple explanation. Analytical frameworks give you a practical way to bring all of these various things that we have discussed together when you're working under pressure, but there is one principle that you need to remember. A clean answer or a clean solution isn't necessarily a true answer or a true solution. A neat framework isn't always a complete picture, and a confident conclusion isn't always a well-supported one. As an analyst, as a critical-thinking analyst, especially as an entrepreneur, your job isn't to make reality look neat. Your job is to understand what the evidence is, what it can actually support, what ideas it can actually support, while staying aware of everything that your frameworks, your methods, your choices may have left out.",
