      "transcript_text": "So, by now you know that critical thinking is not merely an academic exercise. It's not simply a matter of identifying fallacies in a textbook or memorizing the forms of valid deduction. What critical thinking is at its heart is a practical discipline. It is the discipline of thinking well when the stakes are real. It is also the ability to evaluate information carefully, challenge assumptions, compare options logically, and use evidence to understand risks rather than acting on impulse or, you know, feelings. I feel like this, that's why I'm going to act in a particular way, right? It's not that. What we have been doing throughout this course is recognizing that human beings make thousands of decisions every day. Some are not so important, some are very important, some only affect us, and other decisions affect teams, organizations, communities, families, and even public, right, at large. So, every one of these decisions requires you to make some form of reasoning. And very often, reasoning becomes visible through the argument. We justify through reason, we persuade through reason, we object, we defend through reasoning in an argument. We also do other things. For example, we propose something new, we interpret a complex problem, we evaluate what seems normal or regular and see the complex layers in that particular issue or a  problem, and we decide. All of these we do through reasoning, through argumentation. And we have to understand that argumentation is not a culturally neutral thing, right. And  because we as humans don't reason with our mind somewhere and our heart somewhere. We reason as what's known as situated human beings, as human beings who belong to certain cultures. Let me push that line of reasoning a little bit more. Think about this. We reason inside languages, inside histories, religions, regions, professional formations, family patterns, habits, national, international habits, and even within institutional norms. And each of these sites teach us to act or teach us to reason and argue in particular ways, and these are cultural sites. In these sites, the way we present evidence, the way that we appeal to authority, the way that we disagree, the way we persuade, we preserve dignity, or we use silence, or we tell stories and build trust, all of these are shaped by culture. So, that is why we have to be super careful when we use the word critical thinking. See, if you're not attentive, we can accidentally turn critical thinking into nothing more than, \"Think like me.\" We can mistake our own cultural habits for universal standards of reason. I think like this in my culture, therefore everybody everywhere in the world and every other culture should also naturally think like me. And that is a problem, right. We can also treat something that is familiar as validity. Because we have seen something that is close to us, we might think, \"Oh, that is the only valid reason.\" We can treat unfamiliar forms of argument, something that we're not used to as, \"Oh, that is less than, that's inferior. It is confusing, evasive, or it's not  as professional as I am and the way that I reason things.\" But that is not critical thinking at all. What that  is ethnocentrism, thinking that my culture is the center of all other cultures. And especially in a globalized world, we need to fight that sort of a thinking, right, that ethnocentric kind of thinking. So, we know that we are constantly communicating across cultural boundaries. Say, for example, a manager in New York negotiates with a supplier in Chennai. A startup founder in Berlin pitches to investors in Mumbai. A policy analyst in Washington interprets data from Lagos. A marketing team in London designs a campaign for consumers in Kolkata. There are several other examples like this, and in all of these situations, people are not merely exchanging information. They are arguing. They are persuading. They are justifying. They are objecting, proposing, evaluating. And what they're doing is, or how they're doing this, is through cultural lenses that may not be obvious to the other person, anyone, or even anyone else in the room. So, what we're going to be doing today is to study how argumentations changes across contexts. And we will ask questions such as, what counts as good evidence in different cultures? Who is considered a credible authority? How is persuasion accomplished? Is directness or indirectness better? Is storytelling the rational or irrational form of reasoning? All of these things and all of these questions we are going to use to examine whether or not we are good at critical thinking. To explore these various questions, we are going to depart from our normal way of doing things. We are actually going to focus on the Indian tradition, especially the classical Indian systems of logic known as Nyaya and Tarka. We will also examine oral and written argumentation traditions. We'll look at face-saving and high-context communication and the practical implications for global business communication. But here is a small warning. When we speak about Indian communication or Western communication or high-context cultures or low-context cultures, we are dealing with very broad patterns and not absolute laws, because no culture is monolithic. Culture is complex, it's complicated. Let's look at India itself. It's enormous linguistically, regionally, religiously, economically, and socially. A business conversation in Mumbai between 2 Mumbaikars may sound very different to someone who's sitting in Guwahati. A family firm in Surat may operate very different from a technology startup in Bengaluru. A government office in Delhi may have different norms from a manufacturing unit in Coimbatore. So, what we are saying is that not all Indians argue this way or all Americans argue that way. Now, that itself would be a single story, and as we will see today, the single story is precisely the problem. What we are doing instead is to study tendencies, frameworks, and repertoires within cultures. We are learning to recognize patterns so that we can interpret arguments more carefully, not that we want to stereotype more confidently, right. So, let us begin.",
