      "transcript_text": "Now, let's push that discussion a little bit more and take on case examples in textbooks. Now, in the textbook, a case example may be a powerful but risky unit. A single vivid case can persuade the reader disproportionately. Now, a reader can read this one single very nicely written case as a substitute for broader evidence. But case examples also serve another function. They can make very abstract patterns become more easy to understand for people, and they can help an audience, the readers, see how a general principle operates in concrete life. It's especially important when we are going to be discussing the Indian logical tradition of Nyaya, where the example is not just a decorative addition to illustrate the point you're making, but in fact, it is a structural component of an argument. We will talk about that in a little while. Then there is appeal to authority. The reading that we had, the pre-read, rightly warns us against uncritical deference. Not every authority is legitimate. Not every expert speaks outside of their domain. Not every prestigious institution is free from a vested interest or an error. We have to remember that authority itself is culturally mediated. In some settings, authority comes with certain credentials, say, degrees, like having an MBA degree from an IIM or a PhD from a big school, or certifications, a published research, or formal positions. So, in others, the authority comes from character, seniority, community standing, lineage, who your parents or your grandparents are, moral reputation, or lived experience. A chartered accountant, for instance, may carry institutional authority, on the other hand. An elder trader may carry relational authority because you respect that fact that this trader is older to you. A retired professor may carry intellectual authority because they've given so many years of their life to teaching and research. A local leader may carry moral authority. None of these authorities that come from being a local leader or an elder trader or a retired professor are automatically invalid, but each must be evaluated carefully. Now, let's look at research studies  These are often treated as the gold standard. And indeed, well-designed research can provide powerful evidence. But research also is not neutral. It is shaped by questions that are asked, the methods that are chosen, the samples that are selected, the variables that are measured, and finally, the interpretations that are framed. A study conducted in one cultural context may not travel seamlessly to another cultural context. A global benchmark may obscure local realities. A quantitative model may omit social obligations, informal economies, trust networks, et cetera. So, the question from the pre-read, how good is the evidence becomes richer, more complicated when we look at it cross-culturally. So, we must ask ourself not only whether the evidence is good, but also whether our standards of reliability are themselves bounded by the culture that we are doing the evidence collection.",
