      "transcript_text": "Okay. So, let us begin with introduction to risk. What do we know so far? So far, we know that the opportunity cost of capital for a project depends on the risk of the project. However, you do not know how is risk measured and what is the exact relationship between risk and opportunity cost of capital. This is what you are going to learn now. Now, if I may ask you, what is risk? You may have heard about this term very often. What is risk? Essentially, risk means variability in something you are interested in. As part of this topic, we are interested in return, and therefore we are concerned about variability in return. Now, are you aware of any measures which relate to variability? Let me make you recall. You might already be aware of variance and standard deviation. So, the 1st measure of risk which you are already familiar with is variance. Now, variance of a variable X, we will denote it by sigma X square. And, how do we define it as? We define it as summation Xi minus X bar whole square divided by n minus 1. Why are we dividing it with n minus 1? We are dividing by n minus 1 because the variance is estimated from a sample of observations. You must have also heard about something called as standard deviation. So, standard deviation of X, we will denote as sigma X, and this is simply going to be square root of your variance of X. Now, my question to you is, can standard deviation be negative? The answer is no. Standard deviation is a positive square root of variance. It cannot be negative. My another question to you is, what are the units of standard deviation? Standard deviation is measured in same units as the units of your variable. Now, let us take 1 example. Suppose you are given with 4 years of historical data. You are given with annual return for each of those 4 years, and you have to compute variance and standard deviation of annual returns. So, how will you proceed? So, as a 1st step, you will find your mean return. You earned 15% return in the 1st year, 9% in the second year, 6% in the third year, and 12% in the 4th year. And your mean return comes out as 10.5%. After computing mean return, you will compute deviation from the mean return. Yes? So, which means in the 1st year, your deviation will be 15 minus 10.5, which gives you 4.5%. In a similar way, you can find the deviation from mean return for the other 3 years. Now, what you have to do is you have to find square of this deviation, and then sum them up. So, if you square these deviations and sum them up, you will get 45, right? Now, what is going to be your variance? Your variance is going to be 45 divided by n minus 1, and here n is 4. So therefore, your variance is going to be 15. Now, once you know variance, you can easily compute your standard deviation, which is simply going to be the positive square root of variance. So, which will give you a standard deviation of 3.873%.",
