      "transcript_text": "Now, let us try to understand risk by considering 3 different kinds of portfolios. So, we have 3 portfolios. The first 1 is the portfolio of US Treasury bills or T-bills. And as you understand, these Treasury bills mature in less than a year, and these are issued by the US government. The second portfolio is the portfolio of US Treasury bonds or T-bonds. Again, these bonds are issued by the US government, and they are going to mature in a period from 1 year to 10 years. And finally, we have a third portfolio, which is the portfolio of US common stocks, shares of companies. Now, my question to you is, which portfolio do you think has the highest risk? If you look at the 3 portfolios, specifically in the case of first 2 portfolios, the bonds or bills, these are issued by the US government. And obviously, there is no risk of default because the government is not expected to default on its obligations. So therefore, whether it is the portfolio of US Treasury bills or it is the portfolio of US Treasury bonds, in both the cases there is no risk of default. So therefore, my question to you is, out of these 2 portfolios, which of the 2 do you think is more risky? Is it the portfolio of US Treasury bills or is it the portfolio of US Treasury bonds? You understand that Treasury bills are going to mature in less than a year, and you also understand that, the prices of long-term bonds are more sensitive to changes in interest rates than the prices of short-term bonds. In other words, in the case of US Treasury bills, since we have short maturity, we are going to have relatively stable prices. However, in the case of US Treasury bonds, these are going to mature in relatively longer time, and therefore prices of US Treasury bonds will fluctuate as the interest rates will vary. So, if the interest rates will go up, prices of US Treasury bonds will come down, and vice versa. In other words, we can say that the portfolio of US Treasury bonds is subject to interest rate risk, and interest rate risk on the other hand is minimal in the case of US Treasury bills. So therefore, we observe that or at least our intuition is that the portfolio of US Treasury bonds should carry greater risk relative to the portfolio of US Treasury bills. Now, let us focus our attention on the third portfolio, which is the portfolio of US common stocks. Here, the prices of this portfolio will fluctuate with the ups and downs of the issuing companies, and therefore intuition says that this portfolio should carry the greatest amount of risk. Now, let us see if investment performance coincides with intuitive definition of our risk-taking. Now, let us see the annual standard deviations and variances over a long period of time starting from 1900 until 2023. So, if you look at the portfolio of US Treasury bills, on average the annual standard deviation of returns on this portfolio was 2.7%. If you look at the portfolio of US Treasury bonds, you will observe that historically the average standard deviation on this portfolio was 9.3%. And similarly, if you look at the portfolio of US common stocks, you will realize that the average annual standard deviation was 19.6%. So therefore, what do you observe based on data? So, we observe that T-bills as expected were the least variable security and common stocks were the most variable. T-bonds held the middle ground. Now, let us focus our attention on annual average rate of return on each of these portfolios over the period starting from 1900 until 2023. You notice that US Treasury bills gave you a nominal return of 3.4% every year. US Treasury bonds gave you average annual return of 5%, and US common stocks gave you 11.5% of average annual return over this whole period. So, what do you observe? You observe that your portfolio of US Treasury bills, since it was the safest investment, it also earned you the lowest return. In a similar way, the portfolio of US common stocks was most risky, and therefore you also earned the highest return on this portfolio. So essentially, by taking on the risk of investment in a risky security, investors earned a risk premium over the return on Treasury bills. So, since Treasury bills are the safest assets we have, therefore we call Treasury bills as the risk-free assets as well. So therefore, what was the risk premium for Treasury bonds? It means how much return did Treasury bonds earn over and above the rate of return on Treasury bills? It was simply 5 minus 3.4, which is 1.6%. So, what is this 1.6%? This 1.6% represents the risk premium for Treasury bonds. In a similar way, can we compute the risk premium for common stocks? Which means how much return did common stocks earn over and above the return on Treasury bills? It is simply 11.5% minus 3.4%, which will give you 8.1%. In general, we can say that, that return is equal to risk free rate plus risk premium. Or alternatively, we can say that our risk premium on a security I equals to return on that security I minus risk free rate. And what is risk-free rate? Risk-free rate represents, it represents the return on Treasury bills.",
