      "transcript_text": "Now, we will talk about portfolio risk and return. Now, let us make a portfolio consisting of equal investments in securities A and B. Which means what? Which means that you have invested 50% of your funds in security A and another 50% in security B. You understand that in the boom state which has 25% chance of occurrence, you will get 28% return on security A and 10% return on security B. In the normal state which has a 50% chance of occurrence, you will get 15% return on security A and 13% return on security B. And finally, in the recession state which has a 25% chance of occurrence, you will get negative 2% return on security A and 10% return on security B. In this case, can we not find out the expected return on our portfolio in each of the states? Yes. Let us try to find it out. In the boom state, how much return will you get on your portfolio? So, we have state here and we have return here. In the boom state, you will be getting 28% return on security A, and since your portfolio has 50% weight of security A, so therefore, if you find out the weighted average, it will be 0.5 into 28% plus 0.5 into 10%. What will that give you? That will give you simply 19%. Similarly, what is going to be the return on your portfolio in the normal state? Again, you have invested 50% in security A, which will give you a return of 15% in the normal state, and you have invested 50% of your funds in security B, which will give you 13% return in the normal state. And therefore, your return on the portfolio will be 14% in the normal state. In a similar way, let us try to find out the return on the portfolio in the recession state. It will be 0.5 into negative 2% plus 0.5 into 10%. What do you get here? You get 4% here. Okay. You also understand the probability of each of the states occurring. The probability of boom state occurring is 0.25 The probability of a normal state occurring is 0.5 and the probability of a recession state occurring is 0.25. So therefore, what is going to be the expected return on your portfolio. It will be weighted average of the return in different states. So, in the boom state, which has a 25% probability, you will be getting a 19% return. In the normal state, which has a 50% chance of occurrence, you will be getting a return of 14%. And in the recession state, which has a 25% chance of occurring, you will be getting a 4% return. And therefore, what is your expected return on the portfolio? It comes out as 12.75%. Now, the question is, what is the weighted average of the expected returns on individual securities forming the portfolio? So, let us find out weighted average of return on individual securities. So, that will be, since you have invested 50% funds in security A, which is going to give you an expected return of 14%. And another 50% you have invested in security B, which will give you a return of 11.5%. And therefore, weighted average of return on individual securities comes out to be 12.75%. What is the portfolio expected return? It is 12.75%. And therefore, what do you observe? You observe that the expected return on the portfolio is the weighted average of the expected returns on the individual securities that form the portfolio. Now, let us focus our attention on the standard deviation. Let us now try to compute the portfolio standard deviation. Now, let us compute portfolio standard deviation. What is that going to be equal to? Again, we will apply the same formula which we discussed just a while ago. Portfolio standard deviation will be summation Pi into Ri minus R bar whole square, and you will have to take a square root of this term. Okay? So, in this case, what is going to be our portfolio standard deviation? Let us first of all look at the boom state. It has a 25% chance of occurrence, and the return on the portfolio in the boom state is 19% minus the expected return on the portfolio is 12.75%. You will have to take a square of this. Then in the normal state whose chance of occurrence is 15%. So then, normal state has a 50% chance of occurrence. And in the normal state, your portfolio return is 14%, and your expected return on the portfolio is 12.75, and therefore you will take a square of this term. And finally, you have a recession state which has 25% chance of occurrence, and therefore we will multiply 0.25 with the return on the portfolio in recession state, which is 4%, minus expected return on the portfolio, which is 12.75. So, we will take a square of this, and then we will take a square root of this whole term. Now, my question to you is: What is the weighted average of the standard deviations of individual securities forming the portfolio? So, what does that come out as? So, portfolio standard deviation comes out as 6.075%. Now, what is the weighted average of the standard deviations of individual securities forming the portfolio? Let us compute it. So, of, of standard deviations of individual securities, it is equal to 0.5 into standard deviation of returns on security A which is 10.65% plus weight of security B which is 0.5 into standard deviation of returns on security B which is 1.5%. What does that give us? That gives us 5.45%. What do you notice? You notice that the portfolio standard deviation is less than the weighted average of individual security standard deviations. Now, the question is why is the portfolio standard deviation in this case less than the weighted average of the individual security standard deviations? The answer is this is happening because of the benefit of diversification.",
