      "transcript_text": "Now, let us consider the benefit of diversification. So, let us first of all consider a portfolio which comprises of 2 stocks. Now, before we get into studying the portfolio risk, it is important for us to know 2 important terms which you might have come across already. The first term is covariance and the second term is correlation. If I may ask you what is covariance? What does covariance tell you? If you may recall, covariance tells you about relationship between 2 securities. If the covariance is positive, it tells you that the 2 variables or 2 securities, they are positively related. On the other hand, if the covariance is negative, it tells you that the 2 variables are negatively related. Now, the question is, what is the lower bound and upper bound on covariance as a number? Is there a lower and an upper bound? The answer is no. Covariance can take values starting from minus infinity to plus infinity, theoretically speaking. Okay. Now, the question is, how do we define covariance? So, covariance. First of all, covariance between 2 variables X and Y, we will denote it by sigma XY. And what is it equal to? It is equal to summation Xi minus X bar into Yi minus Y bar, this whole divided by n minus 1. And as we just discussed, covariance can take values starting from minus infinity to plus infinity. Now, if covariance between X and Y is positive, it tells us that X and Y are positively related, which means that if X increases, Y is also going to increase. Similarly, if sigma XY is negative, it tells us that X and Y are negatively related. Now, my question to you is, what is covariance of a variable with itself? Let us say covariance of X with X. We will denote it by sigma X, X. And what is it going to be equal to? If you substitute or replace Y with X, what will you get? You will get summation Xi minus X bar into Xi minus X bar divided by n-1. This will give you summation Xi minus X bar whole square divided by n-1. What is this term? If you recall, this is nothing but variance of X. Yes. In other words, covariance of a variable with itself is nothing but variance of that variable. Now, let us look at another concept which is that of correlation. First of all, correlation between 2 variables X and Y is denoted by row XY. It is simply equal to sigma XY, which is covariance of X and Y. This whole divided by sigma X into sigma Y. Now, this correlation coefficient or correlation, is there any upper and lower bound on the values of this coefficient? The answer is yes. Correlation between 2 variables cannot be more than plus 1, and it cannot be less than minus 1. In other words, correlation takes values between minus 1 to plus 1. So, if 2 variables have a correlation of plus 1 between them, it tells us that the 2 variables are perfectly and positively correlated. If the 2 variables, on the other hand, have a correlation coefficient of negative 1, it tells us that the 2 variables are perfectly and negatively correlated. Now, what is the difference between covariance and correlation? After all, both of them are telling us how the 2 variables are related. But what is the difference between the 2? The difference between the 2 is that while covariance gives us the direction of the relationship between 2 variables, correlation on the other hand, not only tells us about the direction between the 2 variables, it also tells us about the strength of the relationship. So covariance, it gives us direction of the relationship between X and Y. Now, having said that, you must remember that whether it is covariance or it is correlation, both of them capture only the linear relationship between the 2 variables. They do not measure a nonlinear relationship between 2 variables.",
