      "transcript_text": "Now, let us talk about a very important model which is called as Capital Asset Pricing Model or CAPM in short. Okay. Until now, we know that the difference between a security's expected return and the return on treasury bills is known as security's risk premium. We also understand that for a well-diversified portfolio, the appropriate measure of risk is beta. We also understand that treasury bills, which are essentially a risk-free security, they have a beta of 0, and therefore what is the risk premium? It is also 0. Further, we know that the market portfolio with expected return Rm has a beta of 1, and therefore what is the risk premium on the market? It is simply Rm minus Rf. So, let me put these facts here. So, we understand that in general, security risk premium is equal to Ri minus Rf. Okay, where i represents our security. Now, if we have to compute risk premium, on, on a risk-free asset such as a treasury bill. It will be here, here, I will replace I with F and therefore my risk premium on a risk-free asset will be RF minus RF, which will be 0. Okay? What is my risk premium, what is my risk premium on the market? Here, I will denote market by letter m, and therefore I will replace i with m as my subscript. And therefore, risk premium on the market will be Rm minus Rf. Now, we also understand that what is beta of the market? If you recall from our discussion, beta of the market is 1. And what is risk premium on the market? Which we have just computed. It is Rm minus Rf. Now, let us similarly talk about a risk-free asset. In the case of a risk-free asset, what is beta? The beta is 0. And what is risk premium? Risk premium is also 0. So, we know what is the risk premium when beta is 0 and when beta is 1. But we do not know what is the expected risk premium when the beta is neither 0 nor 1. In that case, we have something called as capital asset pricing model, which was given by William Sharpe, John Lintner, and Jack Treynor. They helped answered this question. So, they simply said that the risk premium on a security is proportional to its beta. So in other words, CAPM says that the risk premium on a security i, which is obviously Ri minus Rf. So essentially, Sharpe, Lintner, and Treynor, they mentioned that the risk premium on a security varies in direct proportion to its beta. In other words, they mentioned that the risk premium on a security is equal to beta_i into Rm minus Rf, okay? We can rearrange this equation, and we can write that our expected return on a security i equals Rf plus beta_i into Rm minus Rf. So, we will be using this equation to compute the expected return or required return on a security. So therefore, as you can see, we can use CAPM to find out the opportunity cost of capital for a project or an investment. So essentially, CAPM tells you that the opportunity cost of capital for a project depends on the beta of the project. The higher the beta of the project, the higher is going to be the return that you are going to require from the project. Now, let us look at security market line. Now, what is security market line? Security market line is essentially a plot between expected return and beta. Let me denote beta by this letter and expected return by r. You understand that for a risk-free security, what is the beta? Beta is 0. And what is the return that you expect from a risk-free security? That return is essentially Rf. This is your risk-free rate. We understand that for our market portfolio, for our market portfolio, let me denote it by Rm. What is the beta of the market portfolio? The beta of the market portfolio, as you know, it is equal to 1. And what is the expected return on the market? The expected return on the market is Rm. So therefore, this point represents our market portfolio and this point represents our risk free asset. Security market line, which we refer to as SML in short. So what is SML? SML, SML is security market line. This essentially is the equation of the capital asset pricing model. Now, if you draw a line which passes through these 2 points, this line is essentially your security market line. Now, if you remember, the equation of a line is of the form Y equals MX plus C. Okay? Where what is M? M is the slope of the line. And what is C? C is the intercept on the Y-axis. Here, what is our variable on the Y-axis? It is R. Okay? So, R equals. Now, what is our slope of this line here? So, if you see, our slope is Y2 minus Y1 divided by X2 minus X1. Okay, what is the slope? Alternatively, you can also say that your slope is nothing but tan theta. And what is tan theta? It is your perpendicular by base, okay. What is your perpendicular here? And what is your base here? It is 1 minus 0. So here, it is Rm minus Rf divided by 1 minus 0. So therefore, your slope is RM minus RF, okay. So therefore, the equation of the line is R equals Rm minus Rf, okay. What is our X variable? It is beta, okay. What is our intercept on the Y-axis? Our intercept on the Y-axis of this line is Rf. So, this implies that our R equals RF plus beta times Rm minus Rf. So, as you can see, this is nothing but capital asset pricing model. So, security market line represents your capital asset pricing model.",
