      "transcript_text": "Now, let us begin our discussion with different kinds of payouts. You understand that every period a firm generates free cash flow. Free cash flow refers to the cash flow generated by firm's operations after you subtract the investments which are required for growth. Now, this free cash flow can be positive, it can be negative as well. Now, when a firm generates a positive free cash flow, it can make 2 choices. Either the firm can retain that free cash flow or the firm can pay out that cash flow to investors or of course, a firm can partly retain and partly pay out some cash. Now, if a firm decides to retain cash, now this retained cash can be invested in projects which could be positive NPV projects, zero NPV projects or negative NPV projects. The firm can use this cash to retire debt, and the firm can use this cash to increase its existing cash reserves. On the other hand, if the firm decides to pay out this cash, in that case, it can be paid out to shareholders either in the form of dividend or the firm can repurchase shares from existing shareholders and can give them cash. Now, therefore, what is payout policy? Payout policy is the choice that a firm makes between retaining cash, paying dividends or repurchasing shares. For example, if we talk about Infosys, in July 2019, Infosys altered its payout policy to give back 85% of its free cash flow to shareholders effective from financial year 2019-2020. Earlier, the company was distributing up to 70% of its free cash flow to the shareholders. Now, in addition to cash dividends and share repurchases, a firm can also pay another kind of a dividend which does not involve cash. This is called as a stock dividend. So here, the firm does not give dividend in the form of cash, but the firm gives dividend in the form of shares. We will talk about this as we go along, and we will also talk about 2 related concepts which are stock splits and reverse splits.",
