      "transcript_text": "Let us now talk about cash dividends. How do firms pay cash dividends? The decision to pay a dividend rests in the hands of the board of directors of the firm. When a dividend has been declared by the board, it becomes a liability of the firm. In India, dividends proposed by the board of directors become a liability once approved by shareholders. Also, dividend is often expressed in 3 forms. The first form is dividend per share. The second form is dividend payout, which is nothing but dividend per share divided by earnings per share. And the third one is dividend yield, which is nothing but dividend per share divided by price per share. Now, let us talk about mechanics of cash dividends. So here, first of all, we have something called as declaration date or announcement date. What is this date? So, this is the date on which the board of directors of a firm authorizes the dividend. Then, we have something called as ex-dividend date. What is ex-dividend date? It is a date on or after which anyone buying the shares of the firm will not be eligible for the dividend. In other words, dividend becomes ex to the person who is purchasing the shares on or after this date. Before this date, however, the stock is set to trade cum dividend, which means that if you buy the shares of the firm before ex-dividend date, you will be entitled to receiving the dividend of this firm. Also, the stock price of a firm falls on the ex-dividend date, ideally by an amount equal to dividend per share. You must be wondering, why does the stock price of a firm fall on ex-dividend date? The reason is simple. Think about it economically. When a firm gives out dividend, cash dividend, what happens to the size of the firm? So, you understand that now the size of the firm comes down, which means that the market value of the firm comes down. Ideally, this fall in market value is equal to the amount of dividend that the firm is paying out. And therefore, obviously, if you are holding the shares of the firm, now these shares represent fractional ownership on the assets of the firm. And since the market value of the assets has come down, therefore your share price will also come down. Now, let us talk about another date which is called as record date. So what is record date? So essentially, when a firm pays out dividend, only shareholders of record on this date receive the dividend. And finally, we have something called as the payment date. What is the payment date? Payment date, as the name implies, it is the date on which the dividend is actually paid out to shareholders. For example, if we talk about ONGC. ONGC declared a regular dividend of Rs. 1.25 per share on May 21, 2025. Okay. Now shares of this firm started to trade ex-dividend on September 4, 2025, which tells us that September 4, 2025 was the ex-dividend date for ONGC. Also, the record date also happened to be September 4, 2025. So essentially, the dividend will be paid to the shareholders of ONGC who were registered on this date, who were shareholders of record on this date. And finally, we have payment date. The dividend is paid out to shareholders on this date. And this date in the case of ONGC was September 27, 2025. Now, the question before us is, can firms declare any dividend of their choice? The answer is no. Firms cannot declare any dividend of their choice. Why? First of all, for example, lenders or creditors from whom the firm has borrowed money may impose restrictions on dividends. Because excessive dividend payments may not leave enough cash with the firm to service the loans given by the lenders of the firm. Another reason is that some countries have creditor laws which may protect the firm's creditors against excessive dividend payments. And finally, in some countries such as Brazil and Chile, firms are obligated by state laws to pay out a minimum proportion of their earnings as dividends. So all in all, a firm cannot declare any dividend of its choice in under all circumstances.",
