      "transcript_text": "Let us now move on to talking about share repurchases or share buybacks. How do firms repurchase shares? So typically, there are 4 ways which firms can make use of to repurchase shares. The first way or method is open market repurchase. So essentially, under open market repurchase, as the name implies, the firm repurchases or buys back shares in the open market, which means in the secondary market. So here, the firm announces its intention to buy back shares in the open market, and then proceeds to do so just like any other investor over a period of several months. Now, let us talk about the second method, which is the tender offer route. Under this particular route, the firm gives an offer to shareholders to buy back a stated number of shares at a fixed price within a short period of time, usually about 10 days or so. Here, the repurchase price is usually set at a substantial premium to the current market price, and this premium often is in the range starting from 10% to 20%. Now, if shareholders do not tender enough shares, the firm can cancel the offer and no buyback occurs. Now, open market repurchase and tender offer routes are the 2 most commonly used routes to buy back shares by firms in India. Now, let us talk about our third route, which is the Dutch auction route. Here, the firm sets a range of prices within which it is willing to repurchase shares. Now, shareholders indicate how many shares they are willing to sell at each of those prices, and then the firm pays the lowest price at which it can buy the desired number of shares. And finally, we have something called as targeted repurchase. Targeted repurchase, as the name implies, here the firm negotiates with a major shareholder directly.",
