      "transcript_text": "Now, let us talk about 2 related concepts, stock split and reverse split. What is a stock split? In the case of a stock split, each shareholder who owns the shares receives additional shares of the firm's stock. For example, with a 2-for-1 stock split, 1 old share will be replaced with 2 new shares. Now, the question is, why do companies go for a stock split? Companies go for a stock split so as to make the stock more affordable or attractive to small investors. Companies go for stock split to increase the demand for and the liquidity of the stock, which may in turn boost the stock price of the firm. For example, NVIDIA's stock price soared past $1,000 in 2024, and therefore the global chip giant split its stock 10-for-1, making its stock more affordable and more liquid. Now, you must be wondering what happens to the stock price of the firm upon announcement of stock splits. Now, empirical evidence shows that announcements of stock splits on average are associated with a 2% increase in the stock price. Why? The reason is simple. Because now with the stock split, your stock will be easier to buy or sell for a retail investor, and therefore it increases liquidity and demand for the shares of the firm. Now, let us talk about a reverse split. A reverse split, on the other hand, consolidates the number of existing shares of stock into fewer and importantly, higher priced shares. It is essentially the reverse of a stock split. For example, with a 1 for 10 reverse split, every 10 shares of the firm are replaced with a single share. Now, the question before us is, why do companies undertake reverse splits? Companies undertake reverse splits to avoid delisting from major stock exchanges. For example, if we look at New York Stock Exchange and NASDAQ, these exchanges require listed companies to maintain a minimum share price of $1. However, having said that, there is no such requirement by stock exchanges in India for listed companies. Also, companies undertake reverse splits so as to improve the marketability of their shares. Having a higher share price can attract certain investors who would not consider penny stocks for their portfolios. By using splits and reverse stock splits, most firms keep their share prices within a reasonable range. For example, majority of New York Stock Exchange listed firms in the US have their stock prices between $10 and $60. Similarly, if you look at Indian firms, the majority of Nifty 500 firms in India have their stock prices between Rs. 100 and Rs. 1,500.",
