      "transcript_text": "Now, let us summarize implications of different types of payouts and other corporate actions. Let us see what effect each of these actions have on the cash, market value of equity, number of shares outstanding, and price per share. First of all, let us quickly talk about cash dividend. As a consequence of cash dividend, your cash balance of the firm comes down, and therefore your market value of equity also comes down. However, your number of shares outstanding does not change, but price per share comes down. Let us now talk about share repurchase. As a consequence of share repurchase, again, since you are paying out your excess cash, the cash balance of the firm comes down, and so is market value of equity. However, your price per share does not change if the share repurchase is taken at the prevailing market rate, but your number of shares outstanding is going to come down because you are repurchasing shares. Now, let us look at stock dividend or bonus share. In the case of a stock dividend, neither your cash nor your market value of equity changes. However, since you are issuing new shares, your number of shares outstanding goes up and the price per share will come down. Because now the same market value of equity will be divided among greater number of shares. In the case of a stock split, which is very similar to a stock dividend, again, nothing is going to happen to your cash balance or market value of equity. However, your number of shares outstanding will go up and your price per share will come down. And finally, in the case of a reverse split, again, nothing will happen to your cash and market value of equity. However, contrary to stock split, in the case of a reverse split, your number of shares outstanding will come down, but your price per share will go up.",
