      "transcript_text": "Now, let us talk about information content of dividends, which means whether dividend announcements convey any information to investors. Generally, we understand that managers of a firm have private information with themselves, and they can make use of corporate announcements to signal their private information to investors. Now, what do we observe in practice as far as cash dividends are concerned? We observe that managers in general are reluctant to make dividend changes that may have to be reversed going forward. Also, managers tend to smooth dividends. In other words, dividend changes follow shifts in long run sustainable earnings. Put differently, transitory earnings changes are unlikely to affect dividends. For example, if we look at General Motors' history from a period starting from 1985 until 2008, we observe that General Motors changed the amount of regular dividend only eight times over a 23-year period starting from 1985 until 2008. Yet, during the same time, GM's earnings varied widely. Now, another important feature of dividends which we observe from practice is that managers focus more on dividend changes than on absolute dividend levels. For example, paying a dividend of Rs. 5 per share is an important financial decision if the last year's dividend was Rs. 4, but no big deal if last year's dividend was also Rs. 5. In other words, what matters is dividend changes, but not the absolute level of dividends. Now, the question is, why is the announcement of a dividend increase generally good news for investors? The reason is that managers are reluctant to increase dividends, as we discussed, unless they are confident that the dividend payment can be maintained in the future. Therefore, the announcement of a dividend increase signals manager's confidence in future earnings of the firm. In other words, dividend increases can potentially predict future earnings of a firm. Okay. Now, is an increase in dividend necessarily good news for investors? The answer is no. An increase in dividends is not necessarily a piece of good news for investors. It may also signal a lack of positive NPV projects. For example, if we talk about Microsoft, it initiated dividends in 2003, and this initiation was largely interpreted as a result of Microsoft's declining growth prospects as opposed to a signal about its increased future profitability. Therefore, what do you conclude from our discussion so far? We conclude that, that we must interpret dividends as a signal in the context of the type of new information managers are likely to have. Now, the question is, what does the empirical evidence say? The empirical evidence says that dividend increases, on average, lead to a 0.5% increase in the stock price while dividend decreases lead to a 2% decrease. In other words, dividend changes have an asymmetric effect on stock prices. However, the sensitivity of the stock price changes to dividend changes has decreased over time.",
