          "model_answer": "The signaling role of dividends arises from the information asymmetry between managers and investors. Managers possess private information about firm value and use dividend announcements to signal this information. As discussed in the lecture, managers are reluctant to reverse dividend changes, which makes dividend signals credible.\n\nRegarding why dividend increases are not necessarily good news: A dividend increase signals manager confidence in the sustainability of future earnings, as managers will only increase dividends if they are confident the payment can be maintained. However, a dividend increase may also signal a lack of positive NPV projects. For example, Microsoft's initiation of dividends in 2003 was largely interpreted as a result of declining growth prospects, not a signal about increased future profitability.\n\nThe lecture provides two key real-world examples: General Motors changed its regular dividend only eight times over a 23-year period from 1985 to 2008 despite wide earnings variation, illustrating that managers smooth dividends following long-run sustainable earnings rather than transitory changes. Additionally, the lecture notes that investors focus on dividend changes rather than absolute levels, paying a dividend of Rs. 5 per share is a major decision if the previous year's dividend was Rs. 4, but insignificant if it was also Rs. 5.\n\nEmpirically, dividend increases on average lead to a 0.5% increase in stock price, while dividend decreases lead to a 2% decrease, showing an asymmetric market reaction.\n\nMarking Rubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding of dividend signaling (3 marks)\n- Application of GM and Microsoft examples to explain signal interpretation (3 marks)\n- Recognition of dividend smoothing and change-versus-level distinction (2 marks)\n- Identification of asymmetric market reaction (2 marks)",
