      "transcript_text": "Now, let us talk about differences between cash dividends and share prices and the effect they have on the stock prices of the firm. Let us consider this example. Fictitious Corporation has Rs. 20 million in excess cash and no debt. The firm has 10 million shares outstanding. The market value of its assets, excluding cash, is Rs. 400 million. The firm's board is meeting to decide how to pay out its Rs. 20 million in excess cash to shareholders. It is considering 2 options. The first option or the first policy is to pay a cash dividend of Rs. 20 million, and the second option or the policy is to repurchase shares worth Rs. 20 million at the prevailing market price. Now, the question before us is: Will the amount of the current dividend affect the firm's share price? You can assume that the capital markets are perfect, which means that you can ignore taxes, transaction costs, and other imperfections. Let us put the information that we have in the form of a table. Okay, how much surplus cash do we have, surplus or excess cash? If you look at the problem, the firm has 20 million rupees in excess cash. Okay. What is the market value of other assets? Other assets as given to us in the problem are valued at Rs. And therefore, what is the total market value of the firm? The total market value, let me represent market value by MV. So, total market value of the firm in Rs. million therefore, before any of the decisions is 420 million rupees. Now, how many shares are outstanding currently? The problem tells us that the firm has 10 million shares outstanding. So, if we look at number of shares outstanding in million. It is essentially 10 million. Now, therefore, what should the current stock price of the firm be? To compute that, we have to know what is the market value of equity of the firm. What is market value of equity? Market value of equity is equal to market value of the firm because there is no debt in the capital structure of the firm, right? So here, therefore, our market value of equity is 420 million, and if we divide it by number of shares outstanding, which is 10 million, we will get Rs. 42 as our current stock price. Now, let us look at the first policy. What does the first policy say? It says that the firm will pay a cash dividend of Rs. 20 million. So, after paying out this cash dividend, how much excess cash will be left with the firm? The excess cash will essentially be 0, and market value of other assets will continue to remain at 400 million rupees, and therefore, total market value of the firm is now going to be 0 plus 400, which will simply be 400 million rupees. Before paying out cash dividend, the firm had 10 million shares outstanding. Obviously, if the firm pays out cash dividend, it is not going to affect the number of shares outstanding. So, we will continue to have 10 million shares outstanding of the firm. And therefore, what should our stock price be now? Our stock price now will be equal to market value of equity, market value of shares. And since the firm has no debt in its capital structure, the market value of equity will be same as the market value of the firm which is 400 million rupees. And this divided by number of shares outstanding, which is 10 million, and this will give us 40 rupees as our stock price. So, what does this imply? So, this essentially implies that our stock price, as a consequence of paying out dividend, has come down from 42 rupees per share to Rs. 40 per share. So, what inference do we draw from here? So, we conclude that in perfect capital markets, the share price of a firm drops by the amount of dividend per share when the stock begins to trade ex-dividend. Yes, which means that, we can say that our ex-dividend stock price will be equal to stock price cumulative of dividend minus dividend per share. What was our stock price cumulative of dividend? Which means the stock price before paying out the dividend, it was Rs. 42. How much dividend did the firm pay out? The firm, as you figure out, it paid a total dividend of Rs. And since there are 10 million shares outstanding, this translates to a dividend of Rs. 2 per share. Okay? So therefore, ex-dividend price will be equal to Rs. 40. Again, let me reiterate, in perfect capital markets, the share price of a firm drops by the amount of dividend per share when the stock begins to trade ex-dividend. Now, let us talk about repurchases. Let us look at policy 2. What does policy 2 say? It says that under policy 2, the firm will repurchase shares worth Rs. What is the prevailing market price? It is Rs. 42 per share. So therefore, the firm is going to repurchase shares worth Rs. 20 million at a price of Rs. So therefore, how many shares will the firm repurchase? So, number of shares pre-purchased, it will be equal to the repurchase amount divided by price per share of repurchase, price per share of repurchase. Our repurchase amount is given to us as Rs. 20 million and price per share is given to us as Rs. 42. So therefore, how many shares will the firm repurchase? The firm will repurchase 0.476 million shares. And therefore, how many shares will be left outstanding after repurchase? So after repurchase, as you can figure out, we will have 10 million minus 0.476, which is going to be 9.524 million shares, which will be outstanding. Obviously, after the share repurchase, we will have nil surplus cash. Market value of other assets of the firm is 400 as before, and therefore the total market value of the firm is 400 million rupees. Now, from here, can we not find out our stock price? What will our stock price be now? It will be equal to the market value of equity, which is nothing but market value of the firm, which is again 400 million rupees divided by number of shares outstanding, which is 9.524. And therefore, what will our stock price be? Our stock price, if you compute, it will come out as Rs. 42. From here, what do you conclude? You conclude that our stock price has not changed. Our stock price before repurchase was Rs. And after we have carried out the repurchase at the prevailing market price, which means we have carried out the open market share repurchase, our stock price continues to remain at Rs. 42 per share even after the repurchase. So in short, in perfect capital markets, the open market share repurchase has no effect on the stock price.",
