      "transcript_text": "Now, let us consider another case. Suppose the firm pays a Rs. 2 dividend per share today. Show how an investor holding 2,000 shares could create a homemade dividend of Rs. 4.50 per share for 2,000 shares that the investor is holding. Which means that how can the investor create a homemade dividend of Rs. 9,000 on her own? Okay. In this case, again, how much cash dividend has the firm paid? The firm has paid a cash dividend of Rs. 2 per share, and therefore, how much cash will the investor receive in the form of cash dividend from the firm? The investor will receive 2,000 into 2, which is equal to 4,000 rupees in cash. Now, how much total dividend does the investor want to receive? Investor wants to receive a total of Rs. 9,000 in dividends, and therefore, how much cash will the investor need to raise now? It will be 9,000 minus 4,000, which will give us 5,000 rupees. So therefore, investor needs to raise Rs. 5,000 in cash. How can the investor raise Rs. 5,000 in cash? Obviously, by selling off her shares. So, the question is, how many shares will the investor have to sell? Number of shares to be sold. This is equal to additional cash wanted, which is nothing but Rs. 5,000 divided by price per share after paying the dividend, which is Rs. 40. Okay? What does that give you? That gives you 125 shares. Yes? So therefore, what are investors' holdings now? How many shares is the investor holding now? Investor is now holding 2,000 minus 125 shares, which is nothing but 1,875 shares. What is the price per share? The price per share is Rs. 40. And therefore, what is the market value of holdings of the investor? It is nothing but Rs. 75,000. What is the market value of cash with the investor? That is nothing but Rs. 9,000. How did we arrive at Rs. 9,000? So, investor received a cash dividend of Rs. 4,000 from the firm, and the investor created another 5,000 rupees in homemade dividend and as you can see, market value of total holdings of the investor continues to remain at 84,000 rupees in total. Now, this brings us to something called as payout policy irrelevance. What do we conclude from this exercise? We conclude from this exercise that as long as capital markets are perfect, payout policy of a firm is going to be irrelevant because irrespective of whether a firm pays out cash dividend or gives out cash to investors in the form of share repurchases or does nothing, in either of these cases, investors can replicate the other two outcomes on their own. So, this payout policy irrelevance is also called as Miller Modigliani payout policy irrelevance.",
