      "transcript_text": "Now, let us see whether payout policy is actually irrelevant in real life. In practice, we observe that while some firms prefer to pay dividends, while some others rely exclusively on share repurchases. Also, some firms build up large cash reserves while others pay out their excess cash. So, the question before us is, what factors or imperfections drive differences in firms' payout behaviour? In practice, market imperfections do play an important role in influencing a firm's payout policy. And these imperfections include taxes, agency costs, life cycle of the firm, and some behavioural explanations also help explain a firm's payout policy. Let us look at each of these one by one. Let us first of all look at taxes. Now, corporate taxes make it costly for a firm to retain excess cash. Why? So, you understand that when a firm has debt in its capital structure or when a firm has taken loans, what happens? The firm needs to service those loans or debt that the firm has issued, which means that the firm needs to pay interest on the outstanding loans or debt. And therefore, whatever expense the firm is incurring on paying out interest expense, this particular interest expense being a tax-deductible expense brings down the tax liability of the firm. However, when a firm invests its excess cash in interest-bearing securities, what happens? In this case, the firm receives interest income, and the firm has to pay taxes on that interest income. In other words, we can say that while debt has tax advantage, investing surplus cash has tax disadvantage. In other words, we can say that cash is equivalent to negative debt. So, the tax advantage of debt implies a tax disadvantage to holding cash. Let us look at one example. Fictonia Corporation must pay corporate taxes at a 35% rate on the interest it will earn from the 1-year treasury bills that pay 6% interest. The question before us is what would an investor who does not pay taxes on her investment income prefer Fictonia to do? Pay a dividend of Rs. 100 per share immediately or retain the cash for 1 year, and then pay a dividend. What would the investor prefer who does not pay taxes? So, let us consider each of the 2 choices one by one. Let us first consider choice one, which is paying a dividend of Rs. 100 per share immediately. So, in this case, the investor receives Rs. 100 today for every share held by the investor. Yes? Now, if the investor invests this amount in 1-year treasury bills that pay 6% interest, how much money will the investor get after a year? Investor has invested 100 rupees and the interest rate is 6%. It will clearly be 100 into 1.06, which will give you Rs. 106. Now, it is given to us that the investor pays no taxes, which means that the investor will receive the entire dividend income in her account. The investor is going to receive entire 106 rupees in her account under choice 1. Now, let us consider choice 2. What is choice 2? Choice 2 entails retaining cash for one year, investing it in 1-year treasury bills, and then paying it out to the investor as a dividend. Now, if the firm retains the cash for 1 year, how much after-tax return will the firm earn on the treasury bills? You understand that what is your pre-tax return? Your pre-tax return is 6%, okay? What is your tax rate? It is 35%. And therefore, what will be the after-tax return? Your after-tax return will be equal to your pre-tax return into 1 minus tax rate. Okay? So, which means that it will be equal to 6% into 1 minus 0.35, which will give you an after-tax return of 3.90%. And therefore, at the end of the year, how much dividend will Fictonia pay per share? It will be Rs. 100 into 1.039, which will give us Rs. 103.90. Clearly, you can see this amount is less than Rs. 106 that the investor would have earned if she invested Rs. 100 in treasury bills herself, as in choice 1. And therefore, what did the investor prefer? The investor would clearly prefer the company to pay out dividend today. So, clearly we can see that taxes do influence the choice of investors. Also, if dividends and repurchases are taxed at different levels, investors may prefer the payout with lower taxes.",
