      "transcript_text": "After having talked about taxes as an imperfection shaping the payout policy of a firm, let us now talk about agency costs of idle cash. When firms have excessive cash, managers may use the funds inefficiently by continuing money-losing pet projects, paying excessive executive perks or undertaking acquisitions which have negative NPV. So, this particular set of arguments is called as the agency costs of idle cash. In such cases, paying out excess cash either through cash dividends or share repurchases can boost the share price of the firm. Why? Because investors understand that firms whose managers are infected with agency costs, they can essentially undertake negative NPV projects if the excess cash remains with the firm. And therefore, in such cases, whenever those firms whose managers suffer from agency costs pays out excess cash either through dividends or repurchases, the stock price of the firm typically goes up.",
