      "transcript_text": "Now, let us talk about behavioural theories or explanations that influence the payout policy of a firm. Let us first of all talk about dividend clienteles. So, according to this particular theory, some clientele of investors, for example, retirees, prefer stocks with regular and stable cash dividends. Why? In an ideal world, an investor can generate a homemade dividend from a non-dividend paying stock, but regular dividends relieve many of its shareholders of transaction costs and considerable inconvenience. So, these investors might be willing to pay more for shares of companies that pay out cash by dividends rather than repurchases. There is yet another behavioural theory which is called as the bird-in-the-hand theory. It says that, \"a bird in the hand is worth two in the bush.\" So essentially, according to this theory, firms that pay dividends may enjoy higher stock prices than those that do not pay dividends. However, this kind of a behaviour cannot be explained by rational theories. Let us talk about some evidence on the bird-in-the-hand theory. Frontier Communications Corporation, an American telecommunications company providing broadband internet, had 2 share classes, one that paid cash dividends and the other one that paid stock dividends. Although the fundamentals were identical, which means the same company and cash dividends were lower than stock dividends. Despite that, the cash dividend shares traded at a higher price. Again, this particular piece of evidence is consistent with bird-in-the-hand theory. However, the premium that cash dividend shares commanded over stock dividend shares varied over time. This indicated that investor preferences for cash dividends changed over time. Now, let us talk about another behavioural theory which is based on industry herding. According to this theory, firms in an industry follow dividend policies established by their competitors. Essentially, this kind of a behaviour evolves from the fear of being left alone, or in other words, this is FOMO, fear of missing out.",
