      "transcript_text": "So, let us begin our discussion with initial public offerings or IPOs. What is an IPO? An IPO stands for initial public offering. So, it comprises of two words. One is initial and the second one is public. Initial means the equity is being issued for the first time, and public means the issue is being made to the public at large. So therefore, what is an IPO? An IPO therefore involves issuing equity to public at large. Now, this issue of equity can be either issue of new shares or it can be selling of existing shares. The sale of new shares is what we call as primary offering. It is also known as issue of fresh capital. On the other hand, if existing shareholders of the firm are selling off their shares in the IPO, we will call that offering as a secondary offering. In the Indian context, secondary offerings are also called as offer for sale or OFS in short. Now, an IPO can be a mix of a primary offering and a secondary offering. Now, what happens as a consequence of an IPO? As a consequence of an IPO, an unlisted firm becomes a listed firm. The shares of the firm after an IPO are going to get traded on stock exchanges such as BSE and NSE in India. Now, let us look at some examples of IPOs. Let us first of all look at primary offerings or issues of fresh capital. For example, Reliance Power, in its IPO in 2008, it only issued new shares to public at large, and therefore, this particular IPO involved only primary offering. On the other hand, if we look at NSE India IPO, which happened in 2026 or Hyundai Motor India IPO, which happened in 2024 or Life Insurance Corporation of India IPO, which happened in 2022. In all these 3 IPOs, existing shareholders of the firm sold off their shares to the public at large. The firms did not issue any new shares. So therefore, all of these offerings are secondary offerings or offers for sale. In secondary offerings or offers for sale, as you can figure out, the firm is not going to raise any money. Essentially, only existing shareholders of the firm are selling off a part of their shares. Also, if we talk about Tata Capital IPO, which happened in the year 2025 or if we talk about Paytm IPO, which happened in 2021, both these IPOs involved a mix of primary and secondary offerings. So in these IPOs, the firms not only issued new shares, but existing shareholders of these firms sold off a part of their shareholding to the public at large. Now, if we look at top 5 IPOs in India as of the end of September 30th, 2026, we observe that the largest IPO has been by Hyundai Motor India Limited. This happened in the year 2024, and it was purely an offer for sale. The issue amount was close to Rs. 28,000 crore, and issue expenses as a proportion of the issue amount were 2.24%. The second largest IPO has been by National Stock Exchange of India, NSE as we call it as. This happened in the year 2026. Again, this was purely an offer for sale, and the issue amount was little over 22,500 crore rupees. And issue expenses formed less than 2% of the issue amount. The third largest IPO has been by Life Insurance Corporation of India, which happened in the year 2022. Again, it was purely an offer for sale, which means that it was purely a secondary offering. It involved an issue size of close to 20,500 crore rupees. And here, issue expenses as a proportion of the issue amount were merely 0.58%. Further, if we talk about the fourth largest IPO, that has been by Paytm or 197 Communications Limited as the company is formally called as. This IPO happened in the year 2021. And this IPO not only involved offer for sale, but it also involved issue of fresh capital. And the total issue size or issue amount was little over 18,000 crores of rupees. And issue expenses formed 2.25% of the issue size. And the issue expenses formed 2.25% of the issue amount. Now, talking about the fifth largest IPO, it has been by Tata Capital Limited in the year 2025. Again, this was not only an offer for sale, but it also comprised of issue of fresh capital. The offer size here was 15,500 crore rupees roughly, and issue expenses formed 2.1% of the issue amount. Now, let us talk about IPO pricing methods. Now, typically, firms make use of two kinds of pricing methods. The first one is fixed price issue. In a fixed price issue, the firm sets the offer price in advance. So therefore, at the time of applying for the IPO, you as an investor will exactly know what you will be paying per share. However, the actual demand for the shares at the offer price will only be known after the IPO closes. If demand for shares of the firm turns out to be more than the number of shares being sold in the IPO, the shares are allotted either on a proportionate basis, also called as pro rata basis or a lottery basis to investors. Now, let us talk about a book-building issue. In a book-building issue, instead of a fixed price, the firm sets a price range. There is a price band. Now, investors bid for different quantities of shares within this price band or price range. The highest price at which the maximum number of shares can be sold becomes the offered price. In this kind of issue, too, if the demand at the offered price is higher than the number of shares being sold, the shares are allotted either on a proportionate basis or a lottery basis to investors. Currently, book-building issues are most popular method of pricing the IPOs.",
