      "transcript_text": "Let us look at a few problems on IPOs. The first problem is on underpricing in IPOs. Suppose that you can earn an immediate 15% on underpriced IPOs and lose 10% on overpriced IPOs. But because of high demand, you only get half the shares that you apply when the issue is underpriced. Suppose you bid for rupees 1,000 of shares in each of the 2 issues, one underpriced and the other overpriced. The question before you is: What is the net rupee gain or loss on your investment? So, what did you do? So in this problem, you have bid for 1,000 rupee of shares in each of the 2 IPOs, one of which is underpriced and the other one is overpriced. It is given to you that in the IPO that is underpriced, you are only getting half the shares that you applied. So therefore, how many shares in terms of rupees are you going to get? You will only be able to invest Rs. 500 in the underpriced issue. So therefore, what will be your rupee gain for your investment in the underpriced issue? Your gain for, for Rs. 500 investment in the underpriced issue is equal to your investment, which is 500 rupees, multiplied by your percentage gain, which is 15%. What does that give you? That gives you 500 into 0.15, which is nothing but Rs. 75. Now, let us also look at loss. What is your loss, loss for Rs 1,000 investment in the overpriced issue. Remember that you applied for shares worth 1,000 rupees and you will be allotted shares worth 1,000 rupees in the overpriced issue. And therefore, what is your loss? It is equal to your investment which you are making in the overpriced issue, which is Rs. 1,000 multiplied by the percentage loss, which in this case is minus 10%. So, this gives you Rs. 1,000 into negative 0.1. So therefore, your loss is Rs. 100 from your investment in the overpriced issue. So therefore, what is your net loss? Your net loss therefore is equal to Rs. 75 minus Rs. 100. So, this gives you a net loss of 75 rupees. Sorry. So, this gives you, so this gives you a net loss of Rs. 25. Now, let us look at another problem on IPO costs. Fictonia Corporation offered 1,000 shares for sale in an IPO. 60% of the shares in the IPO were sold by the firm and the remaining 40% by existing shareholders. The offer or issue price to the public was Rs. 50, and the underwriters charged a spread of 5%. The issue was oversubscribed and the stock closed at Rs. 100 on the first day of trading. Costs other than those related to underpricing and underwriting were negligible, which means that these costs can essentially be ignored. The first question before you is: What were the proceeds of the issue to the firm and to the shareholders? Okay. Now, in this IPO, how many shares are being offered for sale? 1,000 shares are being offered for sale in this IPO, out of which 60% are new shares, which means that the firm is going to issue 600 primary shares and 40% of 1,000 shares are being sold off by existing shareholders. So therefore, 400 secondary shares will be sold off by existing shareholders of the firm. Now, the first part says we have to find out proceeds of the issue to the firm. So, let us try to find out issue proceeds to the firm. What will issue proceeds to the firm be? You understand that, the firm is issuing 600 shares, right? So therefore, it is equal to number of primary shares that the firm is issuing multiplied by net price which the firm is going to get per share. Number of primary shares being issued as we know is 600. And what is our net price? Our net price is going to be price net of underwriter spread, okay? The offer price. So our net price, as you know, this will be equal to offer price into 1 minus underwriter spread. Okay? So, number of primary shares are 600. So, what is our offer price here? Our offer price here is Rs. 50, okay, into 1 minus underwriter spread. Underwriter spread is 5%, which means 0.05. So if we solve this, what do we get here? We get Rs. 28,500. So therefore, net issue proceeds to the firm will be 28,500 rupees. Now, let us also try to compute issue proceeds to existing shareholders. Let us look at issue proceeds to existing shareholders that will be equal to number of secondary shares that existing shareholders are selling off multiplied by net price. Number of secondary shares that existing shareholders are selling off, it is equal to 400. Our net price is equal to our offer price, which is 50, into 1 minus underwriter spread, which is 0.05. What does that give us? If you compute it, it gives us 19,000 rupees. It tells us that existing shareholders are going to get 19,000 rupees on a net basis. Now, let us look at part B. Part B asks, \"How much commission did the underwriters receive?\" So, let us compute underwriter commission. How many shares are underwriters helping sell? Underwriters are helping sell a total of 1,000 shares. So therefore, it is equal to total number of shares that underwriters are helping sell multiplied by offer price into underwriter spread. So, number of shares which underwriters are helping sell is 1,000. The offer price per share is Rs. 50, and the underwriter spread is 5% or 0.05. So therefore, our underwriter commission comes out to be Rs. 2,500. This is the money which underwriters are making. Now, let us come to part C. It says, how much money was left on the table? Which means that we have to find out the cost of underpricing. What is the cost of underpricing? How do we compute it? How do we compute the cost of underpricing for 1 share? So, the cost of underpricing for 1 share will be equal to your first day closing price minus your offer price. And therefore, what is your total cost of underpricing? It will be total number of shares issued multiplied by this difference between first day closing price and offer price. So therefore, our cost of underpricing, it is equal to total number of shares, total number of shares issued multiplied by the difference between first day closing price minus offer price per share. Total number of shares issued is 1,000. The first day closing price is Rs. 100, and the offer price is Rs. 50. And therefore, what does that give us? It simply gives us Rs. 50,000. So therefore, how much money was left on the table? It tells us that 50,000 rupees were left on the table. Now, let us come to our last part, which is part D. It says, what was the cost of underpricing and underwriting to the selling shareholders? First of all, let us look at cost of underpricing to selling shareholders. Let us look at the cost of under pricing to existing shareholders. What should this be equal to? This should be equal to number of shares that existing shareholders are selling off, which means it is equal to number of secondary shares multiplied by the cost of underpricing for 1 share, which is nothing but your first day closing price minus minus offer price. Number of secondary shares which are issued is 400 multiplied by the first day closing price, which is Rs. 100, minus offer price, which is Rs. 50. This gives us a total of Rs. 20,000. So, 20,000 rupees is the cost of underpricing to existing shareholders. Now, let us also try to compute the cost of underwriting to selling shareholders. Let us compute cost of underwriting to existing or selling shareholders. What will this be equal to? This will again be equal to number of secondary shares or number of shares that existing shareholders are selling off, multiplied by offer price per share and this multiplied by your underwriter spread. Number of secondary shares is 400, offer price is Rs. 50, and the underwriter spread is 5%. So, what does that give us? That gives us 1,000 rupees as the cost of underwriting to existing shareholders. So therefore, what is your total cost, total cost to existing or selling shareholders? Total cost to existing or selling shareholders is equal to the cost of underpricing plus the cost of underwriting, which is equal to Rs. 20,000 plus Rs. 1,000. And this gives us Rs. 21,000 as our total IPO cost to existing or selling shareholders.",
