      "transcript_text": "Now, let us look at how investors behave in IPOs in India. SEBI, which is Securities and Exchange Board of India, the securities market regulator in India, released findings of its study in September 2024. So, SEBI essentially looked at IPOs starting from 1st April 2018 until 31st December 2023. In India, as you might know, the financial year starts from 1st April, and it ends in March 31st of the next calendar year. So now, what did SEBI notice in general? So, SEBI noticed that in general there was an upward trend in terms of number of IPOs and also issue amount. And findings of SEBI were as follows. Interestingly, SEBI observed that 54% of IPO shares in value terms which are allotted to investors are sold off within a week from listing. So, which means that majority of the investors who are investing in IPO shares, they are selling off their shares in less than a week from listing. If you look at individual investors, they sold off 50.2% of shares by value allotted to them within a week from listing. Now, if we look at institutional investors such as mutual funds and banks, let us see how they behaved in IPOs. Mutual funds tended to invest for a longer period in IPO shares, whereas banks tended to sell swiftly. For example, mutual funds sold off 3.3% of allotted value within a week, whereas this figure was 79.8% for banks. So, what does that tell us? It tells us that mutual funds appear to be long-term investors in IPO shares, while banks appear to be short-term investors in IPO shares. Very, very interestingly, we had disposition effect exhibited by investors. You must be wondering what is disposition effect. So, disposition effect is your tendency to prematurely sell winners and holding on to losers, right? So, investors exhibited a disposition effect in investing in Indian IPOs. For example, individual investors sold off 67.6% shares by value allotted to them within a week when returns were more than 20%. However, when returns were negative, individual investors only sold off 23.3% of shares by value which were allotted to them within a week. So essentially, what do we observe from here? We observe that individual investors prematurely sold off the winning stocks, and they continue to hold on to losing stocks. And this effect is what we call as disposition effect. Also, if we look at where did these investors come from. So these retail investors, interestingly, majorly came from Gujarat. Gujarat comprised of 39.3% of retail investors, followed by Maharashtra, which stood at 13.5%, and Rajasthan, which stood at 10.5%. Also, almost half of the total allotted Demat accounts that applied for IPOs during the period starting from April 2021 until December 2023 were opened in the post-COVID period, which means during the calendar year starting from 2021 until 2023.",
