      "transcript_text": "Now, let us talk about seasoned equity offerings. When an issue of equity is made by a firm which is already listed, we call such an issue as a seasoned equity offering or an SEO in short. Now, SEOs can be broadly of 3 different kinds. They can be follow-on public offerings or FPOs, rights offerings, and private placements. So, depending on whom the issue of equity is made to, that is how we classify an SEO into 3 kinds. In the case of an FPO or a follow-on public offering, the issue of equity is intended to be made to the public at large. In the case of a rights offering, the issue of equity is intended to be made to existing shareholders of the firm. And in the case of a private placement, the issue of equity is intended to be made to small group of investors. Now, let us talk about follow-on public offerings in some detail. So, as we discussed, in the case of a follow-on public offering, the issue of equity is made to or at least intended to be made to, the public at large. Now in this case, the firm can either issue new shares, which means that the firm can raise money through issuance of new shares. Alternatively, existing shareholders of the firm can sell off a part of their holding in the case of an FPO. So therefore, in an FPO, we can have a primary offering, a secondary offering or a mix of primary and secondary offering. For example, if we talk about FPO by Vodafone Idea in 2024. So, this FPO was purely a primary offering. So, Vodafone Idea only issued new shares to the public at large so as to raise additional capital. Now, if we talk about ONGC FPO in the year 2004, it was purely an offer for sale or it was purely a secondary offering. So in this FPO, existing shareholders of ONGC decided to cash in by selling a part of their holdings. And finally, if we talk about the FPO by Power Grid Corporation in the years 2010 and 2013. In each of these years, Power Grid Corporation not only sold new shares to the public at large, but existing shareholders of Power Grid Corporation also sold off a part of their holdings. Now, let us look at top 5 FPOs in India as of the end of September 30th, 2026. The largest FPO so far has been by Vodafone Idea Limited. It happened in the year 2024, and it was purely an issue of fresh capital, which means that it was purely a primary offering. So in this case, the issue amount was 18,000 crore rupees, and if we look at issue expenses, they were 2.14% of the issue amount. The second-largest FPO has been by Yes Bank Limited. It happened in the year 2020, and this one also was an issue of fresh capital only, which means that this was again an issue of new shares by the firm. The issue amount was Rs. 15,000 crore, and issue expenses as a percentage of the issue amount formed about 1%. The next 3 FPOs have been by ONGC in the year 2004, ICICI Bank in the year 2007, and NMDC in the year 2010. Now, if you look at issue expenses as a percentage of issue amount in the case of FPOs, and if you contrast this with IPOs, what do you observe? You observe that issue expenses as a proportion of the issue amount for an FPO are generally lower than those for an IPO. Now, let us talk about a few characteristics of FPOs. Now, first of all, let us talk about how prevalent are FPOs. Now, FPOs are not very common in the Indian setting, especially if we compare them with other forms of seasoned equity offerings. However, in contrast, FPOs are quite common in the US context. Now, let us talk about FPO costs. Now, the costs of an FPO are high, but they are not as high as those for an IPO. And FPO costs include the cost of underpricing and issue expenses. Now, similar to IPOs, even FPOs are underpriced. However, the extent of underpricing is not as much as it is in the case of IPOs. If we look at the US setting, there the offer price on average is set below the previous day's closing price by about 2%. So, the extent of underpricing is roughly about 2% in the US setting. Also, if we talk about issue expenses, as we discussed previously, in the case of an FPO, issue expenses as a proportion of the issue amount are relatively lower when compared to IPOs. And they typically vary between 1 to 3% of the issue amount in the Indian setting. And these issue expenses include fees which are paid to investment bankers or merchant bankers or underwriters, fees which are paid to legal advisors, and other components of administrative costs.",
