      "transcript_text": "Now, let us move on to talking about second kind of a seasoned equity offering, which is a rights offering. Rights offerings are also called as rights issues. The first question that you might have is, what is a rights issue? A rights issue gives existing shareholders of the firm an option to buy new shares of the firm. And this option to buy new shares of the firm is typically at a discount to the current market price of the firm. And also, this offer is in proportion to the number of shares which existing shareholders already own. Okay, now the question is, what are the advantages of a rights issue? Now, there are 3 major advantages of a rights issue. The first one is that, it allows existing shareholders of a firm to keep their proportional shareholding or stake intact in the firm. If someone was holding, let us say, 10% stake in the firm, and if that person participates in the rights offering, in that case, the percentage stake of the firm will not change after the rights issue. Another feature of a rights issue is that they have lower costs or issue expenses relative to both IPOs as well as FPOs. And the third important advantage of a rights issue is that it protects existing shareholders of a firm from underpricing. So, you understand that in the case of IPOs and FPOs, and also in the case of private placements, which we are going to talk about in a while, there is more often than not some underpricing, which means that the company is selling shares at a price less than what the true price of the shares is. Now, this underpricing is a cost to existing shareholders of the firm. However, in the case of a rights offering, a firm can protect existing shareholders from this underpricing, and we will look at this in some detail as we go along, right? So therefore, there are 3 major advantages of a rights issue. It allows existing shareholders to maintain their proportional stake intact. It has lower costs, and it protects existing shareholders from underpricing. Now, there is a rights puzzle which is documented in the literature. Now, what is rights puzzle? So, you understand that rights issues are a cheaper way to raise money, and also they protect the existing shareholders of a firm from underpricing. Despite these advantages of rights issues, believe it or not, these issues are rare in the US setting, and therefore it remains a puzzle for many researchers that despite so many advantages of rights issues, why they are not so common method to raise money by firms. So, this is what we call as the rights puzzle. And this puzzle is there in the context of the US setting. However, in India, rights issues are quite common and firms often raise money using rights issues. Now, let us look at top 5 rights issues in India till September 30th, 2026. The largest rights issue was conducted by Reliance Industries in the year 2020, and the issue amount was close to 53,000 crore rupees. The issue expenses were 0.17% of the issue amount. The second largest rights issue was conducted by Vodafone Idea in the year 2019, and the issue amount was 25,000 crore rupees, and the issue expenses were 0.42% of the issue amount. The third largest rights issue was by Bharti Airtel Limited in the year 2019. The issue size was slightly less than 25,000 crore rupees, and the issue expenses were merely 0.09% of the issue amount. The fourth largest issue was by Adani Enterprises, and it was in the year 2025. And again, here also, the issue amount was slightly less than 25,000 crore rupees, and issue expenses were 0.1% of the issue amount. And the fifth largest rights issue was by, again, Bharti Airtel. It happened in the year 2021, and the issue amount was roughly 21,000 crore rupees, and issue expenses were 0.17% of the issue amount. Now, if you look at issue expenses as a percentage of the issue amount in the case of rights offering, and if you compare them with IPOs and FPOs issue expenses, what do you observe? You observe that in the case of a rights issue, the issue expenses as a proportion of the issue amount are on the lower side when compared to an IPO as well as an FPO. So therefore, it tells us that rights issues are a cheaper way of raising money. Now, let us look at a few questions. The first one is, who gets the rights? Does it depend on some cutoff date? Now, an individual who is buying the shares of the firm prior to ex-rights date gets the rights. And anyone who buys the shares of the firm on or after ex-rights date will not be eligible to get the rights. Now, rights are also called as rights entitlements. And remember that these are essentially options. So, what is an option? An option is something which gives you the right but not the obligation to purchase the underlying asset. In this case, the underlying asset is the shares of the firm. Now, another question you might have is, at what price can existing shareholders buy the shares of the firm using rights? So, existing shareholders of the firm can buy new shares of the firm at a price less than the current market price of the firm. And that price is what we call as the offer price or subscription price. So, let us say that, if the firm's shares are currently trading at Rs. 100, a rights offer might give you an option to purchase additional shares of the firm, let us say, at a price of Rs. 70. Now, another question you might have is, what impact does a rights issue have on the stock price? Now, remember that in a rights issue, shares will be issued at a discount often, right? So therefore, the stock price goes down once the shares start trading without rights. So therefore, we are going to talk about 2 kinds of prices. The first one is the rights on price. What is rights on price? So, the share price prior to X rights date is what we call as rights on price. And the second price we are going to talk about is X rights price. What is X rights price? So, the share price on or after the X rights date is what we call as the X rights price. Now, since the share price goes down on or after the X rights date, therefore your X rights price is going to be less than the rights on price. And why is it happening? This is happening because the firm is issuing new shares at a discount. We will talk about it through an example in a while.",
