      "transcript_text": "Now, let us look at a question as to how rights issues protect existing shareholders of a firm from underpricing. Let us look at one example. Suppose there is a company, and suppose this company has 1,000 rupees in cash, which is the only asset of the firm and this firm has 50 shares outstanding. And therefore, how much is each share worth? Each share is now worth Rs. 1,000 divided by 50, which gives us Rs. 20. So therefore, each share is worth Rs. 20. Okay, now suppose this company announces an FPO, a follow-on public offering for 50 new shares at a price of Rs. 10 per share So, what do you notice here? You notice that the firm is underpricing its shares in this FPO. The market price of the shares is Rs. 20, but the firm is selling 50 new shares at a price of Rs. 10 per share. Okay. Now the question is, how much money will the company be raising through this FPO? Since the company is issuing 50 new shares at a price of Rs. 10 per share, the company will be raising Rs. 500 in cash from this FPO. Now, my question to you is, once this offer is complete, what will be the price per share? Can you tell me how much cash will the company have at hand after this FPO is complete? The firm earlier had 1,000 rupees of cash. The firm is raising 500 rupees of cash in this FPO, and therefore, after the FPO, the company will have 1,500 rupees in cash. How many shares will be outstanding after the FPO? Prior to the FPO, the company had 50 shares outstanding. The company is issuing 50 new shares in the FPO, and therefore, after the FPO, there will be a total of 100 shares outstanding. Therefore, can you tell me what will be the price per share after the FPO? It will simply be equal to your market value of equity after the FPO, which is 1,500 rupees, divided by the number of shares outstanding, which is 100. So therefore, 1,500 divided by 100 gives you Rs. 15. So therefore, the price per share after the FPO will be Rs. 15. Okay, now let me ask you a very important question. The question is, how does underpricing affect the wealth of old and new shareholders? Let us first of all talk about market value of equity of old shareholders. How many shares did old shareholders have? Old shareholders had 50 shares before the FPO, and they continue to own these 50 shares after the FPO as well. However, after the FPO, the share price of the firm has fallen from Rs. 20 to Rs. 15 per share. So therefore, what will be the market value of equity of old shareholders? It will be 50 shares multiplied by the current market price after the FPO, which is Rs. 15. This gives you Rs. 750. Okay. What did you notice? The shareholders earlier had market value of equity, which was 1,000 rupees, but it has fallen to Rs. 750 after the FPO. In other words, these old shareholders have suffered a loss of wealth of Rs. 250. Now, let us talk about market value of equity of new shareholders. Those shareholders who have participated in the FPO. How many shares did the firm issue to new shareholders in the FPO? The firm issued 50 new shares to new shareholders in the FPO. And what is the current market price per share after the FPO? It is Rs. 15. So therefore, what is the market value of equity of new shareholders? It is 50 into 15, which gives you Rs. How much money did these new shareholders contribute in the FPO? If you recall, these shareholders only contributed Rs. 500, but after the FPO, their market value of equity has become Rs. 750. In other words, these new shareholders have gained Rs. 250. So, from this exercise, what do we observe? We observe that there has been a transfer of wealth equal to Rs. 250 from old shareholders to new shareholders. Okay? Now, let me ask you another question. My question is, what will be the price per share if the company goes for a rights issue instead of an FPO for 50 shares at Rs. 10 per share? So here, the offer price or subscription price is Rs. 10 per share in the rights offering. You can assume that all existing shareholders of the firm participate in the rights issue. So, in this case, existing shareholders will be issued 50 new shares of the firm at a price of Rs. So therefore, how much money is the company going to raise from this rights issue? The company is going to raise Rs. 500 from existing shareholders of the firm in this rights issue. Now, how much cash will this company have after the rights issue? The company will have 1,000 plus 500, which gives you 1,500 rupees of cash. So, the company will have 1,500 rupees of cash after the rights issue. How many shares are outstanding after the rights issue? It's 50 plus 50, which is 100. So therefore, what is going to be price per share after the rights issue? It will be 1,500 divided by 10, which gives you Rs. 15. So, your price per share is same as that with FPO. Now, the underlying question here is, can a rights issue protect existing or old shareholders of a firm from the transfer of wealth which happened in the case of an FPO? Let us see if a rights issue can really protect existing shareholders of a firm from this wealth transfer. Now, what is going to be market value of equity of existing shareholders after the rights issue? Now, after the rights issue, these existing shareholders have 1,000 shares outstanding and the price per share after the rights issue which we just computed is Rs. 15. And therefore, market value of shares or market value of equity for existing shareholders is Rs. 1,500. Now, my question to you is, by how much has the wealth of existing shareholders gone up? Earlier, the market value of their equity, if you recall, was Rs. 1,000. Now, it is Rs. 1,500. It has gone up by Rs. 500, which is exactly the amount which these shareholders contributed in the rights offering. So therefore, what do we conclude from here? We conclude that existing shareholders of a firm are protected from wealth transfer in the rights offering under the assumption that all existing shareholders participate in the rights offering.",
