      "transcript_text": "Now, let us talk about two different kinds of rights. So technically, rights can be either North American rights or European rights. Let us first of all talk about North American rights. So, companies which issue North American rights, they give shareholders 1 right for every share that they hold. However, several rights are required to buy 1 new share of the firm. Now, the question is, what is the value of 1 North American right? The value of 1 North American right, it is equal to the X rights price minus subscription price divided by N. What is N here? So, here N refers to the number of rights that are needed to purchase 1 new share of the firm at a subscription price of Ps. And what is Px here? Here Px refers to the X rights price, okay. Now, let us talk about European right. Firms issuing European rights give shareholders less than 1 right for each share that they hold. However, 1 right entitles the shareholder to buy 1 new share by paying the subscription price or offer price. So therefore, what is going to be value of 1 European right? It will simply be equal to X rights price minus subscription price, which means Px minus Ps. In India, firms issue European rights. Now, this distinction between North American and European rights only matters when you compute the value of a right. With the North American rights issue procedure, a shareholder receives more rights, but each is worth correspondingly less. Now let us talk about a hypothetical example of a rights issue or a rights offer to illustrate some of the things. Okay, so let us say that we have a firm, and this firm has 100 million shares outstanding. And let us say that the price per share is Rs. 100. Therefore, what will be the market value of equity of the firm? It will be 10,000 million rupees, okay. Now let us say that promoters own 60% stake in the firm, which means they hold 60 million shares of the firm. And let us say that other shareholders have therefore 40% stake in the firm, and therefore they will own 40 million shares of the firm. Now, what will be the market value of equity of promoters? It will be equal to 60 million shares that they hold multiplied by price per share, which is Rs. 100, which is 6,000 million rupees. Similarly, what is the market value of equity of other shareholders? It is equal to 40 million shares that the shareholders own multiplied 100. So, this gives us market value of equity of 4,000 million rupees. Now let us say that this particular firm goes for a rights issue. This firm issues European rights, which means what? Which means that for many shares, the firm will issue 1 right. So, let us say that in this case, the firm issues 1 right for every 5 shares. And let us further assume that using this 1 right, you can buy 1 new share of the firm at a subscription price of Rs. 70, okay. So, let us begin with our first scenario in which there is 100% take up by all shareholders, which means that all shareholders are going to participate in this rights offering, okay. So in this case, can you tell me how many rights will the firm be issuing? You understand that the firm is issuing 1 right for every 5 shares, and since there are 100 million shares outstanding, therefore, how many rights will the firm be issuing? The firm will be issuing 100 divided by 5, which is nothing but 20 million rights. And using each of these rights, existing shareholders can buy 1 new share for every right at a subscription price of Rs. 70. So therefore, how many new shares will be issued in the rights offering? Since, you have 20 million rights and since there is 100% take up by all shareholders, the firm will be issuing 20 million new shares. So therefore, there will be a total of 120 million shares outstanding after the rights issue. Fair enough? Now, let us look at promoters. Promoters had how many shares before the rights offering? They had 60 million shares. And therefore, how many rights will they be getting? They will be getting 60 divided by 5, which is equal to 12 million rights. And using these 12 million rights, they will be able to buy 12 million shares at a price of Rs. 70 per share, right. So therefore, your promoters will have 72 million shares And therefore, what will be their percentage stake in the firm after the rights issue? It will be 72 divided by 120, which again gives you 60%, okay. Now, let us go to other shareholders. Similarly, other shareholders will be issued 8 million new shares, and therefore they will have 48 million shares outstanding after the rights issue. And therefore, what is going to be the percentage stake of other shareholders? It is simply going to be 48 divided by 120, which gives you 40%. So, the firm has issued 20 million new shares, and for each share the firm is getting a subscription price of Rs. 70. So therefore, how much m1y is the firm raising? It is 20 million into 70, which gives us 1,400 rupees. So therefore, what will be your market value of equity of the It will be 10,000 plus 1,400, which will give you 11,400 million rupees. From here, can we not compute the stock price or share price? We know the market value of equity. We know the total number of shares outstanding. So therefore, if you divide 11,400 with 120, what are you going to get? You will get a share price of Rs. 95, okay. So therefore, if your share price is 95 rupees after the rights issue, can you tell me what is going to be your market value of equity of promoters? It will be number of shares that promoters have, which is 72 million, multiplied by price per share after the rights issue, which is 95. This simply gives you 6,840 million rupees. Similarly, what is going to be market value of equity of other shareholders? Market value of equity of other shareholders will be equal to number of shares they have, which is 48 million, multiplied by the price per share after the rights issue, which is 95 million. So, this gives you what? So, this gives you 4,560 million rupees. Now, obviously in this case, there has not been any wealth transfer.",
