      "transcript_text": "Now, let us look at scenario 2. In scenario 2, let us say that there is 100% take up by promoters and there is no take up by other shareholders, which means that only promoters are taking part in the rights issue and other shareholders are not taking part in the rights issue. Okay. So in this case, new shares will be issued only to promoters. So, how many new shares will be issued? So in this case, since promoters have 60 million shares before the rights issue, therefore they will be issued 12 million rights. And therefore using these 12 million rights, these promoters can buy 12 million new shares. And therefore, here the firm will be issuing 12 million new shares. So, number of shares outstanding after the rights issue in scenario 2, therefore is going to be 112 million. Now, promoters will have 72 million shares, and therefore what is going to be their percentage stake? It will be 72 divided by 112, which gives you what? Which gives you 64.3% stake. So, the stake of promoters has gone up. Now, let us look at the stake of other shareholders. Now, if we look at other shareholders, they did not participate in the rights offering. So therefore they will continue to have 40 million shares and therefore what will happen to their stake? So, their stake will now be 40 divided by 112, which gives you 35.7%. So, you observe that the stake of other shareholders in the firm has come down because they did not participate in the rights offering. Now, let us try to find out the market value of equity of the firm after the rights issue. How much money did the firm raise? The firm issued 12 million new shares each at a price of Rs 70. So therefore, the firm will be raising 840 million rupees in money. And therefore, what is going to be market value of equity of the firm after the rights issue in scenario 2? It will be 10,840 million rupees. So from here, can we not find out our share price? Yes. Our share price therefore in this case will be 10,840 divided by 112, which will give us 96.8 rupees. Now, can we compute the market value of equity of promoters? Promoters have 72 million shares, and each share is now priced 96.8 rupees. So therefore, what happens to market value of equity of promoters? Market value of equity of promoters has gone up. It has gone up to 6,968.6 rupees. So, what will be the market value of equity of promoters after the rights issue in scenario 2? It will be equal to 72 million shares they hold multiplied by price per share after the rights issue, which is 96.8. So, this essentially gives you market value of equity, which is 6,968.6 million rupees. On the other hand, if we talk about other shareholders. Other shareholders, they continue to own 40 million shares, and therefore their market value of equity will be equal to 40 million shares multiplied by price per share, which is 96.8. So, this gives you 3,871.4 million rupees. So, what did you notice from here? You notice that the market value of equity of other shareholders has come down from 4,000 million rupees to 3,871.4 million rupees. So essentially, these other shareholders did not do anything. And despite they not doing anything, their market value of equity has come down. So, it tells you that non-participation in a rights issue has a cost. On the other hand, if you look at market value of equity of promoters, how much money did promoters contribute? Promoters contributed 840 million rupees. But by how much has their market value of equity gone up? The market value of equity, however, has gone up by 968.6 million rupees. In other words, we observe that there is a transfer of wealth from other shareholders to promoters. And how much is the transfer of wealth? The transfer of wealth is 128.6 million rupees. So therefore, there is a transfer of wealth in a rights offering from non-participating shareholders to participating shareholders. Now, let us look at our last scenario, which is typically what the case is in the Indian setting. So, there is going to be 100% take up by promoters, and there will be no take up by other shareholders. And also promoters can subscribe to unsubscribed portion of the rights offering. Now, in this case, how many shares will the firm be issuing in this rights offering? So essentially, only promoters are participating, and promoters are not just subscribing to their own rights entitlement, but they are also subscribing to the unsubscribed portion of other shareholders. So therefore, how many shares will the firm be issuing now? The firm will now be issuing 20 million shares. And this entire 20 million shares will be issued to promoters at a price of Rs. 70 per share. Okay? So therefore, how many shares will be there with promoters after the rights issue? So, they will have 80 million shares. 60 million what they had previously, and 20 million shares they have been issued. And therefore, what happens to their percentage stake? So, if you look at their percentage stake, it has increased to 66.7%. Now, let us look at other shareholders. Other shareholders did not participate in the rights offering. They continued holding 40 million shares that they had. And therefore, what is going to happen to their shareholding, which means percentage stake? It will reduce to 33.3% because now it will be 40 divided by 120. Now, let us look at market value of equity of the firm after the rights issue. So in this case again, the firm is issuing 20 million shares at a price of Rs. 70 each share. And therefore, the firm is raising 1,400 million rupees in the rights issue. And therefore, what will be the market value of equity of the firm after the rights issue? It will be equal to market value before, plus whatever funds the firm has raised, which means 10,000 million rupees plus 1400 million rupees. What will that give you? It will give you 11,400 million rupees. Our share price after the rights issue will be 11,400 divided by 120, which will give you again Rs. 95. Now, let us compute market value of equity of promoters. It will be equal to 80 million shares that the promoters are holding multiplied with share price of 95 rupees. What will that be? That will be equal to 7,600 million rupees. How much money did promoters contribute? So in this case, promoters contributed 1,400 million rupees. But their market value of equity has gone up by 1,600 million rupees. So, they are gaining 200 million rupees more than what they contributed. Let us now also look at other shareholders. In the case of other shareholders, they will continue to own 40 million shares, and each share is priced at Rs. 95 after the rights issue. And therefore, market value of equity of other shareholders is going to be 3,800 million rupees. So, if you see here, the market value of equity of other shareholders has come down from 4,000 million rupees to 3,800 million rupees. So essentially, there is a decrease in the market value of equity of other shareholders, and there is increase in the market value of equity of promoters, which is equal to 200 million rupees. In other words, there is a transfer of wealth happening from other shareholders to promoters, from non-participating shareholders to participating shareholders. So therefore, we have 2 major conclusions from this exercise. The first one is that in a rights offering, if not all existing shareholders of a firm participate, in that case, there is going to be a transfer of wealth from non-participating shareholders And also the shareholding of the participating shareholders will go up at the expense of the shareholding of non-participating shareholders.",
