      "transcript_text": "Now, let us talk about participation in rights issues in India. Now, if you are a shareholder of a rights issuing firm, you have 3 options. The first option is you can participate in the rights issue by exercising your right. The second one is you can renounce your right in favour of somebody else. And the third is that you can forfeit your right by allowing it to expire. Now, if you renounce your right in favour of somebody else, and that somebody else is called as a renouncee, and you will be called as the renouncer. Now, renouncee has only 2 options. Renouncee can either participate in the rights issue by exercising her right or the renouncee can forfeit the right by letting it expire. A renouncee, however, cannot renounce her rights any further. Now, both shareholders and renounsees can subscribe to the unsubscribed portion of the rights offering, however, shareholders have a higher priority. Now, let me ask you one question. Do you think rights issues are underwritten by investment bankers in India? The answer is no. Almost in all rights issues in India, you will observe that rights issues are rarely underwritten by investment bankers in India. On the contrary, it's common to find the following statement in a letter of offer of a rights issue. The promoter group has confirmed to the company that they intend to subscribe to their rights entitlement in full. In the event of undersubscription, the promoters, along with the promoter group, intend to apply for additional shares to make the rights issue fully subscribed to the extent of 100% of the offer. So, the above statement effectively implies that promoters themselves underwrite rights issues. Why do promoters underwrite rights issues? The answer is simple. In a rights issues, the shares are issued at a discount, and there is a transfer of wealth from non-participating to participating shareholders. And therefore, if promoters can get new shares of the firm at a cheaper price than what they are available otherwise in the secondary market, why would they not participate in the rights issue? Now, an important fact is that in the Indian setting, despite the transferability of rights issues, only about three-fourths of entitlements of a rights offering on average are exercised by shareholders. This is in contrast with 64% average shareholder participation in rights offerings by US firms. Also, in the US, almost 50% of offerings are non-transferable. In the Indian context, almost all offerings are transferable, which means that you can sell off your right to somebody else. Now, about one-fourth of the rights entitlements are not exercised by their rightful owners, and most of them are oversubscribed predominantly by promoters or insiders. Now, let me ask you this question. What are the consequences of non-participation by other shareholders, primarily the retail shareholders in rights issues? We have already covered this question. As you understand, this leads to the transfer of wealth from non-participating to oversubscribing shareholders who are often the promoters of rights issuing firms. So therefore, as a consequence, you also understand that the promoter ownership jumps up significantly after the rights issue. Now, you must be wondering, given that rights carry value, why wouldn't every existing shareholder participate in the rights issue or at least renounce their rights? There are 3 reasons for that, at least. The first is that prior to the year 2020, rights, unlike shares, were not traded electronically. So, if you wanted to sell your right to somebody else, it was quite cumbersome for you to renounce or sell your rights. So, that is one reason. The second reason is that some shareholders might face liquidity and transaction costs. For example, as an existing shareholder of the rights issuing firm, you may not be in a position to participate in the rights issue because you don't have enough liquidity at hand. You do not have enough money at hand to purchase the new shares of the firm. Also, there are other market frictions like transaction costs. For example, depending on your investment horizon, if you sell off your shares, what will happen? You will attract either long-term or short-term capital gains tax. So, this is another kind of a friction which we have in financial markets, and therefore, you may not want to sell off your shares. And the third reason is that not all shareholders may be equally informed that rights carry value and that their non-participation can potentially transfer their wealth to participating shareholders. So, this one has to relate with investor education. If investors are not fully educated, they may not be able to understand as to how much value do rights carry.",
