      "transcript_text": "So, you understand that a firm can fund investment in real assets either using own capital or loan capital. Until this point in time, we were focusing on equity financing decisions, which means that money which is contributed by shareholders or owners of the company. Now, we will briefly talk about debt financing decisions. So, you understand that a firm can take a loan from a bank or a financial institution or a company can also issue bonds. Now here, as part of our debt financing decisions, we will talk about bonds very, very quickly. Now, first of all, let us talk about bond indenture. What is a bond indenture? It is nothing but a written agreement between the bond issuer and the bondholders. Who is the bond issuer? The bond issuer is the firm which is the borrower. And who are bondholders? Bondholders are lenders or creditors. They are essentially lending the money to the firm. So, this bond indenture is a legal document, and this runs into hundreds of pages. We will look at some provisions of the bond indenture. The first one is the form. Now, depending on the form, bonds can be either bearer bonds or they can be registered bonds. Let us first of all talk about bearer bonds. So, whosoever physically holds the bond certificate, that person owns the bond. So, these kind of bonds are essentially bearer bonds. They are similar to currency. So, if you own the currency, if you have the currency, you essentially own it. The holder of the bond certificate detaches the coupons and presents them to the company's agent at the appropriate time to receive payment. Now, the disadvantage of a bearer bond is that it is difficult to recover if these bonds are lost or stolen. And another disadvantage is that because the company does not know who owns the bonds, it cannot notify bondholders of important events. Now, let us talk about the registered bonds. In the case of a registered bond, the company has a registrar who will record the initial ownership of each bond as well as any changes in ownership. The company will pay the coupons and face value directly to the owner of record. Almost all bonds that are issued nowadays are essentially registered bonds. Now, the second feature of bond indenture that we are going to talk about is security. So sometimes, an issuer sets aside or pledges particular assets as a security for the protection of bondholders. The term collateral is commonly used to refer to any asset pledged on debt. So therefore, depending on whether you have a collateral or not, we can classify our bonds into 2 broad kinds, secured bonds and unsecured bonds. Secured bonds are those bonds which are backed by a collateral. While unsecured bonds are those bonds which are not backed by a collateral. Now, another feature of bond indenture is seniority. Seniority indicates a preference in position over other lenders. Now, bonds or debt are often labeled as senior or junior to indicate seniority. In the event a firm becomes bankrupt, senior debt is paid in full first before junior or subordinated debt is paid. However, debt cannot be subordinated to equity. Equity is the residual claim on the assets of the firm, and equity holders will get anything only after bond holders have been paid in full. Now, let us talk about protective covenants. What are protective covenants? A protective covenant is a clause in a bond contract that affects certain corporate actions. So, these can be either negative covenants or positive covenants. Let us talk about negative covenants. Negative covenants limit or prohibit actions that a company might take. For example, the firm must limit the amount of dividends it pays according to some formula. This is an example of a negative covenant. For example, sometimes you might see a covenant which might state that the firm shall not pay annual cash dividends to shareholders in excess of 25% of its net income. So as you can see, this particular covenant is a negative covenant. This is prohibiting a company to do certain things. We also have something called as positive covenants, which specify an action which a company must take or a condition which a company must abide by. For example, a positive covenant could be that the firm must maintain its working capital above some specified minimum level. A particular bond indenture may feature many different negative and positive covenants. Now, let me ask you one question. My question is, would shareholders try to include as few protective covenants as possible in a bond agreement? What do you think? Not necessarily is the answer. In fact, shareholders might voluntarily choose to put covenants into a new bond issue. Can you think why? Because by including more covenants, issuers can reduce their cost of borrowing. The greater the number of covenants or stronger the covenants in the bond agreement, the less likely the issuer will default on the bond, and so the lower the interest rate required by investors who buy the bond. So therefore, shareholders may not try to include as few covenants as possible. They might want to include more covenants as it brings down the cost of debt. Now, let us look at one last provision or feature of bond indenture, which is call provision. What is a call provision? A call provision gives the bond issuer the right, but not the obligation to retire the entire or part of the bond issue at some price, which is called as the call price over a specified period. Now, call price is often expressed as a percentage of the bond's face value, and call price is usually set at or above the bond's face value. Now, bonds which have a call provision are called as callable bonds. My question is, when will an issuer exercise its right to call the bond? The issuer will exercise its right to call the bond as long as the call provision offers a cheaper way to retire the bonds than repurchasing them in the open market. In other words, if the actual bond price rises above the call price due to fall in interest rates, for example, the company will call it. Now, another question I have for you is, how do you think call provisions affect the price of a bond? So as you can figure out, if the bonds are callable, the bond price will be less than if the bonds are not callable. And therefore, other things equal, the bondholder will not pay as much for a callable bond.",
