      "created_at": "2026-11-06 08:57:19",
      "transcript_text": "Let us look at one example on callable bonds. Fictonia Corporation has an outstanding 30-year bond that pays semi-annual coupons. It is a 7% bond with a Rs. 100 face value. Further suppose that because of potential changes in interest rates, the price of the bond could be either Rs. 80 or Rs. 120 at the end of the year with equal probability, which means that there is 50% probability that the price will be Rs. 80 and another 50% probability that the price will be Rs. 120, 1 year from today. The first question before you is, \"what is the expected price of the bond after 1 year?\" So, let us try to find out expected bond price after 1 year. It will be equal to, you understand that there is 50% probability that the bond price will be Rs. 80 and another 50% probability that the bond price will be Rs. 120. So therefore, our expected bond price will be a probability weighted outcome. It will be 0.5 into 80 plus 0.5 into 120, which is 40 plus 60. So therefore, our expected bond price after 1 year will be Rs. 100, in the case of part A. Now, let us look at part B. It says, \"What is the expected price of the bond after 1 year if it is a callable bond and it can be called at Rs. 100?\" Now, can you tell me under what condition will the company call this bond back? The company will call this bond back, if the bond price happens to be more than Rs. 100 after a year, because it is going to be a cheaper way to retire this bond, right? So therefore, as a bond holder, you are not going to get 120 rupees after a year because as soon as the bond price becomes 120 rupees, the company will call it back. So therefore, what will be your expected bond price of this callable bond after a year? So, it will be 0.5 into 80 because if the bond price is Rs. 80, the company will not call this bond back. Why will the company pay 100 rupees for a bond which is trading at a price of Rs. 80, right? So therefore, the only possibility is that if the bond price becomes Rs. 120, the company is going to call this bond back at a price of Rs. 100. So therefore, plus 0.5 into 100. So, this gives you 40 plus 50, which is nothing but Rs. 90. Thank you.",
