          "difficulty": "Moderate | Tricky",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The lecture explicitly states that investors are risk-averse, meaning they require additional return to compensate for extra risk. If two assets offer the same expected return, a risk-averse investor will prefer the one with lower risk. Distractor A is flawed because risk-averse investors dislike risk, they do not seek it for profit. Distractor C is incorrect because total risk (sigma) includes specific risk, which is diversified away for well-diversified investors and does not offer a rational upside premium in equilibrium. Distractor D violates the core assumption of risk aversion presented at the start of the lecture.",
          "key_concept": "Risk Aversion and Investor Preference"
