      "transcript_text": "Welcome to Startup Dilemmas Founding or Joining. You have already completed multiple entrepreneurship courses in this program. Entrepreneurial methods and mindsets, explorations in entrepreneurship, 250-rupee venture, entrepreneurial hypothesis testing. So I'm not going to go to begin by asking whether entrepreneurship matters or by giving you a list of startup terms. I want to begin with a much more personal and a more difficult question. Suppose you're committed to an entrepreneurial career. You want to work close-closely with new ideas, uncertain markets, fast learning and creation of something that does not exist. What should you do next? Should you go and found a venture right now? Or should you go join a startup first? So notice the wordings. The question is not, am I an entrepreneur or an employee? That framing is not very, what I would say fine. It's pretty slightly crude It turns a career decision into an identity test. It suggests that founders are entrepreneurial and everybody else is standing outside entrepreneurship. That is simply not a useful way to think. Founding and joining can both be routes into an entrepreneurial career. They put you in different positions. They give you different kind of responsibilities, learnings, risk, ownership, and control. And either route can be the next best move for a person at a particular time. That phrase, \"The better next move,\" will be important throughout this session. You're not deciding your identity for your rest of your life. You're deciding what to do next based on what you know now, what you care about now, and what opportunities are actually available now. Someone can join a startup, learn rapidly, build a network, discover a problem, and found later. Somebody can found, close the venture, join another company, found again. Someone can work in an established organization, develop deep domain knowledge, and then build. Careers are sequences. They're not labels. So the objective of this course and this specific module is to help you evaluate that personal career and market circumstances that shape the founding or joining decision, and to articulate your motivations and their implications. So I want you to make a initial private choice. If you had to decide today with the opportunities and abilities that you have today, would you found now or join a startup first? Don't choose the answer that sounds more impressive. Choose the answer that you would actually live with. Then ask yourselves why. Is it because you already see a problem worth solving? Because you want autonomy? Because you want to learn? Because you dislike having a boss? Because founder stories have made founding feel like an ambitious career choice? Because founding feels safer? Because your family circumstances influence how much certainty you can absorb? Keep your answer in mind. By the end, I don't not necessarily want you to change it. I want you to understand it better. This is a decision under uncertainty. There's no formula that can tell every ambitious student to found, and no research finding that can tell every young person to wait. While evidence can improve your judgment, expert opinions can challenge your instincts. Other people's lives can provide some useful constructs but your interpretation remains necessary.",
