      "transcript_text": "Throughout the session, I will use these differences in plain language. When I say research suggests, I mean this is a claim that has been supported by a systematic study. Or when I say Paul Graham argues, I mean somebody influential who practiced and has created wide-scale impact, that's his opinion. So,  when I use names like Falguni Nayar or Kunal Shah, which you have links to in your course outline for you to go and listen to, I'm just using this as a life context, as an illustration. And it is not to present one's person's biography as a proof. When I say one way to interpret this is, it's one data point that can help you choose. It's a practical implication for your choice. And that really distinction matters in your mind. Entrepreneurial careers are very vulnerable to stories. Stories are quite vivid. Evidence is not always out there. And good judgment that you guys are going to make need both So, the central question, which is very simple to ask but very hard to answer for each one of us and for you all is: If one wants an entrepreneurial career, what is the best next move? Founding a venture or joining and learn fast? Wanting to found is not the same as being ready to found. There's a huge difference. Let's start with a distinction that sounds obvious, but it's very easy to ignore. Wanting to be a founder is not the same as having a reason to found now. Founder can become an attractive identity. It signals ambition, interdependence, independence, creativity, courage. Once that identity becomes important to us, we may start to search for a startup idea that allows us to live that identity. So, the sequence is, \"I want to become a founder, therefore, I need an idea.\" But the venture does not exist to complete the founder's identity. It exists only if it creates enough value for customers, users, and other stakeholders to survive. I think multiple times in the past we said that a startup is a temporary organization that is in search of a sustainable business model. So, just because you have started doesn't mean a lot of this business model elements are in place. So, before you do anything, please go and read Paul Graham's essay, Before the Startup. It's a useful text to read and reflect. Most of you all, I'm assuming, knows Paul Graham. But if you don't, he is the founder of Y Combinator, and Y Combinator, as an accelerator, has impacted tens and thousands of individuals by nurturing ventures like Reddit, Airbnb, and the likes. Lots of Indian startups have actually gone to Y Combinator as well, Razorpay, Meesho, and the likes. A lot of his essays are come out of deep reflection and his experience in the valley. So, it's not an empirical research paper that spoke to 100 or 200 and then interpreted the result. So, it's an essay of one person who has an extremely large presence in the valley. Sam Altman, who built OpenAI and unleashed the entire AI to us right now, was part of Y Combinator. He headed Y Combinator for a while. So,  the impact of Paul Graham is immense if you were to look at it. But his essays should be seen as, like, one person's interpretation. It's a very experienced advice, especially at people who would want to start. So, his warning is that young people can become too knowledgeable about outward mechanics of startups and too weakly connected to a real problem. Remember in one of our earlier classes, we said fall in love with the problem and not the solution? So, I have seen this very often when we conducted events here in IIM Bangalore, especially for entrepreneurs. There were a lot of people who would come and they come very often. They know which startup has raised money, which founder has exited, which founder has made money. So, they are very knowledgeable about startups. And they keep saying that we are going to one day start our venture, but haven't seen that happening. So, many of them learn the language of fundraising, evaluation or this pitch deck, that pitch deck and things like that. So, he calls this imitation of startup appearance as playing house, which is imaginary game that children play. The practical point is not the startup mechanics are useless. You will need to learn them when they become more relevant. The point is that the mechanics cannot substitute for making something that people want. So, making something that people want is a very, very important term to keep in mind because 90% of startups fail, and many of them because they have made something that they want and not what their customers want, right. That's very important. So, Paul Graham therefore advises young people and would-be founders to learn important things. That you should work on problems that genuinely interest you and work with people that you like and respect. His argument is that domain expertise grows from genuine curiosity. In his view, the mind that produces good startup ideas is often built indirectly through sustained contact with important problems rather than through forced brainstorming session titled \"Find a startup idea\" like we did. What we did was more in-class exercise, but and I've also mentioned in my course that Maggi came from brainstorming, but they were brainstorming about a problem, not an idea. They were saying, \"What are the problems that people seem to have?\" So, they were brainstorming problems that they think they could address based on their capabilities and not a startup idea that they want to work on. So, Paul Graham deliberately provokes about timing. He says for most people around 20. He favors broad exploration over the all-consuming depth of a startup. But his own essay includes an important exception. If a side project genuinely takes off, it may be reasonable to pursue it. So, his position is not never to found young. It is to do not force founding merely because you want to be an entrepreneur and you like that title.",
