      "transcript_text": "So now consider an alternative perspective. Youth can provide genuine advantages. A young founder may have fewer fixed commitments, lower opportunity cost, proximity to new technologies or emerging behavior, energy for rapid experimentation, and a peer network that sees a problem much before an established organizations do. So some knowledge becomes obsolete quickly. In a fast-changing market, being new sometimes means being close to business. Also, experience is not acquired only through employment. Building a real product, serving real users, making mistakes and repairing them can itself be an extraordinary intense form of experience. So if you have a credible opportunity now, delaying automatically can destroy rather than improve it. So the alternative to Graham is not a slogan such as start as early as possible. It is a good question but what do you do? What do you know, see, or have access to that gives you a reason to found now? So perhaps you would have encountered a painful problem repeatedly. I know somebody who had an extreme skin condition and he, through various micronutrients, was able to overcome that. Right. Some micronutrients and a certain process, he was able to overcome that. So he's talking to a friend of his and the person said, \"Why don't you start this? There are others who would have problems like you, and you have solved it. You figured out, you experimented, you have solved it. You understood how these molecules work. You understood how these molecules can get into your system very rapidly. How do they get carried? So you have done experiments and used science and research papers to understand how these micronutrients get absorbed, and you have solved your problem. Maybe there are a whole bunch of others for whom this is a problem as well.\" Went and found out, yes. And he started this nutraceutical company which is doing reasonably well. So sometimes you encounter problems repeatedly. Or you have a co-founder with complementary abilities and mutual trust. Perhaps you've built a small solution and users are using it. Perhaps a technological regulatory shift creates a temporary opening. Perhaps your current life gives you an unusual room to experiment. Who knows, right. So these are the reasons. I always wanted to call myself a founder is a motivation by itself is not a reason that you should start a venture now. This distinction also helps us avoid an unfair conclusion. Readiness does not mean complete knowledge. No founder is fully ready. If readiness requires knowing every answer, nobody would found. If you go back, we did this Uniphore case. And there, if you remember, Uniphore is a Unicorn right now, but they had no idea, right. So if readiness requires knowing every answer, nobody would found. Readiness means that the combination of opportunity, people, motivation, and capacity is strong enough to justify learning through action. The task is not to eliminate uncertainty. It is to decide whether the uncertainty is worth entering right now, and whether founding is the best position from which to learn. So, so pause for a moment and then ask, \"Am I attracted to a problem or mainly attracted to founderhood?\" If the title disappears, nobody admires the choice. Would I still want to spend years on this problem? What if that I'm a founder, that title disappears and nobody admires? Would you still want to spend years on this problem? That question does not settle the decision but it removes a lot of noise from it. So the question that you need to ask is: What are you optimizing for? A founding or a joining decision becomes clearer when you stop asking which option is more prestigious and start asking, what are you optimizing for? You might care about autonomy. You may want to decide what gets built, whom to work with, and which trade-offs to accept. You might care about learning. You may want exposure to customers, products, technology, hiring, sales, and operations at a speed that a classroom cannot reproduce. You might care about financial upside that somebody whom you know had this killer exit and he bought this Lamborghini, or he's going on a holiday, or is now flying in the front of the aircraft. And you may be willing to accept that although it's a low probability, I still do it. You might care about financial stability or family responsibility. You may need to predict. You may need, you may need predictable income or cannot expose saving to an open-ended experience. So none of these motives automatically are noble or ignoble, but each of them have different implications. So founding usually offers greater initial scope to shape direction, but also that transfers the burden of direction to you. It can offer meaningful ownership but ownership is not the same as liquid wealth. It can offer autonomy while making you dependent on customers, employees, investors, regulation, and cash flow. It can accelerate learning but the learning agenda is determined by survival rates than by the template that you are following. So joining usually offers less control over the organization and less ownership. But it can give you focus, colleagues who are ahead of you, a salary, and an accessible to a system that is already in motion. A good role can compress years of learning, and a poor role can force you into very repetitive execution with little knowledge about the context. So this now brings us to Professor Noam Wasserman's very, very popular book called The Founder's Dilemmas. In his book, chapter two, he examines the career decisions to become a founder and personal circumstances and motivations surrounding that leap of faith. So Professor Wasserman's work was the one we used when we said, \"Do you want to be rich? Do you want to be king?\" Which is do you want to create a lot of wealth or do you want to have a control over the organization that you are working with?",
