      "transcript_text": "So a founder who insists on control may actually have very limited resources to create value. And a founder who brings in resources that he or she may not have may actually build a larger kingdom, but surrendering a part of the throne. So this, this is a recurring tension. Not a rule that every decision has two options, but it's kind of recurring. This lens corrects a common fantasy. If I found I will own the company and be free. Customers can say no. Co-founders can degree, disagree. Employees can leave. Investors and board can demand changes. Growth creates obligations that original founder may not anticipate. So your motive therefore matters. If control is central, then high-growth founding may continuously ask you to exchange control for resources. If wealth or scale is important for you, then shared authority should be acceptable. If learning is central, joining a strong team may sometimes produce more learning with less existential pressure. So if you look at Sanjeev Bikhchandani, he left employment in 1990. Build under financial constraint. Bootstrapped for years before Naukri started to grow. The lesson is not bootstrap for 10 years. It is that preserving control has less consequences. Patience, constraint, and a different growth path. Preserving control has consequences. Patience, constraints, and a different growth path. But when you imagine founding, what benefit are you actually seeking? Is it, If it is autonomy, are you prepared for responsibility without boundaries? If it is wealth, are you prepared for dilution and illiquid equity? If it is learning, have you compared the learning available in a strong startup role, right. So it's important in terms of deciding. You may actually even start to say that, \"How do I know? How do I even know? Because I'm confused.\" I think deep inside you should be able to pinpoint what you like. Or maybe ask your friend. Ask your friend who knows you very well. Ask 2 or 3 of them, \"What do you think?\" In case you're confused, right. \"What kind of person am I?\" They'll be able to tell you. The next one, passion. Passion is a overrated term in my opinion. Because when people say that he's very passionate, she is very passionate, goes and gets things done. But the research has shown that passion is a double-edged sword. It helps, but it also distorts, right. So passion is important because it supplies energy, persistence. You'll continue, you won't give up. And then the attention that you need to put onto something because you're passionate about it. It doesn't seem like work. It doesn't weigh you down. It only energizes you. But it also can be a filter. Because once I want to be an entrepreneur and that idea becomes part of our identity, then we want to look at the world around us In order to support the choices that we are already making, right. This is known as confirmatory bias. It makes us seek information. You must have done it in that economics course, behavioral economics course. It makes us seek information that supports what we want to believe. Overconfidence makes us overestimate our judgment or our control. Planning fallacy makes us underestimate the time and effort. \"No, it'll be done. It'll be done.\" Like planning fallacy. Escalation of commitment makes us commit more because we're already invested, right. So there are a lot of biases that starts to kick in. And one thing you need to understand is that when you listen to entrepreneurial stories, we're listening to survivors. So there is what is known as a survivorship bias. We hear from winners. And less often from equally passionate founders who had to shut down the winters. And this hindsight balance bias FKS joining. Today, if we ask, \"Oh, would you have joined Flipkart?\" I said, \"Yeah, yeah, yeah, would have joined Flipkart in a heartbeat.\" But if you go back to the time that Flipkart started, how many people would have said, \"You know what? I'll join Flipkart.\" We had Amagi on campus. How many people have actually gone and joined Amagi? right. So, so we look back and say, \"You know what? If some of these guys have joined these firms very early, they would have been sitting on millions. They would have been sitting on millions because all these startups were close to campus. They might have said that, \"You know what? I would like to join McKinsey because the title is good. The, the sound of that term was good, but joining a tiny startup.\" But then there were so many other startups around the time of Flipkart, which didn't do well, right. So we could always say that, \"You know what? I should have joined Flipkart.\" But then many didn't. So that hindsight bias, I would have done it is, or I should have done it, is also very prevalent. So decision making and all of this predicting about future is all quite confusing. So one way to look at it is if you say that I'm building the future of education. Instead of that, say what do you believe about the user? What do you believe about the problem? What do you believe about the solution? And search for disconfirming evidence. This is where it's very interesting. I think we did speak about it when we said going and talking to customers. Try to prove yourself wrong. What would make you stop this search, change direction, and then reflect? And see if that's what you start to see outside. While we would like to see things that connect to us and connect to our worldview. While exploring, always have this doubt because people tend to give you information that you would like to hear, right. So therefore, talk to people who are willing to disagree with you. So do not wear passion on your sleeve. So passion will have to be supported by evidence. Ask whether your passion helps you attend more carefully to reality or helps you avoid reality, right. Passionate founders some we found are not willing to listen to worldview which is different from theirs They say, \"Ah, you don't know. You're not the one.\" And there are enough stories where founders say, \"Don't believe in anybody. A lot of people told me that this wouldn't work, but I still went on.\" So did you go on hunch or did you go on this little evidence that you seem to have access to which they don't? So don't go on hunch, don't go on identity, don't go on passion because that's your identity. Go with what is the evidence that is being shown.",
