      "transcript_text": "So, what experience actually gives you? So, research by Abhinav Gupta and a bunch of faculty members, they examine immigrants first time entrepreneurs in US, in the US. So, they use variation in green card waiting periods to estimate the effect of the additional work experience they have before they start their ventures. The study shows that startups which are founded by more experienced entrepreneurs, they  do better on funding patents and workforce growth. So, the important mechanism is that team formation. Experienced founders can recruit former colleagues and begin with a larger team. So, this is a very specific US immigrant founder setting. It does not prove that every student should wait, that age itself creates wisdom, or that any job provides useful entrepreneurial experience. There is another research which shows that, this is by Azoulay and his colleagues that they found out if you look at the data across large time periods and look at the entire, or at least a larger part of the universe of who's founding. They found the research finds that successful founders are not very young. This is against the common thought we have had that most successful founders are generally very young. That's not true, that's what these guys say. So, most young founders get into the media, and some of these very large outliers come from dropouts, very young founders. But this data shows that the mean founder age amongst fastest-growing one in a 1000 ventures was 45. Industry-specific experience was strongly associated with success. So, the useful conclusion is not that 45 is the ideal age. It is that experience can build assets relevant to a venture like domain knowledge, exposure to problems, networks, credibility, pattern recognition, and self-knowledge. But then the problem is, like passion, experience is also a double-edged sword. The comfortable job can make inertia a very hard what I would say, existence to break So, you'll start to say a lot of times that, \"Hey, this hasn't worked out then, this hasn't worked out then.\" You go look at the number of ways something has not really worked out. So, experience can harden into assumption that may or may not actually be true. So, large organizations may teach processes designed for scale rather than discovery, right. So, like zero to 1 and 1 to N, that we saw. Remember that they may be good for one to N, may not be good for zero to 1. So, years of worked are not the same as years learned. So, if    you look at most of this module, we're saying  learn. So, what are you learning? How are you learning? And learning is very important, right. So, experience, what are you learning? So, if you look at Falguni Nayar, she has lots of experience. So, she spent 18 years at Kotak Mahindra, and what she did has nothing got to do with the experience about Nykaa's success. But she understands business. She had networks, she had credibility and insane exposure to managerial tasks. She may not have known about a lot of things about tech, a lot of things about how do you do online. But she had an accumulated social capital, accumulated knowledge capital about business that she could tap onto the right kind of networks. So, Kunal Shah has a different path from Falguni Nayar. He did freelance work, he did programming, repeated ventures, pivoted all the time, Freecharge, Sequoia, and then started CRED, now moved from CRED to WhatsApp. So, his career is completely different from Falguni who was eighteen years in the same space. Kunal Shah had multiple and varied experiences in  one long institutional career.",
