          "model_answer": "Experience acts as a double-edged sword in entrepreneurship. On the beneficial side, experienced founders build better ventures because they can recruit former colleagues to begin with a larger team (Abhinav Gupta et al.). They also accumulate domain knowledge, exposure to problems, networks, credibility, pattern recognition, and self-knowledge. Real-world example: Falguni Nayar leveraged her 18 years at Kotak Mahindra to tap into accumulated social and knowledge capital about business, networks, and managerial exposure to build Nykaa, even though she did not have specific tech experience. On the risky side, a comfortable job creates inertia that is very hard to break; founders may fall into the trap of saying \"this hasn't worked out\" repeatedly, and experience can harden into assumptions that may not be true. Large organizations may teach processes designed for scale (1 to N) rather than discovery (zero to 1). Furthermore, years of worked are not the same as years learned; simply working long hours does not guarantee entrepreneurial assets if the learning is not active. Real-world example: Kunal Shah had a different path with varied experiences through freelance work, programming, and repeated pivots (Freecharge, CRED), illustrating that varied and repeated experiences can build assets differently than long tenure in one role.\n\nRubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding of experience as a double-edged sword: 4 marks\n- Inclusion of at least one benefit example (team formation/assets): 2 marks\n- Inclusion of at least one risk/example (inertia/assumptions): 2 marks\n- Proper use of real lecture examples (Nayar/Shah): 2 marks",
