      "transcript_text": "So coming back, so you need to ask yourself, what would I need to learn before I would be unusually well-positioned to found? Technical depth, customer access, regulation, sales, operations. What are the assets that you don't seem to have, or at least in your mind? Then ask, where can you learn it the quickest and in a manner that's most transparent? If it means that the best thing is to jump into water and then learn, so be it. But if you say that, you know what? Let me just go work for a startup. Let me work for a startup for some time and then see how I get this, right. So, waiting is not preparation. So, that is what you all need to learn. So, then we come to the next point. Joining first as an entrepreneurial strategy. So, joining a startup may sometimes be seen as a fallback for people who lack idea or courage. But that's not true. Joining can be a deliberate investment in entrepreneurial capacity. You can learn inside somebody else's experiment while receiving a salary. You can observe hypotheses are becoming products, how customers resist, how teams coordinate, how limited cash shapes priorities. You can develop experience in product or engineering or sales or design or operations or people, and this will then later become your advantage. But then, just because you joined a startup doesn't mean that the learning is going to be automatic. A startup can be very chaotic without being educational. Or your role could be very narrow and you're not learning much. Or the founders may not be entirely transparent and not expose you to all different kinds of contexts. And you may actually be exposed to a whole bunch of not so good organization practices. You may have equity, and the startup kind of folds and that equity mean nothing, right. So, those are all okay. Those are all okay. In case the startups become successful, you have a large label that will work for you. But joining a startup creates a completely different portfolio of risks and returns. So, the founder  he kind of looks at responsibility, identity and ownership in one venture. So, joinee receives cash. They may be he may actually have a bigger value outside, but in that startup he gets cash. He doesn't get equity, he doesn't have control and, but he has an ability to leave. So, the kind of role is very important for you to ask in order to get the learning that you want. So, if you go back to the Flipkart example, I don't think we should ask whether joining Flipkart was a good decision or not. What we then need to ask is, how did they at that point of time assess that I'd like to join Flipkart? Did they get carried away by the seriousness of the founders? Or the fact that, \"Oh, here are a bunch of early customer traction that seems interesting.\" Or the fact that, \"You know what, there is something that happened in the US and they're experimenting, and in India, Flipkart is the only one which is experimenting at the scale, so it's a good place to start.\" Right. But the thing is, they joined with a purpose and not necessarily because they thought they will have equity which will convert into insane wealth later, right. So, we could look at some of the later modules where how your framework and startup diagnosis that you can use. for now, do not ask whether the startup is promising. Ask what particular role will allow you to learn, contribute, and become the person that you would want to. So, you could be in a successful startup, but in a role that didn't give you too much learning. But you could be in a company that fails but created such a lovely learning experience for you that it created capabilities that you couldn't have asked for at a pace that you're so happy with, right. So, company's success and your learning, there is a connection, but they're not identical. So, joining first is very entrepreneurial if you are choosing intentionally. Founding now is entrepreneurial when it is in an opportunity than as an identity. \"I want to be an entrepreneur, so I'm starting.\" No, because customers out there. That you already have users. There is some traction, right. And right now, going and joining somewhere else would mean I'll have to leave all the learning and then come back Right. So, coming back to the first question that we had asked. So therefore, should you found now or join, right. So, found if your opportunity has substance. Found a venture if you have customers, and you can do all of this by yourself. But then again, going back to  our effectual content, you could also give yourself affordable loss. Let me see for 3 months and see what happens, or let me give myself 6 months and see what happens. If nothing happens, I'll go join a startup, right. So, you could also use that effectual principle to say that, \"Okay, the same thing goes on the flip side. Let me go join a startup for 6 months and see what happens. Am I learning? Is it what I think it is?\" The affordable loss in either cases would be very important. So, in order to decide, you could use these 4 evidences. Perhaps opportunity evidence, is the problem real frequent, costly or urgent? We have done. We have listened to entrepreneurs. We have the fair idea of how to do that. Capability evidence, do you have access to what the next stage requires? Starting a venture, do you have anything that the product requires? If you have, how do you know whether you can have lots of customers? How do you know that you have marketing capability? But whatever, but you are looking at capability evidence. Then commitment evidence. So, how do you get commitments from your team, from yourself? Finally, I think you should look at your life context, your affordable loss, your family situation and responsibility, where you are from. And these are all important choices. Do not ignore them and say that you know what? Because it's very mature to start. And you may have heard an entrepreneur say that, \"My family said no. Difficult situation. I didn't have experience. My team was leaving, but I just went through and then I was successful.\" These are the kind of survivorship stories that we listen to. This is passion. And then we say that for you to survive, passion is important. But we don't know whether those who failed also had passion, but kind of didn't work out for them because they're not out there. We don't know who they are. They're just like a stone dropping into a pond. There are a couple of ripples after which it becomes smooth. We don't know that there was a stone that went through, right. So,  use that. Look at some of these. Think deep. Be true to yourself, right. So, ask  right. So, ask yourself, \"What is my next best move?\" Be true to yourself. Ask your friends, ask your family. Maybe your family will support you. Maybe the family will not support you. Talk to them. Get buy-ins from them saying that, \"Look, you know what? I don't mind trying it out for some time and see what happens. If something doesn't happen, this is an affordable loss. I'll go back and join a company.\" And when you join a company and if you want to start some time later, go ask for roles that is going to give you those learnings. Or if you think you could also join a startup that has just raised series seed fund or Series A, which means that they have some buy-in from investors that they're solving very interesting problems. But yet again, look at the role that you're going to get. And that play, that is very important for you to understand what is it that you're going to learn from there, right. So, we'll go on to subsequent modules and see how you can do this systematically. Finally, to end this module, the decision is not, am I a founder or a joinee? Instead, ask, who am I? What I know? What do I need to learn? And what are the available resources today and therefore, what is my best next move?",
