      "notes_markdown": "# Video\n\n## 1. Executive Mental Model & Core Intuition\nThis lecture explores the structural frictions that cause co-founding relationships to fail, using the Zepto story as a case study. The core intuition is that startup conflicts rarely stem from one person being \"bad\"; instead, they arise from default structural dynamics that go unmanaged. Founders often assume that good intentions will suffice, but the transcript emphasizes that without active governance, agreements become fragile and disputes inevitable, even among well-meaning parties. The central dilemma for students is identifying early warning signs of such fragility before committing resources or equity.\n\n## 2. Concise Point-Wise Conceptual Mechanics (Cause & Effect)\n* **Structural Default**: Co-founding relationships fail predictably due to unmanaged expectations, not random events. The Zepto story illustrates this: a third early member, Ansh Nanda, was verbally promised a 20% stake and co-founder title, conditional on staying through a specific date. This promise was not honored, leading to a legal dispute years later.\n* **Signal Visibility**: Early signals of fragility are visible from outside before an offer is accepted. Students should look for whether verbal promises are translated into formal agreements and whether equity commitments are time-conditioned and documented.\n* **Agency Friction**: The transcript implies that the misalignment between verbal promises and actual equity distribution creates agency costs. When one party contributes significant work (developing product, lining up partnerships) without guaranteed equity, the risk of future dispute increases.\n* **Parameter Sensitivity**: If Equity Commitment Certainty ↓, then Probability of Future Dispute ↑. The structural default is that promises made early are often broken later unless actively enforced through documentation.\n* **Boundary Condition**: This structural risk applies across the entire ecosystem and across countries, as predicted by research on founding teams. It is not unique to Zepto or India-specific laws, but a universal early-stage risk.\n\n## 3. Conceptual Distinctions & Versus Matrix\n| Dimension | Concept A | Concept B | MCQ Diagnostic |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Promise Type | Verbal Promise (Conditional) | Formal / Written Agreement | Examiner tests whether students recognize that verbal, time-conditioned promises are fragile and often unenforceable without written documentation. |\n| Founder Equity | Unconditional / Absolute Stake | Conditional / Time-Vested Stake | Examiner tests the distinction between promised equity that is absolute versus equity contingent on staying through a specific date. |\n| Dispute Nature | Bad Person / Malicious Intent | Structural Default / Misaligned Expectations | Examiner tests the module's central claim: disputes can happen without anyone being a \"bad person.\" |\n| Resolution Path | Screen for Trustworthiness | Active Governance / Documentation | Examiner tests whether students understand that screening for trust alone is insufficient; structural mechanisms are required. |\n\n## 4. Key Formulas & Numerical Application\n*Conceptual Session: No mathematical formulas introduced in this lecture.*\n\nThe lecture is qualitative and case-based, focusing on structural signals and governance. No present value, discount rate, or financial mathematics is discussed. The numerical reference is the Zepto valuation (billions of dollars) and the early stake percentage (20%), used only as illustrative examples of scale and promise, not for calculation.\n\n## 5. MCQ Trap Radar & Examiner Gotchas\n* **Trap 1: The Trustworthiness Fallacy** - Assuming that if a person seems trustworthy verbally, the equity deal will be honored. The transcript explicitly rejects this: the Zepto founders likely did not set out to cheat Ansh Nanda, yet the dispute occurred due to structural default, not moral failure.\n* **Trap 2: The \"One-Time Event\" Distractor** - Thinking this kind of dispute is a freak accident that won't happen again. The lecture states this happens at scale across entire ecosystems and countries; it is a predictable pattern, not an outlier.\n* **Trap 3: The Boundary Assumption Trap** - Believing that equity promises made early are binding regardless of time or documentation. The transcript highlights the condition: the 20% stake was conditional on staying through a particular date, and because that condition was not met (or honored), the dispute arose.\n* **Examiner Gotcha**: Questions will likely present a scenario where a founder is promised equity contingent on time, and ask what the critical risk is. The correct answer will focus on the fragility of verbal, time-conditioned promises without written documentation, not on the moral character of the promisor.\n\n## 6. Revision Flashcards\n* **Front**: Why does the Zepto/Ansh Nanda dispute illustrate that co-founding conflicts are a \"structural default\" rather than a moral failing?\n* **Back**: Because the conflict arose from unmanaged expectations and missing written agreements, not because the founders were \"bad people.\" The transcript emphasizes that this pattern repeats at scale across ecosystems.\n\n* **Front**: What early signal should a prospective founding team member look for to assess if an equity promise is fragile?\n* **Back**: Whether the promise is verbal only and conditional on a specific date, rather than formalized into a written, time-vesting agreement.\n\n* **Front**: According to the lecture, why can't students simply screen for trustworthiness to avoid founding team disputes?\n* **Back**: Because disputes can happen without anyone being a \"bad person\"; the risk is structural, not moral. Screening for trust alone is insufficient; active governance mechanisms are required.\n\n* **Front**: In the Zepto story, what was the specific condition attached to Ansh Nanda's promised 20% stake?\n* **Back**: The 20% stake and co-founder title were conditional on staying through a particular date.",
      "mcqs_json": "[]",
      "subjectives_json": "[]",
      "flashcards_json": "[]",
      "slides_json": "[]",
