      "created_at": "2026-11-06 08:57:19",
      "transcript_text": "Hello, everyone. Welcome to the second module of this course. So, last week, we asked the big question: Should you found a startup or join a startup? So, this week, we kind of assume that you already have some kind of an answer to that, at least provincially. Say you're founding or say you're joining, it doesn't matter. You're still choosing who you are building with. If you're joining, you're just choosing it from outside before you have signed anything. So, the question this week isn't, \"Should I found a startup?\" It is, \"Who should I build with? And how do I tell before it's too late whether that's going to work out?\" It's a huge question. Huge question, and it's very important for us to ask. So, I'm kind of assuming right now that you have watched the Zepto video. If you haven't, just pause, go and watch Zepto video. Most of you all kind of know the story. Like,   2 teenagers, not very older than y'all, Stanford dropouts, and they're thinking of quick commerce, and they became a unicorn in a record time. That's the story Zepto tells, that's the story that has become the dominant narrative outside. But there's a part of it that the video kind of leaves out, if you had seen it. Before Zepto was Zepto, it was called KiranaKart, and in its early months, there was actually a third person in the room, a student named Ansh Nanda, who by his own account, was deep into the founding work, which is developing the product, developing the technology for the product, and he says that he even started lining up early retail partnership. He says that he was verbally promised a 20% stake and the title of a co-founder, but that was conditional on staying through a particular date. Thing is that he didn't get that equity. What followed many years later, once Zepto was worth billions of dollars, was a legal dispute, a police complaint, allegations on either sides. Eventually, it was settled privately. We don't know what the settlement was. I don't think we are here to say who was right or who was wrong. We are here because this is not a freak event or event that happens once in a while. It's what the research on founding teams predicts will happen at scale across entire ecosystem and across countries. We'll see why? So, what makes this genuinely uncomfortable rather than just a cautionary story is that nothing about the outcome required anyone involved to be a bad person. Nobody gets up and says they're a bad person Palicha and Vohra didn't sit down one day and say that, \"Oh, we're going to, we're going to cheat this guy.\" But what happened looks closer to a structural default, the kind of things that happens unless the founding team actively works against it. So, this is a much more scarier and a much more dangerous claim that we can't say watch out for those dishonest founders. Because you get  you can't screen for it. You can't just go by, by asking whether someone is trustworthy. Maybe you should. Maybe you should  talk to a bunch of people, get some inputs. But the final decision-maker is yours, and you just need to know what exactly you should look for. So, the question that I want you all to think about is, if you were Ansh Nanda in January '21, deciding whether to join Kirana Mart or not, what could you have looked for that would have told you that this kind of agreement that I was getting into is actually pretty fragile? So, hold on to that thought. We'll probably look at it much later. So, the thing which we have also discussed in couple of other courses is that co-founding relationships, they don't fail as a random act. They don't fail because of, like, some freak issue. These are something that happens so, predictably and for exactly the same reasons that there are signals that this fragility that we are talking about, that the early founding team kind of navigates before it starts to blow up, that something is visible from outside even before you accept an offer.",
