      "transcript_text": "So,  what are we going to do? We're going to do couple of things. First is we'll try to look at evidence on team formation. We'll look at solo versus team and why founders pick who they pick. That's the first. Second is 2 things that founders most always confuse: trust and complementarity, and why this confusion eventually ends up being a very expensive proposition. And third, what can you actually observe while you're standing outside the company, right. These are the three things that we're going to look at in this particular module. And then we will also talk about one specific category of co-founders. This is the couples co-founder. It actually is a double-edged sword. On one side, it can be a tailwind that'll catch your sails and push you very fast across this entire ocean of doing business, scaling ventures. Or it can suddenly flip and become a headwind that's stopping you from moving very fast. So, we thought we should probably also talk a bit about that. This is not just as a cautionary note here, that this is not, like, how do you pick a co-founder? That's not what I'm talking about. I'm not talking about a checklist. \"Okay, you tick these 3 or 4 boxes, and I think you are good to go, and you are kind of safe.\" Team formation is very hard to predict, even with all this research in hand. Many years ago, many years ago, I'm told, I don't know if it is true, but kind of makes sense, that when Infosys went out for investments, there were a couple of investors who passed Infy request to invest in the company because they said, \"You know what? I don't think that the co-founders are going to stick.\" If there's anything that you need to know about Infosys co-founders, is that they were one of the most strongest team ever to set up a company in India, right. So, just because we have this checklist doesn't mean we're going to be right. It's just that we're just being cautious in terms of figuring out to the extent possible. Because the thing is that hindsight is always twenty-twenty. \"Oh, we should have done that. Oh, we should have done that. I should have probably not sold these shares. I should have probably done something else,\" right. Hindsight is always twenty-twenty. But what can we do when things are still uncertain? I think that's what we are trying to see. So, what we are trying to see is the risk that is already sitting inside a founding relationship. Whether you are the one who's founding it or you are the one who's being asked to join a team. The thing is that seeing the risk doesn't mean you're eliminating it or not knowing means that you know what I'm blissfully unaware. You still can join the team. But the thing is you're not walking in the blind, that you have some strategy or fallback or a bunch of heuristics that you can use, like for instance, right. Many years ago, I went to Chandigarh for a conference to ISB, and the day on which we were supposed to fly back to Bangalore, I had a class here the next day. There was fog. This is early December, and most people were saying that, \"You know what? Fog doesn't come this early. It usually comes after 15th or 16th.\" But then doesn't matter whether it technically comes later. The day on which we were supposed to go, there was fog and there were no aircrafts. They said, like maybe next day, next day we're not sure because next day will be fog. So, we said, \"Okay, let's jump into a car and go,\" because we just really didn't see the fog. They said there is fog. Maybe fog was coming in, fog was elsewhere. We just went, hired a car, 4 of us, and said, \"Let's just split it and then go to Delhi. How hard can it be?\" And like 30, 40 minutes, I think an hour into the journey, it was like total fog, right. Darkness was coming. There was this fog. You couldn't see anything. We eventually reached Delhi, and we had an international faculty amongst us. He just refused to see outside. He was all the time on his phone because what he didn't see, he didn't, he didn't think what was risky. So, then I asked the driver, \"What are you following by? Because everything seems so difficult. We don't even seem to notice anything.\" He said, \"You'll have to follow very close to the separator,\" and you see those like the separator between the roads. So, if you're going very close to it, you don't have to go very far, very fast, but you can actually see that this is where the road is going. Makes sense. Thankfully, nobody parked right next to this. Otherwise, we would have banged into it. But while for us, we think, we thought we were driving in blind, the driver had some heuristic to figure out whether we are on the road or not. So, coming back to this, what we talk about is a bunch of heuristics that you can use in order to say that you're not getting into a theme and a relationship without being aware, just being naive.",
