      "transcript_text": "So, the research on team found formation, two distinct strategies they name founders use without realizing that they are choosing one, interpersonal attraction strategy. You pick a co-founder because you like them, trust them, getting along with them, relationship first, business logic second. This is a zepto pattern, and honestly, the pattern for most early founding. Then there is resource-seeking strategy. You pick a co-founder because there's something you lack: capital, technical skills, distribution networks, domain credibility. Relationship comes second. The gap in your capability comes first. Usually what happens is these kind of conversations, what will you bring? This is what I bring. So, you see that, oh, you don't need to do this because I can do it. You need to do something else. That conversation is easier done with strangers than with friends. Neither is like absolutely 100% always correct. It's just that they carry different risks. Interpersonal teams move fast and fight less early and are more exposed to homophily trap we just covered. Skill overlap, blind spot overlap, and things like that. Resource-seeking teams are more likely to be genuinely complementary on paper and more exposed to a different risk. They haven't really tested whether they can actually work together under pressure because the relationship was never the selection criteria. So, a resource-seeking pair can look perfect on a pitch deck, one technical, one commercial, one with domain capability, and then still fall apart in year 2 because they never actually liked each other enough to survive a bad phase together. So, if you are evaluating a company to join, this is a genuinely useful diagnostic question to ask in an interview directly or indirectly. How did the founders actually meet and why did they pick each other? The answer tells you which strategy and therefore which risk profile you are walking into. If the answer is a warm story about friendship with no mention of what skills each person brought, probe gently for the skill question yourself. If the answer is all skills and credentials with no warmth at all, that's worth noticing too. Now the counterexample, because it shouldn't be that you're walking away thinking homophily is always the villain. Harshil and Shashank also met young engineering students at IIT Roorkee, ran a student tech group together before Razorpay even existed. Same college, same circle, same starting conditions like Palicha and Vohra. High homophily on paper. But if you look at what really happened with the roles, from very early on, they split cleanly. Harshil took strategy, external relationship, and outward-facing vision. Shashank took product and technical build. This is not a skill split that comes from being similar. This is a split that comes from 2 people who, despite similar backgrounds, had genuinely different working styles and strengths and were honest enough with each other to divide along the lines rather than both reaching for the same kind of jobs. So, the research to point here is personality and working style diversity, even with an homogenous pair, has a better outcome than sameness. So, the dangerous version of homophily isn't we went to the same college, it's we went to the same college and we think and work identical. And that is nobody's covering the blind spots. You're talking in an echo chamber, that is a problem. So, why did this particular pair pull it off when so many similar background teams don't? They didn't discover the split by accident. They didn't discover it easily. Before Razorpay found its footing, both of them rejected over 100 investors, and the company itself started as a different idea entity, a crowdfunding platform before they abandoned it after listening hard to what their early users actually needed. There's a shared adversity to sit through together. Plausibly, the rejection itself is what forced the role clarity. When nothing is working, we do a bit of everything, stops being survival. You're being forced to figure it out fast, who's actually better at what.",
