      "transcript_text": "Hello again, and we will start module-3. So we kind of ended module-2 with the question, so how do we know when we are outside whether a startup is healthy, and how do we know health of a startup that is inside? So let's start with one question. \"Is Zepto healthy now?\" Not in terms of venture, not in terms of performance. In terms of, like, being as a place that you would like to join. Because a quick recap was Ansh Nanda's equity never got formalized, and it costed him a stake in the company that's now worth billions of dollars. That was the story we opened module-2 with. Here it is resolved. A documented settlement, a nice role split that is held out for years. So  Palicha  now runs strategy and the outward face for the company. Vora runs product. So there's a lot more clarity now than when Ansh was trying to join the particular company. So using the thought processes that we had developed in module-2, this seems like a very healthy bonding team at least on paper. But then Couple of years ago, there was a Reddit post and it claimed to be from a former Zepto employee. It described a culture built around CEO's own schedule. Meetings start at 2:00 AM because  Palicha  doesn't wake up early and they work long too. So, and a claim that experienced professionals avoid the company, so it ends up staffed mostly by people early in their careers. Palicha said he didn't deny the meeting times. He explained. Separately, he admitted that the company used manipulative dark patterns in its app to extract more money from the customers. But that they have since removed these patterns. So now coming back, is it healthy or not to join such a venture? So the honest answer is we're looking at relationships, roles and rewards that need to be discussed with founders early on, and the founders need to talk to each other about their own position. And employees also need to go and ask the founders about these. So reading whether a team has actually built something healthy on every dimension that matters to you, especially what this module is for. Both stories are true. Both are publicly documented, and most students evaluating the company from outside would only encounter one of them depending upon which article or which video they happen to read first. It is not a coincidence. It's not really about Zepto specifically. This is what happens whenever a diagnostic only measures one dimension of a company and treats the result as the whole answer. So how dimensions actually get made once founder has real power? That's an angle that you need to look at. What can you observe about a team from outside, including signals nobody puts in a pitch deck? Whether the environment will actually teach you something or even hard one? What you're personally being offered and what you're personally giving up? And that is a question that is important to answer.",
