      "transcript_text": "And then while we have been saying a lot about from the company point of view, what about you, the candidate? Are you also being fully honest to the companies? So in the last module, we built a framework for evaluating a founding team from the inside. How relationships form, how roles played, how rewards get divided. Today, everything that we're going to do in this module, ask the same three questions from a different seat. You are not deciding who to build a company with. You're deciding whether to hand 2 or 3 years of your career to people who have already made those decisions when you were not in those rooms. That changes what you're actually allowed to check. A co-founder gets to negotiate roles and equity before day one. A joinee mostly inherits whatever structure already exists and has to read it from outside, usually across a handful of interviews and whatever is publicly available. These are the only data that you have that you need to go and make a judgment out of. So the skills that this particular session builds is into reading a healthy team more accurately from outside with less access to what people who build it had. One more thing before we start. Three of the today's five lessons are things you can genuinely act on. Reading attrition, reading equity documentation, reading posh committee. Each one gives you something to do that you need to search, verify, ask specific questions, and negotiate specific terms. You could also look at is this invested? What is the broad composition? Who are the investors? But as a first time joinee, you have no levers, but it's important to know what you're getting into. So Some, I think a little over a year ago or so, Paul Graham wrote an essay. And like any other Paul Graham's essay, it went viral, a lot of discussions. So he based his essay on a talk that Brian Chesky has given at Y Combinator. And Paul Graham's founder mode versus manager mode was the focus of this essay, which you all should read. So his argument ran against decades of conventional management advice that the founder's job, once the company scales, is to hire good people and get out of the way. Which is also what we talked early on when we said effectual and causal, that early days of entrepreneurship is very effectual, and once a company starts to scale, you need to flip it and start to become causal. That's exactly what the conversation is. That the founder's job, once the company has started to scale, is to hire great people as managers and then get out of the way. Graham says that's actually wrong because founders who delegate too early, who trust an org chart that they can't see into, are the ones who watch their companies move away from their mission vision. So Brian Chesky said he actually got some decision-making rights back towards himself when he saw that company was drifting, and he said that made the company very healthy and not worse. So plenty of experienced operators pushback arguing that founder mode is just a fancier name for micromanagement, right. And this debate got heated that it split people who otherwise agree on most things on how to run the company. Some read Graham's essay as permission to control everything, others as a narrow point about a specific stage of company that gets over applied. Both sides are not completely right. And that's why it kind of matters to you. You need to tell these two apart from outside because you can't rely on the label of a founder to get their own behavior. Nobody describes themselves as a micromanager. Plenty of founders describe themselves as being in a founder mode.",
