      "transcript_text": "So another thing that I want to talk to you about is which again, like yesterday we spoke about a very specific case of couples, couple founders. This is also a little bit similar. It's not a generic, but a little more specific in terms of especially if you're a woman. So what does the company structure tell you about how would it actually handle a woman's complaint, a woman's career once they are inside the company, right. So in India, we have this POSH Act, and requires every employer with 10 or more employees to have internal complaints committee and things like that. Even if they don't, even if they're not stipulated, do they have somebody from outside telling how as a mentor, as an advisor, as a company member, do they have somebody coming from outside as a person that one could talk to? So you will have to, I'm not saying that just because they have this committee, that they are great companies, or just because they don't have these committees, they're bad companies, right. A lot of startups, if you go back and look, is very toxic, masculine, geekish world. Like Uber was a case in point, right. And there are multiple such companies where things were not really normal, but a lot of women have to, had to deal with some kind of a toxic environment which would have upset their mental equanimity. They may have continued because it's a good company, they're doing good stuff but at the borders, they may have kind of excused and then gotten used to it. But figure out, figure out, especially how good is it for somebody to join a company where meetings are at 2:00 a.m. Figure out do they go out for, like, parties deep into the night. I'm really not sure if all these are still indicative. It could be a great company, extremely respectful of all employees, have a partying culture, have a very healthy culture. So it's very difficult to discern which has got toxic culture and which hasn't. But watch out for some of these. At least just be careful that you're stepping in, ask another woman who's in the company who may have worked there to figure out how the culture is. Because I think it is important for you to at least do some activities, do some due diligence. Just being cautious. Like, you could look up on their website. Do they have a committee? Is it even something that if you ask them, how do they respond to it? Who are the committee members, if they have a committee. Because it indicates seriousness. It indicates that the founder wants to create a healthy, safe environment for creating value. If they're saying we're going to disrupt a particular industry, startup is stressful. But startup is also extremely motivating, and you can drive, get a lot of dopamine by working on hard-to-solve problems, especially when they're successful. There are the space startups sending rockets. Highly dopamine-inducing. But are those just as comfortable for everybody, right. Like in the other module, we spoke about how things are different for a man and a woman in a relationship when something happens to either of the family, right. So society looks at them differently. They expects differently from each of these persons. So what happens if somebody falls sick? And more often than not, women have told me it kind of falls onto them. So look at some of the cues from women employees in these companies. If the company has a woman co-founder, things are usually much better, right. Or somebody in the leadership team, somebody who's already there. Then at least give you some signal. You could probably even ask them how things are, right. So look out for these cues. Look out for these cues. What if, what if attrition rate is low in this particular company but only women are leaving? So that would indicate, that would give you some cues, right. So see for all of this.",
