      "transcript_text": "So here, is a framework that you can use. Harvard's Amy Edmondson has a interesting framework which you can use instead of general gut feel. So, imagine two axis. How safe people feel raising a problem and how much is actually expected of them. So low on both, you get empathy zone. Nobody is pushed, nobody cares. High safety, low expectation, you get comfortable. Pleasant, but nothing's happening. High expectation, low safety, that you get anxiety. Real pressure. And people hide mistakes instead of surfacing them, so for, nothing gets learned. High on both is where Edmondson's learning zone is, and that is one place where intensity gets converted into skill. So, Google's internal research on what separates its best teams landed on this same idea independently. Psychological safety was the strongest predictor of high-performing teams ahead of who was in those teams. Again, psychological safety that I feel comfortable to express my views, others feel comfortable to express their views, and these are views, not people, right? And that's how Google found separately why, what constitutes a high-performance team, right? So this, then changes how you read a founder fighting, coward-looking startup. It might be the anx, anxiety zone. All pressure and no safety, which tees, teaches you almost nothing except how to survive stress. Or it might be the learning zone, wearing the same costume from outside. Real accountability, mistakes get named, work through rather than, so or it might actually be a learning zone while it still can be the same like there could be real accountability, mistakes that get named and worked through rather than buried. So, it's the same intensity of interaction, not bickering, but talking to each other, but opposite value to y'all joining a company for the first time, right? So, one cue you can use is how does the founder talk about a past failure, either in interview or in a podcast? Do early employees who have left describe specific things they've learned? So, going back to our first topic, the founder who's genuinely in Paul Graham's version of a founder mode, who's deeply involved, but focusing on the decision rather than on the person, that kind of founder is much more likely to be running a learning zone team because that kind of involvement usually comes from fast, honest feedback. So, Ola is the other thing. So, Ola has enough to say that a lot of teams in Ola would be in anxiety zone as far as these accesses go. So, both companies that have very stressful environment of doing a lot, right? Startups, you do a lot. You have to do multiple, multiple tasks, and it's a high activity zone. But there are some startups where you are rejuvenated at the end of the day, although you have worked so hard. But in other startups, right, you are like absolutely devoid of any energy some hours into the day. So, it then depends on whether your, it depends on the founders and the culture. So, if there is a learning zone, you end up learning, doing, progressing, movement, and that itself gives you a lot of warm glow feelings, which kind of snowballs into progress for the startup. On the other side, those that are anxiety-ridden, where people are blamed, things are not going on time, perhaps things outside the control, but somebody is blamed for it, is also a high activity zone, but very toxic, stressful environment, right? So, the stage in which you are in, the stage in which you are in, the rate of learning matters more than pay or title. I don't think you should be looking at how much money you're getting. That to me is actually one of the worst ways in which you can evaluate a company. And for some reason, India, I'm like extremely unhappy about it. If you had looked at the interview that I just did with Vibhanshu, Vibhanshu left a very high-paying startup. And you go back and listen to what he said. And that's what happens in every campus. That's what happens everywhere. And for some reason, a popular media also talks mostly about what's your LPA? What's your LPA? And this is also one of the reasons why I have not been very comfortable with placements in this particular course because it tends to get down these stupid numbers called what are you earning? The s, stage in which you guys are in, money is the least that you need to understand is important. You are time billionaire. Figure out how you're going to get to better learning, better positions. Doesn't mean that you have started small in a small company that you will end up being small in a small company or that you started in a large company means that you will end up becoming its CEO or partner. It doesn't mean anything. It depends on how quick you will learn, how quickly you interact, and how quickly you start to go. That is important for you all. Which kind of startups gives you those learning zones? Who's the manager who you're reporting to? Because you could have a great founder, but a lousy manager, you won't learn anything or you could have a lousy founder and a great manager, you'll still learn a lot, right? So, so don't worry about pay, don't worry about title. Worry mostly about the scope of the work, who are you going to be working with, who's the manager, see what people are saying about that manager, where has that manager worked earlier, right? These are, these are the things, right? So imagine, two offers with the same job title, product analyst. At the large company, they may be owning one metric on one team and waiting for a decision to move through three layers of approval. At a five-person startup, it means owning the whole onboarding yourself, making the call, finding out within a week whether something worked or not. Bureaucracy in a large corporation or coordination in a large corporation is extremely standardised. And the reason why they're doing it is, nobody is indispensable. If somebody leaves, they'll get another person quite similar to him in the same position, and the company moves on. I remember long time asking a PGP student or actually it was a founder who asked a PGP student, \"You have two offers in my company and HP. What do you think your delta add to HP will be? And what do you think a delta add to a company like mine will be?\" Right? \"Here's what you can do. Here's what you can push the company to. Here's what you can own, and this is what you can actually eventually create. Compare that to HP.\" Right? I think that was a convincing argument. Well, the parents were against her joining this company because they said, \"You know, you have no experience whatsoever. Why would you want to join a startup instead of go joining a large corporation? Because large corporations will give you the experiences. If you had three years of experience, we really wouldn't have worried whether you joined a startup or HP, but you have no experience, so don't join a startup. Join a large company.\" Like any young person who was thinking on her, on her own, she chose the small company, right? So, so, so, so, so, so a large company doesn't give you so much of learning experience. So, what you end up doing in your life depends on how you learn to play the cards. Just because you're in McKinsey doesn't mean you're going to be a great guy, end up being CEO. Just because you joined a small little startup on Bannerghatta Road doesn't mean that you're not going to be doing anything worthwhile in this world, right? You have like 40 years of career in front of you with an absolutely amazingly different world than the one that we were in, right? You guys are getting into a world which is, like, so different. AI. Therefore, what you can end up doing is more your personality, more your ability to talk to people, more your ability to get things done. Those are more important. Knowledge is on a tap.",
