      "transcript_text": "So, I just want to talk to you all about something else, right? I listened to the founder, so I also know that side of the story. While we have all been talking as if you are the innocent person and you need to take care about yourself. But that's only half the truth, right? The other half of the truth is that what if you are the one who's misleading the startup? What if you are the one who's misleading the company, right? A lot of them also believe, right, they are starting, they are naive, maybe their first startup. So, you saying, \"Oh, I intend staying for three years, while you have just applied for a doctor, a PA, master's program outside, right?\" You're not being fair. You're not being fair. You're joining the startup, but then you're trying to build something on your own with a friend of yours. Then you're not being true. It's important to put this up front, saying that, \"Look, I'm trying to build something on my own, but it's completely different domain. I'm doing it over blah, blah, blah.\" So, that level of honesty is required as well, right? And it seems to be okay. It seems to be okay, right for joinies to be little bit lax with truth and little bit lax with commitment that you're asking the founder to do. That's not right, right? So, if the founder has spent time, interviewed people, took you in, you leave after four months because either, well, maybe a better job is okay, but you knew from the beginning that you're going to leave in four months. It's actually not very right. You'll bring dispr-disrepute to yourself. You will bring disip-disrepute to this program if you actually do this, because they're not going to hire people from BBA DBE anymore because they say that, \"You know what? Our experience has been not very good.\" So there are a lot of repercussions if you promise something and then go back on your promise. Right? So, do not withhold information if you are sure about it. You actually know, you know that's not going to happen. Then I don't think you should lead the founders on. You should be honest. You should definitely be honest. So, to kind of conclude, we are saying before you're joining, be careful. Apply relationship roles, reward diagnostic from outside. Read attrition culture and learning environment as disti-distinct signals and lot, not as some vague constructs. And also flip the mirror to yourself and say that, say that, \"Am I also being just as true as that particular founder?\" So because, because it really, it really is going to hurt a lot, right? So, so, so there are lots of companies, there are a lot of founders that I know. Maybe some of them are just few years older to you. It goes very badly. It goes very badly, right? So, you be cautious. You be cautious. And the whole lot of things that you can be, to be cautious. Then you be honest to the extent possible, to the extent possible, that, that I'm doing something on my own. I'm likely to get another offer or I'm planning to go abroad, whatever be it. Have some level of honesty. I do understand that in India, la, some of these things are not seen nicely. But it's better to be in that even keel, that if you're expecting honest answers for the questions that you ask about how things are in your company. Did you have a fight? How did that fight go? Have people left you? What are the conditions under which people left you? Have there been disagreements in your organization? How have you dealt with it? So, you're asking a lot of questions, and you're expecting truthful answers from the founders. And when you are asked those questions, questions that they are judging you on, be honest. See you next week.",
