      "transcript_text": "Hi, and welcome back. So, we are here with the 4th and final module of this particular course, and it is called Making the Call. So imagine, this is Thursday evening. You have an offer from a startup you like. The founder was sharp and the product is interesting. The salary is a little lower than what your friend got at a large firm. There are ESOPs though for you, and you have to reply by Monday. Here, your parents will ask, \"Why would you turn down a safe option?\" Your friends will say the startups are the future, and you are left with a simple question: How do I know if this is a good decision? You already have tools to answer it. In module 1, you learned to read founder's motivation using career dilemmas along with rich versus king framework. In module 2, you'll learn to read the founding team. In module 3, you learned the 3 Rs: relationships, roles, and rewards, and how, if you don't talk about these, ends up being elephant in the room. And you also looked at hiring investors and succession. So, in this module, we're going to bring all of it together. By end of this video, you'll have a 5-question checklist for any startup offer. However, if you plan to found a company, you will see how a good candidate will judge you. Yeah? Let's start. So, we're going to start with the Joinees framework. First question is: Who is this founder and what are they optimizing for? Question 2 is: Is this the founding team in equilibrium? Question 3: Is this startup building something real? Question 4: What stage is this company at, and does that match what I need? And the 5th and the most important, most important is: Can I survive the worst case? These are 5 questions. They follow simple order. First the people, then the product, then the company, and then you. You have used some of these in the assignments that we've given you, so let's quickly recap them. Question 1 asks who the founder is and what they're optimizing for. This rich versus king has been seen from outside. A wealth-focused founder will hire experienced leaders. This founder will give up control if it helps the company grow. A control-focused founder will keep decisions close. They mi, they may avoid hiring people stronger than themselves. Both types can build good companies, but they create very different workplaces, and you should know which one you're joining. Question 2 asks whether the founding team is in equilibrium. This is the 3 Rs used as a check. Ask about equity and roles. Ask who decides what. A healthy team answers these questions easily. A team with problems will avoid these questions. And more importantly, team with problems, the 2 founders will give you different answers. So today, we focus on question 3, 4 and 5. So, question 3 is about the product. Question 4 is about timing, and 5 is about what happens to you if things go wrong. This is not to look at it as a score. There aren't no points and to maximize and say, \"Hey, here are the offers that I have. I will rate them on this framework, and at the end of this, I will figure out which company I would like to join.\" Because it's about whatever number, right? This is a heuristic. This is a simple heuristic. This is a thumb rule, right? Simply helps you look at the decision from every side. A startup can fail one question and still be right for you. So, the final decision maker is you. You don't have to say that it ticks all boxes, but if something about it gels and something about it calls you, I think you should go for it, right? But the thing is, you are deciding based on certain criteria and not flying blind. So therefore, you need to know which question it fails and you can decide is that an acceptable risk to take.",
