      "notes_markdown": "# KPIs vs Diagnostics in the Business Context\n\n## 1. Executive Mental Model & Core Intuition\nA dashboard is a small connected set of screens, not a single page containing everything. The core intuition is that business viewers need different levels of detail depending on their objective. The dashboard designer must organize content across multiple pages or tabs so the viewer can navigate efficiently to the specific insight they need. This structure serves many views of the same data without building separate charts for every possible combination.\n\n## 2. Concise Point-Wise Conceptual Mechanics (Cause & Effect)\n* **Building Block 1: Visuals.** The actual charts (line, bar, etc.) and tables that carry the primary content. This is the part most people first associate with the word \"dashboard.\"\n* **Building Block 2: KPI Cards.** Few in number (usually under 10). Prominently placed, mostly near the top of the screen. These show the most important figures the viewer needs the moment they open the dashboard.\n* **Building Block 3: Filters (Slicers).** A control that lets the viewer narrow down what they are looking at. Examples include picking a specific region, a date range, or a product category. When a selection is made, the rest of the charts update to match the chosen filter. This allows one dashboard to serve many different views of the same data instead of needing a separate chart for every combination.\n* **Building Block 4: Navigation.** The mechanism that lets a user move from one page or tab to another. This might be a simple set of tabs, a sidebar menu, or buttons built into the design. The viewer needs an obvious, easy way to get from one screen to the next.\n* **Building Block 5: Hover and Click Interactions.** Small pop-ups or expansions that reveal more detail without redesigning the whole screen. Hovering over a data point can show an exact figure. More complex versions may show a bunch of charts when hovering over a data point. This also helps contain clutter because the detail appears only when actively engaged.\n* **Parameter Sensitivity: Filters → Content Update.** If a user selects a specific Region (Filter) → the Charts and KPIs update to show data only for that Region (Content Change).\n* **Parameter Sensitivity: Hover → Detail Reveal.** If a user hovers over a data point (Interaction) → exact figures or additional charts appear (Detail Disclosure).\n\n## 3. Conceptual Distinctions & Versus Matrix\n| Dimension | KPI Cards | Filters (Slicers) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Purpose** | Shows the most important figures at a glance | Narrows down what the viewer is looking at |\n| **Placement** | Usually few, near the top of the screen | Can be placed alongside visuals or on the side |\n| **Effect of Viewer Action** | Passive viewing; figures are always visible | Active selection; rest of dashboard updates to match selection |\n| **MCQ Diagnostic / Examiner Test** | Tests if student understands what constitutes a \"key figure\" vs. supporting detail. | Tests if student understands how one dashboard serves many views without needing separate charts for every combination. |\n\n## 4. Key Formulas & Numerical Application\n*Conceptual Session: No mathematical formulas introduced in this lecture.*\nThe lecture focuses on the structural building blocks of dashboards rather than quantitative formulas. The emphasis is on design logic, placement, and user interaction patterns.\n\n## 5. MCQ Trap Radar & Examiner Gotchas\n* **Trap 1: The Distractor Trap.** Confusing KPI cards with regular visuals. Examiners may present a chart and ask if it is a KPI card. The trap is assuming any chart is a KPI. KPI cards are specifically few in number (under 10), prominently placed, and show the most important figures at a glance. Regular visuals show trends or distributions and can be numerous.\n* **Trap 2: The \"One-Page\" Fallacy.** Assuming a dashboard is just a single screen with everything on it. Examiners may describe a single-page layout and ask about its structure. The trap is ignoring the professor's explicit point that most real dashboards are made up of multiple pages or tabs. The correct mental model is a small connected set of screens.\n* **Trap 3: The Filter Inertility Trap.** Assuming filters change the underlying data permanently. The trap is thinking the data itself is altered. In reality, filters narrow what the viewer is looking at, and the rest of the charts update to match the selection without changing the source data. The dashboard still serves many views of the same data.",
