      "transcript_text": "We spent the first part of this module entirely on requirements, who we are designing for, what they need, and then the data audit to figure out whether the data can support it, and how we are going to phase the building of the dashboard. Everything from here is about the design itself. Before we jump into design, it helps to have a clear shared picture of what a dashboard is actually made of. And I want to do this without tying it to any particular software. So, you might end up using Power BI in your job, you might end up using Tableau or Looker or something else entirely that doesn't even exist yet. So, the tools will vary. What doesn't vary nearly as much is the underlying structure. So, the handful of building blocks that show up in almost every dashboarding tool that you're going to come across So, here's a common misconception worth clearing up early. A dashboard is not usually just one screen with everything on it. Most real dashboards are made up of multiple pages or tabs, with the viewer moving between them depending on what they need to see. We'll come back to exactly why this matters and how to decide what goes on which page in future sessions. For now, just take away this one idea. Think of a dashboard as a small connected set of screens, not a single page. Almost every dashboard you ever look at, regardless of the tool behind it, is built from the same handful of pieces, and let's just go through these one at a time. The first and the most obvious piece, which we are very familiar with, is the visual itself, the actual charts: line chart, bar chart, and so on, tables and numbers that carry the content. This is the part most people picture when they hear the word dashboard. But it is really just one of the several building blocks, not the whole picture. Next are the KPI cards. Think of the most important figures someone needs to see the moment they open the dashboard. These will be the KPI cards. Now, these are usually few, under 10, usually placed prominently, mostly near the top of the screen. Then you have filters, sometimes called slicers as well. This is a control that lets the person viewing the dashboard narrow down what they're looking at. They may pick a specific region, a date range, a product category, and the rest of the charts update to match whatever has been selected. Now, this is what allows one dashboard to serve many different views of the same data instead of needing a separate chart built for every single combination someone might want to see. There's also navigation. How a user actually moves from one page or tab to another. It might be a simple set of tabs, it may be a sidebar menu, or there may be buttons built directly into the design. Whatever form it takes, the viewer needs an obvious, easy way to get from one screen to the next. And finally, there are hover and click interactions. These are small pop-ups or expansions that reveal a bit more detail without redesigning the whole screen around it. This also helps contain the clutter because it's only when somebody hovers  over it, then they see the additional information. So, hovering over a data point and seeing an exact figure appear is the simplest version of this. The more complex versions may actually show a bunch of charts when you hover over a data point. One last point before we move on. Whether you end up working in Power BI, Tableau, Looker, or something else altogether, you will find these same pieces, which is pages or tabs, KPI cards, filters, navigation, hover interactions, and of course, you have the familiar chart types. The names might change slightly from tool to tool, and the exact clicks needed to build them will certainly differ. But the underlying design thinking, what a dashboard is actually made of and why, stays the same wherever you go. So, now we have a shared picture of what a dashboard is and a shared vocabulary. In the next few sessions, we will dive deeper into the design process.",
