          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The operations page contains four distinct metric categories requiring different chart types. First, order cycle time by fulfillment center is a comparison question across categories; a bar chart sorted highest to lowest with linked trend lines below should be used as the default. Second, on-time delivery percentage by carrier is a comparison question; a bar chart with trend lines and box plots for consistency is appropriate. Third, on-time delivery percentage by region is a comparison question; if there are only four regions, a bar chart is optimal for immediate comparison, but if the goal is identifying geographical clusters, a map with color gradients is the superior choice. Fourth, return rates by fulfillment center and carrier are distribution questions; box plots are the recommended chart to show variability (box width) and identify outliers. The progressive disclosure mechanism resolves overwhelm by establishing default visible charts (the bar charts and trend lines) that provide the primary insights. Secondary charts, specifically the box plots for consistency and the map for geographical analysis, are hidden by default and revealed via interactive buttons (e.g., \"Check performance consistency\" or \"Show geographical clusters\"). This ensures the user is not initially overwhelmed by too many visuals and can drill down into distribution and geographical details on demand, maintaining a clean default view while preserving full analytical capability.",
