      "transcript_text": "So in the last session, we covered how we can trust the data, how to look beyond the visual misleading axis, and how aggregation can hide patterns which are worth looking at. So in this session, we will look at a few more areas, the importance of context, segmentation, et cetera, to get better patterns. So the whole idea here is that continue with the critical mindset when we actually look at dashboards and make decisions. One important caveat, which is often given by people is correlation is not causation. So supposing a marketing dashboard shows a relationship between marketing spend and sales performance across the campaigns. So you can see in this chart, as spend goes up, sales are also going up. Now, does this prove that spend caused the sales? No, because the visualization is only revealing the relationship between the spend and the sales. It doesn't explain them. So plenty of other explanations could produce the exact same pattern. Perhaps campaigns naturally get more budget precisely when a product is already trending. So we tend to invest in products which are, show better sales. So the sales growth is actually driving the spend increase and not the other way around. Perhaps a festival season is boosting both marketing activity and sales at the same time without either one causing the other. So for example, during Diwali time, the campaigns get more budgets, sales also go up. So it does not mean that the spend is actually causing the sales. So if a marketing head concludes spend causes sales looking at this chart and doubles the budget for every campaign, but the true driver actually was seasonal demand, that was the Diwali period, which was actually driving the higher sales. So now post-Diwali, if this manager is going to spend a lot of money, doubles the spends, the company is going to end up spending a lot of money for very little additional sales. Because the true driver was actually the seasonal pattern. So the fix again isn't to distrust every relationship you see, because that's actually quite nice when we look at a dashboard, we see these kind of patterns. It is really to treat the visible pattern as the start of an investigation before jumping to conclusions. So the question to ask is, what could be causing this? Because that can give you insight, which can lead to much better decisions. Sometimes the overall picture looks like there's barely any relationship at all. So in the previous example, we saw that there was a relationship, but it was not a true relationship. This is the other side where the picture doesn't show any relationship until you segment the data. So take a look at an online marketplace looking at promotional discount versus the conversions across all its sellers combined, and they find that there is a weak and unclear relationship overall, as shown in this chart. Now, we split the same data by seller category. So you can see the colours which are showing are for each seller category. Say, electronic sellers versus the fashion sellers. Now, within the electronic segment specifically, you can see a strong, clear relationship is emerging, right. More discounting reliably brings more conversions. But this pattern was always there in the data, but it was not visible to us when all of the categories of sellers were aggregated. So the lesson, again, even when the overall picture looks unclear or unremarkable, don't stop there. Segment the data by category, region, channel, or any other meaningful grouping, and look again. Real patterns are frequently hiding inside an unimpressive average, and these patterns are the ones which can actually give you the insight which can drive much better sales. So this brings us to another important area, which is the context. Now, a number without context tells us very little. So if a dashboard chart shows average delivery delay, 12 minutes, as it's showing here, the next question should be, \"Compared with what?\". So is 12 minutes better or worse than the last time period? How does it compare with a competitor or how does it compare against an internal target? So 12 minutes could be an excellent result or a serious problem. The number alone can't tell you which. So one very important thumb rule in when you look at dashboards or look at any data visualization, ask for a reference point. The reference point could be historical benchmark, it can be an internal target, or it could be a competitive benchmark. But without a reference point, it's absolutely impossible to judge whether something is good or bad. So a chart that only shows the current number with nothing to compare it against is technically accurate but absolutely useless. So this is also why well-designed dashboards often include a small comparison element built directly into the chart. So you do see KPI cards, for example, and they will show an arrow which is up or down compared to the last month's figure. Or they may show a thin trend line, which shows that over the past few weeks or past few time periods, how has the KPI changed? So these are things that we will learn when we design the dashboards, but even when you're consuming the dashboards, because when you join a company, there may be dashboards already available. But always ask for reference points when you look at a number. So let's summarize the main lessons from both these sessions. So visualization is a powerful tool, but dashboards should be interpreted critically. Dashboards are finally observations, they are not conclusions. So before even looking at a chart, you want to verify the quality and completeness of the underlying data, understand the limitations, and look at the data in that context. Remember that charts can mislead through scales, through aggregation, and selective presentation. So drill down into the data rather than relying only on high-level summaries. Use segmentation to uncover patterns, and these patterns may not be easily visible but will become visible the minute you use the segmentation. Finally, always interpret results in context. Ask for benchmarks, ask for targets, and look at data in the context of these reference points. So here's the single idea to take away from both of these sessions. Visualization supports judgment and decisions. It does not replace judgment. So visuals like charts are extremely good at helping us identify the right questions to ask, but they are not by themselves the answer. So the critical thinking still has to come from you. Before jumping to decisions and conclusions, ask the questions that we have talked about so that you take a better decision.",
