          "id": 1,
          "question": "Based on the lecture, explain why placing complementary KPI cards side-by-side is a critical design practice, and use the example of total orders and average order value to illustrate how this arrangement reveals a hidden business story that a single KPI card might mask.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "Placing complementary KPI cards side-by-side is critical because individual KPI cards can mask underlying business dynamics when viewed in isolation. The professor illustrated this using the example of total orders and average order value. If total orders are rising while average order value is falling, the business is likely acquiring more customers at the expense of basket size, with existing customers buying less per visit. A viewer looking only at the GMV card might see a stable number and conclude business is fine, but the side-by-side arrangement immediately makes the opposing trends visible at a single glance. This arrangement transforms the cards from isolated statistics into a narrative tool that flags strategic issues, such as customer acquisition trade-offs, early enough for executive intervention.\n\nModel Answer Structure:\n- Point 1 (2 marks): Acknowledge that individual KPI cards provide limited insight in isolation.\n- Point 2 (3 marks): Explain the specific dynamic revealed by the total orders vs. average order value pairing (customer acquisition vs. basket size).\n- Point 3 (3 marks): Contrast this with the GMV card's potential to mask the issue, showing how the combined view changes the interpretation.\n- Point 4 (2 marks): Conclude that side-by-side placement is a design practice that transforms data into actionable insight.\n\nRubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding: 5 marks (correctly identifying the masking effect and the revealed story).\n- Analytical application: 5 marks (correctly applying the lecture's specific example and explaining the mechanism of revelation).",
