      "transcript_text": "In this module, we discussed the different steps in the design process of a typical dashboard from scratch. This was for a dashboard that would be used by multiple users from different departments. Let's now zoom out and take a holistic view of the end-to-end design process. So, you see the 8 steps which are involved. We'll just recap these 8 steps which lead us to actually the dashboard creation. The first step was all about identifying the key stakeholders and the user segments. We talked about how important it was to know the senior sponsor, the key stakeholders in every department, and the importance of pilot users from each department who will actually work with you. The second step was to gather and consolidate the requirements, and here's where we talked about how to use questioning and different techniques to get the requirements from the users. At the end of this stage, we would have the alignment on requirements and their priorities from all the departments. The third stage was an important stage of data audit and feasibility. So, here's where it becomes very important to align with the IT and the data owners. We look at the requirements and check what out of that is feasible, who owns it, in what format can we get it, and so on. At the end of this stage, we would get the sign-off on the scope for the version one of the dashboard based on, one, requirements which have been specified by the users, and two, the feasibility of those requirements. So, it's really whatever is feasible out of that gets into the scope for version one. The fourth step was developing this whole phase-wise plan based on the user priorities and the feasibility which has been established in the data audit. We would plan by phase saying what is going to be developed in each of the phases. It's very important to get a sign-off from the senior sponsor at this stage and the stakeholders because this then becomes a reference plan against which we are going to develop the dashboard. Once we have this phase-wise plan, we actually get into the design stage. So, the fifth step is about designing the information flow and the hierarchy. So, here's where we are determining how many pages are going to be there, how does the user move from page to page, and within a page, what is important and what can go to the bottom. So, the output of this stage is going to be a roughly sketched wireframe indicating navigation across pages and the hierarchy within each page. Here again, it's very important to get a sign-off from the senior stakeholders and the users to make sure that they are aligned in terms of what to expect from the dashboard. Note that we haven't populated any charts or anything at this stage. All we are doing is to provide the placement and give a structure to the dashboard. The next stage, once we have the sign-off on the wireframe, is really all about chart finalization. So, we are going to populate the wireframe boxes with appropriate charts based on the user requirements. Now, if you recall, we reviewed a whole range of charts which are available. Again, based on the business question and what the user requires, we would choose the right chart. We would also plan the filtering required in each page and across pages as per user requirements. The next stage is really about designing the look and feel of the dashboard. Now, this is based on the brand guidelines and best practice. So, here's where we design a mock-up of the prototype dashboard, which will show the colors, logo or images, charts, filters. So, in terms of the look and feel and structure, this is close to final without the actual data populating it. So, rest of it is as close to final as possible so that we can get a sign-off from the senior sponsor, the stakeholders, and the pilot users, because this is what establishes and manages expectations. They know what they're going to get. The next stage is really ongoing stage. So, it's not really an end stage. So, this is the eighth step, which is ongoing agile development. If you recall, we talked about the fact that a dashboard is a living project. User needs unfold only as they kind of use the dashboard. Also, some of the data becomes available at later stages, and therefore, it's much better to think of a dashboard development as an agile process. So, we would release the test versions to the pilot users in each sprint. Once they approve, then we launch to all the users. It's very important, again, to set up feedback mechanisms so that users can report any data glitches and dashboard bugs, and we continue to fix these as we go along. So, this is really the eight-step process, which kind of takes us from scratch, where we've just started the process from you know, identifying the requirements and from the users, and take it towards completion in terms of ongoing agile development of the dashboard. Now that we have a clear view of the end-to-end design process, we are ready to move to the next module where we'll enter the world of Power BI and learn how to create actual dashboards.",
