          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain why yields on corporate bonds are higher than those on government bonds, referencing the specific risks identified in the lecture.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The yield differential between corporate and government bonds exists because corporate bonds carry two primary risks that government bonds do not: default risk and liquidity risk. Regarding default risk, corporations can fail to make promised payments to bondholders when financial distress hits, whereas government bonds carry virtually no default risk because a government can always print currency to meet its obligations, making it the last entity to default. Regarding liquidity risk, corporate bonds are less liquid than government bonds, meaning they are more difficult and expensive to trade in large quantities at short notice. Investors demand higher expected returns (yields) to compensate for bearing these additional risks. Therefore, the higher yields on corporate bonds serve as compensation for the greater default risk and lower liquidity compared to government bonds.",
