          "model_answer": "The lecture establishes a positive risk-return tradeoff using historical data from 1900 to 2023. US Treasury Bills, having the shortest maturity and zero default risk, exhibited the lowest variability with an annual standard deviation of 2.7% and the lowest average return of 3.4%. US Treasury Bonds, while also free of default risk, carry interest rate risk due to their long maturity (10-30 years), resulting in higher variability (9.3% standard deviation) and a moderate return of 5%. US Common Stocks, subject to market price fluctuations from company performance, carried the greatest risk with the highest variability (19.6% standard deviation) and the highest return of 11.5%. \n\nTherefore, the risk premium exists to compensate investors for bearing additional risk. The risk premium for Treasury bonds is 1.6% (5% - 3.4%), and for common stocks, it is 8.1% (11.5% - 3.4%). Investors require this extra return as compensation for the increased uncertainty and potential loss associated with holding riskier assets. The data confirms that safer assets (T-bills) earn lower returns, while riskier assets (stocks) earn higher returns, illustrating that risk and return are positively correlated in efficient markets.",
