          "model_answer": "Specific risk (also called diversifiable risk or idiosyncratic risk) is unique to a particular company or its industry. For example, a pilot strike affects only the aviation industry and not other industries. This type of risk can be diversified away if an investor holds stocks from different industries. Market risk (also called undiversifiable risk or systematic risk) affects all companies in the economy. For example, COVID-19 affected almost all companies, some more and some less, but all were affected. If investors are well diversified across many industries, specific risk is eliminated through the portfolio's diversification. Therefore, only market risk is relevant for well-diversified investors because specific risk has been diversified away and does not require a risk premium. As the professor illustrated, an insurance company selling policies faces a cash inflow today and future cash outflows when claims are filed, which is a real-world example of a non-standard cash flow pattern, but in terms of risk, the principle remains: market risk is the risk that cannot be diversified away and thus is the only risk priced in the market.\n\nRubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding distinguishing specific vs. market risk: 4 marks\n- Explanation of diversification effect on specific risk: 3 marks\n- Real-world example integration (e.g., industry-specific vs. economy-wide risk): 2 marks\n- Clear, structured presentation: 1 mark",
