          "id": 1,
          "question": "Based on the lecture, explain the difference between ownership transfer and management transfer in family firms, using the Prem G and Marico examples to illustrate how these two dimensions can decouple.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "Ownership transfer refers to the movement of shareholding and voting power from one entity to another. Management transfer refers to the handover of day-to-day operational control and decision-making authority. These two dimensions can decouple; for example, in the Prem G case, management transferred to Rashad Prem G while Prem G retained a 10% share, meaning operational autonomy was achieved without ownership transfer. Similarly, in the Marico case, Harsh Marivala transferred management to professionals (CEO Shagatha Gupta took over), while the Marico family retained close to 60% ownership, allowing them to influence direction despite not running daily operations. Thus, a family can retain ownership control while professionalizing management, or transfer management while retaining strategic influence through shareholding.",
