          "question": "Based on the lecture transcript, explain the three key dimensions used to evaluate succession success in family firms. For each dimension, provide one specific indicator mentioned in the lecture that signals successful succession. (10 marks)",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "**Dimension 1: Family Unity**\n*Indicator*: The family is 'a lot more united,' with bonding having grown and engagement between family members continuing to increase. No conflicts are arising.\n\n**Dimension 2: Firm Performance**\n*Indicator*: Measurable improvements in the firm's growth rate, return on capital, and profit margins. These are 'easier one[s] to measure' as stated in the lecture.\n\n**Dimension 3: Entrepreneurial Engagement**\n*Indicator*: The ability to raise external funding and engage other external stakeholders in driving new entrepreneurial opportunities.\n\n**Rubric**:\n- Correct identification of all three dimensions: 3 marks\n- Accurate specification of a unique indicator for each dimension from the lecture: 4 marks\n- Clear, concise articulation linking the indicator to the dimension: 3 marks\n\n**Examiner Note**: Students must distinguish between the dimensions. A common error is to conflate firm performance indicators (growth/profit) with family indicators (unity). The lecture explicitly separates these as distinct evaluation lenses.",
