          "id": 1,
          "question": "Discuss the three broad aspects of benefits that accrue to a family firm through professionalisation, as outlined in the lecture. Using the Godrej Consumer Products example, explain how professional structures facilitate transitions between family and non-family leadership.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The three broad aspects of benefits are: (1) People and Talent: Merit-based selection and promotion systems create a continuous pipeline of talent and strengthen non-family commitment by ensuring equal reward for equal performance, dismantling artificial glass ceilings. (2) Governance and Discipline: Independent evaluation and incentive systems apply rules uniformly to family and non-family members, preventing double standards. Institutional pressures from regulators and the next generation's isomorphic expectations further reinforce transparency. (3) Strategy and Capital: Professionalisation enables the firm to handle competitive complexity through specialised functions and brings diverse viewpoints to decision-making. Crucially, it lowers perceived risk for external capital providers (banks, PE), easing access to external resources. Regarding the Godrej example: The transition from a professional outside CEO (Vivek Gambhir), who grew market cap from $700mn to $9bn, to a family CEO (Nisaba Godrej) works to strengthen the firm because the professional structure provides the credible governance mechanisms that make such transitions viable and stable, rather than causing disruption.\n\nRubric:\n- Core conceptual understanding of the three benefit clusters (People, Governance, Strategy/Capital): 5 marks\n- Application of the Godrej example to explain transition feasibility: 3 marks\n- Clear linkage between professional structures and transition stability: 2 marks",
