          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain the three-circle governance framework of a family firm as discussed in the lecture. How does the framework address the risk of the \"dinner table becoming the boardroom\"? In your answer, distinguish between the governance concerns of the family room, the boardroom, and the owner room, and explain why governance must be established prior to scaling.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The three-circle framework separates governance into the Family Circle, Business Circle, and Ownership Circle to prevent decision-making friction. In the Family Room, governance concerns love, unity, harmony, and next-generation development. In the Boardroom, governance concerns wisdom, judgment, strategy, CEO hiring, and risk oversight. In the Owner Room, governance concerns power, influence, ownership rights, and documentation of equity. The risk of the \"dinner table becoming the boardroom\" occurs when these spheres blur, and the loudest or most senior person wins the discussion regardless of whether the decision belongs to the family, business, or ownership domain. Governance must be established in advance of scaling because, as the firm grows beyond 8-10 persons, consensus based on handshakes and dinner table conversations fails; written structures are required to sustain the firm and family system.",
