          "id": 1,
          "question": "A marketing team notices that sales increased after they launched a new social media campaign. The team concludes that the social media campaign caused the sales increase. Identify the argument structure and explain why this conclusion may represent a fallacy. In your answer, reference the claim, evidence, and warrant, and discuss what missing information could break the warrant. (10 marks)",
          "model_answer": "**Claim**: The social media campaign caused the sales increase.\n**Evidence**: Sales data showing an increase after the campaign launch.\n**Warrant**: The assumption that the social media campaign is the cause of the sales increase, rather than other external or internal factors.\n**Analysis**: This conclusion may represent a fallacy because the warrant is broken. The evidence (sales increase) is real, but the reasoning link assumes causation without ruling out alternative explanations.\n**Missing Information / Boundary Conditions**: What else could have caused the sales increase? A competitor may have launched a failed product, reducing their sales and making ours appear relatively better. The sales team may have offered a seasonal discount unrelated to the campaign. A major news event may have positively affected the industry overall. Key customers may have made purchasing decisions months prior, before the campaign launched.\n**Rubric**:\n- Core conceptual understanding of claim/evidence/warrant structure: 3 marks.\n- Identification of the broken warrant as the fallacy source: 3 marks.\n- Discussion of plausible alternative causes breaking the warrant: 2 marks.\n- Clear articulation of missing information needed to strengthen the warrant: 2 marks.",
