          "id": 1,
          "question": "In a management team meeting, a junior analyst presents data showing that the company's flagship product has a high churn rate among new customers. The senior manager dismisses the claim by saying, \"You've only been here 6 months, you don't understand this market yet.\" (a) Identify the fallacy committed by the senior manager. (b) Explain why this fallacy is problematic for evidence-based decision making. (c) Describe what a fallacy-free response would look like, referencing the Kodak example from the lecture.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "(a) The senior manager committed an ad hominem fallacy. By attacking the person (the junior analyst's limited experience of 6 months) rather than engaging with the data-backed claim about churn rate, the manager shifted focus from the argument to the individual.\n\n(b) This fallacy is problematic because it substitutes evidence evaluation with status-based dismissal. The validity of a claim should depend on whether the evidence supports it, not on who made the claim or their tenure. As the lecture emphasized with the Kodak example, the company had both the evidence and technical capability for digital photography, but the argument was dismissed based on who was making it rather than the merits of the argument itself.\n\n(c) A fallacy-free response would look at the churn data itself. The manager should ask: How was the churn calculated? Is the sample size large enough? Was the new customer segment properly defined? Could there be another explanation for the pattern? This mirrors the fallacy-free response in the Kodak case, where the question should have been \"Is this a good argument for the digital shift? Does the evidence support it?\" rather than \"Does this person really understand our old legacy business?\"\n\nMarking Rubric:\n- Identifying ad hominem fallacy: 3 marks\n- Explaining why it's problematic for evidence-based decision making: 3 marks\n- Describing fallacy-free response with evidence assessment: 2 marks\n- Reference to Kodak example/analogy: 2 marks",
