          "id": 1,
          "question": "Based on the lecture, explain the diagnostic question a prospective employee should ask a founder about disagreement, and why vague answers are a red flag for company culture. Use real-world examples from the lecture to support your answer.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The prospective employee should ask: \"What happened when someone last told you you were making a mistake?\" This question tests whether disagreement survives contact with the founder. A functional founder mode allows the person to keep their job the next day, preserving open dialogue. The lecture uses Airbnb as a positive example, where the founder took ownership of decisions to reverse drift, stabilizing the team. In contrast, Ola exemplifies dysfunctional founder mode: Bhavish Aggarwal's deep involvement correlates with 13 senior departures over 5 years and 45% turnover in one year. Vague answers claiming general openness without specifics are red flags because the transcript shows that such undocumented claims indicate power is used arbitrarily day to day, and no specific stories of being wrong and changing course have survived confrontation. This suppresses bad news, leading to a revolving door of executives who stop bringing problems to the founder.",
