          "model_answer": "A structured 5-point assessment approach for evaluating founder organizational culture:\n\n1. **LinkedIn Attrition Analysis**: Examine 2-3 year tenure patterns on LinkedIn. Look specifically for employees leaving in less than a year, which the lecture identifies as a red flag for dysfunctional culture where staff cannot challenge the founder.\n\n2. **Alumni Destination Tracking**: Investigate where former employees have gone. If they joined another company, the lecture suggests this is concerning; if they went for education, it is a natural progression. The lecture recommends trying to talk to these alumni for direct insights.\n\n3. **Founder Social Media Pattern Analysis**: Review the founder's social media activity and comments on past employee posts. Look for whether the founder is commenting on former employees' posts, if they are, it suggests the separation wasn't really bad. Digging deep can reveal patterns even in this 'make-believe world'.\n\n4. **Company Size Contextualization**: Adjust analysis based on firm size. With only 6 people, attrition analysis is difficult, but the lecture notes you can still dig into founder socials and past employee interactions. With 30+ people, statistical patterns become more meaningful.\n\n5. **Founder Business Success vs. Culture Separation**: Remember the Ola/Bhavish Aggarwal example. A founder can build a very large business (Ola has over 2 million shareholders holding over 4 billion shares) while still creating a dysfunctional culture. Do not conflate business success with workplace health.\n\nEach data point should be weighed collectively rather than in isolation.",
